1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Emperor of Byzantium 13 Aug 582-22 Nov 602, abdicated, fled, executed
2 CONT Joint rule with Theodosius (his son), 29 March 590 - 22 November 602 A.D.
2 CONT Maurice Tiberius, a successful general, was selected by Tiberius II
2 CONT Constantine as his successor. Although he achieved a favorable peace in Persia
2 CONT and was able to stem the losses of territory in Italy and Africa, much of the
2 CONT Balkans were lost. Focas, a junior officer lead a military revolt against
2 CONT Maurice and was declared emperor in November 602. Maurice and his son and
2 CONT co-emperor Theodosius were captured and murdered.  5 sons
2 CONT Of Roman descent. Spent youth at court of Justin II.  Given crown and
2 CONT princess to wife on Emperor Tiberius's deathbed.
1 NOTE This officer, born in Cappadocian Arabissus, is one of the most important emperors of Byzantium. In spite of Justinian's great successes, the empire was still largely threatened on three fronts: in Italy and Spain by the impending
2 CONC  loss of the last possessions, by the invasion of the Avars and on the Balkan frontier by the Slavs' seizure of territory, and in the East by the Persian wars against the Sassanian Empire. Already in the reign of Tiberius II Const
2 CONC antinus in 581 Mauricius had defeated the Persian Great King Hormizdas IV after a four-year-long campaign. He married Constantia, the emperor's daughter, by whom he had eight children. He took over a bankrupt state, something whic
2 CONC h forced him to extreme parsimony, but earned him a reputation for avarice among the people and the mercenaries.
2 CONT In 584, Byzantium lost Cordoba, its last possession in Spain. He secured the remaining western territories by establishing the exarchates of Ravenna and Carthage, where military and civil responsibility were united in one hand. Th
2 CONC ereby Mauricius started down the road from the later Roman Empire to the middle-Byzantine arrangement of  "themes." In the matter of the ongoing conflict of the imperial church with the so-called "Monophysites" he insisted on reta
2 CONC ining the decisions of the Council of Chalcedon. The historians John of Ephesus and Michael the Syrian describe the harsh actions of the emperor against the Monophysites.  In Edessa 400 monks, who refused to denounce Monophysitism
2 CONC , were executed.  Mauricius was in touch with the Franks and permitted the patriarch John IV, the "Faster," to designate himself ecumenical patriarch, something which led to conflicts with Popes Pelagius and Gregory the Great. Gre
2 CONC gory in vain begged the emperor not to allow John to assume this title, as such a move amounted to the emperor's non-recognition of the Pope.
2 CONT In 593 Gregory negotiated a truce with the Lombards, contrary to the emperor's desire, and in 598 this truce was secured with the help of the exarch. The assumption of the title "king" in Italy by the Lombard Autari in 584 made it
2 CONC  clear that an organized power, with which Byzantium had to reckon, was developing in Italy. Byzantium was completely helpless against the invasion of the Avars under their Khan Boyan, who conquered Sirmium in 582. Following the A
2 CONC vars, the Slavs advanced into the Balkan peninsula; in 586 they were at the gates of Thessalonike and in 587 at those of Adrianople, and settled in the region permanently. For the moment the Avars were kept quiet by paying tribute
2 CONC ; campaigns that lasted years led to no conclusive result; by 600 they were at the gates of Constantinople, but were nonetheless repulsed by the General, Priscus.
2 CONT During the Persian-Byzantine war of 572-591, Hormizd II in the winter of 586-87 sent the East-Syrian (Nestorian) Katholikos to Mauricius. He gave the emperor a creed, which he in turn had had checked out by the Patriarch, who char
2 CONC acterized it as orthodox and without error, and thus they were able to celebrate the Eucharist together. The emperor was also in correspondance with Sabrisho I. , the katholikos' successor. As a result of a quarrel over the throne
2 CONC  in the Sassanian Empire, Maurice intervened  in 591 in the political situation of the East. Chosrau, who had fled to Constantinople, married Mary, who seems to be Mauricius' daughter and in the same year was reestablished on his 
2 CONC throne, with Byzantine help. The peace treaty guaranteed Byzantium Armenia and the eastern part of Mesopotamia. The emperor intended to deport the Armenians who wanted to remain "monophysite." From 591 to 611 there were two Kathol
2 CONC ikoi, one on imperial territory and the other among the Monophysites in Persia. The emperor's success against Persia induced the Georgians to declare their independence. The Georgian katholikos Kyrion decided to reattach the Georg
2 CONC ian church again theologically to the Eastern-Roman Church. The emperor's will in 597 makes clear that he had by no means given up ideas of universal politics: his son Theodosius was to succeed him in the East and his son Tiberius
2 CONC  in Rome, which was to remain the second capital.
2 CONT The campaign of 593 against the Avars took the offensive across the Danube for the first time in a long time. When the emperor, out of parsimony, ordered the troops to winter on the far side of the Danube, the result was a mutiny,
2 CONC  which the officer Phocas took advantage of to seize power in Constantinople, with the help of the circus-factions and the Senate. Mauricius fled to the asiatic side of the Bosporus and was murdered, together with his sons, by Pho
2 CONC cas' partisans. His widow and their three daughters survived by entering a convent. A senseless mutiny led to the fall of an important ruler, who was followed by a regime of terror which was at the end to lead to a political disas
2 CONC ter. The beheaded bodies seem later to have been interred in the cloister of Mamas in Constantinople. Chosrau II used the murder of his father-in-law as an excuse for a war of conquest against Byzantium, which lasted for one year.
2 CONT The main source for the time of Mauricius is the history of Theophylact Simocatta. The emperor left behind him an extensive military handbook, Strategikon, in twelve books, in which he made use of his military experiences. This wo
2 CONC rk includes plans for the recruitment of a peasant militia, which should replace the mercenary armies. Particularly the eleventh book with its characterization of enemy peoples (Franks, Lombards, Avars, Turks, and Slavs) is of eth
2 CONC nographical interest. The handbook also belongs to legal literature, since it contains a list of military delicts and their appropriate punishments. It cannot be decided whether the emperor himself edited the work or merely initia
2 CONC ted it. The emperor was considered a promoter of learning and,  in the Orthodox Church,  is venerated as a saint.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliographys:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Budge, Ernest A.Wallis  (Ed.).  The Chronography of Gregory Abu?l-Faraj Barhebraeus,  London (1932), ND 1976.
2 CONT Dennis, George T.  and  Ernst Gamillscheg, Ernst (Edd.). Maurikios: strategikon, ed. , Wien 1981.
2 CONT Dölger, Franz. Regesten der Kaiserurkunden des oströmischen Reiches, Bd. 1 (565-1025), Berlin 1924.
2 CONT Mihaescu,  H. (ed.). Mauricii Strategicon. Ars militara, Bukarest 1970.
2 CONT Nau, F. (Ed.).  History of St. Maurice, Emperor of the Romans,  Patrologia Orientalis V, Paris 1910, pp. 772-778.
2 CONT Scheffer, J. (Ed.).Arriani Tactica et Mauricii Artis militaris libri duodecim, Uppsala 1964.
2 CONT Schreiner, Peter  (Ed.). Theophylaktes Simokates: Geschichte, Stuttgart 1985
