2 CONC  clear that an organized power, with which Byzantium had to reckon, was developing in Italy. Byzantium was completely helpless against the invasion of the Avars under their Khan Boyan, who conquered Sirmium in 582. Following the A
2 CONC vars, the Slavs advanced into the Balkan peninsula; in 586 they were at the gates of Thessalonike and in 587 at those of Adrianople, and settled in the region permanently. For the moment the Avars were kept quiet by paying tribute
2 CONC ; campaigns that lasted years led to no conclusive result; by 600 they were at the gates of Constantinople, but were nonetheless repulsed by the General, Priscus.
2 CONT During the Persian-Byzantine war of 572-591, Hormizd II in the winter of 586-87 sent the East-Syrian (Nestorian) Katholikos to Mauricius. He gave the emperor a creed, which he in turn had had checked out by the Patriarch, who char
2 CONC acterized it as orthodox and without error, and thus they were able to celebrate the Eucharist together. The emperor was also in correspondance with Sabrisho I. , the katholikos' successor. As a result of a quarrel over the throne
2 CONC  in the Sassanian Empire, Maurice intervened  in 591 in the political situation of the East. Chosrau, who had fled to Constantinople, married Mary, who seems to be Mauricius' daughter and in the same year was reestablished on his 
2 CONC throne, with Byzantine help. The peace treaty guaranteed Byzantium Armenia and the eastern part of Mesopotamia. The emperor intended to deport the Armenians who wanted to remain "monophysite." From 591 to 611 there were two Kathol
2 CONC ikoi, one on imperial territory and the other among the Monophysites in Persia. The emperor's success against Persia induced the Georgians to declare their independence. The Georgian katholikos Kyrion decided to reattach the Georg
2 CONC ian church again theologically to the Eastern-Roman Church. The emperor's will in 597 makes clear that he had by no means given up ideas of universal politics: his son Theodosius was to succeed him in the East and his son Tiberius
2 CONC  in Rome, which was to remain the second capital.
