1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Reign	6 September 628 – 27 April 629 Predecessor	Kavadh II Successor	Shahrbaraz Born	621 Died	27 April 629 (aged 8) Ctesiphon Burial	Meshan Father	Kavadh II Mother	Anzoy the Roman Religion	Zoroastrianism
2 CONT
2 CONT http://bit.ly/1Qsh6RX
2 CONT
2 CONT Ardashir was the son of king Kavadh II (r. 628) and Anzoy the Roman, who was a princess from the Byzantine Empire,[1][2] which made Ardashir less popular among the Iranians who had recently been in a long and devastating war again
2 CONC st the Byzantines. In 628, a devastating plague spread through western Iran, which claimed the lives of half of the population, including Kavadh II himself.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ardashir III (Middle Persian:ArdashirPahlaviName.png, New Persian: اردشیر سوم), (born c. 621 – 27 April 629) was king of the Sasanian Empire from 6 September 628 to 27 April 629.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ardashir was the son of king Kavadh II (r. 628) and Anzoy the Roman, who was a princess from the Byzantine Empire,[1][2] which made Ardashir less popular among the Iranians who had recently been in a long and devastating war again
2 CONC st the Byzantines. In 628, a devastating plague spread through western Iran, which claimed the lives of half of the population, including Kavadh II himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Kavadh II, the Wuzurgan elected Ardashir as his successor, who was only a 7-year-old boy. In reality, however, he exercised little power and his empire was controlled by his vizier Mah-Adhur Gushnasp, whose duty
2 CONC  was to protect the empire until Ardashir became old enough to rule. The Iranian historian al-Tabari says the following thing about Mah-Adhur Gushnasp's administration of the Empire; "Mah-Adhur Gushnasp carried on the administrati
2 CONC on of the kingdom in [such] an excellent fashion, [and with such] firm conduct, [that] no one would have been aware of Ardashir III's youthfulness."
2 CONT
2 CONT During the same period, a brother of Mah-Adhur Gushnasp named Narsi, was granted with Kaskar as a part of his domains,[4] However, even under a strong vizier, things were still looking grim in Iran; Factionalism had greatly increa
2 CONC sed among the Iranians, and several powerful factions which had emerged during the reign of Ardashir's grandfather Khosrau II, had gained firm control of important parts of Iran, while the Sasanian state was less-centralized than 
2 CONC it had been under Ardashir's predecessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT One year later, Shahrbaraz with a force of 6,000 men[1] marched towards Ctesiphon and besieged the city. Shahrbaraz, however, was unable to capture the city, and then made an alliance with Piruz Khosrow, the leader of the Parsig (
2 CONC Persian) faction, and the previous minister of the Empire during the reign of Ardashir's father. Shahrbaraz also made an alliance with Namdar Jushnas, the spahbed of Nimruz.[5] With the aid of these two powerful figures, Shahrbara
2 CONC z captured Ctesiphon and executed Ardashir III, along with Mah-Adhur Gushnasp himself, including other promient Sasanian nobles. Shahrbaraz then ascended the Sasanian throne. According to Islamic sources, Ardashir was buried at an
2 CONC  unknown place in Meshan.
