2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#EutharicusMAmalaswintha Project MedLands: DYNASTY of the AMAL GOTHS]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ATHAL .  Athal had two children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''ACHIULF''' .  Iordanes names "Achiulf et Oduulf" as the sons of Athal[32]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Achiulf had four children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * a)         '''ANSILA''' .  Iordanes names "Ansila et Ediulf, Vultuulf et Hermenerig" as the sons of Achiulf[33]. 
2 CONT * b)         '''EDIULF''' .  Iordanes names "Ansila et Ediulf, Vultuulf et Hermenerig" as the sons of Achiulf[34]. 
2 CONT * c)         '''VULTWULF''' .  Iordanes names "Ansila et Ediulf, Vultuulf et Hermenerig" as the sons of Achiulf[35].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Vultwulf had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * i)          '''VALARAVANS''' .  Iordanes names "Valaravans" as the son of Vultwulf[36].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Valaravans had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * (a)       '''VINITHARIUS''' .  Iordanes names "Vinitharius" as the son of Valaravans[37].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Vinitharius had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * (1)       '''VANDALARIUS''' .  Iordanes names "Vandiliarum" as son of Vinitharius[38]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * d)         '''HERMENRICH''' .  Iordanes names "Ansila et Ediulf, Vultuulf et Hermenerig" as the sons of Achiulf[39].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hermenrich had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * i)          '''HUNIMUND''' .  Iordanes names "Hunimundum" as son of "Hermanaricus"[40].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hunimund had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * (a)       '''THORISMUND''' (-killed in battle [451/55]).  Iordanes names "Thorismundo" as son of "Hunimundus"[41].  Iordanes records that "Thorismundo filius eius" succeeded as King of the Goths after the death of "Hunimundus fi
2 CONC lius quondam regis…Hermanarici" but was killed fighting the Gepids in the second year of his reign[42].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thorismund had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * (1)       '''BERIMUD''' .  Iordanes names "Berimud" as son of "Thorismundo"[43].  Iordanes records that "Beremud…cum filio Vitiricho" left the Ostrogoths to join "Vallia rex Gothorum" [King of the Visigoths in Toulouse][44].  Th
2 CONC e implication of a later passage in Iordanes, which records that "Valamer…ex consobrino eius genitus Vandalario" succeeded as king after "Thorismundo" was killed[45], is that Berimud's departure was triggered after he was passed o
2 CONC ver in the succession. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Berimud had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * a.         '''VETERICUS''' .  Iordanes names "Vetericum" as son of "Berimud"[46]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Vetericus had one child:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>* (i)         '''EUTHARICH (-[522/23]).  Iordanes names "Eutharicum" as son of "Vetericus" and as husband of "Amalasuentham" and father of their two children[47].  Eutharic was adopted by Emperor Justin in recognition of his fat
2 CONC her-in-law's decision to designate him his successor after his marriage.  He was given Roman citizenship and became first consul in 519 as FLAVIUS EUTHARICUS CILLIGA[48].  Wolfram estimates that Eutharich died in [522/23][49].  Jo
2 CONC rdanes specifies that Eutharich predeceased King Theodoric's nomination of his son Athalaric as his successor.  '''married  (515) AMALASUINTHA, daughter of THEODORIC King of the Ostrogoths in Italy & his wife Audofledis of the Fra
2 CONC nks ([493]-murdered [30 Apr] 535).'''  The Chronicle of Cassiodorus records the marriage in 515 of "Theodericus filiam usam dominam Amalasuintam" and "gloriosi viri dn Eutharici"[50]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2.         '''ODWULF''' .  Iordanes names "Achiulf et Oduulf" as the sons of Athal[51]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =EUTHARICH (-[522/23], Ostrogoth Generation 15). =
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Eutharicum" as son of "Vetericus" and as husband of "Amalasuentham" and father of their two children[47].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic was adopted by Emperor Justin in recognition of his father-in-law's decision to designate him his successor after his marriage.  He was given Roman citizenship and became first consul in 519 as FLAVIUS EUTHARICUS CILLIGA[
2 CONC 48].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolfram estimates that Eutharich died in [522/23][49].  Jordanes specifies that Eutharich predeceased King Theodoric's nomination of his son Athalaric as his successor.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (515) AMALASUINTHA, daughter of THEODORIC King of the Ostrogoths in Italy & his wife Audofledis of the Franks ([493]-murdered [30 Apr] 535).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Cassiodorus records the marriage in 515 of "Theodericus filiam usam dominam Amalasuintam" and "gloriosi viri dn Eutharici"[50]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [46] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 77. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [47] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 77 and 123. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [48] Wolfram (1998), p. 328. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [49] Wolfram (1998), p. 521 footnote 490. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [50] Cassiodori Senatoris Chronica 515, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 159. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Eutharic
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eutharic
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic Cilliga (Latin: Flavius Eutharicus Cillica) was a Visigothic prince from Iberia (modern day Spain) who, during the early 6th century, served as Roman Consul and "son in arms" (filius per arma) alongside the Byzantine empe
2 CONC ror Justin I. He was the son-in-law and presumptive heir of the Ostrogoth king Theodoric the Great but died in AD 522 at the age of 42 before he could inherit Theodoric's title. Theodoric claimed that Eutharic was a descendant of 
2 CONC the Gothic royal house of Amali and it was intended that his marriage to Theodoric's daughter Amalasuintha would unite the Gothic kingdoms, establish Theodoric's dynasty and further strengthen the Gothic hold over Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his year of consulship in 519 relations with the East Roman Empire flourished and the Acacian schism between the Eastern and Western Christian churches was ended. Whilst Eutharic was nominally a statesman, politician and so
2 CONC ldier of the Roman Empire, he was also an Arian, whose views clashed with the Catholic majority; as consul enforcing Theodoric's tolerant policy towards the Jewish people, he incurred resentment from the local Catholics, whose tra
2 CONC ditions were less than tolerant.[1] Following disturbances in Ravenna, where Catholics burnt down a number of synagogues, Eutharic's siding with the Jewish people of Ravenna was reported with resentment in a fragmentary contempora
2 CONC ry chronicle.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the death of Eutharic, his son Athalaric briefly held the Ostrogothic throne but died at the age of 18. After Athalaric's death, Eutharic's widow moved to Constantinople where further attempts at establishing a dynasty f
2 CONC ailed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic was born around AD 480 to a noble Visigoth family of the Amali line.[3] Eutharic's ancestry has been traced back through his father Veteric and grandfather Berismund to Thorismund who was king of the Visigoths from 451–45
2 CONC 3.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic grew up in Iberia (modern day Spain) where he had a reputation for being "a young man strong in wisdom and valor and health of body".[5][6] He was later to become the "son in arms" (filius per arma) to the Byzantine emper
2 CONC or Justin I, a role which indicated a part of his early life may have been spent as a soldier.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic's status in both the Gothic and Roman world was elevated by the attentions of Theodoric the Great who he was related to distantly through their mutual connection with Hermanric.[8][9] Hermanric was an Ostrogoth chief who 
2 CONC ruled much of the territory north of the Black Sea. Eutharic was descended through five generations from Hermanric, whilst Theoderic was a descendant of Hermanric's older brother Vultwulf.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT By the late 5th century Theodoric was king of the Ostrogoths, ruling from Ravenna in Italy and a close ally of the Roman Emperor Zeno. Following the death of a rival, Theodoric Strabo, Theodoric the Great received the titles of pa
2 CONC tricius and magister militum from Zeno and in 484 he was appointed consul.[11] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Though there was tension between Theodoric and Zeno's successor Anastasius I, the emperor who followed Anastasius, Justin I, sought reconciliation with Theodoric whose influence in the Gothic world would make him a powerful ally.[
2 CONC 12][13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Having worked throughout his life to establish a kingdom and strengthen relations with both the church and Rome, Theodoric was keen to establish a dynasty. His marriage to Audofleda however had produced only a daughter, Amalasuint
2 CONC ha. Therefore, to achieve his ambitions Theodoric would have to ensure he chose a son-in-law with an ancestry equal in strength to his own. His investigations into the Gothic royal lines, which were by this time widely distributed
2 CONC  across Europe, led him to Iberia. Here he discovered Eutharic, the last heir of a related branch of the Amali, who had recently assumed the regency of Spain.[14][15]
2 CONT
2 CONT More recent studies however suggest that Eutharic's Amali ancestry may have been a deliberate invention on the part of Theodoric to aid his ambitions of establishing dynastic credibility.[16] According to Gesta Theodorici Eutharic
2 CONC  belonged to the Gothic house of Alan rather than the house of Amal.[17] Whilst Jordanes, in his history of the Goths, does make reference to Eutharic's prudentia et virtus, or pride and valour, this too may have been a fabricatio
2 CONC n on the part of Theodoric.[18] Those qualities were recognised as requirements of Gothic ethnographic ideology, expressed in their code of civilitas. It would have been highly beneficial for Theodoric's chosen son-in-law to posse
2 CONC ss them.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT At the court of Theodoric
2 CONT
2 CONT In AD 515 Eutharic answered a summons by Theodoric the Great and moved to the Ostrogothic court at Ravenna in Italy. Here he was given in marriage Amalasuintha, the daughter of the king.[19] It was Theodoric's intention that this 
2 CONC union would create a long-lasting dynastic connection between the previously sundered Ostrogoths and Visigoths. Theodoric also named Eutharic his presumptive heir.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Whilst in Italy, Eutharic played an important political role within Theodoric's kingdom. With a court background he had the ability to serve in government and he was respected by the Romans, who admired his liberality and magnific
2 CONC ence.[20] Catholic writers of the time however indicate that, whilst his father-in-law was renowned for policies of toleration, Eutharic acted more like a "bigoted Arian".[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Consulship
2 CONT
2 CONT In 498, as the Empire's nominal vice-regent in Italy, Theodoric had been granted the right to nominate the Western candidate for each year's consular pair. He was however bounded by a restriction: to select only a Roman citizen fo
2 CONC r the position. To advance Eutharic's standing in the world Theodoric wished him to be made consul for the year 519. To get around the restriction imposed on his nominations, and as a favour to Theodoric, Justin himself nominated 
2 CONC Eutharic.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT The nomination was successful, and in January 519 Eutharic took up the position of Western Consul. By granting him Roman citizenship, accepting him as co-consul and calling him a "son in arms", Emperor Justin I sought to restore t
2 CONC ies with Theodoric, strained during the reign of Anastasius I. He showed further favour to Eutharic by conceding the senior consulship to him.[22] 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is reported that at the celebrations to mark the assumption there were, "magnificent shows of wild beasts procured from Africa"[23] and that a visiting diplomat, the patricius Symmachus, sent by the eastern Imperial court to It
2 CONC aly was, "amazed at the riches given to the Goths and the Romans".[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT During this period Eutharic was eulogised by Cassiodorus in the Senate.[25] In it he compared Eutharic to great consuls of the past. The short Chronicle, which Cassiodorus wrote to congratulate Eutharic on his consulship, is noted
2 CONC  for focusing on Eutharic's accession to a position of high civilian honour, rather than any military victories, as had been more common for past Gothic nobility.[26] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic's time as consul is portrayed largely as a time of prosperity for the western Roman empire with the code of civilitas being promoted. In March 519, the Acacian Schism which had separated the Eastern and Western Christian 
2 CONC churches for the previous 35 years was ended and the churches reconciled.[3][26] In addition to the prosperity felt by the peoples of the Roman empire, Eutharic's year of consulship has also been described as seeming like "[a year
2 CONC ] of bright promise for the Ostrogothic kingdom".[27]
2 CONT
2 CONT The contemporary Catholic chronicle of the Anonymus Valesianus portrays Eutharic in a negative light, connecting him with taking the Jews' side in anti-Jewish disturbances in Ravenna over the Jewish congregation's rights to their 
2 CONC synagogue;[27] the disagreement prompted a conflict between the Arians and Catholics as the Arian Eutharic chose to side with the Jewish people.[28] It is thought that the outrage expressed by the Catholics at this action was in p
2 CONC roportion to Eutharic's being a symbol of the recent reconciliation between the Eastern and Western Churches brought about under the direction of Theodoric.[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic died in 522 at the age of 42, less than three years after his consulship.[29] His death caused problems for Theodoric who never succeeded in his desire to establish a strong Gothic dynasty.[30] Though Eutharic and Amalasu
2 CONC intha had a son, Athalaric born in 518, and a daughter, Matasuntha, the dynasty was never established convincingly.[31] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodoric outlived both Eutharic and Eutharic's son Athalaric, who Theodoric had named his heir in 526. Athalaric's mother Amalasuintha acted as regent for her son following Theodoric's death that year, but Athalaric died in Octob
2 CONC er 534 at the age of 18.[32] 
2 CONT
2 CONT To maintain her power, Amalasuintha brought her cousin, and a nephew of Theoderic, named Theodahad to the throne.[32] Though he was made to swear fealty to Amalasuintha, Theodahad felt insecure and in December 534 had her imprison
2 CONC ed on an island in Lake Bolsena where she was eventually murdered on 30 April 535.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
