1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Amalasuintha of the Ostrogoths=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Theodoric "the Great," king of the Ostrogoths and Audofledis of the Salian Franks
2 CONT * [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#EutharicusMAmalaswintha '''EUTHARICH'''] (-522/23).  Iordanes names "Eutharicum" as son of "Vetericus" and as husband of "Amalasuentham" and father of their two children[47].  Euthari
2 CONC c was adopted by Emperor Justin in recognition of his father-in-law's decision to designate him his successor after his marriage.  He was given Roman citizenship and became first consul in 519 as '''FLAVIUS EUTHARICUS CILLIGA'''[4
2 CONC 8].  Wolfram estimates that Eutharich died in [522/23][49].  Jordanes specifies that Eutharich predeceased King Theodoric's nomination of his son Athalaric as his successor.  '''Married  (515) AMALASUINTHA, daughter of THEODORIC K
2 CONC ing of the Ostrogoths in Italy & his wife Audofledis of the Franks ([493]-murdered [30 Apr] 535).''' The Chronicle of Cassiodorus records the marriage in 515 of "Theodericus filiam usam dominam Amalasuintam" and "gloriosi viri dn 
2 CONC Eutharici".
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalasuintha Amalasuintha, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * In 515, Amalasuintha married Eutharic (c. 480 – 522), an Ostrogoth noble of the old Amali dynasty who had been previously living in the Arian Kingdom of Hispania. Her husband was the son of Widerich (born c. 450), grandson of Be
2 CONC rismund (born c. 410), and great-grandson of Thorismund (died after 400), king of the Ostrogoths c. 400. It was important to Amalasuintha's father, Theoderic the Great, that she marry into a legitimate royal family, lest her own f
2 CONC amily's legitimacy be questioned. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Italy Kings (covering both birth and married families:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#TheodericKingGothsItalyB
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODORIC, son of THEODEMIR King of the Ostrogoths in Pannonia & his concubine Ereleuva --- ([451][262]-Ravenna 30 Aug 526).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Theodericum" as son of Theodemir, in a later passage naming his mother "Erelieva concubina"[263].  Herimannus names "Theodericus, Theodmari filius, rex Ostrogothorum" when recording his arrival in Italy[264].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was sent as a hostage to Constantinople at the age of seven in [459/61], returning in [469/70] to assume control of the part of the kingdom formerly ruled by his uncle Valamir, under his father as overall king[265].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He left Pannonia with his father in [473], settling at Kyrrhos in Macedonia where his father named him as his successor in 474.  However, by 476 Theodoric had moved back across the Danube and settled in lower Moesia in the city of
2 CONC  Novae-Svištov.  While Emperor Zeno was planning to resettle his contingent in Dacia, Theodoric marched westwards to join his relative Sidimund at Durazzo[266].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He marched against Greece in 482 and forced Emperor Zeno to conclude a treaty under which Theodoric was named consul for 484 at Constantinople and given Dacia ripensis and parts of lower Moesia[267].  He acquired Roman citizenship
2 CONC  to serve as consul, adopting the name FLAVIUS AMALUS THEODERICUS[268].  Procopius records that “duce Theoderico” was "patricius" and later was appointed to the "consularem" by Byzantium[269].  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 487, he began an offensive against Constantinople, but was bought off by rich presents brought by his half-sister Amalafrida.  He led the Ostrogoths on the move again in 488 into Italy, where he put King Odovacar to flight in A
2 CONC ug 489.  Theodoric captured Verona, then Milan and Pavia, establishing his capital in the latter[270].  King Odovacar counter-attacked, but was again defeated 11 Aug 490.  He compromised with Theodoric in Feb 493, agreeing to join
2 CONC t rule over Italy, but Theodoric captured and personally killed Odovacar after entering Ravenna[271].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was proclaimed THEODORIC "the Great" King of Italy[272] by the Gothic army in Mar 493, with his capital at Ravenna.  He was recognised as ruler in the west by Emperor Anastasius at Constantinople in 498[273].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations with the Franks, cemented by King Theodoric's marriage in [492], deteriorated somewhat in [506] when the Franks persecuted the Alamanni who were under Theodoric's protection[274].  However, despite the close family ties 
2 CONC with the Visigothic kingdom after his daughter's marriage with the Visigothic king, King Theodoric was unable or unwilling to provide the necessary military support for his son-in-law against the Franks at the battle of the Vouill
2 CONC é in 507[275].  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theodoric acted nominally as regent in the kingdom of the Visigoths 507-526 during the minority of his grandson Amalric King of the Visigoths, but declared himself king of the Visigothic kingdom in 511, although he appointed 
2 CONC his sword-bearer Teudis (who later succeeded as king of the Visigoths) as governor[276].  He annexed the Visigothic territory between the Alps and the Rhône to the kingdom of Italy, and re-established the Gallic prefecture at Arle
2 CONC s in 510[277].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records the death in 526 of "Theudoricus rex Gothorum in urbe Ravenna"[278].  He died from dysentery[279]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([492]) AUDOFLEDIS, daughter of CHILDERIC I King of the Franks & his wife Basina ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours names Audofleda as the sister of King Clovis, recording that she married Theodoric King of Italy[280].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes records the marriage of Theodoric and "Lodoin Francorum regem filiam eius Audefledam" and names her brothers "Celdebertum et Heldebertum et Thiudebertum"[281], although this is presumably an incorrect reference to her nep
2 CONC hews and great-nephew with similar names. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Concubine: ---.  Iordanes records that Theodoric's two daughters were born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis[282].  The name of the concubine of Theodoric is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theodoric & his wife had one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  AMALASUINTHA [Amalswinde] ([493]-murdered [30 Apr] 535).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Amalasuentham" as daughter of Theodoric[283].  Gregory of Tours records that King Theodoric left his wife Audofleda "with a small daughter…Amalasuntha" when he died[284], although this appears misleading with regar
2 CONC d to her age bearing in mind the chronology of events established in other sources.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In a passage which appears to be a complete fabrication, Gregory records that Amalasuntha eloped with one of her slaves, Traguilla, who was later killed by her mother's emissaries who brought Amalasuntha back after "a good beating
2 CONC ".  Gregory then recounts that she murdered her mother by poisoning her communion chalice, but was herself killed by "Theudat King of Tuscany" whom the people had called to rule over them[285].  This story may have been Gregory's 
2 CONC way of justifying King Theodebert's subsequent attack on Italy, the account of which follows in the succeeding paragraph.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was regent in Italy for her son in 526.  Procopius records that “ex filia nepos Atalaricus” succeeded on the death of "Theoderico" under the rule of "Amalasuntha matre"[286].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She renounced the territory north of the River Durance in favour of the Burgundians in 530 in the name of her son[287].  
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of her son in 534, she declared herself AMALASUINTHA Queen of Italy, appointing her cousin Theodahad as co-regent.  Theodahad arrested her end-534 and imprisoned her on an island in Lake Bolsena, where "after spending
2 CONC  a very few days in sorrow, she was strangled in the bath by his hirelings" according to Jordanes[288].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT m (515) EUTHARICH [Eutarico], son of VETERIC & his wife --- (-[522/23]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Eutharicum" as son of "Vetericus" and as husband of "Amalasuentham" and father of their two children[289].  The Chronicle of Cassiodorus records the marriage in 515 of "Theodericus filiam usam dominam Amalasuintam"
2 CONC  and "gloriosi viri dn Eutharici"[290].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic was adopted by Emperor Justin in recognition of his father-in-law's decision to designate him as his successor after his marriage.  He was given Roman citizenship and became first consul in 519 as FLAVIUS EUTHARICUS CILLI
2 CONC GA[291].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolfram estimates that Eutharich died in [522/23][292].  Jordanes specifies that Eutharich predeceased King Theodoric's nomination of his son Athalaric as his successor.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic & Amalasuintha had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)  ATHALARIC ([517/18]-2 Oct 534).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Athalaricum et Matesuentham" as children of "Amalasuentham…de Eutharico viro suo"[293].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 526 as ATHALARIC King of Italy.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius records that “ex filia nepos Atalaricus” succeeded on the death of "Theoderico" under the rule of "Amalasuntha matre"[294].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that "Atalericus nepos eius" succeeded King Th
2 CONC eodoric in 526[295].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes specifies that Athalric was 8 years old when he succeeded his maternal grandfather, under the regency of his mother[296]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)  MATASUNTHA [Mathesuentha] ([518]-).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Athalaricum et Matesuentham" as children of "Amalasuentham…de Eutharico viro suo", specifying that Matasuntha married firstly "Vitigis" by whom she was childless and secondly "Germanus patricius fratruelis Iustinia
2 CONC ni imp"[297].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius records that “Vitigis” married "Matasuntham Amalasunthæ filiæ, maturam iam viro virginem"  to legitimise his position (in 536)[298].  Procopius records that Germanus married secondly "Amalasuntha Theoderici filia Matasun
2 CONC tham” after the death of Vitigis[299].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (end 536) as his second wife, VITIGIS King of Italy, son of --- (-542).  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (542) as his second wife, GERMANUS, nephew of Emperor JUSTINIAN, son of --- (-[551]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theodoric had two illegitimate daughters by his concubine (half-sisters of Amalasuintha): 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  THEODEGOTHA [Thiudigotho].  Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and were ma
2 CONC rried "unam Alarico Vesegotharum et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[300].  Procopius records that “regi Visigothorum Alarico” married "Theoderici…Theudichusam virginem filiam"[301].  m ([494][302]) ALARIC II King of the Visigoths
2 CONC , son of EURIC King of the Visigoths & his wife --- (-killed in battle Poitiers 507). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. OSTROGOTHO ([475/80]-before [520]).  Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and
2 CONC  were married "unam Alarico Vesegotharum et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[303].  Her father arranged her marriage as part of his negotiations for an alliance with the Burgundians.  According to Settipani[304], this marriage too
2 CONC k place soon after Theoderic arrived in Italy.  Wolfram suggests[305] that Theodoric's alliance with the Burgundians was settled in 496.  m ([494/96]) as his first wife, SIGISMUND of Burgundy, son of GONDEBAUD King of Burgundy & h
2 CONC is wife Caratena (-murdered 523, bur Agaune).  He succeeded his father in 516 as SIGISMUND King of Burgundy. 
2 CONT
