1 NOTE {geni:about_me} We don’t know the name of the Empress Theodora’s daughter, and her husband’s identity is uncertain.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was not the wife of https://www.geni.com/people/Anastasius-Roman-Consul/6000000000701038714?through=6000000006877017103
2 CONT
2 CONT Cameron in his “Stemma” shows her three sons as the grandsons of Caesaria, sister of Emperor Anastasius, and Caesaria's husband Secundinus, who was consul in 511. Cameron shows Theodora's husband as an unnamed son either of Caesar
2 CONC ia's son Probus (consul in 502 and alive in 542) or Caesaria's daughter Caesaria, who died in 556.
2 CONT
2 CONT We do know that she was the mother of (at least) three sons, born, say, 535, when she was 20, grandsons of the empress Theodora:
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. Anastasius. Anastasius married Joannina, the only daughter of General Flavius Belisarius and his wife Antonina, [5]
2 CONT *2. John married Georgia, daughter of Antipatra. [4] "The blessed John, who was sprung from the family of the Emperor Anastasius and also was a son of the Empress Theodora's daughter." [6]
2 CONT *3. Athanasius "Athanasius, son of the Empress Theodora's daughter." Also, in a German rendering of John of Ephesus, p269, Schoenfelder notes: "Athanasius appears in Bar-Hebraeus as an intermediary between Ascosnagh and Philoponus
2 CONC : he says: 'At that time the Empress Theodora had a grandson, by name Athanasius. . . .'. Also Michael the Syrian., p197: "Athanasius, grandson of the Empress Theodora."
2 CONT
2 CONT == References 
2 CONT * (The Secret History of Procopius, Chapter 4. Introduction by H. B. Dewing) “The daughter of Theodora is never named in sources despite the mentions of at least three of her sons.”
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anastasius_(consul_517)_ “ He married Theodora, born circa 515, natural daughter of Empress Theodora, although Emperor Justinian I apparently treated her and her son Athanasius as fully legitimate“
2 CONT * https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavius_Anastasius_Paulus_Probus_Sabinianus_Pompeius “ Ils ont eu trois fils: Anastasius, fl. 571, Johannes et Athanasius.”
2 CONT * Foss, C. “THE EMPRESS THEODORA.” Byzantion, vol. 72, no. 1, 2002, pp. 141–176. JSTOR, http://www.jstor.org/stable/44172751. Accessed 16 May 2020.
2 CONT * https://www.ancient.eu/Empress_Theodora/  “Theodora’s daughter from before her marriage to Justinian had three sons and all of these became prominent figures in the Byzantine court.”
2 CONT * Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire, (vol. 3) “ The daughter of Theodora is never named in sources despite the mentions of at least three of her sons.”
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodora_(6th_century)_ “ When Justinian sought to marry Theodora, he could not: he was the designated heir of his uncle, Emperor Justin I, and a Roman law from Constantine's time prevented anyone o
2 CONC f senatorial rank from marrying actresses. In 525, Justin repealed the law, and Justinian married Theodora.[11] By this point, she already had a daughter (whose name has been lost). ”
2 CONT * Croke, Brian, Count Marcellinus and His Chronicle, Oxford University Press, 2001, (ISBN 0-19-815001-6), p. 89.
2 CONT *Martindale, John R., « Fl. Anastasius Paulus Probus Sabinianus Pompeius Anastasius 17 », Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire, vol. II, Cambridge University Press, 1992, pp. 82–83.
2 CONT *Christian Settipani, Continuité gentilice et Continuité familiale dans les familles sénatoriales romaines à l'époque impériale, Linacre College, Oxford University, coll. « Prosopographica & Genealogica », 2000, 597 p. (ISBN 1-900
2 CONC 934-02-7)
2 CONT *The Power Game in Byzantium: Antonina and the Empress Theodora. By James Allan Evans [https://books.google.com/books?id=w3vvoS9akZoC&lpg=PA35&ots=SzwXF6JvRJ&dq=The%20daughter%20of%20Theodora%20is%20never%20named&pg=PA35#v=onepage
2 CONC &q=The%20daughter%20of%20Theodora%20is%20never%20named&f=false GoogleBooks]
2 CONT *https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Byzantium-36: “Theodora's mother Theodora who became an empress, was in earlier life an actress, prostitute. Her daughter was born in this earlier period of time, from, say, 515 when she was 15. It i
2 CONC s most likely during this period that Syrian official Hesebolus, her mother's companion during this period, was the daughter's father, but this is not established.”
2 CONT * Alan Cameron. Duke University, Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies The House of Anastasius. Columbia University, pp 259-276. June 1978. Page 270. Accessed 9 April 2020.  https://grbs.library.duke.edu/article/viewFile/7451/4919 pa
2 CONC ge 17 of PDF shows the husband of Theodora’s daughter as (unnamed) son of Probus.
2 CONT * Byzantine Empresses: Women and Power in Byzantium AD 527-1204 By Lynda Garland.  Routledge, Jan 4, 2002  Page 37. [https://books.google.com/books?id=a5GFAgAAQBAJ&lpg=PA37&ots=ebYN_t_jcF&dq=grandsons%20of%20theodora&pg=PA37#v=one
2 CONC page&q&f=false GoogleBooks]. “Theodora’s illegitimate daughter made a good marriage into the wealthy family of the Emperor Anastasios.”
