1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasius_(grandson_of_Theodora)_
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Athanasius''' was a Byzantine monk and a grandson of Theodora, wife of Justinian I. The main sources about him are John of Ephesus, Michael the Syrian and Bar-Hebraeus.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Athanasius was son to an unnamed daughter of Theodora, wife of Justinian I.[1] John of Ephesus first mentions him as "Athanasius, the son of queen Theodora's daughter".[2] He received a religious education, his mentors being Amant
2 CONC ius and Sergius of Tella. The former was a monk from Edessa, the latter served as a Syriac Patriarch of Antioch from 544 to 546. Both mentors were Monophysites.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Athanasius later became a Tritheite and a disciple of John Asconaghes.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Athanasius left a will. According to its terms Justin II (reigned 565-578) and empress Sophia were his chief heirs. The slaves in his possession would become freedmen, each receiving a small inheritance from their master. Various 
2 CONC small bequest would pass to various friends. But Conon would receive "the sum of ten pounds of gold immediately and an annuity of two pounds of gold for life".[1][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT But Athanasius and Conon had a severe argument and mutually excommunicated each other. Athanasius considered changing his will. But death prevented him from doing so. John records:"Athanasius purposed to change his will, and exclu
2 CONC de Conon from it, but died suddenly: and when his will was opened, Conon took what was written in it, while still excommunicating him who had left him the money."[1][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *1.  Martindale, John R., ed. (1992). The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire: Volume III, AD 527–641. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. ISBN 0-521-20160-8.  Page 147
