2 CONT From the German Wikipedia page on Audofleda (Source / Forrás):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audofleda
2 CONT
2 CONT Audofleda (* um 470; † nach 526) war die Tochter des Merowingers Childerich I. und der Basena von Thüringen und die Schwester des späteren Königs Chlodwig I. und der Lantechild. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sie heiratete 493 den Ostgotenkönig Theoderich den Großen. Diese Ehe war Teil des machtpolitischen Systems Theoderichs gegen Ostrom. Einziges Kind dieser Ehe war Amalasuntha, die nach Theoderichs Tod 526 die Geschicke Italiens für
2 CONC  einige Jahre bestimmte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalasuntha soll um 530 mit dem Sklaven Traguila durchgebrannt sein. Ihre Mutter Audofleda schickte ein Heer. Traguilanis wurde getötet und Amalasuntha an den Hof zurückgebracht. Bald darauf soll Amalasuntha ihre Mutter vergiftet 
2 CONC haben.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Quellen
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregor von Tours, Historiae (Zehn Bücher Geschichten), Buch III, Kap.31
2 CONT
2 CONT Weblinks
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours, History of the Franks Book III Chap.31 (englisch)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.fordham.edu/halsall/basis/gregory-hist.html#book2
2 CONT
2 CONT Einzelnachweise
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. ↑ Historiae, Buch III, Kap.31
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Audofleda:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audofleda
2 CONT
2 CONT Audofleda was the sister of Clovis I, King of the Franks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Theodoric the Great, King of the Ostrogoths (471-526), around 493 AD (exact date unknown).[1] Theodoric sent an embassy to Clovis to request the marriage.[2] This political move allied Theodoric with the Franks, and by
2 CONC  marrying his daughters off to the kings of the Burgundians, the Vandals, and the Visigoths, he allied himself with every major 'Barbarian' kingdom in the West. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodoric and Audofleda had one daughter, Amalasuntha, who was married to Eutharic and had one daughter and one son; Amalasuntha then ruled as Regent/Queen of the Ostrogoths from 526-34.
2 CONT
2 CONT Audofleda was a pagan prior to her marriage, and was baptised at the time of her wedding by an Arian bishop.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ CW Previte-orton. The Shorter Cambridge Medieval History. Cambridge: University Press. 1966.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. ^ A History of Early Medieval Europe 476-911 by Margaret Deanesly, Methuen 1960 page 41
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Jordanes' Getica, on Boudicca's Bard:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.boudicca.de/jordanes4-e.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT (295) Theodoric first granted it and afterwards deprived him of his life. It was in the third year after his entrance into Italy, as we have said, that Theodoric, by advice of the Emperor Zeno, laid aside the garb of a private cit
2 CONC izen and the dress of his race and assumed a costume with a royal mantle, as he had now become the ruler over both Goths and Romans. He sent an embassy to Lodoin, king of the Franks, and asked for his daughter Audefleda in marriag
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT (296) Lodoin freely and gladly gave her, and also his sons Celdebert and Heldebert and Thiudebert, believing that by this alliance a league would be formed and that they would be associated with the race of the Goths. But that uni
2 CONC on was of no avail for peace and harmony, for they fought fiercely with each other again and again for the lands of the Goths; but never did the Goths yield to the Franks while Theodoric lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT LVIII
2 CONT
2 CONT (297) Now before he had a child from Audefleda, Theodoric had children of a concubine, daughters begotten in Moesia, one named Thiudigoto and another Ostrogotho. Soon after he came to Italy, he gave them in marriage to neighboring
2 CONC  kings, one to Alaric, king of the Visigoths, and the other to Sigismund, king of the Burgundians.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "People and Identity in Ostrogothic Italy, 489-554" by Patrick Amory:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=7ndeDi_fwq0C&pg=PA450&vq=eusebia&dq=people+and+identity+in+ostrogothic+italy+%22489+554%22&sig=JutSxo0EK4syKdIKGPleEbqBv5s#v=onepage&q=eusebia&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT Audefleda (=PLRE2)
2 CONT
2 CONT The wife of Theodoric and mother of Amalasuintha; her predecessor possibly only a concubine (Anonyma 1). She was the sister of Clovis, therefore a Frank by birth (AV 63: "de Francis"). It would be useful to know when she married T
2 CONC heodoric in order to determine her religion (since her brother converted to Catholicism in 497 or 506). But it must have been before the Laurentian schism began in 498 (from the order of events around AV64, which mentions the marr
2 CONC iage). Moorhead, "Theodoric", pp 51-52, suggests 493 or shortly thereafter. The question therefore remains open: she could have arrived in Italy as a pagan. Gregory of Tours states that she and her daughters were Arian (Hist. 3-31
2 CONC ), claiming that Amalasuintha poisoned her mother, in a story that is highly suspect (related to the slave Triwila or Traguila). On other matters, see PLRE 2: 185.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The History of Baptism" page 375, On Aspersion:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.archive.org/stream/MN41874ucmf_0#page/n377/mode/1up/search/Audofledis
2 CONT
2 CONT When it became the fashion to erect baptisteries, the practice of consecration was introduced, and a very solemn ceremony it was. Early in the morning of the Saturday before Easter Day [9] and Whitsunday, and in some places of the
2 CONC  Epiphany, divine service was performed in the church, and infants and Catechumens were prepared for baptism in an adjoining chapel. At a fixed time, the bishop in proper habits, preceded by a procession of clergy and the children
2 CONC  of the choir singing, went to the baptistery, which was at some distance from the church. It is particularly remarked by Roman historians that baptisteries were not adjoined to churches til the year 496, and then they stood witho
2 CONC ut the church [1]. The first of this kind was prepared for the baptism of Clovis, King of France (sic), who with his sister Audofledis was dipped three times by the hand of Remigius.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ben M. Angel notes, other sources indicate that their baptisms took place in separate locations, with Audofledis being baptized in Ravenna, and not in Tornai. But if the dates referenced to the "Ordo Romanus Ord. de sabbato sanct
2 CONC o" are in fact meaningful to her date of baptism - which coincided with her date of marriage - it could provide clues as to her actual wedding date in 492: that year, the Saturdays before Easter and Whitsunday were April 5 and May
2 CONC  24, respectively, while the Saturday before the Epiphany was January 4.)
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [9] Ordo Romanus Ord. de sabbato sancto - Rabani Mauri "De instit. clericor" Lib. i. Cap. xxviii. Post haec cosecratur fons, et ad ipsum baptismum catechumenus accedit, et sic in nomine sanctae trinitatis trina submersione baptizu
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] Joan. Bapt. Casalii de veter. Christian. Cap v. De Baptismo.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: Although not specifically mentioned as her death location, the activities of her husband in Ravenna, including the construction of a palace and such, would lead me to support the hypothesis that she died there.
2 CONC  Source, "Ravenna in Late Antiquity" by Deborah Mauskopf Deliyannis:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.cl/books?id=YSLi46ZIEHoC&pg=PA109&lpg=PA109&dq=Audofleda+Ravenna&source=bl&ots=TnrIZvCrJ6&sig=V6hKF1xCc4PExlhGJlIi5LOyUrM&hl=es&ei=_T7nTOvxHsH88Ab69Py1DQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CBUQ6AEwADgK
2 CONC #v=onepage&q=Audofleda%20Ravenna&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodoric was married once.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a concubine in Moesia, name unknown, and had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodegotha (ca. 473 – ?). In 494, she was married to Alaric II as a part of her father's alliance with the Visigoths.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ostrogotha or Arevagni (ca. 475 – ?). In 494 or 496, she was married to the king Sigismund of Burgundy as a part of her father's alliance with the Burgundians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Audofleda in 493 and had one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalasuntha, Queen of the Goths. She was married to Eutharic and had two children: Athalaric and Matasuentha (the latter being married to Witiges first, then, after Witiges' death, married to Germanus Justinus, neither had childre
2 CONC n). Any hope for a reconciliation between the Goths and the Romans in the person of a Gotho-Roman Emperor from this family lineage was shattered.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death in Ravenna in 526, Theodoric was succeeded by his grandson Athalaric. Athalaric was at first represented by his mother Amalasuntha, who was a regent queen from 526 until 534. The kingdom of the Ostrogoths, however,
2 CONC  began to wane and was conquered by Justinian I starting after the rebellion of 535 and finally ending in 553 with the Battle of Mons Lactarius.
2 CONT
