1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kavadh I (also spelled Kaveh or Kavad) was the nineteenth Sassanid King of Persia from 488 to 531. He was crowned by the nobles in place of his deposed and blinded uncle Balash (484–488). 
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time the empire was utterly disorganized by the invasion of the Ephthalites or White Huns from the east. After one of their victories against his father, Kavadh I had been a hostage among them for two years, pending the pa
2 CONC yment of a heavy ransom. In 484 his father Peroz I had been defeated and slain with his whole army. Balash was not able to restore the royal authority. The hopes of the magnates and high priests that Kavadh I would suit their purp
2 CONC ose were soon disappointed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I gave his support to the communistic sect founded by Mazdak, son of Bamdad, who demanded that the rich should divide their wives and their wealth with the poor. His intention evidently was, by adopting the doctrine of the 
2 CONC Mazdakites, to break the influence of the magnates. But in 496 he was deposed and incarcerated in the "Castle of Oblivion (Lethe)" in Susiana, and his brother Djamasp (496–498) was raised to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I, however, escaped and found refuge with the Ephthalites, whose King gave him his daughter in marriage and aided him to return to Persia. In 498 Kavadh I became King again and punished his opponents. He had to pay a tribut
2 CONC e to the Ephthalites and applied for subsidies to Rome, which had before supported the Persians. But now the Emperor Anastasius I (491–518) refused subsidies, expecting that the two rival powers of the East would exhaust one anoth
2 CONC er in war. At the same time he intervened in the affairs of the Persian part of Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I joined the Ephthalites and began war against the Byzantine Empire. In 502 he took Theodosiopolis (Erzurum) in Armenia; in 503 Amida (Diarbekr) on the Tigris. In 505 an invasion of Armenia by the western Huns from the Cauc
2 CONC asus led to an armistice, during which the Romans paid subsidies to the Persians for the maintenance of the fortifications on the Caucasus.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the last years of King Kavadh I, his favorite son Khosrau I had had great influence over him and had been proclaimed successor. He also induced Kavadh I to break with the Mazdakites, whose doctrine had spread widely and cau
2 CONC sed great social confusion throughout Persia. In 529 Mazdaki doctrine was formally refuted in a theological discussion held before the throne of the King by the orthodox Magians, and were slaughtered and persecuted everywhere; Maz
2 CONC dak himself was hanged. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I evidently was, as Procopius (Pers. i.6) calls him, an unusually clear-sighted and energetic ruler (although somewhat bloodthirsty). Although he could not free himself from the yoke of the Ephthalites, he succeeded in rest
2 CONC oring order in the interior and fought with success against the Romans. He built some towns which were named after him, and began to regulate the taxation.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Justin I (518–527) came to the throne in Constantinople, the conflict between the Persians and the Romans began anew. The Persian vassal, al-Mundhir IV ibn al-Mundhir, laid waste Mesopotamia and slaughtered the monks and nuns
2 CONC . In 531 Belisarius was defeated at the Battle of Callinicum. Shortly afterward Kavadh I died, at the age of 82, in September 531. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavadh_I for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavadh_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I, also spelled Kaveh and Kavad (born 449, ruled 488–531), was son of Peroz I (457–484) and the nineteenth Sassanid King of Persia from 488 to 531. He was crowned by the nobles in place of his deposed and blinded uncle Bala
2 CONC sh (484–488).
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 State of the Sassanid Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Mazdaki sect
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Return from exile
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 War and succession
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Effect on Sassanid Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]State of the Sassanid Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time the empire was utterly disorganized by the invasion of the Ephthalites or White Huns from the east. After one of their victories against Peroz I, Kavadh I had been a hostage among them for two years, pending the payme
2 CONC nt of a heavy ransom. In 484 Peroz I had been defeated and slain with his whole army. Balash was not able to restore the royal authority. The hopes of the magnates and high priests that Kavadh I would suit their purpose were soon 
2 CONC disappointed.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Mazdaki sect
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I gave his support to the communistic sect founded by Mazdak, son of Bamdad, who demanded that the rich should divide their wives and their wealth with the poor. His intention evidently was, by adopting the doctrine of the 
2 CONC Mazdakites, to break the influence of the magnates. But in 496 he was deposed and incarcerated in the "Castle of Oblivion (Lethe)" in Susiana, and his brother Djamasp (496–498) was raised to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Return from exile
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I, however, escaped and found refuge with the Ephthalites, whose King gave him his daughter in marriage and aided him to return to Persia. In 498 Kavadh I became King again and punished his opponents. He had to pay a tribut
2 CONC e to the Ephthalites and applied for subsidies to Rome, which had before supported the Persians. But now the Emperor Anastasius I (491–518) refused subsidies, expecting that the two rival powers of the East would exhaust one anoth
2 CONC er in war. At the same time he intervened in the affairs of the Persian part of Armenia and restored Iberia to Iran's effective control.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]War and succession
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I joined the Ephthalites and began war against the Byzantine Empire. In 502 he took Theodosiopolis (Erzurum) in Armenia; in 503 Amida (Diarbekr) on the Tigris. In 505 an invasion of Armenia by the western Huns from the Cauc
2 CONC asus led to an armistice, during which the Romans paid subsidies to the Persians for the maintenance of the fortifications on the Caucasus.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Justin I (518–527) came to the throne in Constantinople, the conflict began anew. The Persian vassal, al-Mundhir IV ibn al-Mundhir, laid waste Mesopotamia and slaughtered the monks and nuns. In 531 Belisarius was defeated at 
2 CONC the Battle of Callinicum. Shortly afterwards Kavadh I died, at the age of eighty-two, in September 531. During his last years his favourite son Khosrau I had had great influence over him and had been proclaimed successor. He also 
2 CONC induced Kavadh I to break with the Mazdakites, whose doctrine had spread widely and caused great social confusion throughout Persia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Effect on Sassanid Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT In 529 Mazdaki doctrine was formally refuted in a theological discussion held before the throne of the King by the orthodox Magians, and were slaughtered and persecuted everywhere; Mazdak himself was hanged. Kavadh I evidently was
2 CONC , as Procopius (Pers. i.6) calls him, an unusually clear-sighted and energetic ruler. Although he could not free himself from the yoke of the Ephthalites, he succeeded in restoring order in the interior and fought with success aga
2 CONC inst the Romans. He built some towns which were named after him, and began to regulate the taxation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kavadh I
2 CONT
