1 NAME Berimud
2 GIVN Berimud
1 NAME Berimud, Counselor to Theodorid I of the Visigoths
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Scythia (Present Ukraine)
1 DEAT
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Iberia (Present Spain)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''BERIMUD''' 
2 CONT s/o THORISMUND (-killed in battle [451/55])
2 CONT *VETERICUS 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#_Toc146273201
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: Although he was born as whatever was the equivalent of a "Prince" among the Ostrogoths, he abandoned the Ostrogoth nation in search of "valor" among the Visigoths. He was passed up to succeed his father Thorism
2 CONC und basically for whatever the reason it was that caused the 40-year Ostrogoth Interregnum under the Huns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The presumption is that Berimud, or Beresmund, was the son of Thorismund, the last King of the Ostrogoths. Wikipedia presumes Thorismund's reign to be 451-453. However, the Getica specifies a 40-year Interregnum between the death 
2 CONC of Thorismund and the rise of Valamir. Valamir's reign is tied to Attila's reign (more specifically, his invasion of Gaul), while Berimud's son is tied to the invasion of the Huns into the Ostrogoth kingdom in the 370s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eutharic
2 CONT
2 CONT Eutharic's ancestry has been traced back through his father Veteric and grandfather Berismund to Thorismund who was king of the Visigoths from 451–453.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. ^ Jordanes, Getica, p. 81
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Getica by Jordanes, as provided by Boudicca's Bard:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.boudicca.de/jordanes4-e.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT XLVIII
2 CONT
2 CONT (251) When he was dead, the Ostrogoths mourned for him so deeply that for forty years no other king succeeded in his place, and during all this time they had ever on their lips the tale of his memory. Now as time went on, Valamir 
2 CONC grew to man's estate. He was the son of Thorismud's cousin Vandalarius. For his son Beremud, as we have said before, at last grew to despise the race of the Ostrogoths because of the overlordship of the Huns, and so had followed t
2 CONC he tribe of the Visigoths to the western country, and it was from him Veteric was descended. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Also from the Getica:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://people.ucalgary.ca/~vandersp/Courses/texts/jordgeti.html#visi
2 CONT
2 CONT XXXIII
2 CONT
2 CONT 173) Now Valia, king of the Goths, and his army fought so fiercely against the Vandals that he would have pursued them even into Africa, had not such a misfortune recalled him as befell Alaric when he was setting out for Africa. S
2 CONC o when he had won great fame in Spain, he returned after a bloodless victory to Tolosa, turning over to the Roman Empire, as he had promised, a number of provinces which he had rid of his foes. A long time after this he was seized
2 CONC  by sickness and departed this life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (174) Just at that time Beremud, the son of Thorismud, whom we have mentioned above in the genealogy of the family of the Amali, departed with his son Veteric from the Ostrogoths, who still submitted to the oppression of the Huns 
2 CONC in the land of Scythia, and came to the kingdom of the Visigoths. Well aware of his valor and noble birth, he believed that the kingdom would be the more readily bestowed upon him by his kinsmen, inasmuch as he was known to be the
2 CONC  heir of many kings. And who would hesitate to choose one of the Amali, if there were an empty throne? But he was not himself eager to make known who he was, and so upon the death of Valia the Visigoths made Theodorid his successo
2 CONC r. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (175) Beremud came to him and, with the strength of mind for which he was noted, concealed his noble birth by prudent silence, for he knew that those of royal lineage are always distrusted by kings. So he suffered himself to remai
2 CONC n unknown, that he might not bring the established order into confusion. King Theodorid received him and his son with special honor and made him partner in his counsels and a companion at his board; not for his noble birth, which 
2 CONC he knew not, but for his brave spirit and strong mind, which Beremud could not conceal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT This ties Berimud's arrival in Iberia to the death of Walia in 419 and the rise of Theodoric (Theodorid) I of the Visigoths, to whom Berimud serves as counselor for the remainder of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodoric_I
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a reference, the names of the Hun Emperors during this period come from a research thread presented by followers of the video game Total War. From research by user "Borztogai Khan" (includes a lengthy list of sources at the end
2 CONC  of his posting): 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.twcenter.net/forums/showthread.php?t=177854&amp;page=3
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Gazan (c.320-c.343) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Djilka (c.343-363) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Bulümar (Balamber) "Sheke" (c.360-378) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Alyp-bi (Uldin) "Alp-abai" or "Arbat" (378-390) 
2 CONT
