1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Ostrogotho==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Theodoric "the Great," king of the Ostrogoths and Unknown woman of Moesia 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDY%20KINGS.htm#Sigismunddied523 Project MedLands - Kings of Burgundy]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SIGISMOND''' (-murdered 523, bur Agaune).  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica names "filius eius Sigismundus rex" when recording that he succeeded after the death of his father[47].  Gregory of Tours names Sigismond as so
2 CONC n of Gundobad, when recording his succession to his father, specifying that he founded the monastery of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune[48], dated to 515[49].  He was co-regent in Burgundy from [501].  He supported the Byzantine emperor an
2 CONC d was awarded the title patricius[50].  He converted to Catholicism and visited Pope Symachus in Rome[51].  He succeeded his father in 516 as SIGISMOND King of Burgundy.  He issued his law-book, Liber Constitutionem, at Easter 517
2 CONC [52].  Gregory of Tours records that Chlodomer King of the Franks invaded Burgundy and captured King Sigismond, who was held prisoner near Orléans but murdered with his wife and children after his brother Gondemar defeated the Fra
2 CONC nks, their bodies being thrown down a well at Saint-Péravy-la-Colombe in the Orléannais[53].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that "Sigimundus rex Burgundionum" was betrayed to the Franks by the Burgundians in 523
2 CONC  and became a monk in France, before being thrown into a well with his wife and children[54].  '''married firstly ([494/96]) OSTROGOTHO of the Ostrogoths, illegitimate daughter of THEODERIC "the Great" King of the Goths in Italy &
2 CONC  his concubine''' --- ([475/80]-before [520]).  Gregory of Tours refers to the first wife of Sigismond as the daughter of Theodoric King of Italy, but does not name her[55].  Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as
2 CONC  the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and were married "unam Alarico Vesegotharum et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[56].  Her father a
2 CONC rranged her marriage as part of his negotiations for an alliance with the Burgundians.  According to Settipani[57], this marriage took place soon after Theoderic arrived in Italy.  Wolfram suggests[58] that Theodoric's alliance wi
2 CONC th the Burgundians was settled in 496.  '''married secondly ---''' (-murdered 523).  Gregory of Tours records that Sigismond's second wife incited her husband to kill her stepson but does not name her[59].  Gregory of Tours record
2 CONC s that King Sigismond was murdered with his wife and children after his brother Gondemar defeated the Franks[60].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that the wife and children of "Sigimundus rex Burgundionum" were t
2 CONC hrown into a well[61].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sigismond & his first wife OSTROGOTHO had two children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * a)         '''SIGERIC''' (-murdered 522).  Gregory of Tours names Sigeric as son of Sigismond and his first wife, specifying that he was maltreated by his stepmother who incited his father to have him strangled[62].  The Marii E
2 CONC piscopi Aventicensis Chronica names "Segericus filius Sigimundi regis" when recording that he was unjustly killed in 522 by his own father[63]. 
2 CONT * b)         '''SUAVEGOTHA'''(495 or later-[after 549]).  Gregory of Tours records that Theoderich King of the Franks married the daughter of Sigismond but does not name her[64].  Gregory does not name the mother of King Theoderic
2 CONC h´s wife, but chronologically it is probable that she was born from King Sigismond's first marriage, which is also suggested by the root "-gotha" in her first name.  Her name is suggested by Flodoard´s history of the church of Rei
2 CONC ms, dated to the mid-10th century.  This source records that "Suavegotta regina" bequeathed one third of "ville Virisiaci" by testament to the church of Reims during the bishopric of Bishop Mapinius, subject to the life interest o
2 CONC f "Teudichildi prefate regine filie", adding that the latter later confirmed the donation during the bishopric of Bishop Egidius[65].  The identification of "Suavegotha regina" as King Theoderich´s wife depends on the identificati
2 CONC on of "Teudchildi" as his daughter which, as explained in more detail in the document MEROVINGIAN KINGS, is uncertain.  The link cannot therefore definitively be made between "Suavegotha" and the daughter of Sigismond King of Burg
2 CONC undy.  Nevertheless, the chronology for such a link is favourable, as the editor of the Monumenta Germaniæ Scriptores edition of Flodoard dates Mapinius´s bishopric to "ca 549-573" and Egidius´s to "ca 573-590"[66].  m ([507/16]) 
2 CONC as his second wife, THEODERICH, son of CLOVIS I [Chlodovech] King of the Franks & his [first wife/concubine] --- ([485]-end 533, bur Metz).  He succeeded his father in 511 as THEODERICH I King of the Franks, based at Reims. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Sigismond & his second unknown wife had [one/two] children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * c)         --- (-murdered 523).  Gregory of Tours records Sigismond's imprisonment "with his wife and sons" by Chlodomer King of the Franks, and their murder with their father[67].  As he implies in an earlier passage that Siger
2 CONC ic was Sigismond's only son by his first marriage, it is assumed that the text means that the king had at least one son by his second marriage.  This appears corroborated by the Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica which records t
2 CONC hat the wife and children of "Sigimundus rex Burgundionum" were thrown into a well[68]. 
2 CONT * 2.         '''GONDEMAR''' (-541).  Gregory of Tours names Gondemar as brother of King Sigismond when recording that he fled following the invasion of Burgundy by Chlodomer King of the Franks but "mobilised the Burgunds and won b
2 CONC ack his kingdom"[69], succeeding as GONDEMAR II King of Burgundy.  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that "Godemarus frater Sigimundi" succeeded as king of Burgundy in 524[70].  He defeated and killed Chlodomer King
2 CONC  of the Franks at Vézeronce, Viennois after the latter invaded Burgundy for a second time[71].  Gregory of Tours records that Childebert King of the Franks and his brother King Clotaire launched a third attack on Burgundy, besiege
2 CONC d Autun and occupied the whole kingdom, deposing King Gondemar II[72], dated to 534. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 3.         '''daughter (-young before 516).'''  Avitus Bishop of Vienne wrote a consolatory letter to King Gundobad on the death of his (unnamed) daughter[73]. 
2 CONT * 4.         '''GUNTHEUCA''' [Gondioque] .  Gregory of Tours names Guntheuca as widow of Chlodomer King of the Franks and records her second marriage with his brother Clotaire, but does not give her origin[74].  Settipani suggests
2 CONC , for onomastic reasons only, that she may have belonged to the Burgundian royal family which, if correct, means that for chronological consistency she may have been the daughter of either King Gundobad or his brother Godogisel[75
2 CONC ].  However, Gregory makes no mention of this in his lengthy description of King Chlodomer's campaigns in Burgundy, an omission which seems surprising if the king´s wife was related to his opponents.  m firstly ([514] or 521) CHLO
2 CONC DOMER King of the Franks, son of CLOVIS I King of the Franks & his second wife Clotilde of Burgundy ([494/95]-killed in battle Vézeronce 21 Jun 524).  m secondly ([524]) as his first wife, CLOTAIRE I King of the Franks, son of CLO
2 CONC VIS I King of the Franks & his second wife Clotilde of Burgundy ([501/02]-Soissons [30 Nov/31 Dec] 561, bur Soissons, basilique Saint-Médard).]   
2 CONT
2 CONT ———-
2 CONT
2 CONT Unattributed summary (please identify who you are - it helps if there is a question later - Ben):
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodoric was married once.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a concubine in Moesia, name unknown, and had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodegotha (ca. 473 – ?). In 494, she was married to Alaric II as a part of her father's alliance with the Visigoths. Ostrogotha or Arevagni (ca. 475 – ?). In 494 or 496, she was married to the king Sigismund of Burgundy as a par
2 CONC t of her father's alliance with the Burgundians. Married to Audofleda in 493 and had one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalasuntha, Queen of the Goths. She was married to Eutharic and had two children: Athalaric and Matasuentha (the latter being married to Witiges first, then, after Witiges' death, married to Germanus Justinus, neither had childre
2 CONC n). Any hope for a reconciliation between the Goths and the Romans in the person of a Gotho-Roman Emperor from this family lineage was shattered.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death in Ravenna in 526, Theodoric was succeeded by his grandson Athalaric. Athalaric was at first represented by his mother Amalasuntha, who was a regent queen from 526 until 534. The kingdom of the Ostrogoths, however,
2 CONC  began to wane and was conquered by Justinian I starting after the rebellion of 535 and finally ending in 553 with the Battle of Mons Lactarius.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel's summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT Argotta has apparently been identified as Ostrogotha, daughter of Theodoric the Great and an unknown woman from Moesia (present southern Romania). 
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://www.third-millennium-library.com/readinghall/GalleryofHistory/ATTILA/45.html
2 CONT
2 CONT "With these views Alberon married Argotta daughter of Theodemir king of the Goths, formed a strict alliance with the Goths, Vandals, Bohems, and Ostrogoths, and by their aid recovered possession of Arduenna, Lower Alsatia, Brabant
2 CONC ia, Cameracum, and Turnacum, and obtained the title of Rex Cameracensis. His chief residence however was in the Nemus Carbonarium, a part of the forest of Ardennes, where he sacrificed to idols and fortified Mons Hannoniae (Mons i
2 CONC n Hainault), as an asylum against the malice of Meroveus. Argotta bore him Wambert, who married a daughter of the emperor Zeno."
2 CONT
2 CONT (No sources to back this at present, even though it's written well... would advise its use only as guidance for now in the search for sourced information.)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT FMG: Illegitimate daughter of Theodoric "the Great" King of the Goths in Italy & his concubin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours refers to the first wife of Sigismond as the daughter of Theodoric King of Italy, but does not name her [Gregory of Tours III.5, p. 165.].  Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters
2 CONC  of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and were married "unam Alarico Vesegotharum et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum” [Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.
2 CONC 1, p. 131.].  Her father arranged her marriage as part of his negotiations for an alliance with the Burgundians.  According to Settipani [Settipani (1993), p. 61, footnote 97.], this marriage took place soon after Theoderic arrive
2 CONC d in Italy.  Wolfram suggests [Wolfram (1998), p. 311.] that Theodoric's alliance with the Burgundians was settled in 496.
