2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Theodoric the Great: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodoric_the_Great
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodoric was married once.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a concubine in Moesia, name unknown, and had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Theodegotha (ca. 473 – ?). In 494, she was married to Alaric II as a part of her father's alliance with the Visigoths.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Ostrogotha or Arevagni (ca. 475 – ?). In 494 or 496, she was married to the king Sigismund of Burgundy as a part of her father's alliance with the Burgundians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married to Audofleda in 493 and had one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Amalasuntha, Queen of the Goths. She was married to Eutharic and had two children: Athalaric and Matasuntha (the latter being married to Witiges first, then, after Witiges' death, married to Germanus Justinus, neither had child
2 CONC ren). Any hope for a reconciliation between the Goths and the Romans in the person of a Gotho-Roman Emperor from this family lineage was shattered.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death in Ravenna in 526, Theodoric was succeeded by his grandson Athalaric. Athalaric was at first represented by his mother Amalasuntha, who was a regent queen from 526 until 534. The kingdom of the Ostrogoths, however,
2 CONC  began to wane and was conquered by Justinian I starting after the rebellion of 535 and finally ending in 553 with the Battle of Mons Lactarius.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Spanish Wikipedia page on Teodegonda: http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teodegonda
2 CONT
2 CONT Teodegonda o Teodegoda fue hija del rey ostrogodo Teodorico el Grande, y esposa de Alarico II, rey visigodo, con quién se casó para establecer un vínculo más fuerte entre el Reino Ostrogodo y el Reino Visigodo, necesario para comb
2 CONC atir a los francos.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Asimismo, fue madre del rey Amalarico.
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Teodegonda or Teodegoda was the daughter of Theodoric the Great, King of the Ostrogoths, and wife of Alaric II, King of the Visigoths, with whom her marriage was arranged to establish stronger ties that would reunite the Ostrogoth
2 CONC s and Visigoths against the Franks. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also the mother of King Amalric .
2 CONT
