2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Amalafrida:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalafrida
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalafrida was the daughter of Theodemir, king of the Ostrogoths, and his wife Erelieva. She was the sister of Theodoric the Great, and mother of Theodahad, both of whom also were kings of the Ostrogoths.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 500, to further cement his authority over the Vandals, Theodoric arranged a marriage alliance with Thrasamund, king of the Vandals, who became her second husband. She brought a very large dowery, but also 5,000 Gothic troops. I
2 CONC n 523, Hilderic, successor to Thrasamund, had her arrested and imprisoned in a successful bid to overthrow Ostrogothic hegemony; he also had her Gothic troops killed. She died in prison, exact date unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalafrida had two children, the aforementioned Theodahad and Amalaberga, who married Hermanfrid, king of the Thuringii. It is not known who the father of these children was.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Heather. The Goths (1996). Chapter 8.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Jordanes' Getica, on the Boudicca's Bard website:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.boudicca.de/jordanes4-e.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT LVIII
2 CONT
2 CONT (297) Now before he had a child from Audefleda, Theodoric had children of a concubine, daughters begotten in Moesia, one named Thiudigoto and another Ostrogotho. Soon after he came to Italy, he gave them in marriage to neighboring
2 CONC  kings, one to Alaric, king of the Visigoths, and the other to Sigismund, king of the Burgundians.
2 CONT
2 CONT (298) Now Alaric begat Amalaric. While his grandfather Theodoric cared for and protected him-- for he had lost both parents in the years of childhood--he found that Eutharic, the son of Veteric, grandchild of Beremud and Thorismud
2 CONC , and a descendant of the race of the Amali, was living in Spain, a young man strong in wisdom and valor and health of body. Theodoric sent for him and gave him his daughter Amalasuentha in marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT (299) And that he might extend his family as much as possible, he sent his sister Amalafrida ( the mother of Theodahad, who was afterwards king) to Africa as wife of Thrasamund, king of the Vandals, and her daughter Amalaberga, wh
2 CONC o was his own niece, he united with Herminefred, king of the Thuringians.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Theodoric the Goth: The Barbarian Champion of Civilisation":
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.gutenberg.org/files/20063/20063-h/20063-h.htm#p292
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 183: Westward from the Empire, along the southern shore of the Mediterranean, stretches the great kingdom of the Vandals, with Carthage for its capital. They have a powerful navy, but their kings, Gunthamund (484-496) and Thr
2 CONC asamund (496-523), do not seem to be disposed to renew the buccaneering expeditions of their grandfather, the great Vandal Gaiseric. They are decided Arians, and keep up a stern, steady pressure on their Catholic subjects, who are
2 CONC  spared, however, the ruthless brutalities practised upon them by the earlier Vandal kings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The relations of the Vandals with the Ostrogothic kingdom seem to have been of a friendly character during almost the whole reign of Theodoric. Thrasamund, the fourth king who reigned at Carthage, married Amalafrida, Theodoric's s
2 CONC ister, who brought with her, as dowry, possession of the strong fortress of Lilybæum (Marsala), in the west of Sicily, and who was accompanied to her new home by a brilliant train of 1,000 Gothic nobles with 5,000 mounted retainer
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 266: Perhaps about the middle of 523, there came a great and calamitous change. We must revert for a few minutes to the family circumstances of Theodoric, in order to understand the influences which were embittering his spiri
2 CONC t against his Catholic--that is to say, his Roman--subjects. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The year before, his grandson Segeric, the Burgundian, had been treacherously assassinated by order of his father, King Sigismund, who had become a convert to the orthodox creed, and after the death of Theodoric's daughter had mar
2 CONC ried a Catholic woman of low origin. In the year 523 itself, Thrasamund, king of the Vandals, died and was succeeded by his cousin Hilderic, son of one of the most ferocious persecutors of the Catholic Church, but himself a conver
2 CONC t to her creed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notwithstanding an oath which Hilderic had sworn to his predecessor on his death-bed, never to use his royal power for the restoration of the churches to the Catholics, Hilderic had recalled the Bishops of the orthodox party and w
2 CONC as in all things reversing the bitter persecuting policy of his ancestors, Amalafrida, the sister of Theodoric and widow of Thrasamund, who had been for nearly 20 years queen of the Vandals, passionately resented this undoing of h
2 CONC er dead husband's work and put herself at the head of a party of insurgents, who called in the aid of the Moorish barbarians, but who were, notwithstanding that aid, defeated by the soldiers of Hilderic at Capsa. Amalafrida hersel
2 CONC f was taken captive and shut up in prison, probably about the middle of 523.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus everywhere the Arian League, of which Theodoric had been the head, and which had practically given him the hegemony of Teutonic Europe, was breaking down; and in its collapse disaster and violent death were coming upon the me
2 CONC mbers of Theodoric's own family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT If Eutharic himself, as seems probable, had died before this time, and was no longer at the King's side to whisper distrust of the Catholics at every step, and to put the worst construction on the actions of every patriotic Roman,
2 CONC  yet even Eutharic's death increased the difficulties of Theodoric's position, and his doubts as to the future fortunes of a dynasty which would be represented at his death only by a woman and a child. And these difficulties and d
2 CONC oubts bred in him not depression, but an irascible and suspicious temper, which had hitherto been altogether foreign to his calm and noble nature.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 298: The first of the Teutonic states to fall was the kingdom of the Vandals. Its ruin was certainly hastened by the estrangement between its royal house and that of the Ostrogoths. We left Theodoric's sister, the stately and
2 CONC  somewhat domineering Amalafrida in prison at Carthage. Soon after her brother's death (30 August 526) she was executed or murdered, by order of her cousin the Catholic reformer, Hilderic. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This outrage was keenly resented by the court of Ravenna. Hostilities between the two states were apparently imminent, but probably Amalasuentha felt that war, whether successful or unsuccessful, would be too dangerous for the dyn
2 CONC asty, and sullen alienation took the place of the preparation of fleets and armies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In June, 531, five years after the accession of Athalaric, the elderly and effeminate Hilderic was deposed by his martial subjects who had long chafed under the rule of such a sovereign, and his cousin, the warlike Gelimer, ascend
2 CONC ed the throne. The deposition of Hilderic, followed for the present not by his death but by his close imprisonment, furnished the ambitious Justinian with a fair pretext for war, since Hilderic was not only the ally of the Empire,
2 CONC  and a Catholic, but was descended on his mother's side from the great Theodosius and related to many of the Byzantine nobility. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of the opposition of the more cautious among his counsellors, Justinian decided to despatch an expedition for the conquest of Carthage, and about Midsummer, 533, a fleet of 500 ships, manned by 20,000 sailors and conveyin
2 CONC g 15,000 soldiers (10,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry), sailed forth from the Bosphorus into the Sea of Marmora, bound for the Libyan waters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the head of the army was Belisarius, now about 28 years of age, a man who came, like his Imperial master, from the highlands of Illyricum, but who, unlike that master, was probably of noble lineage. Three years before, he had w
2 CONC on the battle of Daras, defeating the Persian general, whose army was nearly twice as numerous as his own, and he had already shown signs of that profound knowledge of the science, and that wonderful mastery of the art of war whic
2 CONC h he was afterwards to display in many a hard-fought campaign, and which entitled him to a place in the innermost circle of the greatest generals that the world has seen.
2 CONT
