2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Peroz I (Middle Persian: PiruzPahlavi.png; New Persian: پیروز "the Victor"), was the eighteenth king of the Sasanian Empire, who ruled from 459 to 484. Peroz I was the eldest son of Yazdegerd II (438–457).
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Peroz I's father, Yazdegerd II, the younger son of the deceased Emperor, Hormizd, seized the throne in the absence of his elder brother Peroz who had been posted as the governor of distant Sistan forcing Peroz to s
2 CONC eek the protection of the Hephthalites. The Hephthalite monarch, Khushnavaz was only too glad to welcome him and aid him in his war against Hormizd. Peroz was also supported by Raham of the Mihran family.[1] During this dynastic s
2 CONC truggle, Denag, who was the mother of the two Sasanian princes, governed all of the Sasanian capital of Ctesiphon, or some parts of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT So, with Hephthalite and Mihranid assistance, Peroz led an army against Hormizd, defeated him and held him captive. He then ceded Taliqan to Khushnavaz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources differ as to what happened to Hormizd after his capture. Some say that he was put to death.However, the Persian historian, Mir-Khvand says that Peroz pardoned his younger brother and amicably spared his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aftermath of the civil war The civil war in Iran had affected the nation so much as to cost a province. Vache II, the king of Caucasian Albania, rebelled against Sasanian rule and declared himself independent while the brothers we
2 CONC re busy fighting amongst each other. So once Peroz I ascended the throne in the year 459, he led an army into Albania and completely subjugated the nation. He then allowed the Armenians of Persian Armenia to practice their religio
2 CONC n, Christianity, freely. He also made an agreement with the Byzantine Empire that they would together defend the Caucasus from incursions. Furthermore, Peroz ordered his foster brother Izad Gushnasp to take the Armenians who had b
2 CONC een held captive during the reign of his father to Herat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seven-year famine (464-471)
2 CONT
2 CONT War with the Kidarites  [The Kidarites, who had established themselves in parts of Transoxiana during the reign of the Sasanian king Shapur II, and had a long history of conflicts with the Sasanians, stopped paying tributes to the
2 CONC m in the early 460s, thus starting a new war between these two states. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two wars with the Hephthalites
2 CONT
2 CONT Trouble in Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT Third war with the Hephthalites and death In 484, Peroz I, before invading the territory of the Hephthalite Empire, had his brother Balash installed as viceroy, and Sukhra appointed as his minister. Peroz I, however, suffered a ma
2 CONC jor defeat to the Hephthalites and was killed at the battle of Herat.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Peroz, the bitter persecutor of Christians, died a wicked death and was  succeeded by Valash who ruled for four years [A.D. 484-488]. :
2 CONT
2 CONT [In this period the Samaritans rebelled and established a certain man named Justus as  their king. He killed many Christians, entering the church in Caesarea [Palestina] and  shedding blood. They pulled down this church [and built
2 CONC  a temple in its place]. The  Byzantine troops went against them and broke them. They sent [the head of\ Justus,  whom the Jews had made king [g212], to Emperor Zeno; and the temple which the Jews  had built they consecrated as a 
2 CONC church in the name of the blessed Mary. The Jews found  in Antioch also were destroyed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In these times there was an earthquake and the city of Nicomedia was destroyed — for the  sixth time by the same means. Then Emperor Zeno died at the age of 60, after reigning  for 16 years. Peroz, the bitter persecutor of Christi
2 CONC ans, died a wicked death and was  succeeded by Valash who ruled for four years [A.D. 484-488]. In his day there was  peace with Christians.
2 CONT
2 CONT events: In the middle of the 5th century, by order of the Persian King Peroz I, King Vache built a city initially called Perozabad in Utik, and later called Partaw and Barda; he made it the capital of Albania.[73] Partaw was the s
2 CONC eat of the Albanian kings and Persian marzban, and in 552 AD the seat of the Albanian Catholicos was also transferred to Partaw
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peroz_I
