2 CONT 1.  	Gibica of Burgundy-3680.
2 CONT Gjúki (also Gebicca, Gifica, Gibica, Gebicar, Gibicho or Gippich) was the King of the Burgundians in the late 4th century until his death in or around 407. He was the father of Gundomar I, Giselher, and Gunther.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned in Widsith as Gifica and as Gjúki in the eddic poem Atlakviða, where he was the father of Gunnar (see Gunther). As one of the earliest kings of the Nibelungs, the clan is called the Gjúkungar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Prose Edda, Snorri Sturluson says that Gjúki was the father of sons Gunnar and Hogni and a daughter Gudrun. Gotthorm (slayer of Sigurd) is his stepson from his wife Grimhild's previous marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prose Edda mentions Gudny, a second daughter of Gjúki and Grimhild. In the Gudrunarkvida, this second daughter is named Gullrond.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gibica 	He had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  	King of Burgundy Godomar / Gundomar I-2854 (Gibica of Burgundy).
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Godomar married (MRIN:309) Krimhild of Burgundy-2948. Gundomar I (also Gundimar, Godomar, or Godemar) was eldest son and successor of Gebicca, King of the Burgundians. He succeeded his father in 406 or 407 and reigned until 411. 
2 CONC He was succeeded by his brother Giselher.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Nibelungenlied, he is named Gernot and he is the brother of Gunther and Giselher.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Norse mythology, he is called Guthormr, and he was the murderer of Sigurd (Sigfried), the dragon slayer. http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gundomar
2 CONT
