2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Childebert_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Childebert I (c. 496 – 13 December 558) was a Frankish King of the Merovingian dynasty, as third of the four sons of Clovis I who shared the kingdom of the Franks upon their father's death in 511. He was one of the sons of Saint C
2 CONC lotilda, born at Reims. He reigned as King of Paris from 511 to 558 and Orléans from 524 to 558.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT The division of the lands of Gaul to the sons of Clovis I upon his death in 511.
2 CONT In the partition of the realm, Childebert received as his share the town of Paris, the country to the north as far as the river Somme, to the west as far as the English Channel, and the Armorican peninsula (modern Brittany).[1] Hi
2 CONC s brothers ruled in different lands: Theuderic I in Metz, Chlodomer in Orléans, and Chlothar I in Soissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 523, Childebert participated with his brothers in a war against Godomar of Burgundy. Chlodomer died in the Battle of Vézeronce (524). Thereafter, concerned that the three sons of Chlodomer would inherit the kingdom of Orléans, 
2 CONC Chlothar conspired with Childebert to oust them. They sent a representative to their mother, Clotilde. The representative presented a pair of scissors and a sword, offering her the choice to shear the three young boys, thereby dep
2 CONC riving them of the long hair considered a symbol of royal power, or to have them killed. She famously replied, "It is better for me to see them dead rather than shorn, if they are not raised to the kingship".[2] After the murder o
2 CONC f Chlodomer's two elder children—the third, Clodoald, escaping to a monastic life—Childebert annexed the cities of Chartres and Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took part in later various expeditions against the kingdom of Burgundy. He besieged Autun in 532 and, in 534, having conquered the kingdom along with his brother Chlothar and Theuderic's son Theudebert I, received as his share 
2 CONC of the spoils of that kingdom the towns of Mâcon, Geneva and Lyons. When Witiges, the king of the Ostrogoths, ceded Provence to the Franks in 535, the possession of Arles and Marseille was guaranteed to Childebert by his brothers.
2 CONC [1] The annexation of that province was completed, with Clotaire's help, in the winter of 536–537.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 531, he received pleas from his sister Chrotilda, wife of King Amalaric of the Visigoths. The Arian king of Hispania, Chrotilda claimed, was grossly mistreating her, a Catholic. Childebert went down with an army and defeated th
2 CONC e Gothic king. Amalaric retreated to Barcelona, where he was assassinated. Chrotilda died on her return journey to Paris of unknown causes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childebert made other expeditions against the Visigoths. In 542, he took possession of Pamplona with the help of his brother Clotaire and besieged Zaragoza but was forced to retreat. From this expedition he brought back to Paris a
2 CONC  precious relic, the tunic of Saint Vincent, in honor of which he built at the gates of Paris the famous monastery of Sainte-Croix-et-Saint-Vincent, known later as St-Germain-des-Prés. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 13 December 558, and was buried in the abbey he had founded, where his tomb has been discovered. [3] St-Germain-des-Prés became the royal necropolis for the Neustrian kings until 675. [4] He left two daughters, Chrodobe
2 CONC rge and Chrodesinde, by his wife Ultragotha. Childebert was an acquisitive monarch. He expanded his domains in more foreign wars than any of his brothers, fighting in Burgundy (more than once), Spain (more than once), Provence and
2 CONC  elsewhere in Gaul. Gregory of Tours, a contemporary Neustrian, cites Childebert as saying: "Velim unquam Arvernam Lemanem quae tantae jocunditatis gratia refulgere dicitur, oculis cernere" ("Would that I could set eyes on the Auv
2 CONC ergne Limagne, which is spoken of so highly"). Childbert was also one of the more religious of the sons of Clovis, cooperating with his brothers, rescuing his sister, and constructing the famous monastery of Saint Vincent to house
2 CONC  his relics.
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart, Roderick W. ''Royalty for Commoners, The Complete Known Lineage of John of Gaunt, Son of Edward III, King of England, and Queen Philippa''. Fourth Edition. Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Company, 2002. 
