2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Merovingians (covering his married life):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEROVINGIANS.htm#ClotaireIdied561B
2 CONT
2 CONT CLOTAIRE 511-561, CHARIBERT 561-567, GONTRAN 561-592
2 CONT
2 CONT CHLOTHACHAR [Clotaire/Lothar], son of CHLODOVECH King of the Franks & his second wife Chrotechildis of Burgundy ([501/02]-Soissons [30 Nov/31 Dec] 561, bur Soissons, basilique Saint-Médard).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours names Clotaire as son of King Clovis and his wife Clotilde, listed after Childebert[144].  "Theodorico, Chlomiro, Hildeberto, Hlodario" are named (in order) as sons of "Chlodoveus" in the Regum Merowingorum Geneal
2 CONC ogia[145].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 511 as CLOTAIRE I King of the Franks, at Soissons, his territory covering Soissons, Laon, Noyon, Arras, Cambrai, Tournai and the lower Meuse, the lands which were later to become the kingdom of Neustria.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours records that King Clotaire and his half-brother King Theoderich invaded Thuringia in 531, deposed King Hermanfred and annexed the kingdom, specifying that Clotaire brought his second wife back as part of his booty
2 CONC [146].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his brother King Childebert launched a third attack on Burgundy, besieged Autun and occupied the whole kingdom, deposing King Gondemar II[147] in 534.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He invaded Spain, with his brother King Childebert, and besieged Zaragoza but was forced to withdraw[148].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited the territories of his great-nephew King Theodebert in 555 and those of his brother King Childebert in 558, when he became sole king of the Franks.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours records his death, in the 51st year of his reign on the first anniversary of the killing of his son Chramn, at Soissons from a fever caught while hunting in the forest of Cuise and his burial at Soissons Saint Med
2 CONC ard[149].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records the death in 561 of "Chlothachrius rex"[150]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ([524]) as her second husband, his sister-in-law, GUNTHEUCA [Gondioque], widow of CHLODOMER King of the Franks, daughter of --- [King of Burgundy].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours names Guntheuc as widow of King Chlodomer and records her second marriage with his brother Clotaire, but does not give her origin[151].  Settipani suggests, for onomastic reasons only, that she may have belonged t
2 CONC o the Burgundian royal family which, if correct, means that she must have been the daughter of either King Gondebaud or his brother Godogisel[152].  However, Gregory makes no mention of this in his lengthy description of King Chlo
2 CONC domer's campaigns in Burgundy, an omission which is surprising if his wife was related to his opponents. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (531, repudiated) RADEGUND of Thuringia, daughter of BERTHECHAR [Bertaire] King of the Thuringians & his wife --- (Erfurt 518-Poitiers 13 Aug 587, bur Poitiers, basilique Sainte-Marie-hors-les-Murs).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours names Radegund as the orphaned daughter of Berthar[153].  The Vitæ Sanctæ Radegundis names "Radegundis natione barbare de regione Thoringa" and her "avo rege Bessino, patruo Hermenfredo, patre rege Bertechario"[15
2 CONC 4].  In a later passage, Gregory records that, after the Frankish invasion of Thuringia, Radegund formed part of the booty taken home by Clotaire I King of the Franks, who later married her[155].  The testament of Radegundis dated
2 CONC  to [584/87] survives[156].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours records the death of St Radegund on 13 Aug[157].  She was canonised, her feast day is 13 Aug[158]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [m] thirdly ([532]) INGUNDIS [Ingonde], daughter of ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours names Ingund as the wife of King Clotaire and mother of six of his children[159].  She was King Clotaire's concubine from [517][160]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [m] fourthly ARNEGUNDIS [Aregonde], sister of his third wife Ingonde, daughter of ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours specifies that King Clotaire's wife Aregonde was the sister of his wife Ingonde, making clear that the marriage was polygamous as he records that Clotaire reported his "marriage" to Aregonde to his wife Ingonde[16
2 CONC 1].  She is named "Chæregundem" in the Liber Historiæ Francorum[162]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [m] [fifthly] (555, repudiated) [as her second husband], WALDRADA, widow of THEODEBALD King of the Franks, daughter of WACCHO King of the Lombards & his second wife Ostrogotha of the Gepides.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Gregory of Tours, King Clotaire "began to have intercourse" with the widow of King Theodebald, before "the bishops complained and he handed her over to Garivald Duke of Bavaria"[163], which does not imply that Clotair
2 CONC e married Waldrada.  Herimannus names "Wanderadam" wife of "Theodpaldus rex Francorum" when recording her second marriage to "Lotharius rex patris eius Theodeberti patruus"[164].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married thirdly (after 555) Garibald Duke in Bavaria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): CHUNSINA, daughter of ---.  Gregory of Tours names Chunsina as the mistress of King Clotaire, mother of Chramn[165].  She is named "Gunsinam" in the Liber Historiæ Francorum[166]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): ---.  The name of King Clotaire's second mistress is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Clotaire & his third [wife] had [seven] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  GUNTHAR ([517]-after 532).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names (in order) Gunthar, Childerich, Charibert, Guntram, Sigibert and a daughter Clothsind as the children of King Clotaire and his wife Ingonde, specifying that Gunthar died in his father's lifetime[167].  
2 CONT *It is assumed that the first three children at least were born illegitimate.  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours reports that he served as an officer in his father's army, but died young[168]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  CHILDERICH (-before 561).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names (in order) Gunthar, Childerich, Charibert, Guntram, Sigibert and a daughter Clothsind as the children of King Clotaire and his wife Ingonde, specifying that Childerich died during his father's lifetime[169]
2 CONC .  
2 CONT *It is assumed that the first three children at least were born illegitimate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  CHARIBERT ([520]-Paris end 567, bur [Paris, Saint-Germain des Prés]).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names (in order) Gunthar, Childerich, Charibert, Guntram, Sigibert and a daughter Clothsind as the children of King Clotaire and his wife Ingonde[170].  It is assumed that the first three children at least were b
2 CONC orn illegitimate.  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 561 as CHARIBERT King of the Franks, his territories covering those previously held by his uncle King Childebert, with Paris as his capital[171].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that "
2 CONC filii ipsius Charibertus, Guntegramnus, Hilpericus et Sigibertus" divided the kingdom between them on the death of their father in 561[172].  
2 CONT *Herimannus names "Hariberti rex libidini deditus" when recording his marriages[173].  
2 CONT *After his death, his kingdom was divided among his brothers.  
2 CONT *m firstly (repudiated) INGOLBERGA [Ingelberge], daughter of --- ([520]-Tours 589).  Gregory of Tours names Ingoberg as the wife of King Charibert, but records that he dismissed her and took Merofled in her place[174].  Herimannus
2 CONC  records the repudiation by "Hariberti" of his wife "Ingoberga"[175].  She retired to Tours after her repudiation.  Gregory of Tours records the death of Queen Ingoberg, widow of Charibert, in the fourteenth year of King Childeber
2 CONC t's reign, saying he thought that she was in her 70th year[176], although this age seems exaggerated considering the likely birth date of her daughter.  
2 CONT *[m] secondly (after 561) MEROFLEDIS, daughter of ---, a wool-worker.  Gregory of Tours records that King Charibert fell in love with the two daughters of a wool-worker, Marcovefa and Merofled, and that after Queen Ingoberg humili
2 CONC ated their father by making him prepare wool for the royal household he dismissed the Queen and replaced her by Merofled[177].  
2 CONT *[m] thirdly (after 561) THEODECHILDIS, daughter of ---.  Gregory of Tours names Theudechild, daughter of the shepherd who looked after King Charibert's flocks, as another of the king's women and that after Charibert died she offe
2 CONC red her hand in marriage to King Gontran, who seized most of her goods and packed her off to a nunnery at Arles from which she unsuccessfully tried to escape[178].  
2 CONT *[m] fourthly his sister-in-law, MARCOVEFA, sister of MEROFLEDIS, daughter of --- (-before end 567).  Gregory of Tours records the marriage of King Charibert and Marcovefa, sister of Merofled, for which they were excommunicated by
2 CONC  "Saint Germanus the Bishop", as well as Marcovefa's death soon after before her husband[179].  
2 CONT *King Charibert & his first wife had one child, Berta (560-601/616) wife of Aethelberht, (550-616) King of Kent.
2 CONT *King Charibert & his [second] [wife] had one child, Berthefledis (561-589)
2 CONT *King Charibert & his third wife had one son (died as an infant).
2 CONT *King Charibert & his --- wife had [one child], Chrotieldis (Clotilde, 561-590) of Leubovera Convent
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  GUNTCHRAMN [Gontran] ([532/34]-28 Mar 592, bur basilique Saint-Marcel, near Chalon-sur-Saône).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names (in order) Gunthar, Childerich, Charibert, Guntram, Sigibert and a daughter Clothsind as the children of King Clotaire and his wife Ingonde[189].  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 561 as GONTRAN King of the Franks, his territories covering those previously held by his uncle King Chlodomer, with Orléans as his capital[190].  The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that "f
2 CONC ilii ipsius Charibertus, Guntegramnus, Hilpericus et Sigibertus" divided the kingdom between them on the death of their father in 561[191].  
2 CONT *He adopted his nephew King Childebert II as his successor in 577, the arrangement being renewed under the treaty of Andelot dated 28 Nov 587[192].  
2 CONT *Fredegar records the death of King Guntram "anno 33 regni…V Kal Apr" and his burial "in ecclesia sancti Marcelli" in the monastery which he had built[193].  
2 CONT *m firstly ([556]) MARCATRUDIS, daughter of MAGNACHAR Duke of the Transjuranian Franks & his wife --- (-after [556]).  Gregory of Tours names Marcatrude, daughter of Magnachar, as the wife of King Gontran, specifying that she pois
2 CONC oned her stepson Gundobald but died soon after her own son[194].  
2 CONT *m secondly (566) AUSTRECHILDIS [Bobilla], daughter of --- ([548]-Sep 580).  Gregory of Tours names Austrechild "also called Bobilla" as the second wife of King Gontran[195].  She was a servant in the household of his first wife's
2 CONC  father.  Gregory of Tours records the death of Queen Austrechild, specifying that "this wicked woman" requested as a dying wish that the two doctors who had unsuccessfully treated her should have their throats cut[196].  An epita
2 CONC ph to “Austrigildis Reginæ” refers to her as “Regum genetrix et Regia conjunx”[197].  The record of the Council of Valence dated 22 Jun 585 names “Guntramni Regis…bonæ memoriæ iugalis sua Austrechildis regina vel filiæ eorum Deo s
2 CONC acratæ puellæ…bonæ memoriæ Clodeberga vel Clodehildis”[198].  
2 CONT *Mistress (1): (before [549]) VENERANDA, daughter of ---.  Gregory of Tours names Veneranda, servant of one of his subjects, as the mistress of King Gontran before his first marriage[199].  
2 CONT *King Gontran & his first wife had one son, died early.
2 CONT *King Gontran & his second wife had four children: Chlothachar (Clotaire, 567-577), Chlodomer (d. 577), Chlodoberga (d. 585), and Chrothieldis (d. 585)
2 CONT *King Gontran had one child by Mistress (1), Gundobald (549-556)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  SIGEBERT ([535]-murdered Vitry [Nov/Dec] 575, bur Soissons, basilique Saint-Médard).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names (in order) Gunthar, Childerich, Charibert, Guntram, Sigibert and a daughter Clothsind as the children of King Clotaire and his wife Ingonde[211].  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 561 as SIGEBERT I King of the Franks, at Reims, later at Metz. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. CHLODESINDIS (-before 567[212]).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names (in order) Gunthar, Childerich, Charibert, Guntram, Sigibert and a daughter Clothsind as the children of King Clotaire and his wife Ingonde, specifying that Clothsind married Alboin King of the Lombards[213
2 CONC ].  
2 CONT *The Origo Gentis Langobardorum names "Flutsuinda…filia Flothario regis Francorum" as the first wife of Albuin[214].  The Historia Langobardorum names "Ludusenda…filia Flothari regis" as the first wife of Alboin[215].  Paulus Diac
2 CONC onus names "Chlotharius rex Francorum, Chlotsuindam ei suam filiam" as wife of Alboin[216].  
2 CONT *m ([556/60]) as his first wife, ALBOIN King of the Lombards, son of AUDOIN King of the Lombards & his wife --- (-murdered 28 Jun 572).  He was crowned King of the Lombards in Italy at Milan in [570]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  [BILICHILDIS .  
2 CONT *The Liber Historiæ Francorum records that "Chlotharius…rex" had seven children by "Ingunde", the same six as are named in Gregory of Tours with a marginal note adding "Blitchildim" as the seventh child and specifying that she mar
2 CONC ried "Ansbertus nobilissimus" and by him was mother of "Arnoldum"[217].  An alternative origin for Bilichildis is provided by the Chronico Marcianensi de Sancta Rictrude which names “Dagobertum Regem et Blithildem sororem eius” as
2 CONC  children of “Lotharius…[et] Beretrudam” (chronologically impossible if she was the grandmother of Arnoul Bishop of Metz), but commenting that “others say” that Bilichildis was the daughter of “primi Lotharii avi istius”, adding t
2 CONC hat Bilichildis married “Ansberto Duci nobili in Germania”[218].  
2 CONT *The Carmen de Exordio Gentis Francorum names "Hlotharius [rex]…filia…Blithild" and records her marriage to "Ansbertus"[219].  The recorded names of the alleged children of Bilichildis do not have a Merovingian ring about them.  I
2 CONC t is uncertain whether Bilichildis existed at all or whether she and her family were invented for the purposes of compiling a Merovingian descent for the Carolingian dynasty, an enterprise undertaken in Metz from the late 8th cent
2 CONC ury onwards.  Her absence from the list of the children of King Clotaire given by Gregory of Tours certainly suggests that she was a spurious later invention, although Gregory's treatment of the families of the early Merovingians 
2 CONC was not exhaustive, as can be seen from the examples of Berthoara, daughter of King Theodebald I, and [Theodechildis] sister of the same king (see above), whom Gregory does not mention at all.  Settipani demonstrates convincingly 
2 CONC that there are sufficient indications in other primary sources that parts, if not all, these reconstructions may be based on historical fact[220].  Sifting the fact from the fiction is inevitably speculative.  
2 CONT *m ANSBERT, son of ---.  A 9th century genealogy names "Ansbertus…ex genere senatorum", his brothers "Deotarium, Firminum, Gamardum, Aigulfum episcopum et Ragnifridum" and their supposed descendants, Ansbert's marriage to "filiam 
2 CONC Hlotarii regis Francorum…Blithildem" and their children as shown below[221].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Clotaire & his fourth wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. CHILPERICH (before 535-murdered Chelles [27 Sep/9 Oct] 584, bur Paris, Saint-Germain-des-Prés).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names Chilperich as the son of King Clotaire and his wife Aregund[222].  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 561 as CHILPERICH I King of the Franks, at Soissons.   
2 CONT
2 CONT King Clotaire had one child by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.  CHRAMN (-murdered [30 Nov/31 Dec] 560).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours names Chramn as the son of King Clotaire and his concubine Chunsina[223].  Gregory records that Chramn was hated by the townsfolk of Clermont-Ferrand, where he lived, for his dissolute way of life, and in a later
2 CONC  passage specifies that he moved on to Poitiers where he conspired against his father[224].  Gregory also says that Chramn allied himself with his uncle King Childebert in Paris, later rebelling once more against his father before
2 CONC  fleeing to Brittany, where he and his wife and daughters went into hiding with "Chanao Count of the Bretons" but were captured and murdered[225].   
2 CONT *The Marii Episcopi Aventicensis Chronica records that "Cramnus filius Chlothacharii regis" sought a hiding place from "Childeberto patruo suo" in 555[226].  
2 CONT *Paulus Diaconus records that "duce Francorum Chramnichis" laid waste to Trentino after defeating a Lombard invasion of France but was defeated by "Euin Tridentum dux" at "Salurnis"[227].  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours records his father, King Clotaire, died on the first anniversary of the killing of his son Chramn[228].  
2 CONT *m CHALDA, daughter of WILLICHAR & his wife --- (-murdered 560).  Gregory of Tours records that the wife of Chramn was the daughter of Willichar, but does not name her[229].  The Liber Historiæ Francorum names "Willecharii filiam…
2 CONC Chaldam" as the wife of Chramn, adding in a later passage that "Willecharius…socer eius" fled with the family to "basilicam sancti Martini"[230].  Gregory of Tours says that Chramn fled to Brittany, where he and his wife and daugh
2 CONC ters went into hiding with "Chanao Count of the Bretons" but were captured and murdered[231].   
2 CONT *Chramn & his wife had [two or more] daughters, murdered in 560.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Clotaire had one possible child by Mistress (2): 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. [GUNDOBALD "Ballomer" (-murdered Mar 585).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours records that Gundobald claimed to be the son of King Clotaire, who disowned him, that he was brought up by King Childebert and later supported by King Charibert.  
2 CONT *He was received in Italy by General Narses, married and had sons there before moving to Constantinople. 
2 CONT * He was invited back to Gaul, landed at Marseille and was welcomed by Bishop Theodore[233].  He was declared king at Brives-la-Gaillarde in Dec 584, but was betrayed and captured at Comminges by Gontran King of Burgundy, who murd
2 CONC ered him[234].]  
2 CONT *m (in Italy) ---.  The name of Gundobald´s wife is not known.  Gregory of Tours records that the two sons of Chramn were born in Italy, and they were taken to Byzantium by their father after the death of their mother[235].   Gund
2 CONC obald & his wife had two sons, taken to Byzantium.
2 CONT
