2 CONT
2 CONT ===Alaric II, king of the Visigoths
2 CONT * Regnal titles: King of the Visigoths 28 December 484 – 507
2 CONT * Reign	28 December 484 – c. August 507
2 CONT * Preceded by: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euric Euric]
2 CONT *	Succeeded by: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gesalec Gesalec]
2 CONT * Died: August 507
2 CONT * House: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balti_dynasty Balti dynasty] 
2 CONT * Parents: [https://www.geni.com/people/Euric-I-king-of-the-Visigoths/6000000003983924311?through=6000000006244496074 Euric] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Ragnagild-Visigoth-queen-consort/6000000002187923905?through=60000000062
2 CONC 44496074 Ragnagild] 
2 CONT * Spouses: 1) [https://www.geni.com/people/Gesaleico-rey-de-los-Visigodos/6000000006946237269?through=6000000006244496074 unknown] 2) [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodegotha-Visigoth-queen-consort/6000000003649751712?through=6000
2 CONC 000006244496074 Theodegotha]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaric_II '''Alaric II'''] (Gothic: 𐌰𐌻𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃, Alareiks, "ruler of all";[1] Latin: Alaricus; c. 458/466 – August 507) was the King of the Visigoths from 484 until 507. He succeeded his father Euric as
2 CONC  king of the Visigoths in Toulouse on 28 December 484;[2] he was the great-grandson of the more famous Alaric I, who sacked Rome in 410. He established his capital at Aire-sur-l'Adour (Vicus Julii) in Aquitaine. His dominions incl
2 CONC uded not only the majority of Hispania (excluding its northwestern corner) but also Gallia Aquitania and the greater part of an as-yet undivided Gallia Narbonensis.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alaric II''', also known as Alarik, Alarich, and Alarico in Spanish and Portuguese or Alaricus in Latin (d. 507) succeeded his father Euric in 485 and became eighth king of the Visigoths. He established his capital at Aire-sur-
2 CONC l'Adour (Vicus Julii). His dominions included not only the whole of Hispania except its north-western corner but also Gallia Aquitania and the greater part of an as-yet undivided Gallia Narbonensis. [edit] Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT In 486 Alaric II denied refuge to Syagrius, the former ruler of the Domain of Soissons (in what is now north western France) who was defeated by Clovis I King of the Franks. Alarmed by a summons from Clovis, Alaric imprisoned and 
2 CONC repatriated Syagrius back to Clovis I, where he was beheaded. Illustration of Alaric II at Biblioteca Nacional de España
2 CONT
2 CONT In 506, the Visigoths captured the city of Dertosa in the Ebro valley. There they captured the Roman usurper Peter and had him executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In religion Alaric was an Arian, like all the early Visigothic nobles, but he greatly mitigated the persecution policy of his father Euric toward the Catholics and authorized them to hold in 506AD the council of Agde. He was on un
2 CONC easy terms with the Catholic bishops of Arelate (modern Arles) as epitomized in the career of the Frankish Caesarius, bishop of Arles, born at Châlons and appointed bishop in 503. Caesarius was suspected of conspiring with the Bur
2 CONC gundians, whose king had married the sister of Clovis, to allow the Burgundians to take over Arles. Alaric exiled him for a year at Bordeaux in Aquitania, then allowed him to return unharmed when the crisis had passed (Wace, Dicti
2 CONC onary).
2 CONT
2 CONT He displayed similar wisdom in political affairs by appointing a commission to prepare an abstract of the Roman laws and imperial decrees, which would form the authoritative code for his Roman subjects. This is generally known as 
2 CONC the Breviarium Alaricianum or Breviary of Alaric. [edit] Battle of Vouillé and aftermath The Kingdom of the Visigoths under Alaric II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alaric endeavoured strictly to maintain the treaty which his father had concluded with the Franks. The Frankish king Clovis I, however, desired to obtain the Gothic province in Gaul and he found a pretext for war in the Arianism o
2 CONC f Alaric. The intervention of Theodoric, king of the Ostrogoths and father-in-law of Alaric, proved unavailing. The two armies met in 507 at the Battle of Vouillé, near Poitiers, where the Goths were defeated and Alaric the king, 
2 CONC who tried to flee, was captured and slain, it is said, by Clovis personally. As a consequence of their defeat the Visigoths lost all their possessions in Gaul to the Franks, except Septimania (i.e. the western region of Gallia Nar
2 CONC bonensis, which includes the contemporary Arles and the Provence).
2 CONT
2 CONT Alaric's heirs were his eldest son, the illegitimate [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gesalec Gesalec], and his younger son, the legitimate [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalaric Amalaric] who was still a child. Gesalec proved inco
2 CONC mpetent, and in 511 King Theodoric assumed the throne of the kingdom ostensibly on behalf of Amalaric - Heather uses the word "hijacked" to describe his action. Although Amalaric eventually became king in his own right, the politi
2 CONC cal continuity of the Visigothic kingdom was broken; "Amalaric's succession was the result of new power structures, not old ones," as Heather describes it. With Amalaric's death in 531, the Visigothic kingdom entered an extended p
2 CONC eriod of unrest which lasted until Leovigild assumed the throne in 568.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Father: Euric I, King of the Visigoths (b. after 435, d. Dec. 484 in Arles of natural causes)
2 CONT *Mother: Ragnahild, daughter of an unknown king
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT *1. Unknown sister, married Ricimer, Magister Militum, Patricius, and power behind the throne of the remnants of the Western Roman Empire (d. 18 August 472, four years before Odoacer toppled the Empire).
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse and mistress and their children: 
2 CONT *Mistress: Unknown.
2 CONT **1. Gesalic, proclaimed King of the Visigoths at Narbonne in 507 in opposition to 5 year old Amalric. Defeated by Amalric's grandfather Theoderic the Great of the Ostrogoths in 510, deposed to Barcelona, then Carthago. Captured a
2 CONC nd killed while traveling to Burgundy to arrange alliance with Burgundian King in 512-514.
2 CONT *Wife: Theodegotha or Thiudigotho, the Ostrogoth (b. c 473 according to Wikipedia)
2 CONT **2. Amalric, proclaimed King of the Visigoths at Toulouse, but challenged by half brother, until his grandfather, Theodoric the Great, stepped in. Theodoric proclaimed himself King of the Visigoths in 511 (when Amalric was 9). In
2 CONC  526 he was restored to the throne (age 24). Was defeated and killed in battle personally by his brother-in-law Childebert of the Franks after his sister, Chlotilde, wife of Amalric, was so badly maltreated that she died enroute t
2 CONC o Paris after being rescued by Childebert.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: c458, according to FMG. (Wikipedia entries appear to not assign a birth date.) Place is likely to be Toulouse, given that this was the Visigoth "capital" at the time. (His uncle Theoderic II, Visigoth king around the time o
2 CONC f his birth, "returned" to Toulouse in March 457 according to FMG. He was killed 9 years later in a plot that likely involved his father.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism: Unknown. (He was an Arian Christian.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 494 with Theodegotho; according to Jordanes, in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Battle of Vouillé near Poitiers, late Summer 507, in hand-to-hand combat ("overtaken in flight" from the battle scene) with King Clovis of the Franks. (The Franks apparently invaded in violation of a treaty made five years 
2 CONC earlier between the two kings, using the pretext of religion - Clovis was Catholic while Alaric was Arian.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Unknown (he may not have been accorded a burial as a "heretic," given that he died in a battle of what appears to have been a "holy war").
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: King of the Visigoths (484-507), in Spanish: Rey de los Visigodos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate names: Alaric in English, Alarico in Spanish, Alaricus in Latin. Also Alarik and Alarich.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Toulouse Kings (covering both birth family and married life):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE.htm#_Toc225040395
2 CONT
2 CONT EURIC 466-484, ALARIC II 484-507, GESALIC 507-511, AMALRIC 507-531
2 CONT
2 CONT EURIC, son of THEODERIC I King of the Visigoths & his [wife/concubine] --- ([after 435]-Arles [Dec] 484).  
2 CONT *Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the b
2 CONC attle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[94].  
2 CONT *He was elected to succeed in 466 as EURIC King of the Visigoths.  Wolfram refers to the gift to his mother in [466/67] of a bowl inscribed with verses by Sidonius which refer to her son, and speculates that King Euric was about 2
2 CONC 6 years old when he assumed power[95].  Iordanes records that Euric was suspected of involvement in the death of Theoderic[96].  
2 CONT *He expanded Visigoth controlled territory to the north and south of the Pyrenees, achieving substantial control over the Iberian Peninsula with the exception of Galicia[97].  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Euricus” devast
2 CONC ated “Lusitaniam” and captured “Pampilonam et Cæsaraugustam”, and also was the first to promulgate laws for the Goths[98].  
2 CONT *Iordanes records that Euric captured Auvergne[99].  Emperor Anthemius conceded Auvergne to King Euric in 475, in return for Provence which the Visigoths had captured two years earlier but which was recaptured by Euric in 476[100]
2 CONC .  
2 CONT *King Euric proclaimed the Visigothic monarchy as an independent state in south-western France in 476, after the deposition of Emperor Romulus "Augustulus", last Roman Emperor, by the Ostrogoths[101].  Wolfram suggests that the Vi
2 CONC sigothic kingdom did not de jure separate itself from the empire[102], although it is not clear what "empire" remained after the overthrow of the last Roman emperor.  He made peace with Odovacar, the Ostrogoth King of Italy, agree
2 CONC ing the Alps as the border between the two kingdoms[103].  
2 CONT *The Franks attacked Visigothic territory in south-west France in the 490s, particularly Saintes and Bordeaux, which probably encouraged Visigothic emigration to Spain[104].  According to Gregory of Tours, Euric King of the Goths 
2 CONC crossed from Spain and persecuted Christians in Gaul[105], although this statement appears inconsistent with Visigothic expansion being in the other direction during Euric's reign.  
2 CONT *During the reign of King Euric, Visigothic legal statutes, known as codex Euricianus, were first committed to writing.  
2 CONT *He died a natural death, unlike all his predecessors.  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Euricus” reigned for 26 years and died “Arelate” under Emperor Zeno[106].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Euricus” reig
2 CONC ned for 15, otherwise 17, years[107]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before [458]) RAGNAHILD, daughter of King ---.  
2 CONT *Sidonius names "reginæ Ragnahildæ" in a letter which also refers to her young son[108].  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Euric and [his wife] had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. ALARIC ([458]-killed in battle Poitiers late summer 507).  
2 CONT *Iordanes names "Eurichus…filius Alarichus" when recording his succession after the death of his father[109].  He was elected to succeed his father at Toulouse 28 Dec 484 as ALARIC II King of the Visigoths.  
2 CONT *The main body of Visigoths entered Spain during his reign, largely resulting from military pressure from the Franks in the north[110].  
2 CONT *King Alaric II formed an alliance with the Ostrogoths and sent military help in 490 in support of Theodoric in his struggle with Odovacar King of Italy[111].  The alliance was later confirmed by King Alaric's marriage to King The
2 CONC odoric's daughter.  
2 CONT *Faced with continuing Frankish military expansion, King Alaric met Clovis King of the Franks in 502 on an island in the Loire near Amboise and agreed the River Loire as the frontier separating the two kingdoms[112].  
2 CONT *He was responsible for compiling the Breviary Lex Romana Visigothorum, a Roman law-book, and supported a catholic church council at Agde in 506[113].  
2 CONT *He was defeated and killed by Clovis King of the Franks at the campus Vogladensis[114], probably Voulan, near Poitiers, athough this is popularly known as the battle of Vouillé[115], which marked the end of Visigothic authority o
2 CONC ver the territory around Toulouse.  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Alaricus” was killed by “Huduildus rex Francorum apud Pictavem” after reigning for 23 years[116].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Alaricus”
2 CONC  reigned for 23 years[117].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([494][118]) THEODEGOTHA [Thiudigotho] the Ostrogoth, illegitimate daughter of THEODORIC I King of the Ostrogoths in Italy & his concubine ---.  
2 CONT *Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and were married "unam Alarico Vesegotharu
2 CONC m et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[119].  Procopius records that “regi Visigothorum Alarico” married "Theoderici…Theudichusam virginem filiam"[120].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ---.  The name of King Alaric's mistress is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric II & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)  AMALRIC (502-murdered Barcelona 531).  
2 CONT *Iordanes names "Amalricus" as the son of "Alarico Vesegotharum" & his wife[121].  Procopius records that the mother of “Amalricus” was "filiam Theoderici"[122].  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours relates that Amalric, son of Alaric, escaped from the battle in which his father was killed and fled to Spain[123].  He succeeded his father in 507 as AMALRIC King of the Visigoths, challenged by his half-brother
2 CONC .  
2 CONT *After his father's defeat, only Arles and Carcassonne remained under Visigoth control in France.  In Jun 508, King Amalric's maternal grandfather Theodoric King of Italy arrived in France, relieved the siege of Arles, recaptured 
2 CONC Marseille and re-established Visigoth hegemony over Septimania[124].  King Theodoric acted nominally as regent for King Amalric, although he proclaimed himself King of the Visigoths in 511 after defeating King Gesalic.  
2 CONT *Amalric was defeated by his brother-in-law Childebert King of the Franks at Narbonne in 531, and fled to Barcelona where he established his capital but was murdered soon after by his cousin Theudis.  The Chronica Regum Visigottho
2 CONC rum records that “Amalricus” reigned for five years[125].  
2 CONT *m (511) CHROTHIELDIS [Clotilde] of the Franks, daughter of CLOVIS I [Chlodovech] King of the Franks & his second wife Chrotechildis [Clotilde] of Burgundy (-531, bur Paris, basilique des Saints-Apôtres [later église Sainte-Genevi
2 CONC ève]).  Gregory of Tours refers to the marriage of the (unnamed) sister of the four brothers Theoderic, Chlodomer, Childebert and Lothar with Amalric King of the Visigoths, arranged after the death of their father, specifying that
2 CONC  she was sent to Spain "with a great dowry of expensive jewellery"[126].  Procopius records that “rex…Visigotthorum Amalaricus” married "Regis Theodeberti sororem"[127].  
2 CONT *Gregory names her Clotilde in a later passage which records that she was maltreated by her husband, brought back to France by her brother King Childebert who attacked and defeated King Amalric, but  [she] died on the journey and 
2 CONC was buried in Paris beside her father[128].   
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric II had one illegitimate son by Mistress (1): 
2 CONT
2 CONT b) GESALIC (-murdered in Burgundy [511/12] or [513/14][129]).  
2 CONT *He was proclaimed as GESALIC King of the Visigoths in Narbonne in 507, in opposition to his infant half-brother.  Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum records that "Gisaleicus…regis [Alarici] filius ex concubina" succ
2 CONC eeded as king of the Visgoths at Narbonne[130].  Procopius names “Giselicum, Alarici ex concubine filium” when recording his accession[131].  
2 CONT *He was defeated by the Ostrogoths in 510, was deposed and fled to Barcelona.  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Gesalaicus” was defeated at Narbonne by “Gundibado Burgundionem rege” and fled to Barcelona, from where he went 
2 CONC “ad Africam Wandalis” for help which was refused, and that he was killed after returning to Barcelona “a duce Teuderici Italiæ regis” during the reign of Emperor Anastasius[132].  
2 CONT *From Barcelona he fled to the Vandal court at Carthage, and was given resources to return and renew his resistance[133].  He was eventually captured on his way to seek support from Gundobad King of Burgundy, and executed near the
2 CONC  River Durance in Gaul by supporters of his half-brother King Amalric[134].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Geselicus” reigned for three years “et in latebra annum I alibi XV”[135]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alaric's Sister:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         daughter.  m RICIMER, son of ---  (-472).  
2 CONT *Iordanes records that Euric "cum Ricemere genero suo" invaded Rome[136].  He was magister militum, and defeated Emperor Avitus in 456. 
2 CONT
