1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Amalaric, King of the Visigoths
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visigothic_Kingdom Rex Hispania]
2 CONT * Reign	522 - 531
2 CONT * Predecessor	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gesalec Gesalec]
2 CONT * Successor	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theudis Theudis]
2 CONT * Born	502
2 CONT * Died	c. 531 (29 years)
2 CONT * Spouse	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clotilde Clotilde]
2 CONT * Father	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaric_II Alaric II]
2 CONT * Mother	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodegotha Theodegotha]
2 CONT * Religion	later [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arianism Arianism]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalaric '''Amalaric'''],  or in Spanish and Portuguese, Amalarico, (502 – 531) was a son of king Alaric II and of Theodegotho, daughter of Theodoric the Great and his first wife. Amalaric was himsel
2 CONC f king of the Visigoths from 526 till he was assassinated in 531.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a child when his father fell in battle against Clovis I, king of the Franks, in 507. Gesalec was chosen king and the child Amalaric was carried for safety into Hispania. After Gesalec was killed in 511, the country and Prov
2 CONC ence was thenceforth ruled by Amalaric's maternal grandfather, Theodoric the Ostrogoth, acting through his vice regent, Theudis, an Ostrogothic nobleman. In 522 the young Amalaric was proclaimed king, and four years later, on Theo
2 CONC doric's death, he assumed full royal power in Hispania and that part of Languedoc called Septimania, relinquishing Provence to his cousin Athalaric. He married Chrotilda, daughter of Clovis I; but his disputes with her, he being a
2 CONC n Arian and she a Catholic, resulted in physical abuse of his wife who summoned her brother Childebert I to her rescue. Amalaric was defeated at Narbonne in 531 and retreated behind the walls of Barcelona, where he was assassinate
2 CONC d by his own troops. Chrotilda made it to Narbonne, but died enroute to Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Father: Alaric II (b. 458), King of the Visigoths (484-507, d. Battle of Vouillé, late summer)
2 CONT *Mother: Theodogotha / Thiudigotho the Ostrogoth
2 CONT
2 CONT Half-sibling:
2 CONT *Gesalic, King of the Visigoths (507 - 511, d. before 514 near the River Durance in Gaul)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT *Chrothieldis/Clotilde of the Franks (d. 531 as a result of maltreatment by Amalric while living as his wife in Toulouse - an attempt to convert her by force to Arianism from Catholicism according to German Wikipedia; she passed a
2 CONC way while being taken back to Paris; bur Paris, basilique des Saints-Apôtres, present église Sainte-Geneviève)
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 502 according to FMG and German Wikipedia, or 500 according to Spanish Wikipedia (no sources throughout article, unfortunately) - Probably in Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism: Unknown, but was an Arian Christian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 511 according to FMG, or 526 according to Spanish Wikpiedia, with Chrothieldis/Clotilde (probably at same time that he ascended as King of the Visigoths) - probably in Nabonne (capital after the death of his father, acco
2 CONC rding to Genealogie Mittelalter).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 531 - Barcelona (Murdered by his cousin Theudis, former governor under Theoderic, after fleeing from Battle of Narbonne fought against the Franks, who were carrying out a punitive war over King Childebert's sister's death)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Disputed King of the Visigoths (507-511), King of the Visigoths under Regency of Theoderic "the Great" of the Ostrogoths (511-526), King of the Visigoths, undisputed (526-531)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate Names: Amalric, Amalaric, Amalarico, Amalaricus, Amalarich
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Medlands page on Toulouse Kings, Dukes, and Counts:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE.htm#_Toc225040395
2 CONT
2 CONT ALARIC II ([458]-killed in battle Poitiers late summer 507).  
2 CONT *Iordanes names "Eurichus…filius Alarichus" when recording his succession after the death of his father[109].  He was elected to succeed his father at Toulouse 28 Dec 484 as ALARIC II King of the Visigoths.  
2 CONT *The main body of Visigoths entered Spain during his reign, largely resulting from military pressure from the Franks in the north[110].  
2 CONT *King Alaric II formed an alliance with the Ostrogoths and sent military help in 490 in support of Theodoric in his struggle with Odovacar King of Italy[111].  The alliance was later confirmed by King Alaric's marriage to King The
2 CONC odoric's daughter.  
2 CONT *Faced with continuing Frankish military expansion, King Alaric met Clovis King of the Franks in 502 on an island in the Loire near Amboise and agreed the River Loire as the frontier separating the two kingdoms[112].  
2 CONT *He was responsible for compiling the Breviary Lex Romana Visigothorum, a Roman law-book, and supported a catholic church council at Agde in 506[113].  
2 CONT *He was defeated and killed by Clovis King of the Franks at the campus Vogladensis[114], probably Voulan, near Poitiers, athough this is popularly known as the battle of Vouillé[115], which marked the end of Visigothic authority o
2 CONC ver the territory around Toulouse.  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Alaricus” was killed by “Huduildus rex Francorum apud Pictavem” after reigning for 23 years[116].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Alaricus”
2 CONC  reigned for 23 years[117].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([494][118]) THEODEGOTHA [Thiudigotho] the Ostrogoth, illegitimate daughter of THEODORIC I King of the Ostrogoths in Italy & his concubine ---.  
2 CONT *Iordanes names "unam…Thiudigoto et aliam Ostrogotho" as the two daughters of Theodoric born "ex concubina…in Moesia" before his marriage to Audofledis, specifying that they came to Italy and were married "unam Alarico Vesegotharu
2 CONC m et aliam Sigismundo Burgundzonorum"[119].  Procopius records that “regi Visigothorum Alarico” married "Theoderici…Theudichusam virginem filiam"[120].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ---.  The name of King Alaric's mistress is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric II & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         AMALARIC (502-murdered Barcelona 531).  
2 CONT *Iordanes names "Amalricus" as the son of "Alarico Vesegotharum" & his wife[121].  Procopius records that the mother of “Amalricus” was "filiam Theoderici"[122].  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours relates that Amalric, son of Alaric, escaped from the battle in which his father was killed and fled to Spain[123].  He succeeded his father in 507 as AMALARIC King of the Visigoths, challenged by his half-brothe
2 CONC r.  After his father's defeat, only Arles and Carcassonne remained under Visigoth control in France.  
2 CONT *In Jun 508, King Amalaric's maternal grandfather Theodoric King of Italy arrived in France, relieved the siege of Arles, recaptured Marseille and re-established Visigoth hegemony over Septimania[124].  King Theodoric acted nomina
2 CONC lly as regent for King Amalaric, although he proclaimed himself King of the Visigoths in 511 after defeating King Gesalic.  
2 CONT *Amalaric was defeated by his brother-in-law Childebert King of the Franks at Narbonne in 531, and fled to Barcelona where he established his capital but was murdered soon after by his cousin Theudis.  The Chronica Regum Visigotth
2 CONC orum records that “Amalaricus” reigned for five years[125]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --- 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (511) CHROTHIELDIS [Clotilde] of the Franks, daughter of CLOVIS I [Chlodovech] King of the Franks & his second wife Chrotechildis [Clotilde] of Burgundy (-531, bur Paris, basilique des Saints-Apôtres [later église Sainte-Geneviè
2 CONC ve]).  
2 CONT *Gregory of Tours refers to the marriage of the (unnamed) sister of the four brothers Theoderic, Chlodomer, Childebert and Lothar with Amalric King of the Visigoths, arranged after the death of their father, specifying that she wa
2 CONC s sent to Spain "with a great dowry of expensive jewellery"[126].  Procopius records that “rex…Visigotthorum Amalaricus” married "Regis Theodeberti sororem"[127].  
2 CONT *Gregory names her Clotilde in a later passage which records that she was maltreated by her husband, brought back to France by her brother King Childebert who attacked and defeated King Amalric, but died on the journey and buried 
2 CONC in Paris beside her father[128].   
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric II had one illegitimate son by Mistress (1): 
2 CONT
2 CONT b) GESALIC (-murdered in Burgundy [511/12] or [513/14][129]).  
2 CONT *He was proclaimed as GESALIC King of the Visigoths in Narbonne in 507, in opposition to his infant half-brother.  Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum records that "Gisaleicus…regis [Alarici] filius ex concubina" succ
2 CONC eeded as king of the Visgoths at Narbonne[130].  Procopius names “Giselicum, Alarici ex concubine filium” when recording his accession[131].  
2 CONT *He was defeated by the Ostrogoths in 510, was deposed and fled to Barcelona.  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Gesalaicus” was defeated at Narbonne by “Gundibado Burgundionem rege” and fled to Barcelona, from where he went 
2 CONC “ad Africam Wandalis” for help which was refused, and that he was killed after returning to Barcelona “a duce Teuderici Italiæ regis” during the reign of Emperor Anastasius[132].  From Barcelona he fled to the Vandal court at Cart
2 CONC hage, and was given resources to return and renew his resistance[133].  
2 CONT *He was eventually captured on his way to seek support from Gundobad King of Burgundy, and executed near the River Durance in Gaul by supporters of his half-brother King Amalric[134].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that
2 CONC  “Geselicus” reigned for three years “et in latebra annum I alibi XV”[135]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
