2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodahad
2 CONT
2 CONT (born c. 480 in Tauresium[1] – died 536) was the King of the Ostrogoths from 534 to 536 and a nephew of Theodoric the Great through his sister Amalafrida.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He might have arrived in Italy with Theodoric and was an elderly man at the time of his succession. Massimilliano Vitiello states the name "Theodahad" is a compound of 'people' and 'conflict'.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He arrested Amalaswintha, queen of the Ostrogoths from 526 to 534, and imprisoned her on an island in Lake Bolsena.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Political instability within the Ostrogothic kingdom served as a pretext to Byzantine general Belisarius to intervene in Sicily and Italy, at the service of the Emperor Justinian, causing the Gothic Wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Witiges ordered him killed, and succeeded him as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodahad had at least two children with a woman named Gudeliva: Theodegisclus and Theodenantha
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#AmalabergaMHerminafredThuringia
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         THEODAHAD (-murdered Dec 536).  Iordanes names "Amalfridam germanam suam [Theoderici]" as the mother of "Theodehadi qui postea rex fuit" but does not name his father[242].  Disappointed in not succeeding his uncle King 
2 CONC Theodoric, Theodahad acquired considerable private estates in Tuscany "where he led a retired life at home" according to Jordanes[243].  He was planning to sell this "kingdom" to the emperor, in return for retirement in Constantin
2 CONC ople and an annual pension, when his cousin Queen Amalasuintha recalled him and appointed him co-regent in 534[244].  Procopius records that “Theodatus, filius Amalafridas sororis Theoderici” possessed "agrorum Tusciæ" and planned
2 CONC  to deliver "Tusciam in Iustiniani Augusti potestatem"[245].  In a later passage, Procopius records that “Theodatus” was appointed regent by "Amalasuntha"[246].  He arrested the Queen end-534 and imprisoned her on an island in Lak
2 CONC e Bolsena[247], assuming the title THEODAHAD King of Italy.  Iordanes records that "Theodahadum consobrinum suum" succeeded Athalric, appointed by the latter's mother whom Theodahad killed soon after his accession[248].  After the
2 CONC  Queen's murder, Emperor Justinian ordered a two-pronged attack against Sicily and Dalmatia in revenge.  Theodahad repelled the initial threat in Dalmatia in 536, but an imperial fleet occupied Split and Dubrovnik.  The forces in 
2 CONC Sicily, under Belisarius, moved into mainland Italy, occupied Naples.  Theodahad marched as far as Rome to meet the invaders, but was murdered while trying to escape back to Ravenna[249].  m GUDELIVA, daughter of ---.  The primary
2 CONC  source which names the wife of Theodahad has not so far been identified.  Theodahad & his wife had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          THEODEGISKLOS (-536 or after).  Procopius records that “Theodati…filium Theodegisclum” was captured by "Vitigis"  (in 536)[250].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         THEODENANDA .  Procopius records that “Theodati…filiam Theodenantham” married "Ebrimuth" who surrendered to Belisarius "cum omni suo comitatu" and was awarded "patricii dignitatem"[251].  m EBRIMUD [Evermud], son of --
2 CONC -.  Iordanes names "Evermud Theodahadi Gothorum regis gener"[252].  He was the military commander of the Ostrogoth forces in Reggio Calabria and surrendered to Belisarius in 536[253].  
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)       [son (-[536]).  The primary source which records the existence of this possible son has not yet been identified.]
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         AMALABERGA .  Iordanes names "Amalabergam" as the daughter of "Amalfridam germanam suam [Theoderici]" and records her marriage to "Thuringorum regi…Herminefredo"[254].  “Theodericus rex” wrote to “Herminafrido Regi Thur
2 CONC ingorum” granting him “neptis” in marriage[255].  Procopius records that “Hermenefrido Thoringorum regi” married "Theoderici…Amelobergam, Amalafridæ sororis suæ filiam"[256].  The Widukindi Res Gestæ Saxonicæ names "Huga rex Franc
2 CONC orum…unicam filiam Amalbergam" who married "Irminfredo regi Thuringorum"[257].  Gregory of Tours calls Amalaberg, wife of Hermanfrid, a "wicked and cruel woman" who sowed the seeds of civil war between her husband and his brother 
2 CONC Baderic[258].  She escaped to Ravenna with her children after the Franks attacked Thuringia and killed her husband[259].  m ([510]) HERMANFRED, King of the Thuringians, son of --- (-534).  
