2 CONT
2 CONT HERMENRICH (Ostrogoth Generation 10)
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Ansila et Ediulf, Vultuulf et Hermenerig" as the sons of Achiulf[39]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT HUNIMUND (Ostrogoth Generation 11)
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Hunimundum" as son of "Hermanaricus"[40]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT THORISMUND (-killed in battle [451/55], Ostrogoth Generation 12).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Thorismundo" as son of "Hunimundus"[41].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes records that "Thorismundo filius eius" succeeded as King of the Goths after the death of "Hunimundus filius quondam regis…Hermanarici" but was killed fighting the Gepids in the second year of his reign[42]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [39] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 77. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [40] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 77. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [41] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 77. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [42] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 122. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Getica, by Jordanes, as found on Boudicca's Bard:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.boudicca.de/jordanes4-e.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT (248) When (Vinitharius) had ruled with such license for barely a year, Balamber, king of the Huns, would no longer endure it, but sent for Gesimund, son of Hunimund the Great. Now Gesimund, together with a great part of the Goths
2 CONC , remained under the rule of the Huns, being mindful of his oath of fidelity. Balamber renewed his alliance with him and led his army up against Vinitharius. After a long contest, Vinitharius prevailed in the first and in the seco
2 CONC nd conflict, nor can any say how great a slaughter he made of the army of the Huns.
2 CONT
2 CONT (249) But in the third battle, when they met each other unexpectedly at the river named Erac, Balamber shot an arrow and wounded Vinitharius in the head, so that he died. Then Balamber took to himself in marriage Vadamerca, the gr
2 CONC and-daughter of Vinitharius, and finally ruled all the people of the Goths as his peaceful subjects, but in such a way that one ruler of their own number always held the power over the Gothic race, though subject to the Huns.
2 CONT
2 CONT (250) And later, after the death of Vinitharius, Hunimund ruled them, the son of Hermanaric, a mighty king of yore; a man fierce in war and of famous personal beauty, who afterwards fought successfully against the race of the Suav
2 CONC i. And when he died, his son Thorismud succeeded him, in the very bloom of youth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page listing Ukrainian rulers:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Ukrainian_rulers
2 CONT
2 CONT Hunimund ("Protege of the Huns"), the Beautiful, born ca. 326 in Ukraine, the first Hunnic vassal prince of the Ostrogoths (376-fl.405)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: The Suavi actually were a proto-German tribe from which the name Swabia came from (note: nothing to do with Serbia or the Slavic Serbs). After migrating north of the Alps to the Right Bank of the Rhine (where t
2 CONC hey became close allies with the Alamanni), a large portion of the tribe joined up with the Vandals at the end of 406 in their westward invasion. A couple decades later, these farthest west Suavi would found in what would later be
2 CONC come Spain the Kingdom of Galicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Hunimund, though, the Ostrogoths successfully resisted Suavi attempts to subordinate them. It is therefore ironic that one of the Amali's most famous descendants would be described in documents contemporary to her time as "d
2 CONC e gente Suavorum" - this descendant would be Hildegard, daughter of Gerold von Anglachgau and wife of Charlemagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Image used: This is what is believed to be a Hun flag (probably from the time of Attila). Hunimund was the first Ostrogoth ruler to cooperate with the Hun overlords (under first Bulümar "Sheke," or Balamber, ruled c.360-378; then 
2 CONC under Alyp-bi or Uldin, ruled 378-390; then under Aybat or Mundzuk, ruled 390-434).
