2 CONT    RC: Clodion, governed the Salic Franks, 428-448.   K: Clodius, surnamed Crinitus or Long-Hair. Conquerored Artois, Cambray, Tournay, etc. Died 445-447. And (K-1772) Clodion le Vhevelu, King de Francs (of the Franks).
2 CONT    K-1657: Chlodio, King of a part of the Salistian Franks.   Collins: King Clodio, Clodion, Clodia, the Long-Haired. 1st King of Salic (Salian) Franks (Westphalia) 428-448.  Born about 380, died about 445-447.
2 CONT    Pfafman: Essentially same as Collins.
1 NOTE OR "CHLODIO""CHLODION VI"; KNOWN AS "ARCHADIUS""THE LONG HAIR"; KING OF CAMBRAI
2 CONT 438-448
1 NOTE Clodio
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT Clodio1 (c. 395 </wiki/395> - 447 </wiki/447> or 449 </wiki/449>) or, the Long-Haired or the Hairy, was the semi-legendary </wiki/Legend> first Salian </wiki/Salian> Frankish </wiki/Franks> king of the Merovingian </wiki/Merovingi
2 CONC an> dynasty </wiki/Dynasty> (426 </wiki/426> - 447 </wiki/447>). His successor was Meroveus </wiki/Meroveus>, who founded that dynasty. Legend has it that his father was duke Pharamond </wiki/Pharamond> and his mother was Argotta 
2 CONC </w/index.php?title=Argotta&action=edit>, from Thuringia </wiki/Thuringia>. His grandfather may have been Marcomer </wiki/Marcomer>, a duke </wiki/Duke> of the Franks.
2 CONT There are basically only two sources of information for Clodio's history: the writings of Gregory of Tours </wiki/Gregory_of_Tours> and Sidonius Apollinaris </wiki/Sidonius_Apollinaris>.
2 CONT Clodio lived in Dispargum, a name that is believed to be that of a castle, rather than a village. Around 431 </wiki/431>, he invaded the territory of Artois </wiki/Artois>, but was defeated near Hesdin </wiki/Hesdin> by Aetius </w
2 CONC iki/Aetius>, the commander of the Roman </wiki/Roman> army in Gaul </wiki/Gaul>. However, Clodio regrouped and soon was able to seize the town Cameracum </wiki/Cambrai>. Eventually, he occupied all the country as far as the Somme 
2 CONC River </wiki/Somme_River> and making Tournai </wiki/Tournai> the capital of the Salian Franks.
2 CONT Clodio's aggressive action to seize more territory led to centuries of expansion by his successors that ultimately created what we know today as the country of France </wiki/France>. Clodio died sometime between 447 and 449 and po
2 CONC wer passed on to Meroveus. It is not known if Meroveus was his son or another chieftain of the tribe who ascended into the leadership role.
1 NOTE SURNAMED "CRINTUS" BECAUSE HE MADE THE FRANKS WEAR LONG HAIR AND BEARDS IN
2 CONT OPPOSITION TO THE ROMANS FOR A SIGN OF LIBERTY
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King of Westphalia; King of the Franks -445
2 CONT Rekken som f*rer fram til Clodio kan vaere autentisk, men den kan also vaere
2 CONT en suksesjonsrekke of chieftainer uten n*dvendigvis aa si noe if slektskapet,
2 CONT eller den kan vaere fiktiv. Maa ikke tas for alvorlig.
1 NOTE He ruled from 428 to 448, living in Westphalia.
1 NOTE He ruled from 428 to 448, living in Westphalia.
