1 OCCU Reine, des Francs, Princesse, de Thuringe, Prinses van Thüringen, Queen of France, Queen d'Franks, 0499 and 0502, [Thuringe], Queen of the Franks, Fraknish Queen, Queen of the Franks/Princess of Thuringia, Queen of Paris, Frankish
2 CONC  Queen, Queen Consort, 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aregund
2 CONT
2 CONT Aregund, Aregunda, Arnegund, Aregonda, or Arnegonda was the wife of Clotaire I, king of the Franks, and the mother of Chilperic I of Neustria. She was the sister of Ingund, one of Clotaire's other wives. She lived during the sixth
2 CONC  century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her sepulchre, among dozens of others, was discovered in 1959 in the Saint Denis Basilica by archaeologist Michel Fleury. It contained remarkably well-preserved clothing items and jewellery, which were used to identify her.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an episode of the television series, Digging for the Truth, aired in May 2006, host Josh Bernstein arranged a DNA test of a sample of her remains to see if it showed any Middle Eastern characteristics. It did not. (She belonged
2 CONC  to mtDNA haplogroup U5a1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.facebook.com/Merovingians-The-Once-The-Present-Future-kings-471707109534948/
2 CONT
2 CONT This article discusses studies done on the remains of her tomb that was unearthed: https://www.ancient-origins.net/news-history-archaeology/researchers-unlock-mystery-mummified-lung-merovingian-queen-005744 and related articles: h
2 CONC ttps://www.scoop.it/topic/the-merovingian-kingdoms
2 CONT
