1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ben M. Angel's summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Father: Valentinian (b. 2 July 419 - Ravenna, d. 16 March 455 - Rome)
2 CONT *Mother: Licinia Eudoxia (b. 422 - Byzantine Empire, d. after 462 - Carthago, Kingdom of the Vandals)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sibling:
2 CONT *Placidia (sister, m. before 455 Roman Senator and future Emperor of the West Olybrius)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses:
2 CONT *1. Paladius (son of Petronius Maximus, m. April 455 - Rome, d. May 455 - Rome)
2 CONT *2. Huneric (son of Vandal King Geiseric, m. after May 455, d. 484 - Carthago)
2 CONT
2 CONT Child by Huneric:
2 CONT *1. Hilderic (b. c 465 - Carthago, d. 534 - Kingdom of the Vandals, in captivity)
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information and justification:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: c 439 (English Wikipedia notes that she was betrothed in the mid-440s at age 5 to Hilderic - exact source not identified, but a list of sources is attached to the page), presumably in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages: 
2 CONT *First in April 455 in Rome to Paladius, son of Petronius Maximus, who had just seized Rome and killed her father. The Vandals invaded a month later in response to the marriage.
2 CONT *Second in late 455, either in Rome or Carthago, to Huneric, son of Geiseric, King of the Vandals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: c 472 - Hierosolyma, or present Jerusalem, in the Palaestina I district of the Oriens region of the Eastern Roman Empire - in accordance with a map of the Roman Empire of 400 AD. (Apparently, the couple came into conflict o
2 CONC ver religion as Eudocia was Catholic, and Huneric was Aryan. As a result, following the birth of their son Hilderic, Eudocia left the Kingdom of the Vandals and settled in Jerusalem, where she died and was buried next to her mothe
2 CONC r. Time frames listed for this - several non-English Wikipedia sources show 471/472, while English Wikipedia shows 462/474, and FMG Medlands shows no death date or place or burial information, but listing her son as being born c. 
2 CONC 480).
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Jerusalem (from several Wikipedia pages with several general sources listed).
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Wife of Paladius (April - May 455), Wife of Huneric (455-472 - Huneric didn't become King of the Vandals until 477)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternative Names: Eudocia, Eudoxia, Eudokia
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Italy Kings (covering her birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#EudoxiaMHunericVandal
2 CONT
2 CONT VALENTINIAN (Ravenna 2 Jul 419-murdered 16 Mar 455).  
2 CONT *The Chronicle of Marcellinus records the birth "419 V Non Iul" at Ravenna of "Valentinianus iunior…patre Constantio et Placidia matre"[107].  The Pauli Historiæ Romanæ records his birth "VI Non Iul"[108].  Iordanes names "Placidi
2 CONC am" as the mother of "Valentiniani iunioris imperatoris"[109].  
2 CONT *Iordanes records that Placidia was created "Augustam" and her son Valentinian "Cæsar" to lead the opposition to Iohannes who invaded the western empire, and that after Iohannes was defeated Valentinian was created joint emperor a
2 CONC t Ravenna by his maternal uncle[110].  The Chronicle of Marcellinus also records the event, dating it to 424[111].  
2 CONT *He succeeded in 423 as Emperor VALENTINIAN III, jointly with his maternal uncle, Emperor in the West.  The Chronicle of Marcellinus records that "Valentinianus iunior" was made emperor at Ravenna in 425[112].  The Chronicon Pasch
2 CONC ale records that "Valentinianus junior" was named augustus "X Kal Nov" in 425 by "Theodosio juniore Augusto"[113].  
2 CONT *Iordanes records that Emperor Valentinian was murdered by Maximus who had invaded the empire[114].  The Chronicon Paschale records that "Valentinianus Augustus" was murdered in 455[115].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (29 Oct 437) as her first husband, EUDOXIA, daughter of Emperor THEODOSIUS II, Emperor in the East & his wife Eudoxia --- (422-after 462).  
2 CONT *The Chronicle of Marcellinus records the betrothal in 424 of "Valentinianus cæsar" and "Theodosii imperatoris Eudoxiam filiam"[116].  Ioannes Malalas records the marriage of "Valentinianus iunior…Constantii Imperatoris et Placidi
2 CONC æ magnæ filius" and "Theodosio…filiam Eudoxiam…ex Eudocia Augusta, Philosophi filia"[117].  
2 CONT *Theophanes records the marriage of "Valentinianus Gallæ Placidiæ et Constantini filius" and "Eudoxiam Theodosii imperatoris filiam ex Eudocia coniuge" in 426[118].  Iordanes records the marriage of Emperor Valentinian III in the 
2 CONC third year of his reign to "Eudoxiam Theodosii principis filiam", specifying that her father transferred Illyria to the western empire as her dowry[119].  The Chronicon Paschale records the marriage "Constantinopolim…XII Kal Nov" 
2 CONC in 437 of "Valentinianus junior Augustus" and "Eudoxia filia Theodosii et Eudocæ Augustæ"[120].  
2 CONT *She married secondly ([Mar] 455) Emperor Petronius Maximus.  
2 CONT *After the murder of her first husband, she was forced to marry his successor, but was captured during the Vandal invasion and taken to north Africa with her two daughters[121].  Iordanes records that "Eudoxia Valentiniani uxore" 
2 CONC invited "Gizericus…rex Vandalorum" to Rome from Africa, but was taken back to Africa by him with her two daughters[122].  
2 CONT *She was freed in 462[123].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Valentinian & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT i)  EUDOXIA .  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon Paschale names "Eudociam et Placidiam" as the two daughters of "Valentinianus junior Augustus" and his wife "Eudoxia filia Theodosii et Eudocæ Augustæ"[124].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus arranged the marriage of “filio suo ex priore coniuge Palladio” and “Valentiniani filiam” in 455[125].  After the death of her father, his successor forced Eudoxia to marry his 
2 CONC son, but she was captured by Genseric King of the Vandals during his attack on Rome and taken back to north Africa with her mother and sister[126].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which records her first marriage has not so far been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius names "Eudocia and Placidia" as children of "Eudoxia…and Valentinian", recording that Eudocia was married to Huneric, the older son of Gaiseric, while the other was the wife of Olybrius, a Roman senator[127].  The Victor
2 CONC is Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that "Hugnericus" married "Valentiniani filiam" who had been abducted from Rome in captivity[128].  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that one daughter of Valentinian married “Gento
2 CONC ni Gaiserici filio” and the other “Olybrio Senatori Urbis Romæ”[129].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly PALADIUS, son of Emperor PETRONIUS MAXIMUS & his first wife --- (-[murdered May 455]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([455]) as his second wife, HUNERIC, son of GENSERIC King of the Vandals (-484). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT ii) PLACIDIA .  
2 CONT *The Chronicon Paschale names "Eudociam et Placidiam" as the two daughters of "Valentinianus junior Augustus" and his wife "Eudoxia filia Theodosii et Eudocæ Augustæ"[130].  
2 CONT *She and her husband were captured by Genseric King of the Vandals during his attack on Rome and taken back to north Africa with her mother and sister, freed in 462[131].  Procopius names "Eudocia and Placidia" as children of "Eud
2 CONC oxia…and Valentinian", recording that Eudocia was married to Huneric, the older son of Gaiseric, while the other was the wife of Olybrius, a Roman senator[132].  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that one daughter of Valenti
2 CONC nian married “Gentoni Gaiserici filio” and the other “Olybrio Senatori Urbis Romæ”[133].  
2 CONT *m (before 455) OLYBRIUS, son of --- (-23 Oct or 2 Nov 472).  He succeeded in 472 as Emperor OLYBRIUS. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT *[107] Marcellini v. c. comitis Chronicon 410, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 70. 
2 CONT *[108] Pauli Historiæ Romanæ Liber XII, MGH SS Auct. ant. II, p. 192. 
2 CONT *[109] Iordanes Romanorum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 40. 
2 CONT *[110] Iordanes Romanorum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 42. 
2 CONT *[111] Marcellini v. c. comitis Chronicon 424, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 76. 
2 CONT *[112] Marcellini v. c. comitis Chronicon 425, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 76. 
2 CONT *[113] Chronicon Paschale, Vol. I, p. 580. 
2 CONT *[114] Iordanes Getarum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 118. 
2 CONT *[115] Chronicon Paschale, Vol. I, p. 592. 
2 CONT *[116] Marcellini v. c. comitis Chronicon 424, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 76. 
2 CONT *[117] Dindorf, L. (ed.) (1831) Ioannes Malalas, Corpus Scriptorum Historiæ Byzantinæ (Bonn) XIV, p. 355. 
2 CONT *[118] Theophanes, Vol. I, 5926/426, p. 142. 
2 CONT *[119] Iordanes Romanorum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 42. 
2 CONT *[120] Chronicon Paschale, Vol. I, p. 582. 
2 CONT *[121] Zosso and Zingg (1995), p. 192. 
2 CONT *[122] Iordanes Romanorum, MGH Auct. ant. V.1, p. 43. 
2 CONT *[123] Zosso and Zingg (1995), p. 200. 
2 CONT *[124] Chronicon Paschale, Vol. I, p. 582. 
2 CONT *[125] Idatii Episcopi Chronicon, España Sagrada III, p. 369. 
2 CONT *[126] Zosso and Zingg (1995), p. 192. 
2 CONT *[127] Procopius, History of the Wars, Book III, c. 7. 
2 CONT *[128] Victoris Tonnennensis Episcopi Chronicon 464, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 187. 
2 CONT *[129] Idatii Episcopi Chronicon, España Sagrada III, p. 379. 
2 CONT *[130] Chronicon Paschale, Vol. I, p. 582. 
2 CONT *[131] Zosso and Zingg (1995), pp. 192 and 200. 
2 CONT *[132] Procopius, History of the Wars, Book III, c. 7. 
2 CONT *[133] Idatii Episcopi Chronicon, España Sagrada III, p. 379. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Italy Kings (covering her first marriage):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ITALY,%20Kings%20to%20962.htm#Paladiusdied455
2 CONT
2 CONT PETRONIUS MAXIMUS ([396]-murdered 27 May 455).  
2 CONT *Theophanes names "Maximus Maximi nepos" when recording that he murdered Emperor Valentinian[139].  A member of the Roman Anicii family[140].  Iordanes records that Maximus murdered Emperor Valentinian after invading the empire[14
2 CONC 1].  
2 CONT *He succeeded in 455 as Emperor PETRONIUS MAXIMUS, Emperor in the West.  After his accession, he forced his predecessor's widow to marry him, and her daughter Eudoxia to marry his son, triggering the invasion by Genseric King of t
2 CONC he Vandals to whom the younger Eudoxia had been promised in marriage for his son.  
2 CONT *He was killed by the Romans after he attempted to flee[142], although the primary sources on which this is based have not yet been identified.  
2 CONT *m firstly ---.  The first wife of Petronius Maximus was lured to the palace of Emperor Valentinian III who raped her[143], although the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([Mar] 455) as her second husband, EUDOXIA, widow of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, daughter of Emperor THEODOSIUS II, Emperor in the East & his wife Eudoxia --- (422-after 462).  
2 CONT *After the murder of her first husband, she was forced to marry his successor, but was captured during the Vandal invasion and taken to north Africa with her two daughters[144].  Ioannes Malalas records the marriage of "Eudoxia Au
2 CONC gusta, Valentiniani Regis vidua" and "Maximo Tyranno"[145].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Petronius Maximus & his first wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)  PALADIUS (-[murdered May 455]).  
2 CONT *The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus installed “filio suo ex priore coniuge Palladio” as cæsar in 455[146].  
2 CONT *It is assumed that he was killed at the same time as his father[147].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([Apr] 455) as her first husband, EUDOXIA, daughter of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, Emperor in the West & his wife Eudoxia .  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus arranged the marriage of “filio suo ex priore coniuge Palladio” and “Valentiniani filiam” in 455[148].  After the death of her father, his successor forced Eudoxia to marry his 
2 CONC son, but she was captured by Genseric King of the Vandals during his attack on Rome and taken back to north Africa with her mother and sister[149].  The primary source which records her first marriage has not so far been identifie
2 CONC d. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
