1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ingoberga''' From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingoberga (c. 520 – 589 in Tours) was the first wife of the Frankish king Charibert I. Her own lineage has not been determined.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingoberga and Charibert were the parents of Bertha van Kent, the later wife of king Æthelberht of Kent and the initiator of the Christian mission under the Anglo-Saxons (the so-called Gregorian mission).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingoberga's marriage was not easy. According to Gregory of Tours, a contemporary historian, "king Charibert married Ingoberga, by whom he had a daughter who afterwards married a husband in Kent and was taken there. At that time In
2 CONC goberga had in her service two daughters of a certain poor man, of whom the first was called Marcovefa, who wore the robe of a nun, and the other was Merofled. The king was very much in love with them. They were, as I have said, t
2 CONC he daughters of a worker in wool. Ingoberga was jealous that they were loved by the king and secretly gave the father work to do, thinking that when the king saw this he would dislike his daughters. While he was working she called
2 CONC  the king. He expected to see something strange, but only saw this man at a distance weaving the king's wool. Upon this he was angry and left Ingoberga and married Merofled."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours (539-594): History of the Franks: Books I-X "In Christ's Name Here Begins The First Book Of The Histories." Medieval Sourcebook
2 CONT
