1 OCCU Eastern Roman Emperor, Kejsare, Imperador Romano do Oriente, @occu00106@, Eastern Roman Emperor (408-450), Empereur d'Orient (2e, Théodose II, 408-450), Emperor of Eastern HRE, keizer Oost Romeinse Rijk
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Theodosius II (10 April 401 – July 28, 450), called the Calligrapher, was a Eastern Roman Emperor (408-450). He is mostly known for promulgating the Theodosian law code as well for the Theodosian Walls of Constanti
2 CONC nople. He also presided over the outbreak of two great christological controversies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosius was born in 401 as the only son of Emperor Arcadius and his Frankish-born wife Aelia Eudoxia. In 408, his father died and the seven-year-old boy became Emperor of the Eastern parts of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Government was at first by the Praetorian Prefect Anthemius, under whose supervision that the Theodosian land walls of Constantinople were constructed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 414, Theodosius' older sister Pulcheria was proclaimed Augusta and assumed the regency. By 416 Theodosius was capable of ruling himself, but his sister remained a strong influence on him. She also assisted her brother in procur
2 CONC ing marriage to the Athenian Aelia Eudocia in June 421. The two had a daughter named Licinia Eudoxia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 423, the Western Emperor Honorius, Theodosius' uncle, died and the primicerius notariorum Joannes was proclaimed Emperor. Honorius' sister Galla Placidia and her young son Valentinian fled to Constantinople to seek Eastern assi
2 CONC stance and after some deliberation in 424 Theodosius opened the war against Joannes. In May 425, Valentinian III was installed as Emperor of the West, with his mother acting as regent. To strengthen the ties between the two parts 
2 CONC of the Empire, Theodosius' daughter Licinia Eudoxia was betrothed to Valentinian.
2 CONT
2 CONT University and Law Code
2 CONT
2 CONT In 425, Theodosius founded the University of Constantinople with 31 chairs (15 in Latin and 16 in Greek). Among subjects were law, philosophy, medicine, arithmetic, geometry, astronomy, music and rhetoric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodosius died in 450 as the result of a riding accident. In the ensuing power struggle, his sister Pulcheria, who had recently returned to court, won out against the eunuch Chrysaphius. She married the general Marcian, thereby m
2 CONC aking him Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fergus Miller: A Greek Roman Empire: Power and Belief Under Theodosius II. University of California Press, Berkeley 2006.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Vasiliki Limberis, Divine Heiress: The Virgin Mary and the Creation of Christian Constantinople (London: Routledge, 1994) has a significant section about Theodosius II and his sister Pulcheria.
2 CONT
