1 NOTE
2 CONT The kingdom of Kent, founded by Oisc (Aesc) son of Hengist circa A.D.488, had prospered in the years of peace following Badon Hill. By the endof the 6th Century it was ruled by Aethelberht, a descendant of Oisc, whoreigned from A.
2 CONC D. 560 to 616. It was to Aethelberht's court that PopeGregory dispatched the famous mission led by Augustine, to convert theheathen English to Christianity. The foundation for this had already beenlaid by Aethelberht's wife, Queen
2 CONC  Bertha, a princess of the Merovingianroyal house in France, who was already a Christian. Mass baptisms of thepopulace took place on Christmas day A.D. 597. From this beginningChristianity was to spread throughout England, first t
2 CONC o Northumberlandand then, gradually to all the other kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Aethelbert of East Anglia
2 CONT (AD 779-794)
2 CONT King of East Anglia
2 CONT Died: 20th May AD 794 at Sutton Walls, Herefordshire
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Kent ( 560 - 616 ). First Anglo Saxon king to be converted toChristianity in 597. He was married to a Christian Frankish princessnamed Bertha. His wife, a Christian, was the deciding factor that led St.Augustine to start h
2 CONC is conversion mission in Kent. Kent was the firstkingdom to become Christian. It became the home of the Archbishop ofCanterbury or Primate of all England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aethelbert was the son and successor of Aethelred, King of East Anglia.He came to the throne very young, at the time that the powerful Offa wasKing of Mercia. Offa was, in many things, a good and just ruler, but hewas guilty of a 
2 CONC single act of treachery towards Aethelbert, apparentlyprompted by his wife, Cwendreda.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young prince, disregarding the forebodings of his mother, came to theCourt of Offa at Sutton Walls, in Herefordshire, to seek the hand of hisbeautiful and pious daughter, Etheldreda. Offa received him with greatrespect and hos
2 CONC pitality; but the Queen, Cwendreda, was full of ambitiousschemes. She took the King to one side and exclaimed, "Look, your enemyhas been delivered into your hands. He whose kingdom you have so longcoveted. Now destroy him secretly
2 CONC  and his kingdom will be yours and yourheirs forever." The King hesitated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Traditionally Aethelbert's head was struck off, but Matthew ofWestminster tells another tale. He says that the Queen placed a richlyadorned chair in the bedroom of the young King, over a trap door in thefloor, and, on the chair, s
2 CONC he placed a number of silk cushions. The youngman, on reaching his room after a banquet, flung himself into the chair.The trap immediately gave way and he was precipitated into a vault wheresome of the Queen's servants were statio
2 CONC ned; and they quickly suffocatedhim with the silk cushions.
2 CONT
2 CONT It can hardly be doubted that Offa was privy to the commission of themurder. He certainly lost no time in taking advantage of it, for he senttroops into East Anglia and annexed it at once to his own possessions.Then, as usual, he 
2 CONC built churches and monasteries to atone for hiswickedness, especially St. Albans Abbey and Hereford Cathedral. The laterwas dedicated to St. Aethelbert and was the place of his burial. Some saythat Offa went a pilgrimage to Rome. 
2 CONC At any rate, he gave much tochurches in that city and especially to the English school there.Ealfreda, abhorring the crime that had been committed by her parents,retired to Crowland Abbey, where she spent forty years in seclusion 
2 CONC anddied in the odour of sanctity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edited from S. Baring-Gould's "The Lives of the Saints" (1877).
2 CONT
