1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Æthelburh of Kent, Queen-Consort of Northumbrian Deira and Benicia, Abbess of Lyminge===
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia - http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86thelburg_of_Kent
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Æthelburh (c. late sixth century - 647), also known as Ethelburg Ædilburh and Æthelburga (Old English: Æþelburh), was the second wife of Edwin of Northumbria. She was the daughter of King Ethelbert of Kent and the Merovingia
2 CONC n princess Saint Bertha, as well as the sister of Eadbald and Edburga. Æthelburh’s marriage to Edwin in 625 triggered the conversion of the north of England to Roman Catholicism.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Secular life
2 CONT
2 CONT It was said by Saint Bede the Venerable that Eadbald would only agree to marry his sister to Edwin if she and her entourage were given the freedom to continue to practise Roman Catholicism in her new home. Accordingly Paulinus of 
2 CONC York accompanied Æthelburh to Northumbria as her chaplain. Edwin converted to Roman Catholicism two years after their marriage and was responsible for building the first York Minster where he was baptised.
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelburh had a prominent role in coverting the Northumbrians and Bede records letters and presents that Pope Boniface V sent to both Edwin and his wife. After Edwin’s death at the Battle of Hatfield Chase in 633, she fled to Kent
2 CONC  with Paulinus and her children.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelburh’s children with Edwin were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Eanfleda of Deira
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethelhun
2 CONT
2 CONT Wuscfrea
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Edwen of Llanedwen, Anglesey
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Religious leader
2 CONT
2 CONT On Æthelburh’s return to Kent, Eadbald gave his sister a ruined Roman villa in Lyminge where she founded an abbey. This was reputedly the first monastery in Kent and is believed to have initially been a religious sanctuary for men
2 CONC  and women (Æthelburh was succeeded in her role as leader by members of both sexes). Æthelburh died at Lyminge and her relics were stored at the Collegiate Church at Canterbury until the time of the Dissolution of the Monasteries 
2 CONC by Henry VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT The probable remains of the abbey still exist close to the modern church (St Mary’s and St Æthelburh’s). There is also a sacred well which can still be seen on the village green which is named in her memory. The successful private
2 CONC  school of "Queen Æthelburh's" near York, which is now approaching its centenary, is named after her.
2 CONT
2 CONT St Æthelburh was canonized and her feast day is April 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Ethelburga of Lyming,
2 CONT
2 CONT Abbess of Lyming
2 CONT
2 CONT (Died 647)
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Ethelburga was the daughter of St. Aethelbert, first Roman Catholic King of Kent and founder of the See of Canterbury, and his wife, St. Bertha. In AD 625, Ethelburga was married to St. Edwin, who, after many wars and vicissit
2 CONC udes, was now sole King of Northumbria, and the fifth and greatest of all the Bretwaldas. He promised her and all her entourage, of whatever rank and sex, full liberty to observe their own religion in his Kingdom. He furthermore u
2 CONC ndertook to consult with wise persons and examine Roman Catholic worship. If he found it more holy and worthy than the religion he up till then professed, he would himself adopt it. With Ethelburga went Paulinus, ordained bishop f
2 CONC or the occasion, that he might strengthen the party's faith, lest any should be corrupted by associating with pagans.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Easter Day, the year after Ethelburga's marriage, an assault was made upon Edwin with a poisoned weapon by an assassin sent by Cwichelm, the heir of Wessex. Edwin's faithful servant, Lilla, interposed his body and died in his m
2 CONC aster's stead. Ethelburga was pregnant at the time, and the shock appears to have initiated the labour that same night. The Queen was safely delivered of a daughter, who was called Enflaed and the King gave thanks to his gods. Pau
2 CONC linus gave thanks to Christ, saying that it was through his intervention that the Queen's life had been spared. Edwin retorted that, if the Roman Catholic deity would procure him victory over Cwichelm and recovery from his wound, 
2 CONC he would be converted. As a pledge of his sincerity and to Ethelburga's delight, he delivered up the new-born princess to Paulinus to be baptised.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King travelled south with an army against Cwichelm but, returning victorious, he hesitated still about adopting Roman Catholicism. Ethelburga and Paulinus offered him much instruction on the subject and he consulted also the w
2 CONC isest men of his own Kingdom. Pope Boniface even took an interest in his conversion and, about this time, wrote two letters, one to Edwin and one to Ethelburga, urging the great religious change and he sending them presents, with 
2 CONC the blessing of St. Peter. The Queen received a silver looking-glass and an ivory gilt comb.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, Edwin convened his Witan at Londesborough where he and his thegns agreed to accept Roman Catholicism. On Easter Day AD 627, in a new wooden church at York, Edwin was baptised with his and Ethelburga's son, Ethelhun, an
2 CONC d several other Royal relations. Their example was soon followed by thousands of people of all ages and conditions. Ethelhun died while still wearing his white baptismal robes and was buried in York Minster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Penda, king of Mercia - a fierce heathen warrior, brother of Edwin's first wife, Cwenburga - invaded Edwin's dominions and defeated the Northumbrians in the great Battle of Hatfield in Nottinghamshire. Edwin and his eldest son, Os
2 CONC frith, were killed. Queen Ethelburga, with Paulinus and her young children, managed to escape by sea to Kent, to the court of her brother King Edbald. She took with her many of Edwin's treasures, especially a golden cup and cross,
2 CONC  which were preserved at Canterbury in Bede's time. Edbald gave Ethelburga a ruined Roman villa at Lyming, between Canterbury and the sea. There, she built the first nunnery in England where she was joined by her sister St. Edburg
2 CONC a of Lyming and there they both took the veil.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethelburga sent her son, Wuscfrea, and Yffi, son of her late step-son, Osfrith, to Dagobert, King of the Franks, to be educated. They died young and were buried in France with Royal honours. Besides Wuscfrea and St. Enflaeda, she 
2 CONC had two children, who died before their father and were buried in York Minster. Ethelburga lived as Abbess of Lyming for several years. She died 8th September AD 647 and her grave may still be seen there. Ethelburga was the first 
2 CONC queen and the first widow of Anglo-Saxon race to take the veil. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edited from Agnes Dunbar's "A Dictionary of Saintly Women" (1904).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
