1 OCCU King of Aquitaine (629-632), koning van Aquitanië, Konge af Aquitanien, Roi d'Aquitaine, Deelkoning van Aquitanië 629–632, Gouverneur d'Aquitaine (628-632), koning van Aquitanie
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charibert_II
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charibert II (c.608–8 April 632), a son of Clotaire II and his second wife Sichilde, was briefly king of Aquitaine from 629 to his death, with his capital at Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father, Clotaire II, King of the Franks, died in 629, Charibert made a bid for the kingdom of Neustria against his elder half-brother Dagobert I, who had already been king of Austrasia since 623. In the ensuing negotiatio
2 CONC ns, Charibert, a minor, was represented by his uncle Brodulf, the brother of Queen Sichilde. Dagobert had Brodulf killed and ceded the near-independent realm of Aquitaine to Charibert. This agreement was confirmed in 631, when Cha
2 CONC ribert stood godfather to Dagobert's son Sigebert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charibert's realm included Toulouse, Cahors, Agen, Perigueux, and Saintes, to which he added his possessions in Gascony. Charibert was married to Gisela, the heiress of Amand of Gascony. His fighting force subdued the resistance o
2 CONC f the Basques, until the whole of the Basque Country was under his control.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 632, Charibert died at Blaye, Gironde—possibly assassinated on Dagobert's orders—and soon after that Charibert's infant son Chilperic was also killed. Aquitaine passed again to Dagobert. Both Charibert and his son are buried in
2 CONC  the early Romanesque Basilica of Saint-Romain at Blaye.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT His ancestry is continued elsewhere in this tree. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charibert II's father was King of all Franks Clothaire Meroving II and his mother was Haldetrude de Bourgogne.  His paternal grandparents were Chilperic I Soissons  Franks and Fredegunde Franks; his maternal grandparents were Rich
2 CONC emeres von Franconia and Garritrude de Hamage. He had a brother and two sisters, named Dagobert I, Bertha and Emma.  He was the youngest of the four children.
