1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King of the Huns (433-451); defeated at Chalons-sur-Marne
2 CONT Murdered brother Bleda (joint king) 445
2 CONT On becoming the King of the Huns in 433, jointly with his brother Bleda, whom
2 CONT he murdered twelve years later, he aroused them from their sedentary life in
2 CONT Hungary & encouraged them to resume their wars with the Romans. Atilla was
2 CONT only interested in power. He rubbed this in by feeding prominent guests off
2 CONT gold crockery but himself ate from a wooden platter & drank from a wooden cup.
2 CONT By levying a tribute of about a ton of gold a year from the Eastern Empire,
2 CONT which was useless in his economy, he strangled the Roman's cash economy. The
2 CONT war of 451 had been triggered by what Atilla interpreted as a marriage offer
2 CONT from Honoria, Valentian's sister, who had asked Attila for help to avoid a
2 CONT forced marriage. Valentinian had refused to let her marry her lover & intended
2 CONT to marry her off to someone who could control her. She sent a ring to Atilla
2 CONT when she tried to recruit his help. He then demanded half the Western Empire
2 CONT as a dowry!
2 CONT Chalons was not yet the end of Attila's career but it was the turning point in
2 CONT pushing back the Huns rampage across two continents, despite further ravages
2 CONT by Attila in Italy. Although Pope Leo I claimed the credit, Attila's movement
2 CONT out of Italy was determined both by the lack of hay & fear of the plague.
