2 CONT show less
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Italy Kings:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm#ThrasamundVandal
2 CONT
2 CONT GENSERIC [Gaiseric], illegitimate son of GODEGISL King of the Vandals & his concubine --- ([400]-477).
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius names “Gizerichus notus” as son of “Godigisclo”[17]. He succeeded his half-brother in 427 as GENSERIC King of the Vandals. Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum names "Gesericus frater Gunderici" when recordin
2 CONC g that he succeeded the latter as Vandal king in Spain[18].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Vandals crossed into North Africa en masse in May 429, although what prompted this move is unclear[19]. The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Gaisericus Rex…cum Wandalis omnibus” left “Bæticæ Provinciæ litore” and went
2 CONC  “ad Mauritaniam et Africam” in May 429[20].
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Iordanes, the Romans lost the province of Africa to the Vandals "per Bonifatium"[21], although this does not explain why they arrived there in the first place. The exploits in Africa of "Geiserici ducis" are described
2 CONC  in detail in the Victoris Vitensis Historia[22].
2 CONT
2 CONT He conquered Hipona in 429, and Carthage in 439, where they established their capital.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Vandal nobles rebelled against their leader in 442[23].
2 CONT
2 CONT Genseric sacked Rome in 455.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Eastern Roman Empire's first expedition against the Vandals in Africa in 468, led by Basiliscus, brother-in-law of Emperor Leo I, failed despite numerical superiority[24]. The Vandal kingdom dominated the western Mediterranean
2 CONC  with Corsica and Sardinia.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Genseric made an agreement with Odoacar King of Italy about Sicily, the latter retaining control over the island in return for yearly tribute[25].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records the death in 464 of "Gensericum Wandalorum rex" after reigning for 40 years[26].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The names of Genseric's wife or concubines are not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genseric had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. HUNERIC (-484).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Victoris Vitensis Historia names "Huniricus maior filius [Geiserici]" when recording that he succeeded on the death of his father[27]. He lived as a hostage at Ravenna in 442[28].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 477 as HUNERIC King of the Vandals in Africa. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that "Hugnericus filius eius" succeeded his father in 464 and reigned for 7 years and 5 months[29]. The 
2 CONC Victoris Vitensis Historia names him "rex Hunirix Wandalorum et Alanorum" when recording his exploits in Africa[30].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ([429] or after[31], repudiated 442) --- the Visigoth, daughter of THEODERIC I King of the Visigoths & his wife ---. The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was sent back to her father at the time of the 442 revolt with her nose and ears mutilated[32].
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([455]) as her second husband, EUDOXIA, widow of PALADIUS, daughter of Emperor VALENTINIAN III, Emperor in the West & his wife Eudoxia. The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that Maximus arranged the marriage of “fili
2 CONC o suo ex priore coniuge Palladio” and “Valentiniani filiam” in 455[33]. After the death of her father, his successor forced Eudoxia to marry his son, but she was captured by Genseric King of the Vandals during his attack on Rome a
2 CONC nd taken back to north Africa with her mother and sister[34].
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius names "Eudocia and Placidia" as children of "Eudoxia…and Valentinian", recording that Eudoxia was married to Huneric, the older son of Gaiseric, while the other daughter was the wife of Olybrius, a Roman senator[35]. The
2 CONC  Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that one daughter of Valentinian married “Gentoni Gaiserici filio” and the other “Olybrio Senatori Urbis Romæ”[36]. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that "Hugnericus" marrie
2 CONC d "Valentiniani filiam" who had been abducted from Rome in captivity[37].
2 CONT
2 CONT Huneric & his second wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) HILDERIC ([480]-murdered 534).
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Ilderich" as fifth Vandal king in Africa but does not specify his relationship to Thrasamund, his predecessor[38]. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records the succession in 523 of "Hilderix, qui ex Val
2 CONC entiniani imperatoris filia…et Ugnerico iuncta natus est" and that he reigned for 7 years and 3 months[39]. He succeeded [his presumed cousin] in 523 as HILDERIC King of the Vandals in Africa 523.
2 CONT
2 CONT He broke the Vandal alliance with Theodoric King of Italy, supporting the emperor at Constantinople instead[40]. He was deposed by his cousin Gailamir in 530. Iordanes records that Hilderic was deposed by "Gelimer" but does not sp
2 CONC ecify the relationship between the two[41]. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that Hilderic was deposed by "Gerlimer" in 531, imprisoned with his sons, and killed in 534 "cum quibusdam generis eius affinibus"[42
2 CONC ].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The name of Hilderic's wife is not known. Hilderic & his wife had --- children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i) sons (-murdered 534). The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that Hilderic was deposed by "Gerlimer" in 531, imprisoned with his sons, and killed in 534 "cum quibusdam generis eius affinibus"[43]. The number of so
2 CONC ns is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. THEODERIC . The Victoris Vitensis Historia records that Huneric exiled "Theodericum fratrem filiosque eius" in order to ensure the succession of his sons[44].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The Victoris Vitensis Historia records the exile of Theoderic after his wife and sons were killed, although neither his wife nor his sons are named[45].
2 CONT
2 CONT Theoderic & his wife had --- children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) sons . The number of sons is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. GENTO [Geiso] (after 420-before 484). The Victoris Vitensis Historia names "Gentunis" as brother of Huneric[47].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---. The name of Gento's wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gento & his wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) GODAGIS .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Victoris Vitensis Historia names "Gentunis maiorem filium nomine Godagis" when recording that he was cruelly exiled by his paternal uncle Huneric "cum uxore absque solacio servuli aut ancillæ"[48].
2 CONT
2 CONT b) GUNDAMUND (before 460-496).
2 CONT
2 CONT Laterculus regum Vandalorum et Alanorum names "Guntamundus Gentunis eiusdem Huneric regis fratris filius" when recording his accession and reign of 11 years[49]. He succeeded his uncle in 484 as GUNDAMUND King of the Vandals in Af
2 CONC rica. Iordanes names "Gunthamundus" as third Vandal king in Africa but does not specify his relationship to Huneric, his predecessor[50].
2 CONT
2 CONT After Theodoric the Ostrogoth forced Odovacar King of Italy to flee in 490, the Vandals considered themselves free from the treaty obligation relating to Sicily and attempted to conquer the island. Theodoric defeated the Vandals i
2 CONC n Sicily in 491[51].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records the death in 497 of "Gunthamundo Wandalorum rege Carthagine"[52].
2 CONT
2 CONT c) THRASAMUND (before 460-523).
2 CONT
2 CONT Laterculus regum Vandalorum et Alanorum names "Trasamundus Gentunis filius" and "Trasamundus frater Guntamundi regis" when recording his accession and reign of 26 years[53]. Iordanes names "Thrasamundus" as fourth Vandal king in A
2 CONC frica but does not specify his relationship to Gundamund, his predecessor[54].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded [his presumed brother] in 496 as THRASAMUND King of the Vandals in Africa. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that "Trasamundus" succeeded "Gunthamundo Wandalorum rege Carthagine" in 497 and reigned 
2 CONC 27 years and 4 months[55].
2 CONT
2 CONT He agreed an alliance with Theodoric King of Italy, confirmed by his marriage with the latter's sister, but failed to provide effective aid to the Ostrogoth king when he was attacked by the imperial navy in 507. He also supported 
2 CONC Gesalic, pretender to the Visigothic throne, against King Amalric the grandson of King Theodoric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gregory of Tours records the persecution of Christians by Thrasamund King of the Vandals and his imposition of Arianism on Spain[56], but his account is anachronistic as he places Thrasamund before Huneric and assumes that the for
2 CONC mer reigned before the Vandals emigrated from Spain to Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records the death in 523 of "Trasamundus Wandalorum rex Carthagine"[57].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([500]) as her second husband, AMALAFRIDA, widow of ---, daughter of THEODEMIR King of the Ostrogoths in Pannonia (-murdered [523/25]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names "Amalfridam germanam suam [Theoderici]" as the mother of "Theodehadi" and wife of "Africa regi Vandalorum…Thrasamundo"[58]. This marriage was arranged by her half-brother, Theodoric King of Italy, as part of his eff
2 CONC orts to foster the support of the Vandals. Amalafrida's dowry was Lilybæum in western Sicily[59].
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband, she unsuccessfully protested his successor's withdrawal of support from her brother, but she was outmanœuvred and killed[60]. The Victoris Tonnennensis Epsicopi Chronicon records that "uxor Amalfrid
2 CONC a" fled after the death of her husband but was captured at Capsa and killed[61].
2 CONT
2 CONT d) GAILAR [Gelaris] (before 460-before 523).
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius names “Gelimer, Gelaridis filius, Genzonis nepos, pronepos Gizerici”[62]. m ---. The name of Gailar's wife is not known. Gailar & his wife had four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
