1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Anicia Faltonia Proba
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Anicia Faltonia Proba (died in Africa, 432) was a Roman noblewoman of the gens Anicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT  [hide] 1 Biography
2 CONT  2 Notes
2 CONT  3 Bibliography 3.1 Primary sources
2 CONT  3.2 Secondary sources
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Proba's father was Quintus Clodius Hermogenianus Olybrius[1] (consul in 379); the famous poet Faltonia Betitia Proba was a relative. She married Sextus Petronius Probus (consul in 371), and had three sons - Anicius Hermogenianus O
2 CONC lybrius and Anicius Probinus, joint consuls in 395, and Anicius Petronius Probus consul in 406 - and one daughter, Anicia Proba. Her son Olybrius married Anicia Iuliana, and his daughter Demetrias was Proba's granddaughter. She wa
2 CONC s related to the aristocratic families of the Petronii, Olybrii and Anicii; in two inscriptions dating to 395 she is described as daughter, wife and mother of consuls.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 395 she was already a widow. A Christian, she was in contact with several members of the cultural circles of her age, among which Augustine of Hippo[3] and John Chrysostom,[4] in favour of whom she acted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Proba was in Rome during the sack of the city in 410; according to Procopius of Caesarea, she opened the gates of the city to relieve the sufferings of the people besieged,[5] but historians have suggested that this story was forg
2 CONC ed by her enemies.[6] She then fled to Africa with her daughter-in-law Anicia Iuliana and her granddaughter Demetrias, but here she was abused by Heraclianus, who imprisoned and then freed them only after receiving a huge sum.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Proba inherited several possessions in Asia, and sold them to give the money to the Church and to the poor. She died in Africa in 432; it is known that her husband had been buried in the Old St. Peter's Basilica in a tomb where Pr
2 CONC oba was to be buried too.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT As several other women in her family, Proba was well-educated. Her grand-mother, Faltonia Betitia Proba, were a poet, Anicia probably composed the epigraph in honour of the husband, and her nephew Demetria was a friend of Jerome's
2 CONC , who describes her as well-educated.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Jones.
2 CONT  2.^ CIL VI, 1754; CIL VI, 1754.
2 CONT  3.^ Augustine addressed to Proba his letters number 130 and 131, to Proba and her daughter-in-law Anicia Iuliana his letter number 150, and cited Proba in De bono vid. (24).
2 CONT  4.^ John wrote to Proba his letter number 169.
2 CONT  5.^ Procopius of Caesarea, Bellum Vandalicum, I.2.27
2 CONT  6.^ P. Laurence, "Proba, Juliana et Démétrias. Le christianisme des femmes de la "gens Anicia" dans le première moitié du Ve siècle", in REAug, 48 (2002), pp. 142-4.
2 CONT  7.^ Jerome, Epistles, 130.
2 CONT  8.^ CLE 1347 A.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Primary sources
2 CONT  CIL VI, 1754; CIL VI, 1755; CIL VI, 1756; CIL VI, 32032=CIL XXV, 7158; CIL XXV, 7159
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Secondary sources
2 CONT  Arnold Hugh Martin Jones, John Martindale, John Morris: The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire (PLRE). Vol. 1, Cambridge 1971, pp. 732-733.
2 CONT  Jane Stevenson: Women Latin Poets. Oxford University Press, 2005, p. 65.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anicia_Faltonia_Proba
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anicia_Faltonia_Proba
