1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ben M. Angel summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents and siblings: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Partner: Godegisl, King of the Vandals (to 406)
2 CONT
2 CONT Child: Genseric/Gaiseric (c. 400 - 477), King of the Vandals (427-477)
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information and justification:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: Date unknown (from the birth of her child, anywhere from 360-385 would be reasonable) - Pannonia (present Hungary)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Date and place unknown. Godegisl died in a battle with the Franks just before the Vandals forced a crossing of the Rhine, beginning their invasion of Gaul. Her fate (as well as a lot of other basic information about her) ha
2 CONC s long since been lost in time.
2 CONT
2 CONT If you have other information with regard to this ancestor, please post the source for it. Primary sources take priority over secondary sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Vandals, Suevi, and Visigoths:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm#GensericA
2 CONT
2 CONT GODEGISL (-406).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  King of the Vandals.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m FLORA, daughter of ---.  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Concubine: ---.  The name of Godegisl's concubine is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Godegisl and his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)  GUNDERIC (-427).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius records that “eius filii Gontharis, ex iusta ipsi uxore natus” succeeded after “Godigisclo” died[8].  He succeeded his father in 406 as GUNDERIC King of the Vandals.   Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum nam
2 CONC es "Gundericus" as first Vandal king in Spain[9].  Gregory of Tours names Gunderic as king of the Vandals who left their homeland and invaded Gaul before attacking Spain[10].   General Maximus, who led the army of anti-Emperor Con
2 CONC stantine III in Spain, invited the Vandals, along with other barbarian peoples, across the Pyrenees in 409 in a personal bid for power but soon found himself overrun[11].   The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Alani et Wa
2 CONC ndali et Suevi” entered Spain in 409[12].  Gunderic and his people settled in Galicia.  They expanded into land held by the Suevi, continuing to support Maximus as anti-Emperor, although the latter was defeated by combined Roman a
2 CONC nd Visigothic forces in 422.  The Vandals turned their attention to southern Spain where they imposed their control from Cadiz to Alicante.   Gunderic died after conquering Seville[13].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ELISA, daughter of ---, from Granada.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Victoris Vitensis Historia records that Gaiseric killed his brother's wife by weighing her down with a stone and throwing her into the river Cirtensis at Amsaga, before killing her sons, although neither the brother nor the br
2 CONC other's wife are named[14].  The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identified.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunderic & his wife had one child: Gelimer (b. 425, m. Eurica, daughter of Adulphus, King of the Visigoths)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Godegisl had one illegitimate son by his concubine: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b) GENSERIC [Gaiseric] ([400]-477).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius names “Gizerichus notus” as son of “Godigisclo”[15].  Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum names "Gesericus frater Gunderici" when recording that he succeeded the latter as Vandal king in Spain[16].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his half-brother in 427 as GENSERIC King of the Vandals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [8] Dindorf, W. (ed.) (1833) Procopius, Vol. 1, Corpus Scriptorum Historiæ Byzantinæ (Bonn), De Bello Vandalico I.3, p. 323.  
2 CONT
