2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Godigisel:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godigisel
2 CONT
2 CONT Godigisel (359-406) was King of the Hasdingi Vandals until his death in 406. He was killed in battle late in 406, shortly before his people forced a crossing of the Rhine River into the territory of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godigisel was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, Gunderic, who led the Vandals into Gaul and later to Spain. But he was best known as the father of Geiseric, who succeeded Gunderic to the kingship in 428 and ruled for 49 years
2 CONC , establishing a powerful kingdom in North Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT (No sources)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the German Wikipedia page on Godigisel:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godigisel
2 CONT
2 CONT Godigisel (häufig auch Godegisel; † wohl 406) war ein König der Vandalen (Wandalen) vom Teilstamm der Hasdingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unter seiner Herrschaft begannen die Vandalen um ca. 400 vermutlich unter dem Druck der Hunnen nach Westen zu ziehen und schließlich in das weströmische Reich einzudringen. In dieser Zeit schlossen sich den Hasdingen Teile der van
2 CONC dalischen Silingen an. Kurz vor der Überschreitung des Rhein zum Jahreswechsel 406/407 (siehe Rheinübergang von 406) kam es zu einem Kampf zwischen den Hasdingen und fränkischen Kriegern, die sich wohl als römische Föderaten den V
2 CONC andalen entgegenstellten. Bei dieser für die Vandalen äußerst verlustreichen Schlacht kam auch Godigisel ums Leben, die Hasdingen wurden nur durch eine Truppe Alanen unter Respendial gerettet.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nachfolger von Godigisel wurde sein ältester überlebender Sohn Gunderich, der die Vandalen über den Rhein nach Gallien und 409 nach Hispanien führte. Dessen Nachfolger Geiserich, unter dem das Vandalenreich in Nordafrika zur mächt
2 CONC igsten Seemacht des westlichen Mittelmeeres aufstieg, war ebenfalls ein Sohn von Godigisel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Godigisel (also often Godegisel ; d. around 406 ) was a king of the Vandals (Vandals) and a member of the Hasdingi tribe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under his rule, the Vandals, probably under pressure from the Huns,  started at about 400 to migrate westward and eventually penetrate into the Western Roman Empire. During this period, the Vandals included both the Hasdingi and t
2 CONC he Silingi. Just before crossing the Rhine in the year 406/407 (the year of great crisis for the Roman Empire as many Germanic tribes invaded Roman Gaul that year), there was a fight between the Hasdingi and Frankish warriors who 
2 CONC were probably Roman allies. In this extremely costly battle, the Vandals lost and Godigisel was killed; it was only because of the intervention of an army of Alans under Respendial that any of the Hasdingi survived.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Godigisel's successor was his eldest surviving son Gunderich, and under him the Vandals crossed the Rhine into Gaul and were led into Hispania in 409. His successor Gaiseric brought the Vandal kingdom into North Africa, creating t
2 CONC he most powerful naval force of the western Mediterranean; Gaiseric was another son of Godigisel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literatur
2 CONT
2 CONT Helmut Castritius: Die Vandalen. Kohlhammer, Stuttgart 2007.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anmerkungen
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. ↑ Renatus Profuturus Frigeridus, Historia, erhalten als Auszug bei Gregor von Tours (Historiae II 9). Frigeridus spricht von angeblich 20.000 toten Vandalen, was sicherlich eine Übertreibung darstellt, aber auf das Ausmaß der v
2 CONC andalischen Niederlage hindeutet. (Frigeridus asserts that there were 20,000 dead Vandals, which is almost certainly an exaggeration, but nonetheless suggests the magnitude of the Vandal defeat.)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Godegisl ( -406), King of the Vandals. His ancestry is thought to have been a late, probably mythological, invention. During his reign, the Vandals were at war with the Franks. He and some 20,000 of his men were killed in a major 
2 CONC battle with them. The Vandals were on the point of extinction when the Alans came to their aid. After Godegisal's death, the Vandals and Alans crossed the Rhine and entered Roman Gaul. In 409 they took Spain.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Godegisl ( -406), King of the Vandals. His ancestry is thought to have been a late, probably mythological, invention. During his reign, the Vandals were at war with the Franks. He and some 20,000 of his men were killed in a major 
2 CONC battle with them. The Vandals were on the point of extinction when the Alans came to their aid. After Godegisal's death, the Vandals and Alans crossed the Rhine and entered Roman Gaul. In 409 they took Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Vandals, Suevi, and Visigoths:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm#_Toc179360427
2 CONT
2 CONT RADAGAISUS the Vandal .
2 CONT
2 CONT m CELLA, daughter of ---. The primary source which confirms her marriage has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Radagaisus & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. GODEGISL (-406).
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified. King of the Vandals.
2 CONT
2 CONT m FLORA, daughter of ---. The primary source which confirms her parentage has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Concubine: ---. The name of Godegisl's concubine is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godegisl and his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) GUNDERIC (-427).
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius records that “eius filii Gontharis, ex iusta ipsi uxore natus” succeeded after “Godigisclo” died[8]. He succeeded his father in 406 as GUNDERIC King of the Vandals. Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum names 
2 CONC "Gundericus" as first Vandal king in Spain[9]. Gregory of Tours names Gunderic as king of the Vandals who left their homeland and invaded Gaul before attacking Spain[10]. General Maximus, who led the army of anti-Emperor Constanti
2 CONC ne III in Spain, invited the Vandals, along with other barbarian peoples, across the Pyrenees in 409 in a personal bid for power but soon found himself overrun[11]. The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Alani et Wandali et
2 CONC  Suevi” entered Spain in 409[12]. Gunderic and his people settled in Galicia. They expanded into land held by the Suevi, continuing to support Maximus as anti-Emperor, although the latter was defeated by combined Roman and Visigot
2 CONC hic forces in 422. The Vandals turned their attention to southern Spain where they imposed their control from Cadiz to Alicante. Gunderic died after conquering Seville[13]. m ELISA, daughter of ---, from Granada. The Victoris Vite
2 CONC nsis Historia records that Gaiseric killed his brother's wife by weighing her down with a stone and throwing her into the river Cirtensis at Amsaga, before killing her sons, although neither the brother nor the brother's wife are 
2 CONC named[14]. The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identified. Gunderic & his wife had one child, Gelimer (b. c425, husband of Eurica, daughter of Adulphus/Ataulf) Godegisl had one illegitimate son by his concu
2 CONC bine:
2 CONT
2 CONT b) GENSERIC [Gaiseric] ([400]-477).
2 CONT
2 CONT Procopius names “Gizerichus notus” as son of “Godigisclo”[15]. Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum names "Gesericus frater Gunderici" when recording that he succeeded the latter as Vandal king in Spain[16]. He succeed
2 CONC ed his half-brother in 427 as GENSERIC King of the Vandals. References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [9] Isidori Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 296. [10] Thorpe, L. (trans.) (1974) Gregory of Tours: The History of the Franks (Penguin), II.2, p. 106. [11] Atkinson, W. C. (1960) A History of Spain an
2 CONC d Portugal (Penguin 1973), p. 35. [12] Idatii Episcopi Chronicon, España Sagrada III, p. 351. [13] García-Guijarro (2002), p. 12. [14] Victoris Vitensis Historia, Liber II, V, MGH Auct. ant., p. 16. [15] Procopius De Bello Vandali
2 CONC co I.3, p. 323. [16] Isidori Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum 467, MGH Auct. ant. XI, p. 296.
2 CONT -------------------------
