1 NOTE SEAXBURH ([620/30]-after 695).  Bede names "Seaxburg" as eldest daughter of King Anna and "wife to Earconbert king of Kent"[64].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle names "Seaxburh, daughter of Anna king of the East Angles", as mother of E
2 CONC orcengota[65].  Her birth and marriage dates are estimated on the basis of probable birth date of her husband and an approximate chronology of her descendants which appears to be reasonably precise.  Bede records that Sexberga suc
2 CONC ceeded her sister Æthelthryth as abbess at Ely, specifying that after her sister and predecessor had been dead 16 years, Sexberga planned to move her remains but found that the body was uncorrupted when the coffin was opened[66]. 
2 CONC  m ([640/45]) EORCENBERHT King of Kent, son of EADBALD King of Kent & his second wife Emma of the Franks ([620]-14 Jul 664).
