1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dead in the Battle of Dun Nechtain, when Northumbria was defeated by the Pictish King Bridei son of Beli - King of Forteiu. The reign of Bridei marks the start of the period known to historians as the Verturian heg
2 CONC emony, a turning point in the history of Scotland, when the uniting of Pictish provinces under the over-kingship of the kings of Fortriu saw the development of a strong Pictish state and identity encompassing most of the peoples n
2 CONC orth of the Forth.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Dun_Nechtain
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Oswy an Anglo-Saxon King of Northumbria, he succeeded as King when his father
2 CONT
2 CONT died. Ecgfrith gave land at the mouth of the River Wear (what we now call
2 CONT
2 CONT Monkwearmouth in Sunderland) in 674 and land at Jarrow in 681 to a monk, Benedict
2 CONT
2 CONT Biscop, for monasteries to be built. The remains of those monasteries (St. Peter’s,
2 CONT
2 CONT Monkwearmouth and St. Paul’s, Jarrow) survive today. Ecgfrith’s first wife was Etheldreda.
2 CONT
