1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Saint Osthryth of Bernicia'''
2 CONT '''Birth''' 648
2 CONT '''Death''' 697 (aged 48–49) West Midlands, England
2 CONT '''Burial'''
2 CONT Bardney Abbey
2 CONT Bardney, West Lindsey District, Lincolnshire, England
2 CONT Memorial ID 198591912 · View Source
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/198591912/osthryth-of_bernicia
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osthryth
2 CONT Osthryth (died 697), queen of the Mercians, was the wife of King Æthelred and daughter of King Oswiu of Northumbria and his second wife Eanflæd. She probably married Æthelred before 679 and was murdered by the nobles of Mercia.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth was not the first of her family to become a Mercian queen. Her sister Alhflæd had married Peada, King of South Mercia 654-656.[2] After Peada's death, who was allegedly murdered with Alhflæd's connivance and possibly Osth
2 CONC ryth's as well, she retreated to Fladbury in Worcestershire, to judge both from the place-name, which means "stronghold of Flæde", and from its subsequent history:[3] sometime in the 690s Æthelred granted Fladbury to Oftfor, Bisho
2 CONC p of Worcester, to re-establish monastic life there;[4] however, this grant was later contested by Æthelheard, son of Oshere, who maintained that Æthelred had no right to give Fladbury away, as it had been the property of Osthryth
2 CONC . Æthelheard claimed it as her kinsman and heir.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelred and Osthryth loved and favoured Bardney Abbey in Lincolnshire. Osthryth placed there the bones of her uncle Oswald of Northumbria, who was venerated as a saint. It is clear from this story that Osthryth played a part in p
2 CONC romoting the cult of St Oswald.[6] Many years later she persuaded Oswald's widow Cyneburh to take the veil.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth had to contend with major conflicts of loyalty. In 679 her brother Ecgfrith of Northumbria fought a battle against Æthelred, in which Ecgfrith's brother Ælfwine was killed. Bede tells us that he was "a young man of about 
2 CONC eighteen years of age and much beloved in both kingdoms, for King Æthelred had married his sister."[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The murder of Osthryth in 697 by Mercian nobles is unexplained in the sources that mention it.[9] Ann Williams attributes it to the hostility between the Mercians and the Northumbrians,[10] while D. P. Kirby suggests that it may h
2 CONC ave been revenge for her sister's alleged involvement in Peada's murder.[11] H. P. R. Finberg speculates that she and her kinsman Oshere were suspected of trying to detach the kingdom of the Hwicce from Mercian overlordship.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth was buried at Bardney Abbey.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth was probably the mother of Æthelred's son, Ceolred, king of Mercia from
2 CONT ∼
2 CONT St. Osthrith, Queen of Mercia
2 CONT (c.AD 648-697)
2 CONT Princess Osthrith was born to high office. A daughter of King Oswiu of Northumbria and his wife, St. Eanflaeda, she became a Queen herself upon her marriage to King Aethelred of Mercia in AD 679. The two made a pious Royal couple 
2 CONC and were great benefactors of the Church. Churches and monasteries were multiplied and endowed during their reign and Aethelred set his niece, St. Werburga of Chester, over all the nunneries in his dominions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthrith, however, seems to have been unpopular among the Mercians. She had a great devotion to her uncle, St. Oswald of Northumbria, and desired to lay his bones in her husband's noble monastery at Bardney in Lincolnshire. The mo
2 CONC nks objected, because St. Oswald had warred against Mercia and reigned over it as a foreign king. When, one evening, a wagon arrived at Bardney, bearing the good king's body, they would not open their gates. So the cart was left, 
2 CONC all night, outside the monastery. No sooner was it dark, than a wondrous light emanated from the bier and was seen for miles around by all the dwellers in the province. A pillar of glorious light stood over the saint's body, reach
2 CONC ing up to heaven. In the morning, the monks, who had wished to send the relics back to Northumberland, were, of course, now eager to have the Royal saint buried in their church.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5th August AD 697, a rebellious faction in Mercia murdered poor Osthrith. She was buried at Bardney and, seven years later, Aethelred decided to resign his throne to Coenred, the son of his elder brother, Wulfhere, and St. Erme
2 CONC ngilda, and became a monk in the same monastery. He died there, in AD 715, and was buried beside his wife. They left a son, Coelred, married St. Werburga of Mercia, who, in AD 709, succeeded his cousin, Coenred to the throne of Me
2 CONC rcia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 697, murdered. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Osthryth (?) and Æthelred I, King of Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Ceolred, King of Mercia+ d. 716 (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p17379.htm#i173787
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth (died 697) was the daughter of Oswiu of Northumbria and the wife of King Æthelred of Mercia. She was murdered by the nobles of Mercia. Also referred to, by Bede, as Queen Ostritha.
2 CONT
2 CONT By a complex chain of reasoning, we can deduce that Osthryth's first husband was Eanhere, King of the Hwicce and that they had sons named Osric, Oswald and Oshere. That would explain why Osric and Oswald are described as Æthelred'
2 CONC s nepotes - usually translated as nephews or grandsons, but here probably meaning stepsons.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelred certainly seems to have gained overlordship of the Hwicce, treating Osric as a sub-king.[1] If Osthryth's sons were young at the time of their father's death, then Æthelred could have taken them into his household and rul
2 CONC ed on their behalf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth was not the first of her family to become a Mercian queen. Her sister Alhflæd had married Peada, King of South Mercia 654-656.[2] After Peada's death in battle, it seems that Alhflæd retreated to Fladbury in Worcestershir
2 CONC e, to judge from the place-name, which means "stronghold of Flæde", and its subsequent history.[3] Sometime in the 690s Æthelred granted Fladbury to Oftfor, Bishop of Worcester, to re-establish monastic life there.S76 However this
2 CONC  grant was later contested. Æthelheard, son of Oshere, maintained that Æthelred had no right to give Fladbury away, as it had been the property of Osthryth. Æthelheard claimed it as her kinsman and heir.[4] Here we have the crucia
2 CONC l evidence that Osthryth was related to Oshere and his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelred and Osthryth loved and favoured the abbey of Bardney in Lincolnshire. Osthryth placed there the bones of her uncle Oswald of Northumbria, who was venerated as a saint. It is clear from this story that Osthryth played a pa
2 CONC rt in promoting the cult of St Oswald.[5] Many years later she persuaded Oswald's widow Cyneburh to take the veil.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osthryth had to contend with major conflicts of loyalty. In 679 her brother Ecgfrith of Northumbria fought a battle against Æthelred, in which Ecgfrith's brother Ælfwine was killed. Bede tells us that he was "a young man of about 
2 CONC eighteen years of age and much beloved in both kingdoms, for King Æthelred had married his sister."[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The murder of Osthryth in 697 by Mercian nobles is unexplained in the sources that mention it.[8]Given the politics of the time, she may have been seen as some kind of threat to Mercian security. Finberg speculates that she and he
2 CONC r son Oshere were suspected of trying to detach the kingdom of the Hwicce from Mercian overlordship.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ John Leland, Collectanea, vol. 1, p. 240.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Bede, The Ecclesiastical History of the English People (1994), 144.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Hooke, in The Anglo-Saxon Landscape: The Kingdom of the Hwicce (1985), p. 11, argues that Fladbury could have belonged to another sister of Osthryth, Ælflæda, Abbess of Whitby, but she had no known connection with Mercia and die
2 CONC d in 713, after Osthryth, so Osthryth could not have inherited Fladbury from her.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ H.P.R.Finberg, The Early Charters of the West Midlands (Leicester 1961), p.170.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Bede, The Ecclesiastical History of the English People ed. J. McClure and R. Collins (1994), 126.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ H.P.R.Finberg, The Early Charters of the West Midlands (Leicester 1961), p.165.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Bede, The Ecclesiastical History of the English People ed. J. McClure and R. Collins (1994), 207.
2 CONT
