1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry, chart page 61.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 67:
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.  DAGOBERT II, alive 656-79, King of Austrasia, was very young when his father died.  The mayor Brimoald arranged for the boy's exile to Ireland, which lasted 20 years, during which little is recorded about the Austrasian kingd
2 CONC om.  He was brought back in 676 only to be murdered three years later.  Here we part company with the Merovingian Dynasty, which continued to reign in name only until 751.  Our descent continues through the female line.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amy, Adam and FAL.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 198 line (240-10):
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.  Dagobert II, King of Austrasia 676-680.  (Generations 1-10 questional and omitted): Larousse, Hist. de France I 49).
1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Feast day December 23. Also called Saint Dagobert Merovingian Frankishking of Austrasia.
2 CONT The son of Sigebert III, Dagobert was packed off to an Irish monasteryfollowing the death of his father in 656, and the Austrasian throne wastaken by Childebert the Adopted, son of Grimoald, the Austrasian mayor ofthe palace. Afte
2 CONC r the downfall of Grimoald and Childebert, Dagobert'scousin Chlotar III, king of Neustria, secured the Austrasian throne (662)for Childeric II. On Childeric's assassination in 675, Dagobert wastraced, with effective assistance fro
2 CONC m Wilfrid, bishop of York, andrestored to the throne in 676. His murder three years after he took thethrone led to the nominal unity of all the Frankish lands under TheodoricIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT To cite this page: "Dagobert II" Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT  <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article?eu=28984>
1 NOTE Dagobert II was co-ruler of the Frankish Kingdom with Thierry III beginning in 674.  Dagobert died in 678.
