2 CONT Balthild
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Balthild, also known as Bathilde d'Ascagnie, Batilde, Bathylle, Bathild, or Bathilda (626 or 627 – January 30, 680), was the wife and queen of Clovis II, king of Burgundy and Neustria (639 – 658). Her name comes from Old Eng
2 CONC lish and means "bold battle". Two traditions, independent and conflicting, trace her career; one is the hagiography, which was intended to further her successful candidature for sainthood, the other the record of chroniclers, and 
2 CONC a chance archaeological find in an East Anglian field.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Both traditions represent her as an Anglo-Saxon of elite birth, perhaps a relative of King Ricberht of East Anglia, the last pagan king there. Ricberht was ousted by his Christian rival Sigeberht, who had spent time in the Frankis
2 CONC h court; he was established as the rightful heir to the throne, with Frankish help. Balthild ("bold in battle") was sold into slavery as a young girl and served in the household of Erchinoald, mayor of the palace of Neustria to Cl
2 CONC ovis.
2 CONT
2 CONT The hagiographic tradition
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Vita S. Bathildis[3] Balthild was beautiful, intelligent, modest, and attentive to the needs of others. Erchinoald (whose wife had died) was attracted to Balthild and wanted to marry her. But she did not want to marry
2 CONC  him: she hid herself away and waited until Erchinoald remarried. Next, King Clovis noticed her and asked her hand in marriage sometime in 649. Balthild was nineteen when she became queen. Different versions of this story suggest 
2 CONC Clovis was somewhere between the ages of twelve and sixteen at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even as queen, she remained humble and modest. She is famous for her charitable service and generous donations. From her donations, the abbeys of Corbie and Chelles were founded (and likely others such as those of Jumièges, Jouarr
2 CONC e, and Luxeuil). She also provided support to Saint Claudius of Besançon and his abbey in the Jura Mountains.[4][5] She bore her husband three children, all of whom became kings: Clotaire, Childeric, and Theuderic.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Balthild's husband died between 655 and 658, Clotaire, the eldest son and heir to the throne, succeeded at age five. Balthild served as the queen regent until he came of age in 664, when she was forced into a convent. As quee
2 CONC n, she was a capable stateswoman. She abolished the practice of trading Christian slaves and even sought the freedom of children sold into slavery. As the story goes, after Balthild's three children were of age and "established in
2 CONC  their respective territories" (Clotaire in Neustria, Childeric in Austrasia, and perhaps Theuderic in Burgundy), Balthild entered the abbey and gave up her royal rank. She dedicated the rest of her life serving the poor and the i
2 CONC nfirm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Balthild died on January 30, 680. She is buried at her foundation, the Abbey of Chelles outside of Paris. Her Vita was first written soon after her death, probably by one of the community of Chelles. The Vita Baldechildis/Vita Bat
2 CONC hildis reginae Francorum in Monumenta Germania Historica, Scriptores Rerum Merovincarum 2, like most of the vitae of royal Merovingian-era saints, provides some useful details for the historian. Her official cult began at the time
2 CONC  her remains were transferred from the former abbey to a new church, in 833, under the auspices of Louis the Pious. Balthild was canonised by Pope Nicholas I about 200 years after her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The chronicles and the seal
2 CONT
2 CONT In the context of seventh-century culture it is clear that Balthild was simply the outright gift of Erchinoald to Clovis as a bedfellow,[6] though her hagiography emphasizes her chastity as a slave. Other Merovingian queens who ar
2 CONC ose from servile status are Fredegund, mother of Clothaire II, Bilichild, wife of Theudebert of Austrasia, and possibly Nanthild, mother of Clovis II. During the minority of Clotaire III she had to deal with the attempted coup of 
2 CONC Grimoald, the major domo of Austrasia, but enjoyed the continued support of her former master Erchinoald.
2 CONT
2 CONT By some chroniclers' accounts[7] she was a ruthless ruler, in continuing conflict with the bishops; she seems to have been responsible for the assassination of several.[8] The vita of Saint Eligius by his companion Dado reports (c
2 CONC h. 32): "Then his widowed queen with her boys obtained the reign for a few years. She was afterward removed by law and left the principate to her sons..."[9] She was frustrated in her desire to have Eligius entombed at her monster
2 CONC y of Chellers (Eligius, vita, II.37) and by an apparition of Eligius (II.41) was convinced to strip off her gold and jewelled ornaments, "keeping nothing except gold bracelets."
2 CONT
2 CONT Her gold seal matrix, which was originally attached to a ring, was uncovered in 1999 by a metal detectorist in a field a few miles east of Norfolk's county town, Norwich, has two sides. One, official side shows her face and her na
2 CONC me BALDAHILDIS in Frankish lettering. The other, private side, shows naked figures, doubtless Balthild and Clovis, in a frank erotic position beneath a cross One sealing identified official documents, the other, apparently, privat
2 CONC e ones. The seal matrix is conserved in the Norwich Castle Museum. It is surmised that the seal matrix was returned to her kin after her death.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Attwater, Donald and Catherine Rachel John. The Penguin Dictionary of Saints. 3rd edition. New York: Penguin Books, 1993. ISBN 0140513124.
2 CONT
