1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hormisdas II .  Hormizd II . Shah of Persia - Persian shaahi. King of Persia. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King Hormizd II, was the eighth Persia King of the Sassanid dynasty, and reigned for seven years and five months, from 302 to 309. Almost nothing is known of his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death his oldest son Adarnases, who had a cruel disposition, was killed by the grandees after a very short reign; another son, Hormizd, was held prisoner, while the throne was reserved for the child of his concubine, Sha
2 CONC pur II. Another version has it that Shapur II was the son of Hormizd II's first wife, and that while still pregnant she was made to wear a crown over her pudenda so that the baby would be born as a king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relief "The Equestrian Victory of Hormizd II"
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Hormizd escaped from prison in 323 with the help of his wife, and found refuge at the court of Constantine I (324–337) (Zosimus ii. 27; John of Antioch, fr. 178; Zonaras 13–5). In 363, Hormizd served against Persia in the 
2 CONC army of the Roman emperor Julian (361–363); in turn his son, of the same name, later served as proconsul (Ammianus Marcellinus 26. 8. 12).
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hormizd_II for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hormizd II, was the eighth Persian king of the Sassanid Empire, and reigned for seven years and five months, from 302 to 309. He was the son of Narseh (293–302),
2 CONT
2 CONT Almost nothing is known of his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death his oldest son Adarnases, who had a cruel disposition, was killed by the grandees after a very short reign; another son, Hormizd, was held prisoner, while the throne was reserved for the child of his concubine, Sha
2 CONC pur II. Another version has it that Shapur II was the son of Hormizd II's first wife, and that while still pregnant she was made to wear a crown over her pudenda so that the baby would be born as a king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relief "The Equestrian Victory of Hormizd II"
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Hormizd escaped from prison in 323 with the help of his wife, and found refuge at the court of Constantine I (324–337) (Zosimus ii. 27; John of Antioch, fr. 178; Zonaras 13–5). In 363, Hormizd served against Persia in the 
2 CONC army of the Roman emperor Julian (361–363); in turn his son, of the same name, later served as proconsul (Ammianus Marcellinus 26. 8. 12).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hormizd II (Persian: هرمز دوم‎‎) was the eighth king of the Sasanian Empire, and reigned for seven years and five months, from 302 to 309. He was the son of Narseh (293–302).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 302, Hormizd II ascended the throne, assuming a crown resembling the crown used by the early Sasanian rulers. Hormizd II, unlike his father, persecuted the Manicheans who had lived peacefully during the reign of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hormizd tried to improve Sasanian relations with Armenia, which had recently under Tiridates III of Armenia declared Christianity as its state religion; he gave his daughter Hormizddukht in marriage to a Mamikonian prince named V
2 CONC ahan.  In 309, while Hormizd was hunting, he was wounded by a Ghassanid army, and was shortly killed by the Sasanian nobles.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, his oldest son Adhur Narseh, who had a cruel disposition, was killed by the grandees after a very short reign; another son, Hormizd, was imprisoned, while the throne was reserved for the child of his concubine, Sh
2 CONC apur II. Another version has it that Shapur II was the son of Hormizd II's first wife, and that while still pregnant she was made to wear a crown over her pudenda so that the baby would be born as a king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hormizd II also had many other sons named Adurfrazgird, Zamasp, Ardashir, and two daughters named Hormizddukht and Asay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hormizd II was one of the Sasanian kings with most children, which he had from his Jewish wife Ifra-Hormizd, and several other wives and concubines:
2 CONT
2 CONT - Prince Adur Narseh (3rd century – 309), the ninth king of the Sasanian Empire.[9] - Prince Shapur II (309–379), the tenth king of the Sasanian Empire.[13] - Prince Adurfrazgird (??? – 4th century), governor of southern Arbayista
2 CONC n.[14] - Prince Zamasp (??? – 4th century), governor of northern Arbayistan.[14] - Prince Shapur Sakanshah (??? – 4th century), governor of Sakastan.[15] - Prince Hormizd (??? – 4th century), imprisoned by the Iranian nobility and
2 CONC  later defected to the Roman Empire.[11] - Prince Ardashir II (379 – 383), the eleventh king of the Sasanian Empire.[4] - Unnamed daughter (??? – 4th century), married the Arsacid king Urnayr.[16] - Princess Hormizddukht (??? – 4t
2 CONC h century), married the Mamikonian prince Vahan.[4]
