1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Alaric I, first king of the Visigoths 
2 CONT * Reign: 395–410
2 CONT *Preceded by: [https://www.geni.com/people/Athanaric-king-of-the-Visigoths/6000000029782370460 Athanaric]
2 CONT *Succeeded by: [https://www.geni.com/people/Ataulf-king-of-the-Visigoths/6000000006206244465 Ataulf]
2 CONT * Died: 411 (aged around 40)
2 CONT * House: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balt_dynasty Balt dynasty]
2 CONT *Spouse: [https://www.geni.com/people/sister-of-Ataulf/6000000003637306624?through=6000000004036546149 sister of Ataulf] 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of the Goths, Wolfram, Herwig-
2 CONT
2 CONT ".....The chieftain who led the Thervingi across the  Danube in 376 was called Alaviv.  Little is known about him other than his name, which represents the type of variation on Alaric's  name which could make Alaviv Alaric's fathe
2 CONC r.  In either case Alaric's name could be explained by Balthic tradition marked by alliteration, variation, and rhythm..."
2 CONT
2 CONT ...."As for Alaric's descendants, the sons of the Visigothic king Theoderid were his grandchildren.  Theoderid succeeded king Valia in 418 and whose family ruled until 531 is now considered the progenitor of the 'younger Balthi.' 
2 CONC  Alaric I had a wife and children.  Perhaps a daughter of the conqueror of Rome became the wife of Theoderid; had the latter been Alaric's son, contemporaries would have no doubt recorded it,  In any case Theodorid's grandson was 
2 CONC given the name Alaric (II).  The last of the younger Balthi to assume the reins of power was the great grandson of Theoderid and the grandson of Theodoric the Great.  He was called Amalaric. He was murdered in 531."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaric_I 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alaric I''' (/ˈælərɪk/; Gothic: 𐌰𐌻𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃, Alarīks, "ruler of all"; c. 370 – 411 AD) was the first king of the Visigoths, from 395 to 410. He rose to leadership of the Goths who came to occupy Moesia—territory acquired a couple 
2 CONC of decades earlier by a combined force of Goths and Alans after the Battle of Adrianople.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE.htm#Alaricdied410
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         '''ALARIC''' (-Bruttium, southern Italy end 410).  First mentioned in 391 when he moved southwards into Greece, he suffered several military setbacks but succeeded in resettling the Goths in Macedonia[7].  He was electe
2 CONC d in 395 as '''ALARIC King of the Visigoths'''.  He entered the western part of the Roman Empire in 401, and by the end of the year had attacked Venetia and was threatening Milan.  Alaric was defeated by the Romans in 402 at Polle
2 CONC nza, and in 403 at Verona, after which the Goths abandoned Italy and settled in Dalmatia and Pannonia[8].  King Alaric returned to Italy in 408, the Goths' attacks culminating in the sack of Rome in Aug 410[9].  Procopius records 
2 CONC that Alaric died from illness[10].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ---, daughter of ---.  The name of King Alaric's wife is not known.  Her family origin and marriage are indicated by Olympiodorus Thebæus who records that Alaric was succeeded by “Adaulphum uxoris fratrem””[11]. ( Uxoris f
2 CONC ratrem - "husband of his daughter" i.e., son-in-law.)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Alaric had [one child]:
2 CONT *a)         [daughter.  Her parentage and marriage are indicated by Wolfram who says that Walia was succeeded by “Theoderid, son or rather son-in-law of Alaric I”[12].  As, in another passage, Wolfram says that nothing is known ab
2 CONC out the earlier life of Theoderic[13], the basis for the latter being Alaric’s son is unknown.  Wolfram quotes no primary source which corroborated his assertion that Theoderic was Alaric’s son-in-law.  He cites Olympiodorus Thebæ
2 CONC us in a footnote, but the passage in question makes no mention of any relationship between Theoderic and Alaric[14].  <br> ''m'' THEODERIC, son of --- (-killed in battle near Troyes 451).  He was elected to succeed in 418 on the d
2 CONC eath of King Walia as THEODERIC I King of the Visigoths.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [9] Wolfram (1998), pp. 156-59. 
2 CONT [10] Dindorf (1833) Procopius, Vol. 1, De Bello Persico III.3, p. 318. 
2 CONT [11] Muller (1851) Fragmenta historicorum Græcorum, Vol. 4, Olympiodorus Thebæus, 10, p. 59.
2 CONT [12] Wolfram (1998), p. 174. 
2 CONT [13] Wolfram (1998), pp. 174-5. 
2 CONT [14] Olympiodorus Thebæus (1851), 35, p. 65.  
