1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gunderic (379-428), King of the Vandals and Alans (407-428) led the Vandals, a Germanic tribe originally residing near the Oder River, to take part in the barbarian invasions of the western Roman Empire in the fift
2 CONC h century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of King Godigisel, the Vandal king when his people breached the Rhine River frontier of the empire on the last day of 406. During that year, the Vandals had become heavily involved in a war with the Franks, who were a
2 CONC lready settled in Gaul as allies of the Romans, and who attempted to keep the Vandals out. Godigisel was killed in the fighting and Gunderic succeeded him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunderic and his people ultimately crossed the Pyrenees into Spain. With the Hasdingi portion of the Vandals he established the kingdom in the roman province of Gallaecia (north-western Spain). Conflicts with the Suebi drive him i
2 CONC nto Baetica in the south, where he joins the Silingi portion of the Vandals. Some scholars believe that the modern term of the region, Andalusia, is derived from the Vandals, because they invaded North Africa from the Iberian Peni
2 CONC nsula.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 426, Attaces, the king of the Alans, fell in battle against the Visigoths, and most of the surviving Alans appealed to Gunderic. Gunderic accepted their request and thus became King of the Vandals and Alans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Late in his reign, the Vandals themselves began to clash more and more with the Visigoths, often getting the worse of these battles because the Visigoths were so much more numerous. After Gunderic died early in 428, the Vandals el
2 CONC ected his half-brother Geiseric as his successor, and Geiseric left Spain to the Visigoths in favor of invading Roman Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunderic
