1 NAME Eanhere
2 GIVN Eanhere
1 NAME Eanhere, King of the Hwicce
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} HistoryThe territory of the Hwicce may roughly have corresponded to the Roman civitas of the Dobunni.[8] The area appears to have remained largely British in the first century or so after Britain left the Roman Emp
2 CONC ire, but pagan burials and place names in its north-eastern sector suggest an inflow of Angles along the Warwickshire Avon and perhaps by other routes;[9] they may have exacted tribute from British rulers.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle there was a battle at Dyrham in 577 in which the Gewisse (West Saxons) under Ceawlin killed three British kings and captured Gloucester, Cirencester and Bath. West Saxon occupation of the are
2 CONC a did not last long, however, and may have ended as early as 584, the date of the battle of Fethanleag, according to the A.S.C., in which Cutha was killed and Ceawlin returned home in anger, and certainly by 603 when, according to
2 CONC  Bede, Saint Augustine attended a conference of Welsh bishops "at St. Augustine's Oak on the borders of the Hwicce and the West Saxons".
2 CONT
2 CONT The Angles strengthened their influence over the area in 628, when (says the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle), the West Saxons fought the (Anglian) Penda of Mercia at Cirencester and afterwards came to terms. Penda had evidently won but had
2 CONC  probably forged an alliance with local leaders since the former Dobunnic polity did not immediately become part of Mercia but instead became an allied or client kingdom of the Hwicce.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hwicce sub-kingdom included a number of distinct tribal groups including the Husmerae, the Stoppingas and the Weorgoran.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT The first probable kings of whom we read were two brothers, Eanhere and Eanfrith. Bede notes that Queen Eafe "had been baptised in her own country, the kingdom of the Hwicce. She was the daughter of Eanfrith, Eanhere's brother, bo
2 CONC th of whom were Christians, as were their people."[12] From this we deduce that Eanfrith and Eanhere were of the royal family and that theirs was a Christian kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that the Hwicce were converted to Christianity by Celtic Christians rather than by the mission from Pope Gregory I since Bede was well-informed on the latter yet does not mention the conversion of the Hwicce.[13] Thou
2 CONC gh place-names show that Anglo-Saxon settlement was widespread in the territory, the limited spread of pagan burials, along with two eccles place-names that invariably identify Roman-British churches, suggests that Christianity su
2 CONC rvived the influx. There are also probable Christian burials beneath Worcester Cathedral and St Mary de Lode Church, Gloucester.[14] So it seems that incoming Anglo-Saxons were absorbed into the existing church. The ruling dynasty
2 CONC  of the Hwicce were probably key figures in the process. Perhaps they sprang from intermarriage between Anglian and British leading families.
2 CONT
2 CONT By a complex chain of reasoning one can deduce that Eanhere married Osthryth, daughter of Oswiu of Northumbria, and had sons by her named Osric, Oswald and Oshere. Osthryth is recorded as the wife of Æthelred of Mercia. An earlier
2 CONC  marriage to Eanhere would explain why Osric and Oswald are described as Æthelred's nepotes — usually meaning "nephews" but here probably "stepsons".[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osric was anxious for the Hwicce to gain their own bishop,[16] but it was Oshere whose influence was seen behind the creation of the see of Worcester in 679–80. Presumably Osric was dead by that time. Tatfrid of Whitby was chosen 
2 CONC as the first bishop of the Hwicce but he died before ordination and was replaced by Bosel.[17] A 12th-century chronicler of Worcester comments that that town was selected as the seat of the bishop because it was the capital of the
2 CONC  Hwicce.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Oshere was succeeded by his sons Æthelheard, Æthelweard and Æthelric. At the beginning of Offa's reign we find the kingdom ruled by three brothers, named Eanberht, Uhtred and Aldred, the two last of whom lived until about 780. Aft
2 CONC er them the title of king seems to have been given up. Their successor Æthelmund, who was killed in a campaign against Wessex in 802, is described only as an earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT The district remained in possession of the rulers of Mercia until the fall of that kingdom. Together with the rest of English Mercia it submitted to King Alfred about 877–883 under Earl Æthelred, who possibly himself belonged to t
2 CONC he Hwicce.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Kings of the HwicceNo contemporary genealogy or list of kings has been preserved, so the following list has been compiled by historians from a variety of primary sources.[19] Some kings of the Hwicce seem to have reigned in
2 CONC  tandem for all or part of their reign. This gives rise to an overlap in the dates of reigns given below. Please consult individual biographies for a discussion of the dating of these rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Name Dates Notes 
2 CONT  628 Kingdom conquered by Penda of Mercia.  Eanhere mid-7th century   Eanfrith mid-7th century Brother of Eanhere.  Osric active 670s Entombed in Gloucester Cathedral.  Oshere active 690s Brother of Osric. Died before 716.  Æthelh
2 CONC eard active 709 Son of Oshere. Issued charter with Æthelweard.  Æthelweard active 709 Son of Oshere.  Æthelric active 736 Son of Oshere.  Eanberht active 750s Not recorded after 759.  Uhtred active 750s – 779   Ealdred active 750s
2 CONC  – 778  
2 CONT  780s Assimilation of the Hwicce into Mercia is completed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ealdormen of the HwicceName Dates Notes  Æthelmund c. 796-802 Died in battle 802.[20]  ?Æthelric fl. 804 Son of Æthelmund. His will of 804 requests burial at Deerhurst.[1]  Leofwine d.c.1023 Father of Leofric, Earl of Merci
2 CONC a  Odda d.1056 Built Odda's Chapel at Deerhurst for the soul of his brother Ælfric.[21] Buried at Pershore.[22] The area of his jurisdiction probably did not include the Hwicce.[23] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Other notables of the HwicceÆthelmod granted land to Abbess Beorngyth in October 680 and was probably a member of the royal family.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Osred (c. 693) was a thegn of the Hwicce, who has been described by some historians as a king.[24]
