1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT http://royaumeurope.wordpress.com/germains/germains-occidentaux/les-rois-francs-de-therouanne/
2 CONT
2 CONT Richomer de Toxandrie – Richomer de Thérouanne (350 – † entre 384 et 393 à Constantinople)
2 CONT  – Magister Militum en 383, Consul de Rome en 384 Flavius Richomeres
2 CONT  – épouse Ascyla de Francie – Ascilla de Francie (354 – † le 15 août 414 assassinée à Rome avec son fils) fille de (…) ■Richomer de Toxandrie et son épouse ont eu 2 enfants
2 CONT  d.Théodemir de Toxandrie (374 à Constantinople – † le 15 août 414 assassinée à Rome avec sa mère) – voir ci-dessous
2 CONT  e.Hildegonde de Toxandrie – Aldégonde de Lombardie (vers 375 – ?)
2 CONT  – épouse vers 370-373 Marcomir de Francie (vers 370 – † en 424) – Chef Francs
2 CONT ________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ---- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toxandri
2 CONT
2 CONT The Toxandri (or Texuandri, Taxandri, Toxandrians etc.) were a tribe living in the Roman empire times. Their territory was called Toxandria, Toxiandria or Taxandria, a name which survived into the Middle Ages. It was roughly equiv
2 CONC alent to the modern Campine (Dutch Kempen) geographical region of northeastern Flanders and southern Netherlands. In modern terms this covered all or most of North Brabant, the east of Antwerp Province, and the north of Belgian Li
2 CONC mburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germania_Inferior
2 CONT
2 CONT Provincia Germania Inferior Province of the Roman Empire 83–475 AD
2 CONT
2 CONT Germania Inferior was a Roman province located on the west bank of the Rhine. According to Ptolemy (2.9), Germania Inferior included the Rhine from its mouth up to the mouth of the Obringa, a river identified with either the Aar o
2 CONC r the Moselle. The territory included modern Luxembourg, southern Netherlands, part of Belgium, and part of North Rhine-Westphalia in Germany, west of the Rhine.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salian_Franks
2 CONT
2 CONT The Salian Franks, also called the Salians, were the western subgroup of the early Franks who first appear in the historical records in the third century. At that time they lived between the Rhine and the IJssel in the modern day 
2 CONC Dutch region of the Veluwe, Gelderland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As the Salians initially lived north of the Rhine delta they were also north of the limes of Roman Gaul, which ran along the Rhine. They were characterised as both warlike Germanic people and pirates, and as Laeti (allies of the R
2 CONC omans). Shortly thereafter, some were settled permanently on Roman land. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After moving into Batavia, a border island in the Rhine, in 358, they came to some form of agreement with the Romans who allowed them to settle south of the Rhine in Toxandria (roughly the area of the current Dutch province of Noo
2 CONC rd-Brabant, and adjacent parts of the two bordering Belgian provinces of Antwerpen and Belgian Limburg.
