2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodoric_I ''[52 languages]''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Theodoric I ''' (Gothic: ''Þiudarīks''; Latin: ''Theodericus''; c. 390 or 393 20 or 24 June 451) was the King of the Visigoths from 418 to 451. Theodoric is famous for his part in stopping [https://www.geni.com/people/Attila-fl
2 CONC agello-di-Dio-re-degli-Unni/6000000000722248117 Attila the Hun] at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451, where he was killed. .. ... ....
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE.htm#TheodericIB 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''THEODERIC''' [Theoderid], son of --- (-killed in battle near Troyes summer 451).  According to Grote[54], King Theoderic I was the son of King Walia but Wolfram says that nothing is known about the earlier life of Theoderic[55]
2 CONC .  He was elected to succeed in 418 as THEODERIC I King of the Visigoths.  Olympiodorus Thebæus records the death of “Valia, principe Gothorum”, and the succession of “Theuderichus”[56].  Iordanes names "Theoderidum" as successor 
2 CONC of "Vallia rex Gothorum" but does not specify any relationship between the two[57].  He completed the transfer of Visigothic activity from Spain to France based on Toulouse[58]. 
2 CONT In 422, the Visigoths marched against the Vandals in Spain but deserted their Roman allies who suffered a serious defeat[59].  The Visigoths became more aggressive in their raids against Roman towns in Gaul, besieging Arles severa
2 CONC l times between 425 and 430, and Narbonne in 437.  The Romans counter-attacked Toulouse in 439, although their leader Litorius was killed[60].  The Visigoths fought for the Romans against the Suevi in Spain in 446, but made an all
2 CONC iance with the latter in 449, confirmed by the marriage of Theoderic’s daughter to the Suevi king[61].  
2 CONT King Theoderic marched with his two eldest sons into Champagne in summer 451 against Attila the Hun.  He was killed in the battle of the Catalaunian fields in which the combined Roman/Visigothic forces defeated the Huns[62].  Iord
2 CONC anes records that he was killed "in campis statim Catalaunicisis"[63].  Gregory of Tours records that Theoderic King of the Goths was killed in battle against Attila in support of his allies the Franks[64].  The Chronica Regum Vis
2 CONC igotthorum records that “Theuderedus” reigned for 33 years[65]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' [--- of the Visigoths, daughter of ALARIC I King of the Visigoths & his wife ---.  The primary source which records Theoderic’s marriage has not been identified.  His wife’s parentage and marriage are indicated by Wolfram 
2 CONC who says that Walia was succeeded by “Theoderid, son or rather son-in-law of Alaric I”[66].  As, in another passage, Wolfram says that nothing is known about the earlier life of Theoderic[67], the basis for the latter being Alaric
2 CONC ’s son is unknown.  Wolfram quotes no primary source which corroborated his assertion that Theoderic was Alaric’s son-in-law.  He cites Olympiodorus Thebæus in a footnote, but the passage in question makes no mention of any relati
2 CONC onship between Theoderic and Alaric[68].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  King Theoderic I had more than nine children, although it is not known whether these were by his wife or by concubines:
2 CONT #       daughter. m. Hunneric of the Vandals 
2 CONT #         THORISMUND (-murdered 453).  
2 CONT #        THEODERIC (-murdered 466). 
2 CONT #         FRIDERIC [Federico].  
2 CONT #         EURIC ([after 435]-Arles [Dec] 484). 
2 CONT #        RETEMERIS [Ricimer]. 
2 CONT #        HIMNERITH. 
2 CONT #         daughter.
2 CONT #       other daughters[97].   
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT  Ben M. Angel summary:
2 CONT FMG supports the proposition that Alaric was his father-in-law, rather than father. Seeing nothing else that supports his direct descent, I'd have to go with their research.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result, these are his relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Unknown daughter of Alaric I, King of the Visigoths (395-410)
2 CONT
2 CONT All children presumed to be from spouse (though likely some were from concubines).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Unknown daughter (d. after 442), married to (in 429) and repudiated by (in 442) Huneric I, King of the Vandals
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Thorismund, King of the Visigoths (451-453)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Theoderic II, King of the Visigoths (453-466)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Frideric (Friderico), Visigoth military leader
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Euric I, King of the Visigoths (b. after 435, 466-484)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Retemiris (Ricimer), Visigoth military leader
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Himnerith, Visigoth military leader
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Unknown daughter, m. 449 in Toulouse Richgar/Requiario, King of the Suevi (d. 456)
2 CONT
2 CONT Other children may exist, but they are unidentified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information and justification:
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: Unknown. We don't even know who his parents are (barring additional proof, Alaric is his father in law).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 20 June 451, Chalons (Battle of the Catalaunian Plains) was either trampled to death after falling from his horse during a charge, or pierced with a spear (likely the former), from the English Wikipedia article.
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried: Pouan (though there is some skepticism about the assertion that the "Treasures of Pouan" were associated with his remains).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedding: Unknown date and location.
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: King of the Visigoths (418-451)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodoric (418-451) is listed as the 5th Balti King of the Balti Dynasty: These kings were Arians, they tended to succeed their fathers or close relatives on the throne and thus constitute a dynasty, the Balti.
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Toulouse (birth family not covered):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE.htm#_Toc225040394
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODERIC I 418-451, THORISMUND 451-453, THEODERIC II 453-466
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODERIC [Theoderid], son of --- (-killed in battle near Troyes summer 451).  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Grote[53], King Theoderic I was the son of King Walia but Wolfram says that nothing is known about the earlier life of Theoderic[54].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed in 418 as THEODERIC I King of the Visigoths.  Iordanes names "Theoderidum" as successor of "Vallia rex Gothorum" but does not specify any relationship between the two[55].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He completed the transfer of Visigothic activity from Spain to France based on Toulouse[56].  In 422, the Visigoths marched against the Vandals in Spain but deserted their Roman allies who suffered a serious defeat[57].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Visigoths became more aggressive in their raids against Roman towns in Gaul, besieging Arles several times between 425 and 430, and Narbonne in 437.  The Romans counter-attacked Toulouse in 439, although their leader Litorius 
2 CONC was killed[58].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Visigoths fought for the Romans against the Suevi in Spain in 446, but made an alliance with the latter in 449, confirmed by the marriage of Theoderic’s daughter to the Suevi king[59].  King Theoderic marched with his two elde
2 CONC st sons into Champagne in summer 451 against Attila the Hun.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in the battle of the Catalaunian fields in which the combined Roman/Visigothic forces defeated the Huns[60].  Iordanes records that he was killed "in campis statim Catalaunicisis"[61].  Gregory of Tours records that 
2 CONC Theoderic King of the Goths was killed in battle against Attila in support of his allies the Franks[62].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Theuderedus” reigned for 33 years[63]. [64]
2 CONT
2 CONT m --- of the Visigoths, daughter of ALARIC I King of the Visigoths & his wife ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theoderic I had more than nine children, although it is not known whether these were by his wife or by concubines:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  daughter (-after 442).  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was sent back to her father at the time of the 442 revolt with her nose and ears mutilated[65].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([429] or after[66], repudiated 442) as his first wife, HUNERIC, son of GENSERIC King of the Vandals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  THORISMUND (-murdered 453).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[67].  Herimannus names "Torismod filius Theodorus rex Gothorum" when recording that he succeeded his father[68].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed his father in 451 as THORISMUND King of the Visigoths.  Iordanes records that he also fought "in campis statim Catalaunicisis" and succeeded after the death of his father in the same battle[69].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He made war on the Alans at Orléans and marched again on Arles[70].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Thorismo Rex Gothorum” was killed by “Theuderico et Frederico fratribus” in 453[71].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Thurismodus” reigned for three years[72]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  THEODERIC (-murdered 466).  (Son of King Theodoric 418-451)
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[73].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Herimannus names "Theodericus frater Torismodo regi Gothorum" when recording that he succeeded his brother[74].  He was elected to succeed after murdering his brother in 453 as THEODERIC II King of the Visigoths.  The Chronicon of
2 CONC  Bishop Idatius records that “Thorismo Rex Gothorum” was killed by “Theuderico et Frederico fratribus” in 453 and that Theoderic succeeded to the throne[75].  Iordanes records that "Thederidus germanus eius" succeeded after the de
2 CONC ath of Thorismund but does not specify that he was responsible for his brother's death[76].  He invaded Spain in 454 in support of Emperor Avitus and inflicted a resounding defeat on the Suevi in 456[77].  The Chronicon Albeldense
2 CONC  records that “Teudericus” entered Spain, defeated “Ricciarium Suevorum regem” in battle “Asturica apud Urbicum fluvium”, and pursued him into “Portucale” where he killed Requiario and captured “Bracarum”[78].  He returned to Toul
2 CONC ouse in Mar 457 but left a Visigoth contingent which advanced through Betica, eventually taking partial control of Seville[79].  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Teudericus” returned to Gaul after leaving Portugal and was ki
2 CONC lled “ab Eurico…fratre”[80].  Iordanes records that his brother Euric was suspected of involvement in the death of Theoderic[81].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Theudoricus” reigned for 7, otherwise 13, years[82].
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  FRIDERIC [Federico].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[83].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Thorismo Rex Gothorum” was killed by “Theuderico et Frederico fratribus” in 453[84].  He shared power jointly with his brother King Theoderic II[85].  He led a military incursion into 
2 CONC Spain in 454[86]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  EURIC ([after 435]-Arles [Dec] 484).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[87].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed in 466 as EURIC King of the Visigoths after murdering his brother King Theoderic II.     
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  RETEMERIS [Ricimer].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[88]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.   HIMNERITH. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[89]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.  daughter.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum records that "Recciarius Reccilani filius" married "Theuderedi regis Gothorum filia"[90].  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Rechiarius” married “Theodoris Regis filia” 
2 CONC in 449[91].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage was arranged to confirm her father's alliance with the Suevi in Spain.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Toulouse 449[92]) REQUIARIO King of the Suevi in Spain, son of REQUILA King of the Suevi (-456). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.  other daughters[93].   
2 CONT
