1 OCCU Rey de los Visigodos, Roi des Wisigoths (467), Empereur d'Hispanie (475), Roi, des Wisigoths, King of WEST GOTHS, Rei dos Visigodos, roi des Wisigoths, empereur d'Espagne, Roi des Wisigoths (466-484), emperor of Spain, aka Euric [
2 CONC Baux]
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.tacitus.nu/historical-atlas/regents/iberian/visigoths.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Father: Theoderic I, King of the Visigoths
2 CONT *Mother: Daughter of Alaric I, King of the Visigoths.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known siblings:
2 CONT *1. Repudiated wife of Huneric of the Vandals (m. in 429, repudiated with nose and ears mutilated in 442)
2 CONT *2. Thorismund (murdered 453 by Theoderic II and Frederic), King of the Visigoths (451-453)
2 CONT *3. Theoderic II (murdered 466 by Euric), King of the Visigoths (453-466)
2 CONT *4. Frederic or Frederico (fl. 451-454), led Visigoths in Spain in 454.
2 CONT *5. Euric (b. after 435, d. December 484), King of the Visigoths (466-484)
2 CONT *6. Retimiris or Ricimer
2 CONT *7. Himneris
2 CONT *8. Unspecified number of other sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT *Ragnahild, daughter of an unknown king
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *1. Alaric II, 
2 CONT *2. Unknown sister, married Ricimer, Magister Militum, Patricius, and power behind the throne of the remnants of the Western Roman Empire (d. 18 August 472, four years before Odoacer toppled the Empire).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: after 435, according to FMG. Probably at Toulouse, considering that the Romans attacked the city as the Visigoth capital four years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism: Unknown. (He was an Arian Christian.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: Before 458 (date of Alaric II's birth) to Ragnahild, the daughter of an unspecified king, according to FMG.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: December 484 in Arles (Arelate, part of the Visigoth kingdom for over a decade). He was one of the only Visigoth kings to die of natural causes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: King of the Visigoths (475-484), in Spanish: Rey de los Visigodos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate names: Evaric, Erwig, Eurico in Spanish and Portuguese.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Toulouse Kings (covering birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TOULOUSE.htm#EuricA
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODERIC [Theoderid], son of --- (-killed in battle near Troyes summer 451).  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Grote[53], King Theoderic I was the son of King Walia but Wolfram says that nothing is known about the earlier life of Theoderic[54].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed in 418 as THEODERIC I King of the Visigoths.  Jordanes names "Theoderidum" as successor of "Vallia rex Gothorum" but does not specify any relationship between the two[55].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He completed the transfer of Visigothic activity from Spain to France based in Toulouse[56].  In 422, the Visigoths marched against the Vandals in Spain but deserted their Roman allies who suffered a serious defeat[57].  The Visig
2 CONC oths became more aggressive in their raids against Roman towns in Gaul, besieging Arles several times between 425 and 430, and Narbonne in 437.  The Romans counter-attacked Toulouse in 439, although their leader Litorius was kille
2 CONC d[58].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Visigoths fought for the Romans against the Suevi in Spain in 446, but made an alliance with the latter in 449, confirmed by the marriage of Theoderic’s daughter to the Suevi king[59].  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theoderic marched with his two eldest sons into Champagne in summer 451 against Attila the Hun.  He was killed in the battle of the Catalaunian plains in which the combined Roman/Visigothic forces defeated the Huns[60].  Jord
2 CONC anes records that he was killed "in campis statim Catalaunicisis"[61].  Gregory of Tours records that Theoderic King of the Goths was killed in battle against Attila in support of his allies the Franks[62].  The Chronica Regum Vis
2 CONC igotthorum records that “Theuderedus” reigned for 33 years[63]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [64]m --- of the Visigoths, daughter of ALARIC I King of the Visigoths & his wife ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Theoderic I had more than nine children, although it is not known whether these were by his wife or by concubines:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. daughter (-after 442).  
2 CONT *She was sent back to her father at the time of the 442 revolt with her nose and ears mutilated[65].  
2 CONT *m ([429] or after[66], repudiated 442) as his first wife, HUNERIC, son of GENSERIC King of the Vandals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. THORISMUND (-murdered 453).  
2 CONT Jordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[67].  Herimannus names "Torismod filius Theodorus rex Gothorum" when recording that he succeeded his father[68].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed his father in 451 as THORISMUND King of the Visigoths.  Jordanes records that he also fought "in campis statim Catalaunicisis" and succeeded after the death of his father in the same battle[69].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He made war on the Alans at Orléans and marched again on Arles[70].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Thorismo Rex Gothorum” was killed by “Theuderico et Frederico fratribus” in 453[71].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Thurismodus” reigned for three years[72].  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. THEODERIC (-murdered 466).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Jordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[73].  Herimannus names "Theodericus frater Torismodo regi Gothorum" when recording that he succeeded his brother[74].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed after murdering his brother in 453 as THEODERIC II King of the Visigoths. The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Thorismo Rex Gothorum” was killed by “Theuderico et Frederico fratribus” in 453 and 
2 CONC that Theoderic succeeded to the throne[75].  Iordanes records that "Thederidus germanus eius" succeeded after the death of Thorismund but does not specify that he was responsible for his brother's death[76].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He invaded Spain in 454 in support of Emperor Avitus and inflicted a resounding defeat on the Suevi in 456[77].  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Teudericus” entered Spain, defeated “Ricciarium Suevorum regem” in battle “Ast
2 CONC urica apud Urbicum fluvium”, and pursued him into “Portucale” where he killed Requiario and captured “Bracarum”[78].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to Toulouse in Mar 457 but left a Visigoth contingent which advanced through Baetica, eventually taking partial control of Seville[79].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Teudericus” returned to Gaul after leaving Portugal and was killed “ab Eurico…fratre”[80].  Iordanes records that his brother Euric was suspected of involvement in the death of Theoderic[81].
2 CONC   The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Theudoricus” reigned for seven, otherwise thirteen, years[82]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. FRIDERIC [Federico].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Jordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[83].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Thorismo Rex Gothorum” was killed by “Theuderico et Frederico fratribus” in 453[84].  He shared power jointly with his brother King Theoderic II[85].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He led a military incursion into Spain in 454[86]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. EURIC ([after 435]-Arles [Dec] 484).  
2 CONT Jordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[87].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected to succeed in 466 as EURIC King of the Visigoths after murdering his brother King Theoderic II.     
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. RETEMERIS [Ricimer].  
2 CONT Jordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[88]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  HIMNERITH.  
2 CONT Jordanes names (in order) "Friderichum et Eurichum, Retemerim et Himnerith" as four of the sons of "Theoderido", specifying that their father took his two older sons "Thorismud et Theodericum maiores natu" when he fought at the ba
2 CONC ttle of the Catalaunian Fields, dated to 451[89]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. daughter.  
2 CONT Isidor's Historia Gothorum, Wandalorum, Sueborum records that "Recciarius Reccilani filius" married "Theuderedi regis Gothorum filia"[90].  The Chronicon of Bishop Idatius records that “Rechiarius” married “Theodoris Regis filia” 
2 CONC in 449[91].  Her marriage was arranged to confirm her father's alliance with the Suevi in Spain.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Toulouse 449[92]) REQUIARIO King of the Suevi in Spain, son of REQUILA King of the Suevi (-456). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.  other daughters[93]. 
2 CONT
