2 CONT
2 CONT Desde Wikipedia, Eurico:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurico
2 CONT
2 CONT Eurico (c. 440 – 484) fue rey de los visigodos (después de asesinar a su hermano Teodorico II) desde 466 al 484.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biografía
2 CONT
2 CONT Era hijo de Teodorico I y le sucedería a su muerte, su hijo Alarico II. Se casó con Ragnagilda, una supuesta hija de Meroveo, rey de los francos.
2 CONT
2 CONT El reinado de Eurico coincidió con la disolución del Imperio Romano de Occidente, al ser destronado el último emperador Rómulo Augústulo en 476. Los visigodos habían firmado un foedus con Roma en el 418, en virtud del cual se habí
2 CONC an convertido en federados del Imperio y se les había permitido asentarse en el sur de las Galias. Este rey no reconoció a Odoacro, rey de los hérulos e independizó definitivamente el reino visigodo y abolió la dependencia anterio
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se apoderó de diversos territorios de las Galias e Hispania. Guerreó en las Galias contra los francos y sajones. Fue su reino, en su tiempo, el más poderoso e influyente de Europa. Su corte residía en Tolosa (Toulose), aunque nomi
2 CONC nalmente en Arlés. Pero fue sobre todo Tolosa la ciudad que tuvo la categoría de lo que ahora consideramos como capital del reino. La historiografía española lo menciona como Reino de Tolosa y abarcaba una gran extensión de la pen
2 CONC ínsula Ibérica (salvo la región de Galicia, controlada por los suevos), y gran parte de la Galia al oeste del Rin y al sur del Loira.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue un rey que demostró un gran talento político. Publicó un cuerpo legal de derecho visigodo, el Codex Euricianus o Código de Eurico. También fue un fanático arriano.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Euric:
2 CONT
2 CONT Euric, also known as Evaric, Erwig, or Eurico in Spanish and Portuguese (c. 415–484), was the younger brother of Theodoric II and ruled as king of the Visigoths, with his capital at Toulouse, from 466 until his death in 484.
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited a large portion of the Visigothic possessions in the Aquitaine region of Gaul, an area that had been under Visigothic control since 415. Over the decades the Visigoths had gradually expanded their holdings at the expe
2 CONC nse of the weak Roman government, advancing well into Hispania in the process.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon becoming king, Euric defeated several other Visigothic kings and chieftains in a series of civil wars and soon became the first ruler of a truly unified Visigothic nation. Taking advantage of the Romans' problems, he extended
2 CONC  Visigothic power in Hispania, driving the Suevi into the northwest of Iberia. By the time the Western Roman Empire ended in 476 he controlled nearly the entire Iberian peninsula.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 470 Euric defeated an attempted invasion of Gaul by the Celtic magnate Riothamus and expanded his kingdom even further north, possibly as far as the Somme River, the March of Frankish territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Previous Visigothic kings had officially ruled only as legates of the Roman Emperor but Euric was the first to declare his complete independence from the puppet emperors. In 475 he forced the western emperor Julius Nepos to recogn
2 CONC ize his full independence in exchange for the return of the Provence region of Gaul. The Roman citizens of Hispania then pledged their allegiance to Euric, recognizing him as their king. In the same year Clermont(-Ferrand) surrend
2 CONC ered to him after a long siege, and its bishop, Sidonius Apollinaris, sued for peace. He divided the Western Roman Empire with Odoacer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euric was one of the more learned of the great Visigothic kings and was the first German to formally codify his people's laws. The Code of Euric of 471 codified the traditional laws that had been entrusted to the memory of designa
2 CONC ted specialists who had learned each article by heart.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Euric's death in 484 the Kingdom of the Visigoths encompassed all of Iberia except for the region of Galicia (ruled by the Suebi) and more than two-thirds of modern France. Edward Gibbon, in Chapter 38 of the History of the Dec
2 CONC line and Fall of the Roman Empire, remarks:
2 CONT
2 CONT The fortune of nations has often depended on accidents; and France may ascribe her greatness to the premature death of the Gothic king, at a time when his son Alaric was a helpless infant, and his adversary Clovis an ambitious and
2 CONC  valiant youth.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Roi des Wisigoths (467) & Empereur d'Espagne (475) -
2 CONT
2 CONT Koning der Wisigothen (467) & Keizer van Spanje (475) -
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Visigoths (467) & Emperor of Spain (475) 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 7º REI VISIGODO da Espanha, reinando entre os anos de 466 e 484
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eurico (För att 420-484) Var kung över visigoterna ( Efter att ha dödat sin bror Theoderik II) Från 466 till 484. Son till Theoderik I och efterträddes av sin son Alarik II. Ragnaxilda gifte sig med en dotter som kallas Merovius, 
2 CONC Kung av FF. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eurico regeringstid sammanföll med upplösning av Romerska riket West, som avsatt den siste kejsaren Romulus Augustus i 476. Den Visigoterna hade undertecknat ett foedus med Rom i 418Under vilka riket blev federala och låt det sätt
2 CONC a sig i de södra Gallien. Denna kung independizou aboliu den visigotiske rike och den tidigare enheten. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Han tog olika territorier Gallien och Hispania. Guerreou i Gallien mot FF och Saxarna. Hans rike var vid denna tid den mest mäktiga och inflytelserika i Europa. Dess domstol bosatta i Tolosa (Toulouse ), även om nominellt Arles. M
2 CONC en det var mest Tolosa den stad som hade den kategori vi nu se hur kapital riket. Spanska historieskrivning nämner hur Konungariket Tolosa och omfattade ett stort område i Iberiska halvön ( Utom regionen Galiza, Kontrollerat av Su
2 CONC evos) , och mycket av Gallien väster om Rhin och söder om Loire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var en kung som uppvisade stor politisk skicklighet. Han publicerade ett lagstadgat organ Visigotisk lagDen Codex Euricianus eller Eurico kod. Han var också en fläkt Arian.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Eurico''' (c. 420-484). Foi rei dos visigodos (depois de assassinar o seu irmão Teodorico II), entre 466 e 484.
2 CONT
2 CONT Era filho de Teodorico I e sucedeu-lhe o seu filho Alarico II.
2 CONT
2 CONT O reinado de Eurico coincidiu com a queda do Império Romano do Ocidente, quando o último imperador, Rómulo Augústulo, foi destronado em 476 pelo ostrogodo Odoacro. Em virtude do tratado de federação (foedus) firmado com Roma em 41
2 CONC 8, os Visigodos eram um povo admitido a viver no seio do Império, concretamente no sul das Gálias. Eurico denunciou o foedus e declarou o seu reino independente de Roma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casou-se com Ragnagilda, aparentemente filha de Meroveu, rei dos Francos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apoderou-se de muitos territórios nas Gálias e na Hispânia. Combateu os Saxões e os Francos, e fez do reino visigodo o mais influente e poderoso da sua época. Apesar de a corte se ter nominalmente deslocado para Arles, permanecia 
2 CONC em Toulouse. Alguns historiadores chamam ao reino dos Visigodos o Reino de Toulouse, uma entidade que acabou por se estender desde a margem sul do rio Loire até à foz do Tejo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Demonstrando grande capacidade política, mandou registar, metodicamente, os antigos costumes e práticas jurídicas dos Visigodos no Código de Eurico (Codex Euricianus), que vigoraram até que o rei Recesvinto os fundiu com o direito
2 CONC  romano. Eurico foi também um ariano fanático.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''in: Wikipédia, a enciclopédia livre. <http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurico>''
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Euric''', also known as Evaric, Erwig, or Eurico in Spanish and Portuguese (c. 415–484), Son of Theodoric I and the younger brother of Theodoric II and ruled as king of the Visigoths, with his capital at Toulouse, from 466 unti
2 CONC l his death in 484.
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited a large portion of the Visigothic possessions in the Aquitaine region of Gaul, an area that had been under Visigothic control since 415. Over the decades the Visigoths had gradually expanded their holdings at the expe
2 CONC nse of the weak Roman government, advancing well into Hispania in the process.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon becoming king, Euric defeated several other Visigothic kings and chieftains in a series of civil wars and soon became the first ruler of a truly unified Visigothic nation. Taking advantage of the Romans' problems, he extended
2 CONC  Visigothic power in Hispania, driving the Suevi into the northwest of Iberia. By the time the Western Roman Empire ended in 476 he controlled nearly the entire Iberian peninsula.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 470 Euric defeated an attempted invasion of Gaul by the British king Riothamus and expanded his kingdom even further north, possibly as far as the Somme River, the March of Frankish territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Previous Visigothic kings had officially ruled only as legates of the Roman Emperor but Euric was the first to declare his complete independence from the puppet emperors. In 475 he forced the western emperor Julius Nepos to recogn
2 CONC ize his full independence in exchange for the return of the Provence region of Gaul. The Roman citizens of Hispania then pledged their allegiance to Euric, recognizing him as their king. In the same year Clermont(-Ferrand) surrend
2 CONC ered to him after a long siege, and its bishop, Sidonius Apollinaris, sued for peace. He divided the Western Roman Empire with Odoacer. Illustration of Euricat Biblioteca Nacional de España
2 CONT
2 CONT Euric was one of the more learned of the great Visigothic kings and was the first German to formally codify his people's laws. The Code of Euric of 471 codified the traditional laws that had been entrusted to the memory of designa
2 CONC ted specialists who had learned each article by heart.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Euric's death in 484 the Kingdom of the Visigoths encompassed all of Iberia except for the region of Galicia (ruled by the Suebi) and more than two-thirds of modern France. Edward Gibbon, in Chapter 38 of the History of the Dec
2 CONC line and Fall of the Roman Empire, remarks:
2 CONT
2 CONT The fortune of nations has often depended on accidents; and France may ascribe her greatness to the premature death of the Gothic king, at a time when his son by his wife Ragnachildis, Alaric II was a helpless infant, and his adve
2 CONC rsary Clovis an ambitious and valiant youth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euric
