2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p15027.htm#i150267
2 CONT
2 CONT Æðelbeorht II, King of Kent ▼1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #150267, d. 748
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=20 Nov 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT      Æðelbeorht II, King of Kent was the son of Eadric, King of Kent. ▼1 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 748. ▼2 He was also reported to have died in 762. ▼3
2 CONT
2 CONT      Æðelbeorht II, King of Kent succeeded to the title of King Æðelbeorht II of Kent in 725. ▼2
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Æðelbeorht II, King of Kent
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ecgbeorht II, King of Kent ▼1 d. 784
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Eadbeorht II, King of Kent ▼1 d. a 798
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. unknown daughter (?)+ ▼1
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Æthelbert II (Old English: Æðelberht; 725 – 762) was king of Kent . Upon the death of his father Wihtred , the kingdom was ruled by his three sons, Æthelbert II, Eadberht I  and Ælfric . Æthelbert seems to have outlived both of hi
2 CONC s brothers and later reigned jointly with his nephew Eardwulf . He died in 762, according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle (recorded under 760 due to chronological dislocation). He seems to have left a son, Eadberht II.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also said that an unnamed daughter married Ealhmund , King of Kent.
2 CONT
2 CONT He issued a charter before his accession, dated 11 July 724, that was witnessed by his father. As king he issued further charters, confirmed a charter of his brother Eadberht I, and witnessed a charter of his nephew Eardwulf.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the latter half of Æðelberht II's rule, Kent was under the overlordship of Mercia , but Æðelberht II maintained his position as king.
