1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Shapur I (also spelled Šahpur I) was the second Sassanid King of the Second Persian Empire. The dates of his reign are commonly given as 241 - 272, but it is likely that he also reigned as co-regent (together with 
2 CONC his father, Ardashir) prior to his father's death in 241.
2 CONT
2 CONT The picture is of a wax figure in the Fars History Museum in Iran.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur accompanied his father's campaigns against the Parthians, who--at the time--still controlled much of the Iranian plateau through a system of vassal states that the Persian kingdom had itself previously been a part of. Befor
2 CONC e an assembly of magnates, Ardeshir "judged him the gentlest, wisest, bravest and ablest of all his children" and nominated him as his successor. Shapur also appears as heir apparent in Ardeshir's investiture inscriptions at Naqsh
2 CONC -e Rajab and Firuzabad. The Cologne Mani-Codex indicates that, by 240, Ardeshir and Shapur were already reigning together. In a letter from Gordian III to his senate, dated to 242, the "Persian Kings" are referred to in the plural
2 CONC . Synarchy is also evident in the coins of this period that portray Ardashir facing his youthful son, and which are accompanied by a legend that indicates that Shapur was already referred to as King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of Shapur's coronation remains debated, but 241 is frequently noted. That same year also marks the death of Ardeshir, and earlier in the year, his and Shapur's seizure and subsequent destruction of Hatra, about 100 km sou
2 CONC thwest of Nineveh and Mosul in present-day Iraq. According to legend, al-Nadirah, the daughter of the king of Hatra, betrayed her city to the Sassanids, who then killed the king and had the city razed. (Legends also have Shapur ei
2 CONC ther marrying al-Nadirah, or having her killed, or both).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ardashir I had, towards the end of his reign, renewed the war against the Roman Empire. Shapur I conquered the Mesopotamian fortresses Nisibis and Carrhae and advanced into Syria. Timesitheus, father-in-law of the young emperor, G
2 CONC ordian III, drove him back and defeated him at the Battle of Resaena in 243, regaining Nisibis and Carrhae. Timesitheus died shortly afterward, and Philip the Arab (244–249) murdered Gordian III after his defeat at the Battle of M
2 CONC isiche. Philip then concluded an ignominious peace with the Persians in 244. With the Roman Empire debilitated by Germanic invasions and the continuous elevation of new emperors after the death of Trajan Decius (251), Shapur I res
2 CONC umed his attacks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur conquered Armenia, invaded Syria, and plundered Antioch. Eventually, the Emperor Valerian (253–260) marched against him and by 257, Valerian had recovered Antioch and returned the province of Syria to Roman control. In 259,
2 CONC  Valerian moved to Edessa, but an outbreak of plague killed many and weakened the Roman troops defending the city which was then beseiged by the Persians. In 260, Valerian arranged a meeting with Shapur to negotiate a peace settle
2 CONC ment but was betrayed by Shapur who seized him and held him prisoner for the remainder of his life. Shapur advanced into Asia Minor, but was driven back by defeats at the hands of Balista, who captured the royal harem. Septimius O
2 CONC denathus, prince of Palmyra, rose in his rear, defeated the Persian army and regained all the territories Shapur had occupied. Shapur was unable to resume the offensive and lost Armenia again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur personally considered one of the great achievements of his reign to be the defeat of the Roman Emperor Valerian. This is presented in a mural at Naqsh-e Rustam, where Shapur is represented on horseback wearing royal armour 
2 CONC and crown. Before him kneels Philip the Arab, in Roman dress, asking for grace. In his right hand the king grasps the uplifted arms of what may be Valerian; one of his hands is hidden in his sleeve as the sign of submission. The s
2 CONC ame scene is repeated in other rock-face inscriptions. Shapur is said to have publicly shamed Valerian by using the Roman Emperor as a footstool when mounting his horse. Other sources contradict and note that in other stone carvin
2 CONC gs, Valerian is respected and never on his knees. This is supported by reports that Valerian and some of his army lived in relatively good conditions in the city of Bishapur and that Shapur enrolled the assistance of Roman enginee
2 CONC rs in his engineering and development plans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur I left other reliefs and rock inscriptions. A relief at Naqsh-e Rajab near Istakhr, is accompanied by a Greek translation. Here Shapur I calls himself "the Mazdayasnian (worshipper of Ahuramazda), the divine Sapores, King o
2 CONC f Kings of the Aryans, Iranians, and non-Aryans, of divine descent, son of the Mazdayasnian, the divine Artaxerxes, King of Kings of the Aryans, grandson of the divine king Papak." Another long inscription at Istakhr mentions the 
2 CONC King's exploits in archery in the presence of his nobles. From his titles we learn that Shapur I claimed the sovereignty over the whole earth, although in reality his domain extended little farther than that of Ardashir I. Shapur 
2 CONC I built the great town Gundishapur near the old Achaemenid capital Susa, and increased the fertility of the district by a dam and irrigation system - built by the Roman prisoners - that redirected part of the Karun River. The barr
2 CONC ier is still called Band-e Kaisar, "the mole of the Caesar." He is also responsible for building the city of Bishapur, also built by Roman soldiers captured after the defeat of Valerian in 260.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur is mentioned many times in the Talmud, as King Shabur. He had good relations with the Jewish community and was a friend of Shmuel, one of the most famous of the Babylonian Amoraim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Shapur's reign, the prophet Mani, the founder of Manichaeism, began his preaching in Western Iran, and the King himself seems to have favoured his ideas. The Shapurgan, Mani's only treatise in the Middle Persian language, is
2 CONC  dedicated to Shapur.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shapur_I for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Shapur I or also known as Shapur I the Great was the second Sassanid King of the Second Persian Empire. The dates of his reign are commonly given as 240/42 - 270/72, but it is likely that he also reigned as co-regent (together wit
2 CONC h his father) prior to his father's death in 242 (more probably than 240).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 War against the Roman Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Builder of cities
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Interactions with minorities
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur was the son of Ardashir I (r. 226–240 [died 242]), the founder of the Sassanid dynasty and whom Shapur succeeded. His mother was Lady Myrōd,[3] who—according to legend[4]—was an Arsacid princess. The Talmud cites a nickname
2 CONC  for her, "Ifra Hurmiz", after her bewitching beauty.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Coin of Shapur I the Great, showing the ruler, and a fire altar with two stylized attendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur accompanied his father's campaigns against the Parthians, who - at the time - still controlled much of the Iranian plateau through a system of vassal states that the Persian kingdom had itself previously been a part of. Bef
2 CONC ore an assembly of magnates, Ardashir "judged him the gentlest, wisest, bravest and ablest of all his children"[3] and nominated him as his successor. Shapur also appears as heir apparent in Ardashir's investiture inscriptions at 
2 CONC Naqsh-e Rajab and Firuzabad. The Cologne Mani-Codex indicates that, by 240, Ardashir and Shapur were already reigning together.[3] In a letter from Gordian III to his senate, dated to 242, the "Persian Kings" are referred to in th
2 CONC e plural. Synarchy is also evident in the coins of this period that portray Ardashir facing his youthful son, and which are accompanied by a legend that indicates that Shapur was already referred to as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of Shapur's coronation remains debated. 240 is frequently noted,[3] but Ardashir lived very probably until 242.[6] 240 also marks the year of the seizure and subsequent destruction of Hatra, about 100 km southwest of Nine
2 CONC veh and Mosul in present-day Iraq. According to legend, al-Nadirah, the daughter of the king of Hatra, betrayed her city to the Sassanids, who then killed the king and had the city razed. (Legends also have Shapur either marrying 
2 CONC al-Nadirah, or having her killed, or both).[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]War against the Roman Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT Rock-face relief at Naqsh-e Rustam of Shapur (on horseback) with Philip the Arab and Emperor Valerian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ardashir I had, towards the end of his reign, renewed the war against the Roman Empire. Shapur I conquered the Mesopotamian fortresses Nisibis and Carrhae and advanced into Syria. Timesitheus, father-in-law of the young emperor, G
2 CONC ordian III, drove him back and defeated him at the Battle of Resaena in 243, regaining Nisibis and Carrhae. Timesitheus died shortly afterward, and Philip the Arab (244–249) murdered Gordian III after his defeat at the Battle of M
2 CONC isiche. Philip then concluded an ignominious peace with the Persians in 244. With the Roman Empire debilitated by Germanic invasions and the continuous elevation of new emperors after the death of Trajan Decius (251), Shapur I res
2 CONC umed his attacks.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fine cameo showing an equestrian combat of Shapur I and Valerian in which the Roman emperor is seized, according to Shapur’s own statement, “with our own hand”, in year 256.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur conquered Armenia, invaded Syria, and plundered Antioch. Eventually, the Emperor Valerian (253–260) marched against him and by 257, Valerian had recovered Antioch and returned the province of Syria to Roman control. In 259,
2 CONC  Valerian moved to Edessa, but an outbreak of plague killed many and weakened the Roman troops defending the city which was then besieged by the Persians. In 260, Valerian arranged a meeting with Shapur to negotiate a peace settle
2 CONC ment but was betrayed by Shapur who seized him and held him prisoner for the remainder of his life.[8] Shapur advanced into Asia Minor, but was driven back by defeats at the hands of Balista, who captured the royal harem. Septimiu
2 CONC s Odenathus, prince of Palmyra, rose in his rear, defeated the Persian army and regained all the territories Shapur had occupied. Shapur was unable to resume the offensive and lost Armenia again.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Humiliation of Valerian by Shapur I. Pen and ink, Hans Holbein the Younger, c. 1521
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the great achievements of Shapur's reign was the defeat of the Roman Emperor Valerian. This is presented in a mural at Naqsh-e Rustam, where Shapur is represented on horseback wearing royal armour and crown. Before him knee
2 CONC ls Philip the Arab, in Roman dress, asking for grace. In his right hand the king grasps the uplifted arms of what may be Valerian; one of his hands is hidden in his sleeve as the sign of submission. The same scene is repeated in o
2 CONC ther rock-face inscriptions. Shapur is said to have publicly shamed Valerian by using the Roman Emperor as a footstool when mounting his horse.[10] Other sources contradict and note that in other stone carvings, Valerian is respec
2 CONC ted and never on his knees. This is supported by reports that Valerian and some of his army lived in relatively good conditions in the city of Bishapur and that Shapur enrolled the assistance of Roman engineers in his engineering 
2 CONC and development plans.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Builder of cities
2 CONT
2 CONT Wax figure of Shapur I in Fars History museum
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur I left other reliefs and rock inscriptions. A relief at Naqsh-e Rajab near Istakhr, is accompanied by a Greek translation. Here Shapur I calls himself "the Mazdayasnian (worshipper of Ahuramazda), the divine Sapores, King o
2 CONC f Kings of the Aryans, Iranians, and non-Aryans, of divine descent, son of the Mazdayasnian, the divine Artaxerxes, King of Kings of the Aryans, grandson of the divine king Papak." Another long inscription at Istakhr mentions the 
2 CONC King's exploits in archery in the presence of his nobles. From his titles we learn that Shapur I claimed the sovereignty over the whole earth, although in reality his domain extended little farther than that of Ardashir I. Shapur 
2 CONC I built the great town Gundishapur near the old Achaemenid capital Susa, and increased the fertility of the district by a dam and irrigation system - built by the Roman prisoners - that redirected part of the Karun River. The barr
2 CONC ier is still called Band-e Kaisar, "the mole of the Caesar." He is also responsible for building the city of Bishapur, also built by Roman soldiers captured after the defeat of Valerian in 260.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Interactions with minorities
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur is mentioned many times in the Talmud, as King Shabur. He had good relations with the Jewish community and was a friend of Shmuel, one of the most famous of the Babylonian Amoraim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Shapur's reign, the prophet Mani, the founder of Manichaeism, began his preaching in Western Iran, and the King himself seems to have favoured his ideas. The Shapurgan, Mani's only treatise in the Middle Persian language, is
2 CONC  dedicated to Shapur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur I
2 CONT
2 CONT Sassanid dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Ardashir I	"King of kings of Iran and Aniran"
2 CONT
2 CONT 240/42 –272	Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Hormizd I
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Two wars against the Roman Empire. Shapur on his coins and inscriptions calls himself a "worshiper of Mazda" the god of Zoroastrianism.
2 CONT
2 CONT [In the third year of Constantine, Shapur reigned as king of the Persians for 70 years.  Constantine went to battle against Maximian who was in Rome] Chronicles of Michael the great patriarch of the Syrians.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 224 the Persian king Ardashir I overthrew the Arsacids in Parthia and found the new Persian Sassanid dynasty. The Sassanids were determined to restore the old glory of the Achaemenid Persia (Medo-Persian Empire), so they procla
2 CONC imed Zoroastrianism as the state religion and considered Armenia as part of their empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT To preserve the autonomy of Arsacid rule in Armenia, Tiridates II sought friendly relations with Rome. This was an unfortunate choice, because the Sassanid king Shapur I defeated the Romans and made peace with the emperor Philip. 
2 CONC In 252 Shapur I invaded Armenia and forced Tiridates II to flee. After the deaths of Tiridates II and his son Khosrov II, Shapur I installed his own son Hurmazd on the Armenian throne. When Shapur I died in 270, Hurmazd took the P
2 CONC ersian throne and his brother Narseh ruled Armenia in his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur I (Middle Persian: 𐭱𐭧𐭯𐭥𐭧𐭥𐭩; New Persian: شاپور), also known as Shapur I the Great, was the second shahanshah (king of kings) of the Sasanian Empire. The dates of his reign are commonly given as 240/42 – 270/72, but it is li
2 CONC kely that he also reigned as co-regent (together with his father) prior to his father's death in 242 (more probably than 240).
2 CONT
2 CONT Amongst the most notable events of his reign was the decisive defeat of the Romans at the infamous Battle of Edessa, where the Roman army was defeated and captured in its entirity, including the Roman Emperor, Valerian. He became 
2 CONC the only Roman Emperor who was captured as a prisoner of war, causing instability in the Empire. As such, the battle is generally viewed as one of the worst disasters in Roman military history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur was the son of Ardashir I (r. 224–242 [died 242]), the founder of the Sasanian dynasty and whom Shapur succeeded. His mother was Lady Myrōd, who—according to legend—was an Arsacid princess. The Talmud cites a nickname for h
2 CONC er, "Ifra Hurmiz", after her bewitching beauty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur also had a brother named Ardashir, who would later serve as governor of Kirman. Shapur may also had another brother with the same name, who served as governor of Adiabene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur accompanied his father's campaigns against the Parthians, who — at the time — still controlled much of the Iranian plateau through a system of vassal states, in which the Persian kingdom had itself previously been a part. B
2 CONC efore an assembly of magnates, Ardashir "judged him the gentlest, wisest, bravest and ablest of all his children"[2] and nominated him as his successor. Shapur also appears as heir apparent in Ardashir's investiture inscriptions a
2 CONC t Naqsh-e Rajab and his capital, Firuzabad.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT see: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shapur_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur I (Middle Persian: 𐭱𐭧𐭯𐭥𐭧𐭥𐭩; New Persian: شاپور), also known as Shapur I the Great, was the second shahanshah (king of kings) of the Sasanian Empire. The dates of his reign are commonly given as 240/42 – 270/72, but it is li
2 CONC kely that he also reigned as co-regent (together with his father) prior to his father's death in 242 (more probably than 240).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the most notable events of his reign was the decisive defeat of the Romans at the infamous Battle of Edessa, where the Roman army was defeated and captured in its entirety, including the Roman Emperor, Valerian. He became th
2 CONC e first Roman Emperor to be captured as a prisoner of war, causing instability in the Empire. As such, the battle is generally viewed as one of the worst disasters in Roman military history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur was the son of Ardashir I (r. 224–242 [died 242]), the founder of the Sasanian dynasty and whom Shapur succeeded. His mother was Lady Myrōd,[2] who—according to legend[4]—was an Arsacid princess. The Talmud cites a nickname
2 CONC  for her, "Ifra Hurmiz", after her bewitching beauty.[5] Shapur also had a brother named Ardashir, who would later serve as governor of Kirman. Shapur may also had another brother with the same name, who served as governor of Adia
2 CONC bene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur accompanied his father's campaigns against the Parthians, who — at the time — still controlled much of the Iranian plateau through a system of vassal states, in which the Persian kingdom had itself previously been a part. B
2 CONC efore an assembly of magnates, Ardashir "judged him the gentlest, wisest, bravest and ablest of all his children"[2] and nominated him as his successor. Shapur also appears as heir apparent in Ardashir's investiture inscriptions a
2 CONC t Naqsh-e Rajab and his capital, Gor.
