1 NAME Abu Abdallah Mahdī
2 GIVN Abu Abdallah Mahdī
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 970
2 PLAC Hawsam, Tabaristan, Iran
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} sent to make a campaign in Tabaristan by Abu Jafar Abbaside and to make his quarters in Ray. He will take the daughter of the Masmughan, Bakhtyariah, as his concubine and she will bear him a son: Mansur. ref: page 
2 CONC 43  The History of al-Tabari Vol. 28: 'Abbasid Authority Affirmed: The Early ... By Jane Dammen McAuliffe
2 CONT ===
2 CONT [ ...  In his youth Abū ʿAbdallāh Mahdī had traveled widely in Iran, ʿOmān, and Iraq and had won the hearts of many Zaydīs as an attractive candidate for the imamate. Their support had rendered him suspect to the rulers, and he ha
2 CONC d repeatedly been imprisoned and deported. In 337/948-49 he came to Baghdad where he studied law with the prominent Hanafite Abu’l-Ḥasan Karḵī and theology with the Muʿtazilite Abū ʿAbdallāh Baṣrī. In 348/959-60 he was pressed by 
2 CONC the Buyid Moʿezz-al-dawla into accepting the position of syndic (naqīb) of the ʿAlids; on his own insistence he was exempted from the obligation of visiting the ʿAbbasid caliph and from wearing the official black dress of the ʿAbb
2 CONC asids. Accepting the invitation of Manāḏer, he left Baghdad secretly in 353/964. In his struggle against Amīrkā, he was soon joined by the Naserid Abū Moḥammad Ḥasan, who had been provided by Rokn-al-dawla with money to fight the 
2 CONC Thaʾerid. They took Hawsam from Amīrkā, and Abū Moḥammad left the rule to Abū ʿAbdallāh Mahdī, since the latter was his maternal uncle and more learned than himself. Mahdī was indeed the first Caspian ʿAlid after Nāṣer to gain uni
2 CONC versal recognition by the later Zaydīs as an Imam. He devoted his teaching efforts in particular to overcoming the antagonism between the Nāṣerīya and the Qāsemīya, who clung to the doctrine of Qāsem b. Ebrāhīm, maintaining that t
2 CONC he doctrines of both schools were equally valid. Though this view was later generally accepted by the two communities, it did not prevent them from recognizing often different ʿAlid rulers in their midst.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amīrkā continued to harass Mahdī from a castle near Hawsam, but was eventually captured and soon pardoned. When Mahdī set out to conquer Rūyān, he left Amīrkā in Hawsam as his deputy. Amīrkā revolted in his absence forcing him to 
2 CONC return in haste. Though Mahdī soon regained Hawsam, Amīrkā escaped and kept up his attacks. In 358/969 Amīrkā captured him, but after a few months was forced by Manāḏer and other Daylamites to release him. Mahdī continued to rule 
2 CONC in Hawsam until he died, perhaps poisoned, in 360/970. He was buried in Hawsam. ref: http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/alids-of-tabarestan-daylaman-and-gilan
2 CONT
2 CONT ===
2 CONT also: https://www.academia.edu/27313100/Reading_Guide_A_Short_History_of_the_Ismailis_by_Farhad_Daftary?email_work_card=view-paper
