2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Helena (Latin: Flavia Iulia Helena Augusta) also known as Saint Helen, Helena Augusta or Helena of Constantinople (c. 250 – c. 330) was the consort of Emperor Constantius Chlorus, and the mother of Emperor Constantine I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She is traditionally credited with the finding of the relics of the True Cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena's birthplace is not known with certainty. The sixth-century historian Procopius is the earliest authority for the statement that Helena was a native of Drepanum, in the province of Bithynia in Asia Minor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son Constantine renamed the city "Helenopolis" after her death in 328, giving rise to the belief that the city was her birthplace. Although he might have done so in honor of her birthplace, Constantine probably had other reaso
2 CONC ns for doing so. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop and historian Eusebius of Caesarea states that she was about 80 on her return from Palestine] Since that journey has been dated to 326–28, Helena was probably born in 248 or 250. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of her early life. Fourth-century sources, following Eutropius' "Breviarium," record that she came from a low background. Saint Ambrose was the first to call her a stabularia, a term translated as "stable-maid" or 
2 CONC "inn-keeper". He makes this fact a virtue, calling Helena a bona stabularia, a "good stable-maid". Other sources, especially those written after Constantine's proclamation as emperor, gloss over or ignore her background.
2 CONT
2 CONT The precise legal nature of the relationship between Helena and Constantius is unknown: the sources are equivocal on the point, sometimes calling Helena Constantius' "wife," and sometimes calling her his "concubine." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena gave birth to the future emperor Constantine I in 272. In 293, Constantius was ordered by emperor Diocletian to divorce her in order to qualify as Caesar of the Western Roman Empire, and he was married to the stepdaughter o
2 CONC f Maximian, Theodora. Helena never remarried and lived in obscurity, though close to her only son, who had a deep regard and affection for her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine was proclaimed Augustus of the Roman Empire in 306 by Constantius' troops after the latter had died, and following his elevation his mother was brought back to the public life and the imperial court, and received the t
2 CONC itle of Augusta in 325. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena died in 330 with her son at her side. Her sarcophagus is on display in the Pio-Clementino Vatican Museum. During her life, she gave many presents to the poor, released prisoners and mingled with the ordinary worshippers in 
2 CONC modest attire, exhibiting a true Christian spirit.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helena_of_Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena Britannica ferch Princess of Britain	
2 CONT
