2 CONT
2 CONT The story goes that St. Helena went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem afterher famous son, Constantine, became Emporer. There she uncovered many ofthe holy sites where Jesus walked and even found the true cross, wherebyfragments of the
2 CONC  cross were placed in many churches throughout Europe.(IJL)
1 NOTE St. Helen of the Cross
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=bef343ad-bf3c-42ef-baad-6104755df31b&tid=2440653&pid=-1813507501
1 NOTE helena1
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=8d992d40-f0db-4c91-9ce7-965c79d17f8b&tid=904323&pid=-2029334920
1 NOTE In Great Britain, later legend, mentioned by Henry of Huntingdon but made popular by Geoffrey of Monmouth claimed that Helena was a daughter of the King of Britain, Cole of Colchester, who allied with Constantius to avoid more war
2 CONC  between the Britons and Rome. Geoffrey further states that she was brought up in the manner of a queen, as she had no brothers to inherit the throne of Britain. Monmouth and Huntingdon's source may have been Sozomen. However, Soz
2 CONC omen doesn't claim Helena was British though he does claim in Historia Ecclesiastica that her son, Constantine I, picked up his Christianity there. There is no other surviving evidence to support this legend, which may be due to c
2 CONC onfusion with St. Elen, wife of the later Emperor, Magnus Maximus.
2 CONT
2 CONT At least twenty-five holy wells currently exist in the United Kingdom that are dedicated to a Saint Helen or Elen. She is also the patron saint of Colchester and Abingdon.
