2 CONT Fausta ( ca. 293-326 A.D.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans A. Pohlsander
2 CONT
2 CONT SUNY Albany
2 CONT
2 CONT Fausta's full name was Flavia Maxima Fausta. 1 She was the daughter of the emperor Maximian and his Syrian wife Eutropia .2 The date of her birth has been the subject of scholarly debate. 3 She was born and raised in Rome. 4
2 CONT
2 CONT In 307 Fausta was married to the emperor Constantine .5 An anonymous Gallic orator composed a panegyric for the occasion. 6 The marriage quite possibly took place at Trier. 7 Although both Constantine's and Fausta's specific dates
2 CONC  of birth are subject to question, it is certain that he was her senior by fifteen or more years. Constantine's drive for sole power must have put a strain on the relationship on at least two occasions. In 310, when Maximian plott
2 CONC ed against Constantine , Fausta revealed the plot, thus contributing to her own father's end. 8 On 28 October 312 Fausta's brother, the emperor Maxentius , suffered defeat and lost his life in the famous Battle of the Milvian Brid
2 CONC ge. The next day his body was recovered from the Tiber River. Constantine had the severed head affixed to a pike and carried through the streets of Rome. 9 We do not know Fausta's reaction to this act of brutality.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fausta was mother to three sons and two daughters. The three sons were Constantine II , born on 7 August 316 (?), Constantius II , born on 7 August 317, and Constans , born in 320 or 323. 10 These three were appointed Caesars on 1
2 CONC  March 317, 8 November 324, and 25 December 333, respectively. 11 The two daughters were Constantina (not to be confused with Constantine's half-sister Constantia ) and Helena (not to be confused with Constantine's mother and Cris
2 CONC pus' wife of the same name). We do not know the years of their birth, only that Constantina was the older one of the two. 12 As the emperor's wife Fausta held the rank of nobilissima femina .13 Not too long after his victory over 
2 CONC Licinius , that is in 324 or 325, Constantine granted her the rank of Augusta, 14 as he also did to his mother Helena 15
2 CONT
2 CONT Fausta's life ended in a most unfortunate way. Her own husband ordered her to be executed in 326, shortly after he had his son Crispus executed. 16 We even learn the means of execution: she was suffocated in an over-heated bath. 1
2 CONC 7 Two later sources, Zosimus and Zonaras, report that Fausta and Crispus were involved with each other in an illicit Phaedra-and-Hippolytus type of relationship. 18 We may entertain doubts about the nature of their offense and als
2 CONC o about the means of Fausta's execution. 19 But the two deaths are so close together in time and our sources so unanimous on this point that there must be some connection between the two cases. 20 We shall not be surprised to find
2 CONC  that Fausta suffered damnatio memoriae .21
2 CONT
2 CONT We must deny any validity to Zosimus' 22 claim that Constantine was driven to accept Christianity by a sense of guilt over his deeds. A similar claim had already been made by Julian the Apostate .23 That Fausta and Crispus togethe
2 CONC r plotted treason is reported by Gregory of Tours, 24 but is very unlikely: what would have been their common interest? It has also been claimed that Fausta took her own life; 25 there is no support for this claim in any of our so
2 CONC urces. Nor do our sources say where Fausta met her end. 26 Strangely enough, Julian the Apostate has words of high praise for Fausta in the panegyric which he addressed to Constantius II , probably in 355. 27 Did Constantius II tr
2 CONC y to restore his mother's honor?
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Recent and concise accounts of the life of Fausta, with reference to the scattered primary sources, are the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adolf Lippold in Der kleine Pauly II (1967) 522.
2 CONT
2 CONT A. H. M. Jones, J. R. Martindale, and J. Morris, The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire I (Cambridge, 1971) 325-26.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy D. Barnes, The New Empire of Diocletian and Constantine (Cambridge MA, 1982) 9, 33, 34, and 43.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dietmar Kienast, Römische Kaisertabelle, 2nd ed. (Darmstadt, 1996) 305.
2 CONT
2 CONT The account by Otto Seeck , RE VI.2 (1909) 2084-86, has become in part obsolete.
2 CONT
2 CONT More extensive or specialized studies are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Vogt, "Heiden und Christen in der Familie Constantins des Grossen," in Eranion: Festschrift för Hildebrecht Hommel (Tübingen 1961) 149-68, esp. 161-63. Also in English: "Pagans and Christians in the Family of Constantine th
2 CONC e Great," in Arnaldo Momigliano, ed., The Conflict between Paganism and Christianity in the Fourth Century (Oxford 1963) 38-55, esp. 48-50.
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Guthrie, "The Execution of Crispus," Phoenix 20 (1966) 325-31.
2 CONT
2 CONT François Paschoud, Cinq études sur Zosime (Paris, 1975) 24-39.
2 CONT
2 CONT J. Rougé, "Fausta, femme de Constantin: criminelle ou victime," CahHist. 25 (1980) 3-17.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans A. Pohlsander, "Crispus: Brilliant Career and Tragic End," Historia 33 (1984) 79-106 at 99-106.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan Willem Drijvers, "Flavia Maxima Fausta: Some Remarks," Historia 41 (1992) 500- 506.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel N. C. Lieu and Dominic Montserrat, edd., From Constantine to Julian: Pagan and Byzantine Views. A Source History (London 1996). Contains translation of and commentary on the Anonymus Valesianus, Origo Constantini .
2 CONT
2 CONT Additionally references to Fausta will be found in all of the books on Constantine.
