0 @N6000000002187918638@ NOTE Khosrau I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Khosrau I, (Most commonly known as Anooshiravan also spelled Anushirvan, Persian: ????????? meaning the immortal soul), also known as Anooshiravan the Just (????????? ????, Anooshiravan-e-adel) (ruled 531–579), was the favourite s
1 CONC on and successor of Kavadh I (488–531), and the most famous and celebrated of the Sassanid Kings. He laid foundations of many new cities and magnificent palaces, trade roads were repaired and new bridges and dams were built. Durin
1 CONC g Khosrau I's ambitious reign art and science flourished in Persia and the Sassanid empire was in its peak of glory and prosperity. His rule preceded by his father's and succeeded by Khosrau II's (590–628) reign altogether is cons
1 CONC idered the Second golden era in the history of the Sassanid empire.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Conquests
1 CONT     * 3 Religious tolerance
1 CONT     * 4 Reforms
1 CONT     * 5 See also
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT Please improve and expand this section. There may be comments on what is required on the requests for expansion page or on this article's talk page.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to one account, Khosrau I was Kavadh I's son through a peasant girl, and was originally considered unworthy of inheriting his father's throne. His brothers contested his claim, so Khosrau I had them killed. He had a majo
1 CONC r influence over his father Kavadh I of Persia and helped him in worst situations during his rule. He was also behind many of his father's decisions.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquests
1 CONT Silver bowl showing Khosrau I Anushirvan, of the righteous soul seated on his throne. This became a model representation of kingship for Byzantine art and from there, in Carolingian art.
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Silver bowl showing Khosrau I Anushirvan, of the righteous soul seated on his throne. This became a model representation of kingship for Byzantine art and from there, in Carolingian art.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the beginning of his reign Khosrau I concluded an "eternal" peace with the Byzantine Emperor Justinian I (527–565), who wanted to have his hands free for the conquest of Africa and Sicily. But his successes against the Vandals 
1 CONC and Goths caused Khosrau I to begin the war again in 540.
1 CONT
1 CONT He invaded Syria and carried the inhabitants of Antioch to his residence, where he built for them a new city near Ctesiphon under the name of Khosrau-Antioch or Chosro-Antioch. During the next years he fought successfully in Lazic
1 CONC a or Lazistan (the ancient Colchis), on the Black Sea, and in Mesopotamia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Byzantines, though led by Belisarius, could do little against him. In 545, an armistice was concluded, but in Lazica the war went on till 556. At last, in 562, a peace was concluded for fifty years, in which the Persians left 
1 CONC Lazistan to the Romans, and promised not to persecute the Christians, if they did not attempt to make proselytes among the Zarathustrians; on the other hand, the Romans had again to pay subsidies to Persia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile in the east, the Hephthalites had been attacked by the Turks (Gokturks). Khosrau I united with them and conquered Bactria, while he left the country north of the Oxus to the Turks. Many other rebellious tribes were subje
1 CONC cted. About 570 the dynasts of Yemen, who had been subdued by the Ethiopians of Axum, applied to Khosrau I for help. The King sent a fleet with a small army under Vahriz, who expelled the Ethiopians. From that time till the conque
1 CONC sts of Muhammad, Yemen was dependent on Persia, and a Persian governor resided here. In 571 a new war with Rome broke out about Armenia, in which Khosrau I conquered the fortress Dara on the Euphrates, invaded Syria and Cappadocia
1 CONC , and returned with large booty. During the negotiations with the Emperor Tiberius II (578–582), Khosrau I died in 579, and was succeeded by his son Hormizd IV (579–590).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Religious tolerance
1 CONT Please improve and expand this section. There may be comments on what is required on the requests for expansion page or on this article's talk page.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Khosrau I had in the last years of his father extirpated the heretical and communistic Persian sect of the Mazdakites (see Kavadh I of Persia), he was a sincere adherent of Zoroastrian orthodoxy and even ordered that the 
1 CONC religion's holy text, the Avesta be codified, but he was not fanatical or prone to persecution. He tolerated every Christian confession. When one of his sons had rebelled about 550 and was taken prisoner, he did not execute him; n
1 CONC or did he punish the Christians who had supported him.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Justinian I closed the Academy of Athens in 529, the last seat of paganism in the Roman Empire, the last seven teachers of Neoplatonism emigrated to Persia. But they soon found out that neither Khosrau I nor his state corresp
1 CONC onded to the Platonic ideal, and Khosrau I, in his treaty with Justinian I, stipulated that they should return unmolested.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Reforms
1 CONT
1 CONT Khosrau I introduced a rational system of taxation, based upon a survey of landed possessions, which his father had begun, and tried in every way to increase the welfare and the revenues of his empire. In Babylonia he built or res
1 CONC tored the canals. His army was in discipline decidedly superior to the Romans, and apparently was well paid. He was also interested in literature and philosophical discussions. Under his reign, chess was introduced from India, and
1 CONC  the famous book of Kalilah and Dimnah was translated. He thus became renowned as a wise prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under Khosrau I's auspices, many books were brought from India and translated into Pahlavi. Some of these later found their way into the literature of the Islamic world. His famous minister Burzoe translated Indian Panchatantra fr
1 CONC om Sanskrit into middle persian language of Pahlavi and named it Kelileh va Demneh which later on from its Persian version it transmitted to Arabia and Europe.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Burzoe (The renowned minister of Khosrau)
1 CONT     * Sassanid Music
1 CONT     * Science and medical academy of Gundishapur
1 CONT     * Khosrau I's reforms
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Dr. Abd al-Husayn Zarrin’kub "Ruzgaran : tarikh-i Iran az aghz ta saqut saltnat Pahlvi" Sukhan, 1999. ISBN 964-6961-11-8
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Kavadh I Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 531–579 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Hormizd IV
0 @N6000000003051245788@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Khosrau I", retrieved 15 Dec 07]
1 CONT Khosrau I or Khosrow I (Chosroes I in classical sources, most commonly known in Persian as Anushirvan, meaning the immortal soul), also known as Anushiravan the Just (ruled 531-579), was the favourite son and successor of Kavadh I
1 CONC  (488-531), twentieth Sassanid King of Persia, and the most famous and celebrated of the Sassanid Kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT He laid the foundations of many cities and opulent palaces, and oversaw the repair of trade roads as well as the building of numerous bridges and dams. During Khosrau I's ambitious reign, art and science flourished in Persia and t
1 CONC he Sassanid Empire reached its peak of glory and prosperity. His rule was preceded by his father's and succeeded by Khosrau II's (590-628) who's reign came to be considered the dark age in the history of the Sassanid Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT According to one account, Khosrau I was Kavadh I's son through a peasant girl, and was originally considered unworthy of inheriting his father's throne. His brothers contested his claim, so Khosrau I had them killed (ca. 532). He 
1 CONC appears to have had a major influence over his father Kavadh I of Persia and helped him in the worst situations during the later years of his rule. He was apparently also behind many of his father's decisions.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Roman Historian Procopius of Caesarea, Kavadh I tried to have his third son Khosrau adopted by the Eastern Roman emperor Justin I in the mid-520s. This is the first time that Khosrau is mentioned in the sources. A
1 CONC fter Romans and Persians had failed to reach an agreement about the adoption, a new war began in 526 which was to last until 532.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquests
1 CONT At the beginning of his reign Khosrau I concluded an "Eternal Peace" with the Roman Emperor Justinian I (527-565) in 532, who wanted to have his hands free for the conquest of Africa and Sicily. But (according to Procopius) his su
1 CONC ccesses against the Vandals and Goths caused Khosrau I to begin the war again in 540.
1 CONT
1 CONT He invaded Syria and sacked the great city of Antioch, deporting its people to Mesopotamia, where he built for them a new city near Ctesiphon under the name of "Khosrau-Antioch" or "Chosro-Antioch". During the following years he s
1 CONC ecured the defection of Lazica and fought inconclusively in Mesopotamia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 545, an armistice was concluded, but in 547 the Lazi returned to their Roman allegiance and the Lazic War resumed, continuing until a truce was agreed in 557. At last, in 562, a peace was concluded for fifty years, in which the
1 CONC  Persians left Lazica to the Romans, and promised not to persecute the Christians, if they did not attempt to make proselytes among the Zarathustrians; on the other hand, the Romans had to pay annual subsidies to Persia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile in the east, the Hephthalites had been attacked by the Turks (Göktürks). About 560, Khosrau I united with them to destroy the Hephthalite Empire. In 567 he conquered Bactria, while he left the country north of the Oxus t
1 CONC o the Turks. Many other rebellious tribes were subjected. About 570 the Himyarite dynasts of Yemen, who had been subdued by the Ethiopians of Axum, applied to Khosrau I for help. The Emperor Khosrau sent a fleet with a small army 
1 CONC under Vahriz, who expelled the Ethiopians. From that time till the conquests by Islam, Yemen was dependent on Persia, and a Persian governor resided here. In 572, Armenia and Iberia rebelled against Persia with Roman support, begi
1 CONC nning a new war in which Khosrau I conquered the city of Dara on the Euphrates in 573, but after a largely unsuccessful incursion of Anatolia in 576 he was heavily defeated by the Romans in a battle near Melitene. He sued for peac
1 CONC e in 579, but while negotiations with the Emperor Tiberius II (578?582) were still going on, Khosrau I died and was succeeded by his son Hormizd IV (579?590).
1 CONT
1 CONT Religious tolerance
1 CONT Although Khosrau I had in the last years of his father extirpated the heretical and communistic Persian sect of the Mazdakites (see Kavadh I of Persia), he was a sincere adherent of Zoroastrian orthodoxy and even ordered that the 
1 CONC religion's holy text, the Avesta be codified, but he was not fanatical or prone to persecution. He tolerated every Christian confession. When one of his sons had rebelled about 550 and was taken prisoner, he did not execute him; n
1 CONC or did he punish the Christians who had perhaps supported him.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Justinian I had closed the Academy of Athens, one of the last seats of paganism in the Roman Empire, the last seven teachers of Neoplatonism emigrated to Persia in 531. But they soon found out that neither Khosrau I nor his 
1 CONC state corresponded to the Platonic ideal, and Khosrau I, in his treaty with Justinian I, stipulated that they should return unmolested.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reforms
1 CONT Khosrau I introduced a rational system of taxation, based upon a survey of landed possessions, which his father had begun, and tried in every way to increase the welfare and the revenues of his empire. In Babylonia he built or res
1 CONC tored the canals. His army was in discipline decidedly superior to the Romans, and apparently was well paid. He was also interested in literature and philosophical discussions. Under his reign, chess was introduced from India and 
1 CONC many books were brought from India and translated into Pahlavi. Some of these later found their way into the literature of the Islamic world. His famous minister Burzoe translated Indian Panchatantra from Sanskrit into middle Pers
1 CONC ian language of Pahlavi and named it Kelileh va Demneh which later on from its Persian version it transmitted to Arabia and Europe.
