0 @N6000000003495151901@ NOTE
1 CONT Information on him is mostly legendary.
1 CONT
1 CONT Childeric I c.436–481, Merovingian king of the Salian Franks (c.457–481), a Germanic tribe; son of Meroveus and father of Clovis I.  His rule was that of a tribal chieftain. He defeated (463) the Visigoths at Orléans as an ally of
1 CONC  the Roman general Aegidius. Subsequently he defeated the Saxons and the Alemanni. His tomb, containing armor and ornaments, was discovered in 1653 at his capital, near Tournai, Belgium.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia® Copyright © 2005, Columbia University Press. Licensed from Columbia University Press. All rights reserved. www.cc.columbia.edu/cu/cup/
1 CONT
1 CONT Childeric I (c. 437- c. 482) was the Merovingian king of the Salian Franks from 457 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech (Latinised as Meroveus or Merovius) as king, traditionally in 457 or 458. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at Tournai, on lands which he had received as a foederatus of
1 CONC  the Romans, and for some time he kept the peace with his allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT In about 463 at Orléans, in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius, who was based at Soissons, he defeated the Visigoths, who hoped to extend their dominion along the banks of the Loire River; after the death of Aegidius he f
1 CONC irst assisted Comes ("count") Paul of Angers in a mixed band of Gallo-Romans and Franks, defeating the Goths and taking booty. Odoacer reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next day and a battle ensued. Count Paul was killed an
1 CONC d Childeric took the city. Childeric having delivered Angers, he followed a Saxon warband to the islands at the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there. In a change of alliances, he also joined forces with Odoacer, a
1 CONC ccording to Gregory of Tours, to stop a band of the Alamanni who wished to invade Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT These are all the facts known about him, and they are not secure. The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his stay of eight years in Thuringia with King Basin and his wife Basina; of his return wh
1 CONC en a faithful servant advised him that he could safely do so by sending to him half of a piece of gold which he had broken with him; and of the arrival at Tournai of Queen Basine, whom he married, are entirely legendary and come f
1 CONC rom Gregory of Tours' Libri Historiarum (Book ii.12).
1 CONT He died in 481 and was buried at Tournai, leaving a son Clovis, afterwards king of the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT His Tomb
1 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653, by a mason doing repairs at the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai when numerous precious objects were found, a richly ornamented sword, a torse-like bracelet, jewels of gold and cloisonné en
1 CONC amel with garnets, gold coins, a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified the tomb. Some 300 golden bees were also in the find. Archduke Leopold William, Spanish
1 CONC  governor of the Netherlands, had the find published in Latin, and the treasure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV, who was not impressed with them and stored them in the royal library, which became
1 CONC  the Bibliothèque nationale de France at the Revolution. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees: looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourbon fleur-de-lys, he settled on Childeric's bees as symbols of the French E
1 CONC mpire.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the night of November 5-6, 1831, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved where they had been hidden in the Seine, including two 
1 CONC of the bees, but record of the treasure now exists only in the fine engravings made at the time of its discovery, and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Merovingian Dynasty
1 CONT Born: 437; Died: 482
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Merovech
1 CONT King of Salian Franks
1 CONT 457–482
1 CONT Succeeded by:
1 CONT Clovis I
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT • Northvegr webite: Gregory of Tours, Historia Francorum
1 CONT • "A note on Childeric's bees": the discovery of his tomb: follow the links for the engravings of Childeric's treasure and the two remaining gold bees.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT • This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
