0 @N6000000009304899908@ NOTE #Générale##Générale#Profession : Roi des Francs Saliens de 457 à 481.
0 @N6000000006602640894@ NOTE Childeric I (c. 437 </wiki/437>- c. 481 </wiki/481>) was the Merovingian </wiki/Merovingian> king of the Salian Franks </wiki/Salian_Franks> from 457 </wiki/457> until his death, and the father of Clovis </wiki/Clovis_I>.
1 CONT He succeeded his father Merovech </wiki/Merovech> (Latinised </wiki/Latinised> as Meroveus or Merovius) as king, traditionally in 457 </wiki/457> or 458 </wiki/458>. With his Frankish warband he was established with his capital at
1 CONC  Tournai </wiki/Tournai>, on lands which he had received as a foederatus </wiki/Foederati> of the Romans </wiki/Roman_Empire>, and for some time he kept the peace with his allies.
1 CONT In about 463 </wiki/463> in Orléans </wiki/Orl%C3%A9ans>, in conjunction with the Roman General Aegidius </wiki/Aegidius>, who was based inSoissons </wiki/Soissons>, he defeated the Visigoths </wiki/Visigoths>, who hoped to extend
1 CONC  their dominion along the banks of the Loire </wiki/Loire> River. After the death of Aegidius, he first assisted Comes("count") Paul of Angers </wiki/Angers>, together with a mixed band ofGallo-Romans and Franks, in defeating the 
1 CONC Goths and taking booty. Odoacer </wiki/Odoacer> reached Angers but Childeric arrived the next dayand a battle ensued. Count Paul was killed and Childeric took the city. Childeric, having delivered Angers, followed a Saxon warband 
1 CONC to theislands on the Atlantic mouth of the Loire, and massacred them there.In a change of alliances, he also joined forces with Odoacer </wiki/Odoacer>, according to Gregory of Tours </wiki/Gregory_of_Tours>, to stop a band of the
1 CONC  Alamanni </wiki/Alamanni> who wished to invade Italy </wiki/Italy>.
1 CONT The stories of his expulsion by the Franks, whose women he was taking; of his eight-year stay in Thuringia </wiki/Thuringia> with King Basin and his wife Basina; of his return when a faithful servant advised him that he could safe
1 CONC ly do so by sending to him half of a piece of gold </wiki/Gold> which he had broken with him; and of the arrival in Tournai of Queen Basina </wiki/Basina>, whom he married come from Gregory of Tours' Libri Historiarum (Book ii.12)
1 CONC .
1 CONT He died in 481 and was buried in Tournai </wiki/Tournai>, leaving a son Clovis </wiki/Clovis_I>, afterwards king of the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Childeric_I&action=edit&section=1>] His tomb
1 CONT Childeric's tomb was discovered in 1653 </wiki/1653> (May 27) by a mason doing repairs in the church of Saint-Brice in Tournai </wiki/Tournai>, a city in modern Belgium, where numerous precious objects were found, including a rich
1 CONC ly ornamented sword, a torse </wiki/Torse>-like bracelet, jewels of gold and cloisonné enamel with garnets, gold coins,a gold bull's head and a ring with the inscription CHILDERICI REGIS ("of Childeric the king"), which identified
1 CONC  the tomb. Some 300 golden bees </wiki/Bee> were also found. Archduke Leopold William </wiki/Archduke_Leopold_Wilhelm_of_Austria>, governor of the Southern Netherlands (today's Belgium), had the find published in Latin, and the tr
1 CONC easure went first to the Habsburgs in Vienna, then as a gift to Louis XIV </wiki/Louis_XIV_of_France>, who was not impressed with them and stored them in the royal library, which became the Bibliothèque Nationale de France </wiki/
1 CONC Biblioth%C3%A8que_Nationale_de_France> during the Revolution </wiki/French_Revolution>. Napoleon was more impressed with Childeric's bees when he was looking for a heraldic symbol to trump the Bourbon fleur-de-lys </wiki/Fleur-de-
1 CONC lys>. He settled on Childeric's bees as symbols of the French Empire </wiki/First_French_Empire>.
1 CONT On the night of November 5-6, 1831 </wiki/1831>, the treasure of Childeric was among 80 kilos of treasure stolen from the Library and melted down for the gold. A few pieces were retrieved where they had been hidden in the Seine, i
1 CONC ncluding two of the bees. The record of the treasure, however, now exists only in the fine engravings made at the timeof its discovery, and in some reproductions made for the Habsburgs.
0 @N6000000000435214430@ NOTE King of the Salian Franks 456-481 Childeric succeeded his semi-legendary fath er Merovech as king of theSalian Franks of northern Gaul in 456, during Roman times. He becameinfatuated with the daughters of his subjects, who were so
1 CONC  inc ensedabout this that they forced him to give up the throne. He discovered thatthey intended to assassinate him, and he fled to Thuringia, leaving aclose fr iend and telling him to send him a message when Childeric couldreturn
1 CONC  to his k ingdom. Childeric took refuge with Bisinus, King of the Thuringian Franks, an dhis wife Basina. The king elected by the Franks was cruel, and soon afterChi lderic was re-called to his kingdom by his friend, and was resto
1 CONC redto the thro ne. Once Bisinus and Childeric were both kings, Basinadeserted her husband and went to live with Childeric, who married her andhad a son Clovis. After a b attle with Odoacer, King of the Saxons (and conquerer of the
1 CONC Western Roman Empi re, 476), at Orleans, Childeric and the Saxon kingmade a peace treaty and toge ther subdued the Alamanni, who had invaded apart of Italy. In 481 Childeric di ed and was succeeded by Clovis, hisson by Basina. [Ne
1 CONC w Cunard.ged]
1 CONT
