1 CONT Bisinus, Basinus, Besinus, or Bisin (Lombardic: Pisen) was the king of the Thuringii (fl. c. 460 ? 506/510).
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Gregory of Tours, he supplied refuge from Childeric I, the Frankish king who was exiled by his own people. His wife, Basina, left him for Childeric and the two returned to Tournai together, after eight years.
1 CONT
1 CONT The historical Bisinus bears some resemblance to the Bisinus of Gregory, but the details are different. Bisinus was the leader of a Thuringian confederation on the Rhine and his wife was a Lombard named Menia. He left three sons, 
1 CONC Baderic, Herminafred, and Berthachar, who inherited the throne from him. His daughter Radegund married the Lombard king Wacho.
0 @N6000000000337167914@ NOTE (Research):Clotilda, Saint Clotilda, Saint Pronounced As: kltild , d. 545, Frankish queen. She converted her husband, Clovis I, to Christianity and built with him in Paris the Church of the Apostles Peter and Paul, later renamed (
1 CONC 10th cent.) Sainte-Geneviève. After her husband's death she spent her life caring for the poor. Feast: June 3.
