0 @N6000000002187918696@ NOTE Yazdegerd II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd II, ("made by God," Izdegerdes), King of Persia, was the son of Bahram V of Persia (421–438) and reigned from 438 to 457.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the beginning of his reign, Yazdegerd II quickly attacked the Eastern Roman Empire with a mixed army of various nations, including his Indian allies, to eliminate the threat of a Roman buildup. (The Romans had been constructing
1 CONC  fortifications in the nearby Persian territory of Carrhae, in anticipation of subsequent expeditions.) The Romans were caught by surprise, and only a heavy flood prevented a Persian advance deep into in Roman territory. The Byzan
1 CONC tine Emperor, Theodosius II (408–450), asked for peace and sent his commander personally to Yazdegerd II's camp. In the ensuing negotiations in 441, both empires promised not to build any new fortifications in their border territo
1 CONC ries. Yazdegerd II, in spite of having the upper hand, did not make further demands on the Romans due to incursions by Kidarites in Parthia and Khwarezmia. He gathered his forces in Neishabur in [[443] and launched a prolonged cam
1 CONC paign against Kidarites. After numerous battles, he crushed them and drove them out beyond Oxus river in 450.
1 CONT
1 CONT Durin his eastern campaign, Yazdegerd II grew suspicious of Christians in the army and the nobility and expelled many of them. He then persecuted the Christians and, to a much lesser extent, Jews. Advancing his pro-Zoroastrian pol
1 CONC icy, he crushed an uprising of Armenian Christians in the Battle of Vartanantz in 451.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his later years, Yazdegerd II became engaged again with Kidarites until his death in 457. Other than his strict religious policies and minorities persecution, commoners saw him as a just, moderate ruler.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bahram V Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 438–457 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Hormizd III
0 @N6000000003051336703@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Yazdgerd II", retrieved 15 Dec 07]
1 CONT Yazdegerd II, ("made by God," Izdegerdes), fifteenth Sassanid King of Persia, was the son of Bahram V (421-438) and reigned from 438 to 457.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the beginning of his reign, Yazdegerd quickly attacked the Eastern Roman Empire with a mixed army of various nations, including his Indian allies, to eliminate the threat of a Roman build-up. The Romans had been constructing fo
1 CONC rtifications in the nearby Persian territory of Carrhae, in anticipation of subsequent expeditions. They were caught by surprise, and only a heavy flood prevented a Persian advance into in Roman territory. The Byzantine Emperor, T
1 CONC heodosius II (408-450), asked for peace and sent his commander personally to Yazdegerd's camp. In the ensuing negotiations in 441, both empires promised not to build any new fortifications in their border territories. Yazdegerd, i
1 CONC gathered his forces in Neishabur in 443 and launched a prolonged campaign against the Kidarites. After numerous battles, he crushed them and drove them out beyond the Oxus river in 450.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his eastern campaign, Yazdegerd grew suspicious of Christians in the army and the nobility and expelled many of them. He then persecuted the Assyrians Christians and, to a much lesser extent, Jews. Advancing his pro-Zoroast
1 CONC rian policy, he crushed an uprising of Armenian Christians in the Battle of Vartanantz in 451.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his later years, Yazdegerd became engaged again with the Kidarites until his death in AD 457. He pursued strict religious policies and persecuted various minorities.
0 @N6000000002188193895@ NOTE Empress Yifu
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Yifu (????, personal name unknown) (510-540), formally Empress Wen (???, literally "the civil empress"), was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state of Northern Wei. Her husband was 
1 CONC Emperor Wen (Yuan Baoju).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 As empress
1 CONT     * 3 Death
1 CONT     * 4 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Her ancestors were ancestral chiefs of a branch tribe of Tuyuhun, and later became vassals of Northern Wei. After Northern Wei conquered Northern Liang in 439, her great-great-grandfather Yifu Mogui (????) led his tribe into North
1 CONC ern Wei and became a Northern Wei general. For three generations following his, the Yifus married Northern Wei princesses, and their daughters often married Northern Wei imperial princes. Her father Yifu Yuan (???) was a provincia
1 CONC l governor, and her mother was the Princess Huaiyang, a daughter of Emperor Xiaowen.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 525, when she was 15, she married Yuan Baoju, who was then 21 and a general under his cousin Emperor Xiaoming, but who carried no noble title because his father Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Jingzhao had had his title stripped pos
1 CONC thumously after dying in a rebellion. In 535, after Northern Wei had split into Eastern Wei and Western Wei, he was made emperor (as Emperor Wen) by the general Yuwen Tai after the death of his cousin Emperor Xiaowu. He created he
1 CONC r empress and created her son Yuan Qin crown prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As empress
1 CONT
1 CONT As empress, Empress Yifu was said to be frugal, avoiding extravagant clothing, jewelry, and food, often eating just vegetables. She was also said to be kind and tolerant and never jealous, and Emperor Wen respected her greatly. Sh
1 CONC e bore him 12 children, although only Yuan Qin and Yuan Wu (??) the Prince of Wudu survived infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 538, with Western Wei occupied with wars against Eastern Wei and unable to fend itself against attacks by Rouran, Yuwen Tai wanted to further relationships with Rouran through an imperial marriage. He first had Emperor Wen crea
1 CONC te the daughter of the official Yuan Yi (??) as the Princess Huazheng and marry her to the Rouran Chiliantoubingdoufa Khan Yujiulü Anagui's brother Yujiulü Tahan (?????), and then further asked Emperor Wen to marry Yujiulü Anagui'
1 CONC s daughter. Emperor Wen was forced to agree. He deposed Empress Yifu and made her become a Buddhist nun, and he created Yujiulü Anagui's daughter empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Even though Empress Yifu had been deposed and made a nun, however, Empress Yujiulü was still jealous of her. To avoid conflict, in 540, Emperor Wen made Yuan Wu the governor of Qin Province (??, roughly modern Tianshui, Gansu), an
1 CONC d had Empress Yifu accompany Yuan Wu to his post. Emperor Wen, still harboring love for her in his heart, secretly requested that she start keeping her hair uncut so that he could eventually welcome her back to the palace. Unfortu
1 CONC nately, around the same time, Rouran launched a major attack against Western Wei, and a number of officials believed that the Rouran attack was made on behalf of Empress Yujiulü. Emperor Wen, with the officials pressuring him, sen
1 CONC t his eunuch Cao Chong (??) to Qin Province to order Empress Yifu to commit suicide. She made the comment to Cao:
1 CONT
1 CONT     May it be that His Imperial majesty live for a thousand years, and the empire is happy and calm. I do not have regrets about death.
1 CONT
1 CONT She then committed suicide, and was buried with honors due an empress, albeit not near the capital Chang'an, but instead at the Maiji Cliffs (???), near Shanggui (??) the capital of Qin Province. It was not until later, when Yuan 
1 CONC Qin was emperor, that she was reburied with Emperor Wen.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Gao Empress of Northern Wei (Western)
1 CONT 535-538 Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Yujiulü
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Zizhi Tongjian, vols. 157, 158.
1 CONT     * History of Northern Dynasties, vols. 5[1], 13[2].
0 @N6000000002188193921@ NOTE Emperor Fei of Western Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Xi) Wei Feidi ((?)???)
1 CONT Family name: Yuan (?, yuán)
1 CONT Given name: Qin (?, qin)
1 CONT Posthumous name: None
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Fei of Western Wei ((?)???) (d. 554), personal name Yuan Qin (??), was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei state Western Wei -- a branch successor state of Northern Wei. He, even more so than his father Emperor Wen, held lit
1 CONC tle actual power in the face of overwhelming control of power by the paramount general Yuwen Tai. In 554, he tried to plot to have Yuwen killed, but his plot was discovered, and Yuwen deposed him, and soon had him killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As crown prince
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not known when Yuan Qin was born. What is known is that he was the oldest son of Yuan Baoju the Prince of Nanyang, a grandson of Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei, by his wife Princess Yifu, and that he had one younger brother
1 CONC  by Princess Yifu, Yuan Wu (??). Around the new year 535, Emperor Xiaowu of Northern Wei -- a cousin of Yuan Baoju and the final emperor of the undivided Northern Wei state but who by now was only emperor over the western part of 
1 CONC the state (i.e., Western Wei) -- was poisoned by the paramount general Yuwen Tai over a dispute developed when Yuwen disapproved of his incestuous relationship with Yuan Baoju's sister Yuan Mingyue (???). Yuwen Tai made Yuan Baoju
1 CONC  the new emperor (as Emperor Wen). Emperor Wen created Princess Yifu empress, and created Yuan Qin crown prince. In 538, while both Emperor Wen and Yuwen Tai were away on a campaign against Eastern Wei, Yuan Qin was nominally put 
1 CONC in charge of the capital Chang'an, although the official Zhou Huida (???) was actually in charge. When former Eastern Wei captives, upon hearing initial news of Eastern Wei victories, rebelled within Chang'an with Zhao Qingque (??
1 CONC ?), Zhou and Li Hu (??) were forced to escort the crown prince out of Chang'an to flee the rebellion. Eventually, when Yuwen returned, Zhao's rebellion collapsed, and Emperor Wen and Yuan Qin were both able to return to Chang'an.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Wen had little actual power, and also in 538, over Yuwen's desire for an alliance with Rouran, he was forced to depose Empress Yifu and marry the daughter of Rouran's Chiliantoubingdoufa Khan Yujiulü Anagui as his empress.
1 CONC  In 540, he was further forced to initially send Empress Yujiulü away from Chang'an (to live with Yuan Qin's younger brother Yuan Wu), and then forced to order her to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sometime during Emperor Wen's reign, Yuan Qin married Yuwen Tai's daughter as his crown princess. It was said that he favored her greatly, and that he had no concubines.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 542, during an assault that Eastern Wei's paramount general Gao Huan launched on the border city Yubi (??, in modern Yuncheng, Shanxi), Yuan Qin was made the defender of the important city Puban (??, in modern Yuncheng as well)
1 CONC , while Yuwen launched an army to try to catch up with Gao as he retreated. It is not known how much responsibility or authority Yuan Qin actually had during this campaign.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 551, Emperor Wen died. Yuan Qin took the throne (as Emperor Fei).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As emperor
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Fei appeared to have even less actual power than his father, to whom Yuwen Tai paid formal deference. He was able to rebury his mother Empress Yifu with his father Emperor Wen. He created his wife, Crown Princess Yuwen, em
1 CONC press. Apparently because Yuwen wanted to restore many Zhou Dynasty customs and traditions, Emperor Fei did not use an era name; rather, the years of his reign were merely referred to as "the first year," "the second year," and so
1 CONC  on.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 553, Yuwen Tai's nephew Weichi Jiong conquered the western provinces of Liang Dynasty, which had been previously under the control of the Liang pretender to the throne, Xiao Ji, and added them to Western Wei territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In late 553, the official Yuan Lie (??) plotted killing Yuwen, but his plot was discovered, and Yuwen killed him. After Yuan Lie's death, Emperor Fei became angry at Yuwen and often spoke against him. He also secretly plotted kill
1 CONC ing Yuwen, despite advice from Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Linhuai and Yuan Zan (??, the nephew of Emperor Xiaowu) the Prince of Guangping that plotting was dangerous. Emperor Fei's plot, however, was eventually discovered by Yuwen
1 CONC 's other sons-in-law. Yuwen deposed and imprisoned Emperor Fei, making his younger brother Yuan Kuo emperor (as Emperor Gong). Soon thereafter, Emperor Fei was put to death. According to the History of Northern Dynasties, his wife
1 CONC  Empress Yuwen "also suffered death because she was faithful to Wei's imperial house," although it is unclear exactly how she died or whether she died the same year he did.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Wen of Western Wei
1 CONT     * Mother
1 CONT           o Empress Yifu
1 CONT     * Wife
1 CONT           o Empress Yuwen (created 551?, d. 554?)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Emperor Wen of Western Wei Emperor of Northern Wei (Western)
1 CONT 551-554 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emperor Gong of Western Wei
1 CONT Emperor of China (Western)
1 CONT 551-554
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Xiao Ji of Liang Emperor of China (Southwestern)
1 CONT 553-554
