0 @N6000000003495151916@ NOTE
1 CONT Clodio (c. 395 - 447 or 449) or, the Long-Haired or the Hairy, was a semi-legendary king of the Salian Franks from the Merovingian dynasty (426 - 447). His successor was Meroveus, after whom the dynasty was named. Legend has it th
1 CONC at his father was duke Pharamond and his mother was Argotta, from Thuringia. His grandfather may have been Marcomer, a duke of the Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT There are basically only two sources of information for Clodio's history: the writings of Gregory of Tours and Sidonius Apollinaris.
1 CONT
1 CONT Clodio lived in Dispargum, a name that is believed to be that of a castle, rather than a village. Around 431, he invaded the territory of Artois, but was defeated near Hesdin by Aëtius, the commander of the Roman army in Gaul, Wes
1 CONC tern Roman Empire. However, Clodio regrouped and soon was able to seize the town Cameracum. Eventually, he occupied all the country as far as the Somme River and making Tournai the capital of the Salian Franks.
1 CONT
1 CONT Clodio's aggressive action to seize more territory led to centuries of expansion by his successors that ultimately created what we know today as the country of France. Clodio died sometime between 447 and 449 and power passed on t
1 CONC o Meroveus. It is not known if Meroveus was his son or another chieftain of the tribe who ascended into the leadership role.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT • List of Frankish Kings
1 CONT • History of France
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 - There is much disagreement over this ruler's name. Variations of Clodio include Chlodio, Chlodion, Clodion, Clodius, Chlogio, and Clodian.
0 @N6000000003637338395@ NOTE Clodion "le Chevelu" des François nacido circa 394. Se lee en las listas consulares "que Clodio, un hombre de alto nacimiento y magnífica habilidad entre su gente, era Rey de los Francos y vivia en el castillo de Duisburg en el te
1 CONC rritorio de Turingia". Envió espias al pueblo de Cambrai y descubrió como vencer a los romanos y tomar el trono del pueblo. Vivió allí un corto tiempo . Tenía el pelo y la barba largas, que significaban libertad frente al romano q
1 CONC ue llevaba el pelo corto y rasurada la barba. Fue hijo de Pharamond y de Argotta. Casó con Basina hija de Weldelphus de Thuringen antes del 414. El renunció al vasallaje de Roma y cruzó el rio Scheldt extendiendose al sur en la Ga
1 CONC lia hasta Tournai y Cambrai. Dicen los Anales de Monte Fernando del 430: "Secundus rex Francie Glodus." Conquistó Turnacum (Tournai en el 430 en Bélgica, en la Galia. Fue vencido por el general romano Aetius en Hélesme hoy Helena 
1 CONC entre Tournai y Cambrai, cerrando asi la posibilidad de expansión. Residió en Amiens. Falleció en el 448. Fue su hijo Merovech rex Francorum.
0 @N6000000003949647103@ NOTE Chlodio, also spelled CLODION, French CHLODION (d. c. 447), king of atribe of Salian Franks, considered the founder of the Merovingiandynasty. Chlodio's tribe renounced the suzerainty of Rome after 428 andbroke across the Scheldt 
1 CONC River, spreading southward into Gaul until theyreached Tournai and Cambrai. Their defeat (c. 431) by the Roman generalAetius at Helena (Hélesme), between Tournai and Cambrai, preventedfurther expansion. Chlodio id occupy all the c
1 CONC ountry as far as the SommeRiver, however, and he made Tournai the capital of the Salian Franks. Hissuccessor was Merovech. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, CHLODIO]
