0 @N6000000003949656109@ NOTE Vinitharius retained the "insiginia of his princely rank" and tried to escape Hunnic subjugation.  To that end he invaded the land of the Antes and after an initial setback, had their king, royal sons and 70 nobles crucified "as a
1 CONC  warning example".  After about a year, Balamber. the Hunnic king, put an end to Ostroghothic independence.  With the Amali Geismund, he marched against Vinitarius.  A long conflict ensued with Vinitharius twice victorious who inf
1 CONC licted heavy losses on the Huns.  On the third battle on the river Erac, Vinitharius was killed by an arrow launched by B alamber himself.  (per "History of Goths" by Herwing Wolfram (p251) publ 1979 
0 @N6000000001353874431@ NOTE Bahram V (421-438), son of Yazdegerd I, after whose sudden death (or assassination) he gained the crown against the opposition of the grandees by the help of al-Mundhir, the Arabic dynast of Hira. He began a systematic persecution
1 CONC  of the Christians, which led to a war with the Roman empire. But a treaty was soon concluded by which both empires promised toleration to the worshippers of the two rival religions, Christianity and Zoroastrianism.
1 CONT Bahram deposed the vassal king of the Persian part of Armenia and made it a province. He is a great favourite in Persian tradition and is called Bahram Gor, "the wild ass," on account of his strength and courage. [Encyclopaedia Br
1 CONC itannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 2, p. 928, BAHRAM]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Bahram V, also called BAHRAM GUR (fl. 5th century AD), Sasanian king (reigned 420-438). He was celebrated in literature, art, and folklore for his chivalry, romantic adventures, and huntsmanship.
1 CONT He was educated at the court of al-Mundhir, the Lakhmid Arab king of al-Hira, in Mesene, whose support helped him gain the throne after theassassination of his father, Yazdegerd I. He was apparently also supported by Mihr-Naresh, 
1 CONC chief minister of Yazdegerd's last years, to whom Bahram later delegated much of the governmental administration.
1 CONT Bahram carried on an inconclusive war with the Romans (421-422), and in 427 he crushed an invasion in the east by the nomadic Hephthalites,extending his influence into Central Asia, where his portrait survived for centuries on the
1 CONC  coinage of Bukhara (in modern Uzbekistan). [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, BAHRAM V]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT They say the Lion and the Lizard keep
1 CONT The Courts where Jamshýd gloried and drank deep;
1 CONT And Bahrám, that great Hunter--the Wild Ass
1 CONT Stamps o'er his Head, but cannot break his sleep.
1 CONT The Rubáiyát (xviii)
1 CONT Omar Khayyam
