0 @N6000000002187576081@ NOTE In 423 his uncle, Emperor Honorius, died and for two years the Imperial throne was taken by a usurper. Then Valentinianus's mother, Galla Placida, controlled the West in his name until 437, although the powerful patrician Aetius b
1 CONC ecame the effective ruler toward the end of this regency. In 429 the Vandals went to Africa and, ten years later, threw off the overlordship of Valentinanus's government. However, at no time in his long reign were the affairs of s
1 CONC tate personally managed by Valentinianus. In 437 he married Licinia Eudoxia, daughter of his cousin, the Eastern Emperor Theodosius II. He spent his life in the pursuit of pleasure while Aetius controlled the government. In 444 Va
1 CONC lentinianus, acting in conjunction with Pope Leo I, issued the famous "Novel 17", which assigned to the bishop of Rome supremacy over the provincial churches. In 451 and 452 the Huns invaded Gaul and Northern Italy but it is not k
1 CONC nown whether Valentinianus took part in their repulsion. However, false information made him distrust Aetius and, on 21 September 454, he personally murdered Aetius in the imperial palace in Rome. A year later, Optila and Thrausti
1 CONC le, retainers of Aetius, avenged their master by murdering the Emperor in the Campus Martius. Source: Leo van de Pas. *Stammtafeln zu Europaeischen Staaten* by Prinz von Isenburg.*Les seize quartiers des reines et imperatrices de 
1 CONC Frances* by Jacques Saillot. 
