0 @N6000000003796485756@ NOTE Merovech
1 CONT Born: ?
1 CONT Died: 578 Father: Chilperic I, King of Soissons
1 CONT Mother: Audovera
1 CONT
1 CONT Married (1): Brunhild
1 CONT Children: ?
1 CONT Merovech was the son of King Chilperic of Soissons by one of his first wives. His father ordered him to march on Poitiers, but he instead went to Tours, took the city, and married Brunhild, the widow of his late uncle King Sigeber
1 CONC t of Metz. Chilperic was very angry, and the two agreed to separate while in Rouen. There in Rouen, Chilperic and Merovech drove out Clovis (Chilperic's son) and Queen Fredegund (Chilperic's wife), taking the city. Merovech was pu
1 CONC t in the custody of his father and was made a priest to Le Mans. He instead returned to Tours seeking sanctuary from his father. Chilperic found out and Merovech fled the city to Champagne. In 578, Merovech again sought sanctuary 
1 CONC in Tours but was killed on the order of his father. return to Imperium e-mail Joe Shetler at: shetler@ghgcorp.com
1 CONT
1 CONT ==================================
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Brunhild
1 CONT Born: 545
1 CONT Died: 613 Father: Anthanagild, King of the Visigoths
1 CONT Mother: Goiswinth
1 CONT
1 CONT Married (1): Sigebert I, King of Metz
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT Childebert II, King of Austrasia and Burgundy
1 CONT Ingund
1 CONT Chlodosind
1 CONT
1 CONT Married (2): Merovech
1 CONT Children: ?
1 CONT Brunhild was the daughter of the Visigothic king Anthanagild and wife of the Frankish king Sigebert I of Metz. Born an Arian, she was converted to Catholocism. When Sigebert was assassinated and her son Childebert II took the thro
1 CONC ne, Sigebert's brother King Chilperic of Soissons banished Brunhild and imprisoned Ingund and Chlodosind. Chilperic's son Merovech then disobeyed his father and married Brunhild. Chilperic was outraged, and the two agreed to separ
1 CONC ate. Later Fredegund, wife of Chilperic, nearly had her assassinated. Until the end of her life she was the target of many assassination attempts, and present during many political meetings between her son and his uncles and cousi
1 CONC ns.
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Childebert was assassinated in 595, Queen Brunhild ruled in the place of his two sons Theudebert II and Theuderic II. Brunhild lived in the court of Theudebert in Austrasia until he married Bilichild. Brunhild saw her as
1 CONC  compitition, but could not convince her grandson not to marry her. Instead, she left the court and was received warmly in Burgundy by Theuderic. He married the Catholic Ermenberta, daughter of King Recared I of the Visigoths. Aft
1 CONC er a year, Brunhild convinced this one to divorce her and take back his concubines. In 612, Brunhild urged Theuderic to attack his brother, whom he defeated and killed. The next year, however, the 26 year old king died of dysenter
1 CONC y. Brunhild chose Sigebert II, son of Theuderic, to rule as king, but Chlotar, King of Soissons, ordered him executed, taking control of all the Frankish kingdoms. That year, he had Brunhild brutally tortured and executed (68 year
1 CONC s old).
1 CONT return to Imperium e-mail Joe Shetler at: shetler@ghgcorp.com
