0 @N6000000002187572233@ NOTE Clotaire II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Clotaire II (584-629), called the Great (le Grand) or the Young (le Jeune), King of Neustria, and from 613-629 King of all the Franks, was not yet born when his father, King Chilperic I died in 584. His mother, Queen Fredegonde, w
1 CONC as regent until her death in 597, at which time the thirteen year old Clotaire II began to rule for himself. As King, he continued his mother's feud with Queen Brunhilda of Austrasia with equal viciousness and bloodshed.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In 599, he made war with his cousins, Theuderic II of Burgundy and Theudebert II of Austrasia, who defeated him at Dormelles (near Montereau). At this point, however, the two brothers took up arms against each other. In 605, he in
1 CONC vaded Theuderic's kingdom, but did not subdue it. He remained often at war with Theuderic and the latter died in Metz in late 613 while preparing a campaign against him. At that time, Warnachar, mayor of the palace of Austrasia, a
1 CONC nd Rado, mayor of the palace of Burgundy, abandoned the cause of Brunhilda and her great-grandson, Sigebert II, and the entire realm was delivered into Clotaire's hands. Brunhilda and Sigebert met Clotaire's army on the Aisne, but
1 CONC  the Patrician Aletheus, Duke Rocco, and Duke Sigvald deserted the host and the grand old woman and her king had to flee. As far as the Orbe they got, but Clotaire's minions caught up with them by the lake Neuchâtel. Both of them 
1 CONC and Sigebert's younger brother Corbo were executed by Clotaire's orders.
1 CONT
1 CONT In that year, Clotaire II became the first king of all the Franks since his grandfather Clotaire I died in 561 by ordering the murder of the infant Sigebert II (son of Theuderic), whom the aging Brunhilda had attempted to set on t
1 CONC he thrones of Austrasia and Burgundy, causing a rebellion among the nobility. This led to the delivery of Brunhilda into Clotaire's hands, his thirst for vengeance leading to his formidable old aunt enduring the agony of the rack 
1 CONC for three whole days, before suffering a horrific death, chained between four horses that were goaded in separate directions, eventually tearing her apart.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 615, Clotaire II promulgated the Edict of Paris, a sort of Frankish Magna Carta that reserved many rights to the Frankish nobles while it excluded Jews from all civil employment for the Crown. The ban effectively placed all lit
1 CONC eracy in the Merovingian monarchy squarely under ecclesiastical control and also greatly pleased the nobles, from whose ranks the bishops were ordinarily exclusively drawn. Clotaire was induced by Warnachar and Rado to make the ma
1 CONC yoralty of the palace a lifetime appointment at Bonneuil-sur-Marne, near Paris, in 617. By these actions, Clotaire lost his own legislative abilities and the great number of laws enacted in his reign are probably the result of the
1 CONC  nobles' petitions, which the king had no authority not to heed.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 623, he gave the kingdom of Austrasia to his young son Dagobert I. This was a political move as repayment for the support of Bishop Arnulf of Metz and Pepin I, mayor of the palace of Austrasia, the two leading Austrasian nobles
1 CONC , who were effectively granted semi-autonomy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Clotaire II died in 629 after 45 years on the throne, longer than any other Merovingian dynast. He left the crown greatly reduced in power and prepared the way for the rise of the mayors and the rois fainéants.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Franks (main history of Frankish kingdoms) 
1 CONT List of Frankish Kings 
1 CONT Merovingians 
1 CONT Merovingian Dynasty
1 CONT Born: 584; Died: 629 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Chilperic I King of Neustria
1 CONT 584–613
1 CONT with Fredegonde (584–597) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Dagobert I
1 CONT in Austrasia & Neustria
1 CONT
1 CONT Charibert II
1 CONT in Aquitaine 
1 CONT Vacant
1 CONT Title last held by
1 CONT Clotaire I King of the Franks
1 CONT 613–629 
0 @N6000000002176649528@ NOTE The son of Fredegund, Chlotar (an infant when his father Chilperic I was
1 CONT assassinated in 584) was assured accession by the power of his mother and
1 CONT protection of his uncle, Guntram. Sigebert II, son of Brunhild, allowed
1 CONT Chlotar to seize both the crowns of Austrasia and Burgundy and thus to unite
1 CONT the Frankish lands. Chlotar killed both Sigebert II and his mother, Brunhild.
1 CONT As to governing, he maintained separate mayors of the palace for the three
1 CONT districts over which he ruled, and was succeeded by his son, Dagobert I.
