0 @N6000000003949789660@ NOTE He was installed as AUDOIN King of the Lombards in Hungary in [547] in succession to King Walthari.  The Origo Gentis Langobardorum records that "Auduin" reigned after Walthari, specifying that he brought the Lombards into Pannoni
1 CONC a and, in a later passage, stating that they remained in Pannonia for 43 years.  Byzantium encouraged the Lombards to consolidate their position in Pannonia by granting them the city of Noricum and other strongholds, though it is 
1 CONC reported that they celebrated by raiding Dalmatia and Illyricum.  The war with the Gepids, which started in [547], was settled by a peace treaty imposed by Emperor Justinian in 552, under which the Lombards sent troops to Italy to
1 CONC  help Narses rout the Ostrogoths.  The Historia Langobardorum records that Audoin died in Pannonia
0 @N6000000001353874442@ NOTE 421 in [murdered] Khorasan 1
1 CONT Event: Ruled BET. 399 - 421 Sassanid King of Persia 2 3
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Yasdegerd. Izdegerdes, "made by God."
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Yazdegerd I, son of Shapur III, 399-421, called "the sinner" by the Persians, was a highly intelligent ruler, who tried to emancipate himself from the dominion of the magnates and the Magian priests. He punished the nobles severel
1 CONC y when they attempted oppression; he stopped the persecution of the Christians and granted them their own organization.With the Roman empire he lived in peace and friendship, and is therefore as much praised by the Byzantine autho
1 CONC rs as he is blamed by the Persians. After a reign of 20 years he appears to have been murdered in Khurasan. [Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 23, p. 880, YAZDEGERD
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Yazdegerd I (fl. 5th century), king of the Sasanian Empire (reigned 399-420).
1 CONT Yazdegerd was a highly intelligent ruler who tried to emancipate himself from the dominion of the magnates and of the Magi (a priestly caste serving a number of religions); thus, his reign is viewed differently by Christian and Ma
1 CONC gian sources. Because he stopped the persecutionof the Christians, the Christian writers praise his clemency, but thesources dependent on Magian tradition refer to him as "Yazdegerd the Sinner." He also tried to limit the power of
1 CONC  the nobles, but their resistance finally was answered with severity. He lived in peace and friendship with the Roman Empire and was therefore praised by Byzantine authors. He appears to have been murdered in Khorasan and was succ
1 CONC eeded by one of his sons, Bahram V. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, YAZDEGERD]
