0 @N6000000002187918744@ NOTE Yazdegerd I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd I ("made by God" Izdigerdes), King of Persia (399–421), son of Shapur III of Persia (383–388)
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd I was a highly intelligent ruler, who tried to emancipate himself from the dominion of the magnates and the Magian priests. He punished the nobles severely when they attempted oppression; he granted Christians their own 
1 CONC organization.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the Roman Empire Yazdegerd I lived in peace and friendship, and is therefore as much praised by the Byzantine authors
1 CONT
1 CONT After a reign of twenty-two years he appears to have been murdered in Khorasan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bahram IV Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 399–421 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bahram V
0 @N6000000003051336683@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Yazdegerd I", retrieved 15 Dec 07]
1 CONT Yazdegerd I ("made by God" Izdigerdes) was thirteenth Sassanid King of Persia (399?421), son of Shapur III of Persia (383-388) succeeded Bahram IV as the King of Persia and ruled from 399 to 420.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the early part of his reign, he was entrusted by Arcadius, the Roman Emperor of the East with the care of his son Theodosius. Flattered, he performed his newfound role with utmost sincerity providing him the best possible educa
1 CONC tion and assistance.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd I was a wise, benevolant, and astute ruler. He was also known for his religious tolerance, towards both Christians and Jews. For example, the Talmud (Ksubos, 61a) relates that Ameimar, Rav Ashi, and Mar Zutra would sit i
1 CONC n his court. However, excessive zeal of the Christian bishop of Ctesiphon, Abdaas, provoked a reaction, and when he tried to burn the Great Fire temple of Ctesiphon,Yazdegerd I turned against the Christians. Christian churches all
1 CONC  over the nation were destroyed and Christians driven off from the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year 413, Varahran-Shapur, the King of Armenia died and the crown passed on to a boy of ten. Yazdegerd I released Khusro whom Bahram IV had imprisoned in the Castle of Oblivion and reinstated him upon the throne of Armenia 
1 CONC in order to stabilize the condition. But Khusro died soon afterwards and Armenia fell into chaos once again. Yazdegerd I responded by placing his own son Shapur on the throne of Armenia. When Yazdegerd I was overcome by mortal ill
1 CONC ness in the year 419, Shapur immediately rushed to Ctesiphon to claim the Sassanian throne. But in the battle of succession that followed, Shapur was squarely defeated by Yazdegerd's other son, Bahram, who ascended the throne as B
1 CONC ahram V. Yazdegerd I meanwhile died soon afterwards.
0 @N6000000002188195092@ NOTE Empress Feng Qing
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Feng Qing (??) was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. She was Emperor Xiaowen's first empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Feng Qing was a daughter of Feng Xi (??) the Prince of Changli, who was a brother of the powerful Grand Empress Dowager Feng, the wife of Emperor Wencheng and regent over his son Emperor Xianwen and grandson Emperor Xiaowen. (Thro
1 CONC ugh her father, she was therefore also a great-granddaughter of the Northern Yan emperor Feng Hong.) Her mother was probably Feng Xi's wife Princess Boling. She had two older sisters (by Feng Xi's concubine Lady Chang) who were co
1 CONC ncubines of Emperor Xiaowen -- Feng Run and an unnamed sister, but the unnamed sister died early, and after Feng Run had a major illness, she left the palace and became a Buddhist nun.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 493, Emperor Xiaowen took Feng Qing as his empress. She initially was much favored. However, after Emperor Xiaowen found out that Feng Run had recovered, he took Feng Run back as a concubine. (There is some evidence, but not co
1 CONC nclusive evidence, that Feng Qing might have been involved in her sister's being returned to the palace.) Feng Run, because she was Emperor Xiaowen's concubine earlier and an older sister, refused to submit to Feng Qing, and tried
1 CONC  to undermine Feng Qing's position as an empress, including accusing her of being against Emperor Xiaowen's Sinicization program. In 496, Emperor Xiaowen deposed Feng Qing. Because Feng Qing was virtuous and much interested in Bud
1 CONC dhism, she became a Buddhist nun and remained one for the rest of her life.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Feng (Wencheng) Empress of Northern Wei
1 CONT 493–496 Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Feng Run
1 CONT Empress of China (Northern)
1 CONT 493–496
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Wang Zhenfeng of Liu Song Empress of China (Shandong/Northern Jiangsu)
1 CONT 493–496
0 @N6000000002188195047@ NOTE Emperor Xuanwu of Northern Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Bei) Wei Xuanwudi ((?)????)
1 CONT Family name: Initially Tuoba (??, tuò bá),
1 CONT later Yuan (?, yuán)
1 CONT (changed 496)
1 CONT Given name: Ke (?, kè)
1 CONT Temple name: Shizong (??, shì zong)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Xuanwu (??, xuan wu),
1 CONT literary meaning:
1 CONT "responsible and martial"
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu of Northern Wei ((?)????) (483-515), personal name né Tuoba Ke (???), later Yuan Ke (??), was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. During his reign, Northern Wei appeared, outwardly, to be at its 
1 CONC prime, but there was much political infighting and corruption, particularly by Emperor Xuanwu's uncle Gao Zhao.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu was an avid Buddhist, and during his reign Buddhism effectively became the state religion, as Emperor Xuanwu himself often personally lectured on the Buddhist sutras.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 Early reign
1 CONT     * 3 Late reign
1 CONT     * 4 Burial
1 CONT     * 5 Era names
1 CONT     * 6 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Ke was born in 483, as the second son of Emperor Xiaowen. His mother was Emperor Xiaowen's concubine Consort Gao. (As he was born the same year as his older brother Tuoba Xun, he was probably born just briefly after Tuoba Xu
1 CONC n, whose mother was Consort Lin.) Little is known about his childhood, including whether he was raised by his mother Consort Gao or not. In 496, Emperor Xiaowen changed the name of the imperial clan from Tuoba to Yuan, and thereaf
1 CONC ter he would be known as Yuan Ke.
1 CONT
1 CONT In fall 496, Yuan Xun, who was then crown prince, but who could not endure the hot weather of the capital Luoyang after Emperor Xiaowen moved the capital there from Pingcheng (??, in modern Datong, Shanxi) in 494, plotted to flee 
1 CONC back to Pingcheng with his followers, but his plot was discovered. Emperor Xiaowen deposed him, and in 497 created Yuan Ke crown prince to replace Yuan Xun. (The creation was in Luoyang, but it is unclear whether prior to his crea
1 CONC tion, Yuan Xun was at Luoyang or Pingcheng.) Later that year, Consort Gao, who was in Pingcheng, travelled south to rejoin her son in Luoyang, but she died on the way. Historians generally believe that she was murdered by Emperor 
1 CONC Xiaowen's wife, Empress Feng Run, who wanted to raise Yuan Ke herself. Whether she was actually able to do so is unclear, but after she was discovered to have carried on an affair with her attendant Gao Pusa (???) in 499, she was 
1 CONC put under house arrest, and Emperor Xiaowen ordered Yuan Ke to have no more contact with her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later in 499, Emperor Xiaowen, while on a campaign against rival Southern Qi, grew ill and died. Emperor Xiaowen's brother Yuan Xie the Prince of Pengcheng was put into command of the withdrawing army on an emergency basis, and Yu
1 CONC an Xie kept Emperor Xiaowen's death a secret while summoning Yuan Ke to join the army. Yuan Ke's attendants largely suspected Yuan Xie of wanting to take the throne himself, but Yuan Xie, once he met Yuan Ke, showed great deferenc
1 CONC e to Yuan Ke, convincing Yuan Ke of his loyalty. Yuan Ke, at age 16, then took the throne as Emperor Xuanwu at Luyang (??, in modern Pingdingshan, Henan), before the army could return to Luoyang.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu initially wanted to make Yuan Xie, who was popular and well-regarded, prime minister, but Yuan Xie refused, and was made a provincial governor instead. The governmental affairs were largely in the hands of six offic
1 CONC ials: Emperor Xiaowen's brothers Yuan Xi (??) the Prince of Xianyang and Yuan Xiang (??) the Prince of Beihai, Emperor Xiaowen's cousin Yuan Cheng (??) the Prince of Rencheng, Emperor Xiaowen's distant uncle Yuan Jia (??) the Prin
1 CONC ce of Guangyang, and the officials Wang Su (??) and Song Bian (??), although Yuan Cheng was soon stripped of his post because he falsely arrested Wang on suspicion of treason. By 500, Emperor Xuanwu recalled Yuan Xie to be prime m
1 CONC inister.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu, once he returned to Luoyang, posthumously honored his mother Consort Gao as an empress, and he created his maternal uncles Gao Zhao and Gao Xian (??), as well as his cousin Gao Meng (??), none of wom he had previou
1 CONC sly met, dukes. Gao Zhao, in particular, became increasingly powerful during Emperor Xuanwu's reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 500, with Southern Qi in disarray because of the tyrranical rule of its emperor Xiao Baojuan, Northern Wei annexed the important city of Shouyang (??, in modern Lu'an, Anhui) when the Southern Qi general Pei Shuye (???) surrend
1 CONC ered the city to Northern Wei in fear of adverse actions by Xiao Baojuan. However, Northern Wei did not take further actions when Southern Qi was subsequently thrown into civil war during the rebellions of the generals Cui Huijing
1 CONC  (???) and Xiao Yan.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 501, the general Yu Lie (??) and Yuan Xiang warned Emperor Xuanwu that Yuan Xi was growing corrupt and Yuan Xie was growing too popular, and suggested that they be relieved of their posts. Emperor Xuanwu did so, and formally pe
1 CONC rsonally took over governmental matters, but at his age, he could not actually properly handle governmental affairs himself, so his trusted attendants and Gao Zhao began to become growingly powerful and corrupt. Traditional histor
1 CONC ians generally regard this as the starting point of Northern Wei's decline. Late in 501, Yuan Xi, displeased that his power wa being stripped and fearful that he would be killed, plotted a rebellion to secede with the provinces so
1 CONC uth of the Yellow River. His plot was discovered, however, and he was executed. From this point on, Emperor Xuanwu grew increasingly suspicious of members of the imperial clan.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later in 501, Emperor Xuanwu created Yu Lie's niece, Consort Yu, empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later that year, with Xiao Yan's forces crushing Xiao Baojuan's, Emperor Xuanwu's general Yuan Ying (??) suggested that a major attack be launched against Southern Qi to take advantage of Southern Qi's civil war. However, Emperor 
1 CONC Xuanwu only authorized small scale attacks, which were generally fruitless. Xiao Yan soon defeated Xiao Baojuan and by 502 had overthrown Southern Qi and established Liang Dynasty as its Emperor Wu. The Liang general Chen Bozhi (?
1 CONC ??) subsequently tried to surrender Jiang Province (??, modern Jiangxi and Fujian) to Northern Wei, but Liang forces defeated both Chen and the Northern Wei forces sent to reinforce him, and Chen fled to Northern Wei. For the next
1 CONC  few years, however, there would continually be war between the two rivals, particularly with Northern Wei creating Southern Qi's prince Xiao Baoyin, who fled Southern Qi as Xiao Yan was beginning to kill members of the Southern Q
1 CONC i imperial clan, as the Prince of Qi and announcing that it would help him reestablish Southern Qi.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 504, Xuan Xiang, who had taken over Yuan Xi's posts, was accused by Gao Zhao of corruption. He was demoted to commoner rank, and died soon thereafter. At Gao's suggestion and despite Yuan Xie's opposition, Emperor Xuanwu subseq
1 CONC uently put the imperial princes under heavey guard, effectively putting them under house arrest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, the wars with Liang continued, and while both sides had gains and losses, Northern Wei made a substantial gain in 505 when the Liang general Xiaohou Daoqian (????) surrendered Nanzheng (??, in modern Hanzhong, Shaanxi),
1 CONC  as well as the surrounding region, to Northern Wei, which further annexedd the semi-independent state Chouchi in spring 506. In winter 505, Liang launched a major counterattack on the eastern part of the border, commanded by its 
1 CONC Emperor Wu's brother Xiao Hong (??) the Prince of Linchuan, but with Xiao Hong being an incompetent general, who mishandled a night terror situation in summer 506, while his army was at Luokou (??, in modern Bengbu, Anhui), the Li
1 CONC ang forces collapsed by themselves without engaging Northern Wei forces. Yuan Ying and Xiao Baoyin followed up by attacking the Liang fortress Zhongli (??, in modern Chuzhou, Anhui), but were dealt a crushing defeat by the Liang g
1 CONC eneral Wei Rui (??) in spring 507. The two states largely stopped their military activities against each other after that point.
1 CONT
1 CONT In winter 507, Empress Yu died suddenly, and in early 508, her young son Yuan Chang (??), Emperor Xuanwu's only son by that point, died suddenly as well. Because Gao Zhao was exceeding powerful by that point, and his niece Consort
1 CONC  Gao was Emperor Xuanwu's favorite concubine, it was largely suspected that Gao Zhao and Consort Gao murdered them, but there was no conclusive proof. In 508, Emperor Xuanwu created Consort Gao empress to replace Empress Yu, despi
1 CONC te opposition by Yuan Xie, and from this point on Gao Zhao became resentful of Yuan Xie.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Late reign
1 CONT
1 CONT In fall 508, Emperor Xuanwu's younger brother Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Jingzhao, who had just been demoted by Emperor Xuanwu for corruption, became angry at both his demotion and that his favorite consort Lady Li had been severe
1 CONC ly battered by Empress Yu briefly before Empress Yu's death (because Yuan Yu's wife was Empress Yu's sister but was unfavored by him), declared a rebellion at his provincial post of Xindu (??, in modern Hengshui, Hebei) and procla
1 CONC imed himself emperor. Gao Zhao used this opportunity to falsely accuse Yuan Xie of acting in concert with both Yuan Yu (because Yuan Yu had forced Yuan Xie's uncle Pan Senggu (???) into joining his rebellion) and Liang Dynasty. Em
1 CONC peror Xuanwu believed Gao, and forced Yuan Xie to commit suicide. The populace and the officials greatly mourned Yuan Xie's death, and grew increasingly resentful of Gao. Soon, Yuan Yu's rebellion was defeated, and while Emperor X
1 CONC uanwu contemplated not putting Yuan Yu to death, Yuan Yu was killed on Gao's orders.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 510, Emperor Xuanwu's concubine Consort Hu gave birth to a son, Yuan Xu. Because Emperor Xuanwu had lost multiple sons in childhood by that point, he carefully selected several experienced mothers to serve as Yuan Xu's wet nurs
1 CONC es, and disallowed both Empress Gao and Consort Hu from seeing him. In winter 512, Emperor Xuanwu created Yuan Xu crown prince, and, abolishing the Northern Wei custom that the crown prince's mother must be forced to commit suicid
1 CONC e, he did not force Consort Hu to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT During these years, Northern Wei and Liang continued to have relatively minor border battles, with each side having gains and losses. In 514, however, Emperor Xuanwu commissioned Gao Zhao to launch a major attack against Liang's Y
1 CONC i Province (??, modern Sichuan and Chongqing). Soon thereafter, however, in spring 515, he died suddenly, and Yuan Xu succeeded him (as Emperor Xiaoming). Yuan Cheng, Emperor Xuanwu's brother Yuan Yong (??) the Prince of Gaoyang, 
1 CONC and Yu Lie's son Yu Zhong (??) seized power and, after recalling Gao, put him to death, and Empress Gao was removed. Emperor Xiaoming's mother Consort Hu became empress dowager and regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Burial
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu was buried north of Luoyang. His tomb is now open to the public as part of the Luoyang Ancient Tombs Museum.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Era names
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Jingming (?? jing míng) 500-503
1 CONT     * Zhengshi (?? zhèng shi) 504-508
1 CONT     * Yongping (?? yong píng) 508-512
1 CONT     * Yanchang (?? yán chang) 512-515
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei
1 CONT     * Mother
1 CONT           o Consort Gao (d. 497), posthumously honored as Empress Wenzhao
1 CONT     * Wives
1 CONT           o Empress Yu (created 501, d. 507), mother of Prince Chang
1 CONT           o Empress Gao (created 508, d. 518), mother of Princess Jiande and an unnamed son
1 CONT     * Major Concubines
1 CONT           o Consort Hu, mother of Crown Prince Xu
1 CONT     * Children
1 CONT           o Yuan Chang (??) (b. 506, d. 508)
1 CONT           o Yuan Xu (??), the Crown Prince (created 512), later Emperor Xiaoming of Northern Wei
1 CONT           o Unnamed son, died early
1 CONT           o Princess Taiyuan
1 CONT           o Princess Jiande
1 CONT           o Princess Yangping
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei Emperor of Northern Wei
1 CONT 499-515 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emperor Xiaoming of Northern Wei
0 @N6000000002188195028@ NOTE Yuan Xun
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Xun (??) (483-497), né Tuoba Xun (???, changed 496), courtesy name originally Yuandao (??), then Xuandao (??, changed 496), was a crown prince of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Xun was born in 483, as Emperor Xiaowen's oldest son. His mother was Consort Lin. At the time he was born, Emperor Xiaowen's step-grandmother Grand Empress Dowager Feng was the regent, and she opined that, pursuant to Northe
1 CONC rn Wei's tradition that a crown prince's mother should be forced to commit suicide, Consort Lin, because Tuoba Xun would presumably eventually become crown prince, should be forced to commit suicide. Even though Emperor Xiaowen di
1 CONC d not want to follow that tradition, he did not want to disobey Grand Empress Dowager Feng, and Consort Lin was forced to commit suicide. He did, however, posthumously honor her as Empress Zhen, and buried her at the ancestral tom
1 CONC b of the emperors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Grand Empress Dowager Feng raised Tuoba Xun herself. In 486, when he was three, she formally named him Xun, and also gave him the courtesy name Yuandao. She died in 490, and it is not clear who raised him after that point. In 493,
1 CONC  Emperor Xiaowen created him crown prince. In 494, when Emperor Xiaowen moved the capital from Pingcheng (??, in modern Datong, Shanxi) to Luoyang as a major part of sinicization campaign, Tuoba Xun accompanied his father south. I
1 CONC n 496, when Emperor Xiaowen changed the imperial clan's family name from Tuoba to Yuan, the crown prince's courtesy name was changed from Yuandao to Xuandao.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Xun was described as obese and uninterested in studies, but liked horseriding. Because he could not endure Luoyang's much warmer weather, he often wanted to return to Pingcheng, but did not get a chance to do so until 495, wh
1 CONC en Emperor Xiaowen sent him to Pingcheng to mourn Grand Empress Dowager Feng's brother Feng Xi (??), who was in charge of the old capital. From this point on, whenever Emperor Xiaowen conducted campaigns against rival Southern Qi,
1 CONC  Yuan Xun would often be formally in charge of the capital, although high level officials were actually responsible for important matters. It was also around this time that Emperor Xiaowen considered having him marry Feng Xi's gra
1 CONC nddaughter as his wife, but because she was young, Emperor Xiaowen took for Yuan Xun two concubines first -- Consorts Liu and Zheng, both daughters of his officials. However, on advice of Yuan Xun's teachers, who believed that ove
1 CONC rly engaging in sexual relations would stunt the crown prince's growth and interfere with his studies, Emperor Xiaowen did not permit him to have sexual intercourse with them during the day. (The implication of the passage in ques
1 CONC tion from the Book of Wei appears to suggest that he did not have sexual relations with them at all, but it is not clear; in any case, neither had children.)
1 CONT
1 CONT In fall 496, while Emperor Xiaowen was away from the capital, Yuan Xun, unable to endure the heat in Luoyang, made an impulsive decision to seize horses and flee with his attendants to Pingcheng. His assistant Gao Daoyue (???) tri
1 CONC ed to persuade him not to carry out this action, and Yuan Xun killed him. However, the news leaked, and the general Yuan Yan (??) closed the city gates and prevented the crown prince from leaving. Once Emperor Xiaowen returned to 
1 CONC the capital, he, along with his brother Yuan Xi (??) the Prince of Xianyang, caned Yuan Xun more than 100 times, and then imprisoned him. About one and a half months later, Emperor Xiaowen deposed Yuan Xun and demoted him to commo
1 CONC ner rank, putting him under house arrest with minimal supplies. In spring 497, Emperor Xiaowen created Yuan Xun's oldest younger brother Yuan Ke as crown prince, to replace him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Once deposed, Yuan Xun much regretted his actions. However, the official Li Biao (??), in charge of guarding him, then made a false report to Emperor Xiaowen that Yuan Xun was still plotting with his attendants. In summer 497, Emp
1 CONC eror Xiaowen sent Yuan Xi and the official Xing Luan (??) to give Yuan Xun poison and order him to take it. He was given a commoner's burial near his place of house arrest. His mother Consort Lin was posthumously demoted from empr
1 CONC ess to commoner status as well.
0 @N6000000002188195103@ NOTE Empress Feng Run
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Feng Run (??) (d. 499), formally Empress You (???, literally "the lonely empress") was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. She was the second empress of Emperor Xiaowen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Feng Run was a daughter of Feng Xi (??) the Prince of Changli, who was a brother of the powerful Grand Empress Dowager Feng, the wife of Emperor Wencheng and regent over his son Emperor Xianwen and grandson Emperor Xiaowen. (Throu
1 CONC gh her father, she was therefore also a great-granddaughter of the Northern Yan emperor Feng Hong.) Her mother was Feng Xi's concubine Lady Chang. When she was 13, Grand Empress Dowager Feng selected her and a sister of hers to be
1 CONC  Emperor Xiaowen's concubines. Both were favored by Emperor Xiaowen, but her sister soon died of an illness, and she herself grew ill (may be of smallpox) and was sent back to her home. She subsequently became a Buddhist nun, but 
1 CONC was said to have been sexually immoral during this timespan as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 493, Feng Run's younger sister by a different mother (probably Feng Xi's wife Princess Boling), Feng Qing, became Emperor Xiaowen's empress. Subsequently, Emperor Xiaowen, perhaps through Feng Qing, found out that Feng Run had 
1 CONC recovered from her illness, and took her back into the palace as an imperial consort, with the title Zhaoyi (??). She became Emperor Xiaowen's favorite again. Because Feng Run was Emperor Xiaowen's concubine earlier and was an old
1 CONC er sister, she refused to submit to Feng Qing and tried to undermine Feng Qing's position as an empress, including accusing Feng Qing of disobeying Emperor Xiaowen's Sinicization regime. In 496, Emperor Xiaowen deposed Feng Qing, 
1 CONC and in 497, he created Feng Run empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Because Emperor Xiaowen often spent his time on the frontlines battling rival Southern Qi, he was not often at the palace in the capital Luoyang. Empress Feng therefore carried on an affair with her attendant Gao Pusa (???). With 
1 CONC the eunuch Shuang Meng (??) protecting her, few knew or dared to say anything about the affair, and when her attendant Ju Peng (??) tried to counsel her to stop the affair, she would not, and Ju died in fear and anger. At the same
1 CONC  time, however, Empress Feng tried to force Emperor Xiaowen's sister Princess Pengcheng (who had been widowed after the death of her husband Liu Chengxu (???), to marry her brother Feng Su (??). Emperor Xiaowen approved of the mar
1 CONC riage, Princess Pengcheng was unwilling to marry Feng Su, so she fled out of Luoyang and sought out Emperor Xiaowen on the frontline, revealing to him Empress Feng's affair with Gao. Emperor Xiaowen was initially unwilling to beli
1 CONC eve the accusation and kept it secret, but Empress Feng became nervous and, along with her mother Lady Chang, engaged witches to try to curse Emperor Xiaowen, who was already ill by this time, to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Emperor Xiaowen did not die, and after he returned to Luoyang in 499, he interrogated Gao and Shuang, and both admitted. He then summoned Empress Feng and confronted her with Gao and Shuang's testimony—Æat an interrogatio
1 CONC n that no one else other than himself was at. After he finished the interrogation, he then summoned his brothers Yuan Xie the Prince of Pengcheng and Yuan Xiang (??) the Prince of Beihai, stating to them, "She used to be your sist
1 CONC er-in-law, but treat her now as a passerby, and you need not avoid her." (By traditional Chinese custom, a brother-in-law and a sister-in-law may not sit together or speak with each other.) Emperor Xiaowen then stated, "This woman
1 CONC  wanted to stick a knife in my ribs. Because she is a daughter of Empress Dowager Wenming's clan, I cannot depose her, but I hope that one day she will find her conscience and kill herself. Do not believe that I have any remaining
1 CONC  feelings for her." After the two princes exited, Emperor Xiaowen gave her a final goodbye, indicating that he would not see her again. The concubines still greeted her as empress, but Emperor Xiaowen ordered his crown prince Yuan
1 CONC  Ke not to see her again as well. When Emperor Xiaowen sent eunuchs to give her instructions on certain matters, she rebuked the eunuchs, stating that she was an empress and would not take instructions from eunuchs. In anger, Empe
1 CONC ror Xiaowen sent a cane to her mother Lady Chang, and Lady Chang was forced to cane Empress Feng herself as punishment.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later that year, Emperor Xiaowen grew seriously ill, and he left instructions to Yuan Xie to force Empress Feng to commit suicide after his own death, but to bury her with imperial honors still to avoid shame to the Feng clan. He 
1 CONC then died, and Yuan Xie sent the palace official Bai Zheng (??) to give her poison. Empress Feng refused to drink the poison, stating, "My husband did not make such an order! It is the princess who wants to kill me." Bai seized he
1 CONC r physically and forced poisonous peppers into her mouth, and she died. She was buried with imperial honors with her husband.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Empress Feng Qing Empress of Northern Wei
1 CONT 497–499 Succeeded by
1 CONT Empress Yu
0 @N6000000002188195188@ NOTE Emperor Xianwen of Northern Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Bei) Wei Xianwendi ((?)????)
1 CONT Family name: Tuoba (??; tuò bá)
1 CONT Given name: Hong (?, hóng)
1 CONT Temple name: Xianzu (??, xian zu)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Xianwen (??, xiàn wén),
1 CONT literary meaning:
1 CONT "wise and civil"
1 CONT Tuoba Hong redirects here. For his son, the emperor who later changed the imperial clan name to Yuan, whose personal name is rendered the same way in pinyin, see Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xianwen of Northern Wei ((?)????) (454-476), personal name Tuoba Hong, was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. He was the first emperor in Chinese history who, after retiring in favor of his son Emperor
1 CONC  Xiaowen to become Taishang Huang (retired emperor) in 471, continued to hold onto power until his death in 476 -- when he was killed by his stepmother Empress Dowager Feng over his having executed a lover of hers.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family background
1 CONT     * 2 Reign
1 CONT           o 2.1 Under Yifu Hun's regency
1 CONT           o 2.2 Under Empress Dowager Feng's regency
1 CONT           o 2.3 After assuming imperial authority
1 CONT     * 3 As retired emperor
1 CONT     * 4 Era names
1 CONT     * 5 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family background
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Hong was born in 454, as Emperor Wencheng's oldest son. His mother was Consort Lin, who had previously been captured in war and had become a concubine of Tuoba Ren (???) the Prince of Yongchang, a distant relative of Emperor
1 CONC  Wencheng, who was executed in 453 after having been accused of crimes. After Tuoba Ren's death, she was seized and taken into the palace, where she became Emperor Wencheng's concubine. In 456, Emperor Wencheng created Tuoba Hong 
1 CONC crown prince -- and, at the same time, according to Northern Wei customs, ordered Consort Li to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Under Yifu Hun's regency
1 CONT
1 CONT In 465, Emperor Wencheng died, and the 11-year-old Crown Prince Hong ascended the throne as Emperor Xianwen. Emperor Wencheng's wife Empress Feng was honored as empress dowager, and Emperor Xianwen's deceased mother Consort Li was
1 CONC  posthumously honored as Empress Yuan. Power soon fell into the hands of the official Yifu Hun, who assumed dictatorial powers and killed a large number of other officials, including Yang Baoping (???), Jia Airen (???) the Duke of
1 CONC  Pingyang, and Zhang Tiandu (???), Qiumuling Duohou (?????), Tuoba Yu (???) the Duke of Shunyang, and Emperor Wencheng's prime minister Buliugu Li. However, in 466, Empress Dowager Feng staged a coup and had Yifu arrested and exec
1 CONC uted. She assumed regency herself.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Under Empress Dowager Feng's regency
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Dowager Feng engaged Jia Xiu (??), Gao Yun, and Gao Lü (??) as her assistants in the regency. Later, she also incorporated her brother Feng Xi (??) into the decision-making circle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Dowager Feng was soon presented with a major opportunity to expand Northern Wei territory, as also in 466, rival Liu Song had a major dynastic succession struggle after Emperor Qianfei of Liu Song was assassinated in 465. 
1 CONC Emperor Qianfei's uncle Emperor Ming of Liu Song was declared emperor in the capital Jiankang, while his brother Liu Zixun the Prince of Jin'an was declared emperor in early 466 in Xunyang (??, in modern Jiujiang, Jiangxi). After 
1 CONC Emperor Ming's forces defeated Liu Zixun's and captured and executed Liu Zixun in fall 466, the Liu Song general Xue Andu (???), the governor of Xu Province (??, modern northern Jiangsu and northern Anhui), who had initially decla
1 CONC red for Liu Zixun, was apprehensive that he would be punished by Emperor Ming, and so decided to surrender to Northern Wei, and soon, he was joined by Bi Zhongjing (???) the governor of Yan Province (??, modern western Shandong) a
1 CONC nd Chang Zhenqi (???) the governor of Ru'nan Commandery (??, roughly modern Zhumadian, Henan). Empress Dowager Feng sent the general Weichi Yuan (???) to accept the surrenders of these Liu Song generals and to secure the region ju
1 CONC st north of the Huai River, and Weichi subsequently defeated two attempts by Emperor Ming to recapture those provinces. She also sent the general Murong Baiyao (????) to attack and try to capture Liu Song's Qing (??, modern centra
1 CONC l and eastern Shandong) and Ji (??, modern northwestern Shandong) Provinces, which were cut off from the rest of Liu Song after Xue's defection, and by 469, both provinces fell into Northern Wei hands, and all of the regions north
1 CONC  of the Huai River was by now Northern Wei territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 467, Emperor Xianwen's concubine Consort Li -- a daughter of his uncle Li Hui (??) and therefore his cousin -- bore his oldest child Tuoba Hong (different character than his own), and Empress Dowager Feng personally raised the 
1 CONC young prince. She soon terminated her regency and returned imperial powers to Emperor Xianwen, who was 13 years old at this point.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] After assuming imperial authority
1 CONT
1 CONT As emperor, Emperor Xianwen was described as hardworking and appropriate in his awards and punishments -- in particular, in promoting honest officials and demoting corrupt ones, and it was said that only starting in his reign were
1 CONC  Northern Wei officials praised for being honest. He was also said to be heavily into studies of religions and philosophies, including both Taoism and Buddhism.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 469, Emperor Xianwen created Tuoba Hong crown prince. As Tuoba Hong's mother Consort Li died the same year and was described in traditional histories to be missed by all in the palace, she was probably forced to commit suicide 
1 CONC pursuant to Northern Wei customs.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 470, resentful that Murong Baiyao had flattered Yifu Hun while Yifu was in power, Emperor Xianwen, in an act that appeared to be uncharacteristic for his reign, falsely accused Murong Baiyao of treason and put him and his son M
1 CONC urong Ruyi (????) to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 470, an event would damage Emperor Xianwen's relationship with his stepmother Empress Dowager Feng. Empress Dowager Feng had taken the official Li Yi (??) as her lover. In 470, the official Li Xin (??), who was a close fri
1 CONC end of Li Yi's brother Li Fu (??), was accused of corruption, and Emperor Xianwen became aware of the accusations even though Li Fu tried to suppress the reports. He had known about his stepmother's relationship with Li Yi and, wh
1 CONC ile he had not taken any actions against it at that point, disapproved of it. He sented Li Xin to death, but then informed Li Xin that if he could report on crimes that Li Fu and Li Yi had committed, he would be spared. After init
1 CONC ial reluctance, Li Xin did so, and another man named Fan Biao (??) also did so. Emperor Xianwen then executed Li Fu and Li Xin. Empress Dowager Feng became resentful of Emperor Xianwen after that point.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 471, because of Emperor Xianwen's philosophical interests, he wanted to leave the throne to spend more time on his studies. He wanted to pass his throne to his uncle Tuoba Zitui (????) the Prince of Jingzhao, who was highly pra
1 CONC ised by all officials. He therefore summoned an imperial council to discuss the matter. The officials largely opposed Emperor Xianwen's proposal, and suggested instead that if Emperor Xianwen wanted to leave the throne, he should 
1 CONC pass the throne to Crown Prince Hong. He therefore did so, and the four-year-old crown prince took the throne as Emperor Xiaowen. Emperor Xianwen himself took the title of Taishang Huangdi (retired emperor).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] As retired emperor
1 CONT
1 CONT As retired emperor, however, Emperor Xianwen continued to be in control of the imperial regime, and all important matters were still submitted to him. He spent much time on criminal law matters, and during this time, the criminal 
1 CONC cases generally were paid more attention, and while they took longer to process, the results were considered more just.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 476, still resentful of Emperor Xianwen, Empress Dowager Feng killed him. (Most historians, including Sima Guang, believed that she poisoned him, but another version indicated that Empress Dowager Feng readied assassins who, wh
1 CONC en Emperor Xianwen came to her palace to greet her, seized and smothered him.) She assumed regency over Emperor Xiaowen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Era names
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Tian'an (?? tian an) 466-467
1 CONT     * Huangxing (?? huáng xing) 467-471
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Emperor Wencheng of Northern Wei
1 CONT     * Mother
1 CONT           o Consort Li (forced to commit suicide 456), posthumously honored as Empress Yuan
1 CONT     * Major Concubines[1]
1 CONT           o Consort Li (d. 469[2]), mother of Prince Hong, daughter of Li Hui (??) the Prince of Nan Commandery, posthumously honored as Empress Si
1 CONT           o Consort Feng or Shiben, mother of Prince Xi
1 CONT           o Consort Han or Budahan, mother of Princes Gan and Yong
1 CONT           o Consort Meng, mother of Prince Yu
1 CONT           o Consort Poduolo or Pan, mother of Prince Xie
1 CONT           o Consort Gao, mother of Prince Xiang
1 CONT     * Children
1 CONT           o Tuoba Hong (???), the Crown Prince (created 469), later Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei
1 CONT           o Tuoba Xi (???), the Prince of Xianyang (created 485, forced to commit suicide 501)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Gan (???) (b. 469), initially the Prince of Henan (created 485, later Prince Ling of Zhao Commandery (created 494, d. 499)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Yu (???) (b. 470), Prince Hui of Guangling (created 485, died from injuries inflicted by lover's husband 501)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Yong (???), initially the Prince of Yingchuan (created 485), later Prince Wenmu of Gaoyang (created 494, killed by Erzhu Rong 528)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Xie (???), initially the Prince of Shiping (created 485), later Prince Wuxuan of Pengcheng (created 496, forced to commit suicide 508), posthumously honored as Emperor Wenmu
1 CONT           o Tuoba Xiang (???), Prince Ping of Beihai (created 485, d. 504)
1 CONT           o Princess Changshan
1 CONT           o Princess Leliang
1 CONT           o Princess Pengcheng
1 CONT           o Princess Le'an
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Despite the substantial length of his reign, Emperor Xianwen never created an empress. The reason is unknown.
1 CONT    2. ^ Consort Li was probably forced to commit suicide pursuant to Northern Wei customs as the mother of the crown prince, as it was that year that her son was created crown prince, but historical accounts are not conclusive on 
1 CONC the matter.
1 CONT
