0 @N6000000002187801232@ NOTE Charibert II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Merovingian Dynasty Kings of All the Franks 
1 CONT
1 CONT Charibert II (after 618–8 April 632), a son of Clotaire II and his second wife Sichilde, was briefly king of Aquitaine from 629 to his death, with his capital at Toulouse.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father, Clotaire II, King of the Franks, died in 629, Charibert made a bid for the kingdom of Neustria against his elder half-brother Dagobert I, who had already been king of Austrasia since 623. In the ensuing negotiatio
1 CONC ns, Charibert, a minor, was represented by his uncle Brodulf, the brother of Queen Sichilde. Dagobert had Brodulf killed and ceded the near-independent realm of Aquitaine to Charibert. This agreement was confirmed in 631, when Cha
1 CONC ribert stood godfather to Dagobert's son Sigebert.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charibert's realm included Toulouse, Cahors, Agen, Perigueux, and Saintes, to which he added his possessions in Gascony. Charibert was married to Gisela, the heiress of Amand of Gascony. His fighting force subdued the resistance o
1 CONC f the Basques, until the whole of the Basque Country was under his control.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 632, Charibert died at Blaye, Gironde—possibly assassinated on Dagobert's orders—and soon after that Charibert's infant son Chilperic was also killed. Aquitaine passed again to Dagobert. Both Charibert and his son are buried in
1 CONC  the early Romanesque Basilica of Saint-Romain at Blaye.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charibert's surviving son, Boggis (c.626-c.688), became duke of Aquitaine and father of Saint Hubert, who resigned his worldly claims to his younger brother, who began a line of Merovingian dukes of Aquitaine that lasted until 769
1 CONC , when the last, Hunold, was subdued by Charlemagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Franks (main history of Frankish kingdoms) 
1 CONT List of Frankish Kings 
1 CONT Merovingians 
1 CONT History of France 
1 CONT List of Kings and Dukes of Aquitaine 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Historical Atlas: Kings and Dukes of Aquitaine 
1 CONT Oxford Merovingian Page 
1 CONT Festival Le Raisin d'Or: Blaye 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Further reading
1 CONT E. James The Franks, 1987 
1 CONT I. Wood The Merovingian Kingdoms 450-751 1994 
1 CONT Gregory of Tours' history (translated bt L. Thorpe, 1974 
1 CONT (Fredegar) Wallace-Hadrill, J.M., translator, 1960. The Fourth Book of the Chronicle of Fredegar with its Continuations (Connecticut:Greenwood Press) 
