0 @N6000000002187918764@ NOTE Shapur III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shapur III was King of Persia from 383 to 388. Son of Shapur II (309–379), he was elevated to the throne by the magnates against his uncle, Ardashir II (379–383), and killed by them after a reign of five years. He concluded a trea
1 CONC ty with the Roman Emperor Theodosius I (379–395).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ardashir II Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 383–388 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bahram IV
0 @N6000000003051311350@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Shapur III", retrieved 15 Dec 07]
1 CONT Shapur III was the eleventh Sassanid King of Persia from 383 to 388. Shapur III succeeded his brother Ardashir II in the year 383.
1 CONT
1 CONT Treaty with Rome
1 CONT Negotiations between the Romans and the Persians which had begun in the reign of Ardashir II culminated in a treaty of mutual friendship in the year 384.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to this treaty, Armenia was partitioned between the Romans and the Persians. Therefore two kingdoms were formed, one a vassal of Rome and the other, of Persia. The smaller of these, which comprised the more western distr
1 CONC icts, which was assigned to Rome was committed to the charge of the Arsaces who had been made king by Manuel, the son of the unfortunate Bab, or Para, and the grandson of the Arsaces contemporary with Julian. The larger portion, w
1 CONC hich consisted of the regions lying towards the east, passed under the suzerainty of Persia, and was handed over to an Arsacid, named Chosroes, a Christian. Thus friendly relations were established between Rome and Persia which su
1 CONC rvived for thirty-six years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Memorials of Shapur's Reign
1 CONT Shapur III left behind him a sculptured memorial, which is still to be seen in the vicinity of Kermanshah. It consists of two very similar figures, looking towards each other, and standing in an arched frame. On either side of the
1 CONC  figures are inscriptions in the Old Pahlavi character, whereby we are enabled to identify the individuals represented with the second and the third Shapur. They are identical in form, with the exception that the names in the righ
1 CONC t-hand inscription are "Shapur, Hormizd, Narses," while those in the left-hand one are "Shapur, Shapur, Hormizd." It has been supposed that the right-hand figure was erected by Shapur II and the other afterwards added by Shapur II
1 CONC I; but the unity of the whole sculpture, and its inclusion under a single arch, seem to indicate that it was set up by a single sovereign, and was the fruit of a single conception. If this be so, we must necessarily ascribe it to 
1 CONC the later of the two monarchs commemorated, i.e. to Shapur III, who must be supposed to have possessed more than usual filial piety, since the commemoration of their predecessors upon the throne is very rare among the Sassanians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT Shapur III died in 388, after reigning a little more than five years. He was a man of simple tastes, and was fond of spending his time outdoors in his tent. One version says that, on one such occasion, when he was thus enjoying hi
1 CONC mself, there was a violent hurricane which blew the tent under which he was sitting. The falling tent-pole struck him fatally on his head resulting in his death a few days later. However, though most of his subjects believed in th
1 CONC e authencity of this story there were whispers that he could have been the victim of a conspiracy hatched by his courtiers.
0 @N6000000002188195266@ NOTE Yuan Xie
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Xie (??) (d. 508), né Tuoba Xie (???, changed 496), courtesy name Yanhe (??), formally Prince Wuxuan of Pengcheng (?????), later posthumously honored as Emperor Wenmu (????) with the temple name of Suzu (??), was an imperial 
1 CONC prince of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. He was a son of Emperor Xianwen, and he often assisted his brother Emperor Xiaowen both in military and governmental matters. After Emperor Xiaowen's death, he briefly served as 
1 CONC regent for Emperor Xiaowen's son Emperor Xuanwu. Eventually, due to suspicions and jealousy of Emperor Xuanwu's uncle Gao Zhao, Emperor Xuanwu believed false reports that Yuan Xie was going to rebel, and forced him to commit suici
1 CONC de. He was later posthumously honored as an emperor by his son Emperor Xiaozhuang, although subsequently Emperor Jiemin retracted the honors.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not known when Tuoba Xie was born, but he was a younger brother to Emperor Xiaowen and was younger to all of the six other sons of their father Emperor Xianwen except Tuoba Xiang (???) the Prince of Beihai. His mother was Co
1 CONC nsort Pan, who died shortly after giving birth to him. In 476, his father Emperor Xianwen (who was then retired emperor after having passed the throne to Emperor Xiaowen in 471) died as well, probably having been poisoned by Tuoba
1 CONC  Xie's step-grandmother Empress Dowager Feng, who then assumed regency over Emperor Xiaowen as grand empress dowager. Tuoba Xie was considered studious and well-mannered in his youth, and was much favored by both Emperor Xiaowen a
1 CONC nd their step-grandmother Grand Empress Dowager Feng.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] During Emperor Xiaowen's reign
1 CONT
1 CONT In 485, Emperor Xiaowen created Tuoba Xie the Prince of Shiping. During the years, as Emperor Xiaowen assumed more and more of governing responsibilities himself, he increasingly had Tuoba Xie participate in his decision-making. I
1 CONC n 496, as part of Emperor Xiaowen's sinicization campaign, the entire imperial clan's surname was changed from Tuoba to Yuan, and at the same time, Yuan Xie's title was changed from Prince of Shiping to Prince of Pengcheng. Yuan X
1 CONC ie was apprehensive about having too much power, and several times requested to be relieved of his responsibilities; each time, Emperor Xiaowen refused. Also in 496, Emperor Xiaowen, angry that another brother, Yuan Xi (??) the Pr
1 CONC ince of Xianyang, had married a daughter of a servant as his princess, forcibly ordered all of his brothers to demote their wives to concubine status and marry the daughters of several high level officials, and Yuan Xie was requir
1 CONC ed to marry the daughter of Li Chong (??). (As it is not known conclusively whether Yuan Xie had already married by this point, it is not known whether he was forced to demote his wife; however, the fact that he had a son who was 
1 CONC older than Yuan Shao (??), the first born son of Princess Li, suggested that he had a wife who was demoted.)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 498, when Emperor Xiaowen carried out a major southern campaign against rival Southern Qi, Yuan Xie was one of the key commanding generals, although it is not clear whether he saw combat. Also that year, Emperor Xiaowen made hi
1 CONC m in charge of the imperial clan members' education (presumably of a sinicized education). Later that year, due to the military expenditures, Yuan Xie proposed that he himself donate one-year salary for the war effort; in response
1 CONC , Emperor Xiaowen ordered a general one-third reduction in salary for imperial princes and dukes. That winter, Emperor Xiaowen got extremely ill, and Yuan Xie personally attended to him in his illness, praying to the gods and thei
1 CONC r father Emperor Xianwen, offering to die instead of Emperor Xiaowen. Emperor Xiaowen subsequently recovered from this illness.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was during this illness that Emperor Xiaowen's (and Yuan Xie's) sister the Princess Pengcheng, not willing to marry Feng Su (??) the Duke of Beiping, the brother of Emperor Xiaowen's wife Empress Feng Run, fled out of the capit
1 CONC al Luoyang and joined Emperor Xiaowen and Yuan Xie, informing them that Empress Feng had been carrying on an affair with her attendant Gao Pusa (???). Upon Emperor Xiaowen's return to Luoyang in early 499, he personally interrogat
1 CONC ed Gao and another attendant of Empress Feng, Shuang Meng (??), and then personally interrogated Empress Feng as well. Upon the completion of these interrogations, Emperor Xiaowen summoned Yuan Xie and Yuan Xi, informing them of E
1 CONC mpress Feng's indiscretions and, while not deposing her (because she was a daughter of Grand Empress Dowager Feng's brother Feng Xi (??)), told them that he had no intention for them to continue to treat her as a sister-in-law, an
1 CONC d they did not after this point. Later that year, Emperor Xiaowen became ill again, and he made Yuan Xie his prime minister, with intention that Yuan Xie serve as regent for his son Yuan Ke. It was said that Emperor Xiaowen was hi
1 CONC ghly irritable during his illness and often ordered attendants' deaths, but Yuan Xie would plead on their behalf and have them saved. When Emperor Xiaowen grew sicker still in the summer while on a campaign against Southern Qi's k
1 CONC ey general Chen Xianda (???), he told Yuan Xie that he intended for Yuan Xie to serve as regent, and that Yuan Xie should force Empress Feng to commit suicide after his death. Yuan Xie declined the regency, explaining that this wo
1 CONC uld bring too much suspicion on himself, and Emperor Xiaowen agreed, leaving instructions for Yuan Ke to, once his rule was firm, to allow Yuan Xie to resign his posts. Instead of a sole regency by Yuan Xie, Emperor Xiaowen also, 
1 CONC pursuant to his suggestions, entrusted the crown prince to Yuan Xi, his brother Yuan Xiang (??) the Prince of Beihai, his cousin Yuan Cheng (??) the Prince of Rencheng, his distant uncle Yuan Jia (??) the Prince of Guangyang, and 
1 CONC the officials Wang Su (??) and Song Bian (??). He soon died, while still on the front, and Yuan Xie, after discussing with Yuan Cheng, chose not to announce the emperor's death but instead headed back toward Luoyang while summonin
1 CONC g Yuan Ke to rendezvous with them. They met at Luyang (??, in modern Pingdingshan, Henan), and only then was the death of Emperor Xiaowen announced. Yuan Ke succeeded to the throne as Emperor Xuanwu.
1 CONT
1 CONT During this transition, many people, particularly Emperor Xuanwu's attendants, suspected Yuan Xie of wanting the throne himself, but Yuan Xie handled the situation carefully and treated his nephew with respect due an emperor, and 
1 CONC eventually the suspicion subsided. Meanwhile, pursuant to Emperor Xiaowen's instructions, he sent Yuan Xiang to force Empress Feng to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] During Emperor Xuanwu's reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu, who was 16 at the time of his ascension, initially wanted Yuan Xie to stay as his prime minister, but Yuan Xie repeatedly declined, citing Emperor Xiaowen's agreement for him to leave his posts. Emperor Xuanwu, ins
1 CONC tead, made him the governor of Ding Province (??, modern central Hubei), and initially Yuan Xie declined as well, but Emperor Xuanwu forced him to accept. In 500, when the Southern Qi general Pei Shuye (???) surrendered his city S
1 CONC houyang (??, in modern Lu'an, Anhui) to northern Wei, Emperor Xuanwu sent Yuan Xie and Wang Su to secure Shouyang and to assist Pei. They were able to, and Emperor Xuanwu subsequently rebestowed the prime minister title on Yuan Xi
1 CONC e, but had him take the post of governor of Yang Province (??, modern central Anhui) and defend Shouyang. Soon, he recalled Yuan Xie to the capital to be prime minister again.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 501, over a power conflict between Yuan Xi and the general Yu Lie (??), Yu Lie's son Yu Zhong, a close attendant of Emperor Xuanwu warned Emperor Xuanwu that the princes were too powerful, and that he should assume power himsel
1 CONC f. Yuan Xiang also reported on Yuan Xi's faults and suggested that Yuan Xie was also too powerful. In spring 501, Emperor Xuanwu summoned his princely uncles to the palace under heavy guard, and then relieved them (except Yuan Xia
1 CONC ng) of their posts, and announced that he was assuming power himself. From this point on, however, Emperor Xuanwu's government was dominated by his attendants and his maternal uncle Gao Zhao. In 503, Emperor Xuanwu recalled Yuan X
1 CONC ie to his administration with the honorific title Taishi (??), but the title, while highly honored, carried little power.
1 CONT
1 CONT in 504, Yuan Xiang, who was then powerful, was accused by Gao Zhao of corruption, and he was arrested. He soon died while imprisoned. As a result of this episode, Gao further suggested to Emperor Xuanwu to put the imperial princes
1 CONC  under heavy guard, a suggestion that Yuan Xie objected to but Emperor Xuanwu accepted. It was said that Yuan Xie was often depressed from this point on. Late that year, Emperor Xuanwu made Yuan Xie be in charge of supervising a t
1 CONC eam of officials led by Yuan Fan (??) in revising the empire's laws. In 506, when the official Zhen Chen (??) suggested that the state monopoly on salt be ended, Yuan Xie, along with Xing Luan (??), opposed on account that ending 
1 CONC the monopoly would lead to wastefulness and draining of the treasury, but Emperor Xuanwu approved Zhen's proposal anyway.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the years went by, Gao Zhao grew increasingly powerful, particularly more so after his niece Consort Gao became a favorite consort of Emperor Xuanwu. After Emperor Xuanwu's first wife, Empress Yu died in 507, Emperor Xuanwu wan
1 CONC ted to make Consort Gao empress. Yuan Xie opposed, but Emperor Xuanwu created her empress in 508 anyway. Gao Zhao thereafter despised Yuan Xie. When Emperor Xuanwu's brother Yuan Yu (??) the Prince of Jingzhao rebelled in fall 508
1 CONC , Yuan Yu seized Yuan Xie's maternal uncle Pan Senggu (???) and forced Pan to join his rebellion, and Gao Zhao used this incident to falsely accuse Yuan Xie of conspiring with Yuan Yu and rival Liang Dynasty (which had replaced So
1 CONC uthern Qi). Yuan Xie's attendants Wei Yan (??) and Gao Zuzhen (???), wanting to please Gao Zhao, testified against Yuan Xie, and Emperor Xuanwu believed him. Later that year, Emperor Xuanwu held a feast and invited the princes and
1 CONC  Gao Zhao to attend -- requiring Yuan Xie to do so despite the fact that his wife Princess Li was due to give birth. After the feast, the princes were directed to bedrooms in the palace to spend the night. That night, Emperor Xuan
1 CONC wu sent the guard commander Yuan Zhen (??) to take poisoned wine to Yuan Xie, ordering him to commit suicide. Yuan Xie initially refused, wanting to make one last appeal to Emperor Xuanwu, but Yuan Zhen's soldiers battered his rib
1 CONC s until he complied. Once he drank the wine, the soldiers killed him even before the poison could take effect. His body was wrapped in blankets and returned to his mansion, under the pretense that he had died from alcohol poisonin
1 CONC g, although quickly the populace came to believe that Gao Zhao had killed him. Emperor Xuanwu outwardly mourned Yuan Xie and posthumously honored him.
0 @N6000000002188195249@ NOTE Yuan Yong
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Yong (??) (d. 528), né Tuoba Yong (???), courtesy name Simu (??), formally Prince Wenmu of Gaoyang (?????), was an imperial prince of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. He was very powerful during the reign of his gran
1 CONC dnephew Emperor Xiaoming, and by corrupt means grew very rich. This, however, drew resentment from the populace, and after Emperor Xiaoming's death in 528 and the subsequent overthrowing of Emperor Xiaoming's mother Empress Dowage
1 CONC r Hu by the general Erzhu Rong, Erzhu had him and over 2,000 other officials slaughtered at Heyin (??, in modern Luoyang, Henan).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 During Emperor Xuanwu's reign
1 CONT     * 3 During Emperor Xiaoming's reign
1 CONT     * 4 Death
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not known when Tuoba Yong was born; what is known is that he was the sixth of seven sons of Emperor Xianwen, and that his mother was Emperor Xianwen's concubine Consort Han, who was also the mother of one of his older brothe
1 CONC rs, Tuoba Gan (???). Emperor Xianwen yielded the throne to Tuoba Yong's oldest brother, Emperor Xiaowen, in 471, and died subsequently in 476, probably murdered by Tuoba Yong's stepgrandmother, Empress Dowager Feng, who then proce
1 CONC eded to assume regency over Emperor Xiaowen. Emperor Xiaowen liked Tuoba Yong for his lack of inhibitions, and while Yuan Yong did not display much talent while young, he believed that Yuan Yong might show those talents later. In 
1 CONC 485, Tuoba Yong was created the Prince of Yingchuan. In 494, when Emperor Xiaowen moved the capital from Pingcheng (??, in modern Datong, Shanxi), Tuoba Yong's title was changed to the Prince of Gaoyang. In 496, when Emperor Xiaow
1 CONC en changed the name of the imperial clan from Tuoba to Yuan, Tuoba Yong's name was changed to Yuan Yong. Subsequently, when Emperor Xiaowen conducted his campaigns against rival Southern Qi, Yuan Yong was put in charge of the impo
1 CONC rtant Xiang Province (??, roughly modern Handan, Hebei), but he, unlike most of his other brothers, was not given any greater authority during Emperor Xiaowen's reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] During Emperor Xuanwu's reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Yuan Yong was honored, but not particularly powerful, after Emperor Xiaowen's death in 499 and succession by his oldest son Emperor Xuanwu, unlike his brothers Yuan Xi (??) the Prince of Xianyang, Yuan Xie the Prince of Pengcheng,
1 CONC  and Yuan Xiang (??) the Prince of Beihai, each of whom received substantial powers. After Yuan Yong served a further stint as the governor of Ji Province (??, modern central Hebei), Emperor Xuanwu recalled him to the capital. He 
1 CONC participated in Yuan Xiang's corruption trial in 504. Whenever Emperor Xuanwu visited Yuan Yong's mansion, Emperor Xuanwu would pay proper respect to him as an uncle, rather than allowing Yuan Yong to bow to him as a subject to an
1 CONC  emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] During Emperor Xiaoming's reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanwu died suddenly in 515. The officials Yu Zhong and Cui Guang (??) quickly proclaimed Emperor Xuanwu's young crown prince Yuan Xu emperor (as Emperor Xiaoming) and seized power away from Emperor Xuanwu's wife Empress G
1 CONC ao, whose uncle Gao Zhao, who was also Emperor Xuanwu's uncle, was extremely powerful. In order to counteract Gao Zhao, Yu and Cui forced Empress Gao to yield regent powers to Yuan Yong and Emperor Xiaowen's cousin Yuan Cheng (??)
1 CONC  the Prince of Rencheng, and then, Yu and Yuan Yong ambushed Gao Zhao and killed him. Empress Gao was also removed and replaced as empress dowager by Emperor Xiaoming's mother Consort Hu.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Yuan Yong and Yuan Cheng were titular regents, real regency powers were in Yu's hands, and Yu quickly grew exceedingly arrogant and autocratic. The officials Pei Zhi (??) and Guo Zuo (??) secretly suggested to Yuan Yong t
1 CONC o have Yu's power stripped. When Yu found out about this, he falsely accused Pei and Guo of crimes, and they were put to death. Yu also wanted to kill Yuan Yong, but Cui resisted, so Yuan Yong was only removed from his posts. Late
1 CONC r in 515, however, Empress Dowager Hu assumed regency powers and quickly stripped Yu of most of his posts, making Yu the governor of Ji Province. As soon as Yu left the capital, most of his acts were reversed, and Yuan Yong accuse
1 CONC d him of crimes and asked that he be punished. However, Empress Dowager Hu, because Yu had saved her life before, refused to act against Yu, although she recalled Yuan Yong back to the administration to serve in high capacity.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Empress Dowager Hu's regency, she tolerated officials' corruption and wealth-gathering, and often added to the wealth-gathering herself by awarding them money and luxuries out of the imperial treasury. Yuan Yong became exce
1 CONC edingly rich at this time, and historians described his wealth as being so great as like a nation's treasury, and he had 6,000 male servants and 500 female servants. While two other princes, Yuan Chen (??) the Prince of Hejian and
1 CONC  Yuan Rong (??) the Prince of Zhangwu tried to compete with him, they could not, and it was commonly recognized that Yuan Yong was the richest official at the time. This type of corruption among high level officials had a corrosiv
1 CONC e effect on Northern Wei's rule, and it was about this time that there began to be large-scale agrarian revolts throughout the empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 520, Empress Dowager Hu's brother-in-law, the general Yuan Cha, along with the eunuch Liu Teng (??) and the palace attendant Hou Gang (??), carried out a coup against her, putting her under house arrest. Yuan Cha, however, was 
1 CONC respectful of Yuan Yong, and he remained in a position of power, although Yuan Cha was effectively the regent. In 525, Yuan Yong plotted with both Empress Dowager Hu and Emperor Xiaoming to restore Empress Dowager Hu, and later th
1 CONC at year she seized power back from Yuan Cha and forced Yuan Cha to commit suicide. Yuan Yong, as one who participated in her plan, continued in his position of power.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT In 528, with Empress Dowager Hu and Emperor Xiaoming in a conflict after Emperor Xiaoming wanted to put Empress Dowager Hu's lover Zheng Yan (??) to death by conspiring with the general Erzhu Rong, Empress Dowager Hu poisoned Empe
1 CONC ror Xiaoming and put the young child Yuan Zhao, a great-grandson of Emperor Xiaowen from a collateral line, on the throne. Erzhu refused to recognize Yuan Zhao's imperial authority and advanced on Luoyang, quickly capturing it. He
1 CONC  made Yuan Xie's son Yuan Ziyou emperor (as Emperor Xiaozhuang) and threw Empress Dowager Hu and Yuan Zhao into the Yellow River to drown.
1 CONT
1 CONT Erzhu then believed that, in order to show his might, he needed to massacre the high level officials, whom the people viewed as corrupt. Under guise that they were needed to attend Emperor Xiaozhuang in sacrificing to heaven, he h
1 CONC ad them, led by Yuan Yong, report to Heyin, near Luoyang. Then, he had his cavalry surround them and massacre them. Erzhu would later regret this action, and he had Yuan Yong and the other officials killed buried with honor.
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuan_Yong"
1 CONT
1 CONT Categories: Northern Wei | 528 deaths
0 @N6000000002188195323@ NOTE Emperor Wencheng of Northern Wei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT (Bei) Wei Wenchengdi ((?)????)
1 CONT Family name: Tuoba (??; tuò bá)
1 CONT Given name: Jun (?, jùn)
1 CONT Temple name: Gaozong (??, gao zong)
1 CONT Posthumous name: Wencheng (??, wén chéng),
1 CONT literary meaning:
1 CONT "civil and successful"
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Wencheng of Northern Wei ((?)????) (440[1]-465), personal name Tuoba Jun (???), was an emperor of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei. He became emperor in the aftermaths of the eunuch Zong Ai's assassination of his g
1 CONC randfather Emperor Taiwu and uncle Tuoba Yu, and he was generally described by historians as a ruler who sought foremost to allow his people to rest after his grandfather's expansionist policies and extensive campaigns, who also r
1 CONC eformed the laws to become more lenient.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Background
1 CONT     * 2 Early reign
1 CONT     * 3 Late reign
1 CONT     * 4 Notes
1 CONT     * 5 Era names
1 CONT     * 6 Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Tuoba Jun was born in 440, when his father Tuoba Huang was the crown prince of his grandfather, Emperor Taiwu. (Some historical sources give his birthdate as 448, a date that appears rather unlikely because that would make him onl
1 CONC y six years older than his son, Emperor Xianwen.) His mother Consort Yujiulü was the sister of the general Yujiulü Pi (????), who had been a member of the Rouran royal house but who had surrendered to Northern Wei. When he was lit
1 CONC tle, he was much favored by his grandfather Emperor Taiwu, and often accompanied Emperor Taiwu, even on military campaigns. While he carried no official titles, he was known as the Prime Imperial Grandson (???). Around the new yea
1 CONC r 452, after his father Tuoba Huang had died in 451 after falling ill over his fear of false accusations by the eunuch Zong Ai, Emperor Taiwu created him the Prince of Gaoyang, but then reconsidered, believing that a princely titl
1 CONC e was inappropriate for his oldest grandson, and therefore cancelled the title -- signifying strongly that he intended for Tuoba Jun to succeed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Zong Ai, in fear that Emperor Taiwu would punish him over his false accusations against Crown Prince Huang, assassinated Emperor Taiwu in spring 452, and, skipping over Tuoba Jun, made Tuoba Huang's younger brother Tuoba 
1 CONC Yu the Prince of Nan'an emperor. Zong controlled all important matters of state, and when Tuoba Yu, displeased about Zong's arrogance, tried to strip him of power in fall 452, Zong assassinated him as well. The officials Dugu Ni (
1 CONC ???), Yuan He, Baba Kehou (????), and Buliugu Li, however, then overthrew Zong and executed him, making Tuoba Jun emperor at the age of 12, as Emperor Wencheng.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Immediately following Emperor Wencheng's taking the throne, it appeared that the high level officials began internecine struggles against each other, as for several years officials would be put into honored positions, only to be e
1 CONC xecuted days or months later. These included senior officials held over from Emperor Taiwu's administration as well as those who had accomplished much in putting Emperor Wencheng on the throne, and included:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Tuoba Shoule (????), chief commander (winter 452)
1 CONT     * Baba Kehou, a major participant in the coup bringing Emperor Wencheng to the throne (winter 452)
1 CONT     * Tuxi Bi (???), prime minister under Tuoba Yu (winter 452)
1 CONT     * Zhang Li (??), chief commander under Tuoba Yu (winter 452)
1 CONT     * Tuoba Zhouniu (????) the Prince of Leling, chief commander (winter 452)
1 CONT     * Duguhun Yuanbao (?????) the Prince of Jingzhao (spring 453)
1 CONT     * Tuoba Chong (???) the Prince of Jianning, Emperor Wencheng's granduncle, and his son Tuoba Li (???) the Prince of Ji'nan (spring 453)
1 CONT     * Yujiulü Ruowen (?????) the Prince of Puyang (fall 453)
1 CONT     * Tuoba Ren (???) the Prince of Yongchang, Emperor Wencheng's distant uncle (fall 453)
1 CONT     * Tuoba Ba (???) the Prince of Leping, Emperor Wencheng's distant uncle (spring 455)
1 CONT
1 CONT It appeared that Yuan He and Buliugu Li were the key victors out of these struggles, for not only did they not suffer during the infighting, but became exceedingly honored late in Emperor Wencheng's reign. Both they and Dugu Ni we
1 CONC re created princes. Whether Emperor Wencheng himself was involved in these internecine struggles is unclear, although the edicts were issued in his name. Weichi Juan (???), already a decorated general during Emperor Taiwu's reign,
1 CONC  also became powerful.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another characteristic of Emperor Wencheng's reign was that he often toured the provinces throughout his empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT In winter 452, Emperor Wencheng's mother Consort Yujiulü died, and he subsequently posthumously honored his parents as emperor and empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around the new year 453, Emperor Wencheng, a Buddhist, officially ended the prohibitions against Buddhism that Emperor Taiwu had instituted in 445 (which became known as the first of Three Disasters of Wu), and he personally perfo
1 CONC rmed tonsure on five monks. However, as per the traditions instituted by Emperor Taiwu, he also publicly received Taoist amulets in spring 454.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spring 453, Emperor Wencheng honored his wet nurse Lady Chang as empress dowager, and treated her brothers as if they were his biological uncles.
1 CONT
1 CONT In fall 454, Emperor Wencheng's concubine Consort Li gave birth to his oldest son Tuoba Hong. In 456, he created another imperial consort, Consort Feng, empress and created Tuoba Hong crown prince -- and then, pursuant to Northern
1 CONC  Wei tradition, ordered Tuoba Hong's mother Consort Li to commit suicide.
1 CONT
1 CONT In winter 458, Emepror Wencheng launched a major attack against Rouran, but considered abandoningit when his troops encountered a snow storm. At Weichi Juan's urging (arguing that a withdrawal would unduly signal weakness to Roura
1 CONC n), however, Emperor Wencheng continued, and while he was not able to deal a major defeat to Rouran's Chuluo Khan Yujiulü Tuhezhen (??????), a number of Rouran tribesmen surrendered. Meanwhile, in his absence, his generals Feng Ch
1 CONC iwen (???) and Pi Baozi (???) engaged Liu Song forces near the Ji River (??, the route of which had been overtaken by the Yellow River in modern times), with inconclusive results.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Late reign
1 CONT
1 CONT In summer 460, Emperor Wencheng's wet nurse Empress Dowager Chang died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in summer 460, based on advise given by Cao An (??) the Marquess of Dingyang, Emperor Wencheng launched a major attack on Tuyuhun's khan Murong Shiyin (????), who had drawn Northern Wei officials' ire by accepting commissions
1 CONC  by both Northern Wei and Liu Song and by showing off his wealth. The commanding generals were Emperor Wencheng's brother Tuoba Xincheng (????) the Prince of Yangping and Li Hui (??) the Duke of Nan Commandery. However, while the 
1 CONC armies were able to capture a large number of livestock, Murong Shiyin received advance warning and was able to flee into the mountains, and the armies also suffered from illnesses.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 464, while on one of his tours to various parts of the empire, Emperor Wencheng personally attended a Gaoche tribal ceremony to sacrifice to the heavens, and the Gaoche people were very pleased.
1 CONT
1 CONT In summer 465, Emperor Wencheng died and was succeeded by his son Tuoba Hong (as Emperor Xianwen). Power soon fell into the hands of the official Yifu Hun, who tried to monopolize power and executed a number of other officials, in
1 CONC cluding Buliugu Li, but in 466 was in turn ambushed by Emperor Wencheng's wife Empress Dowager Feng.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Historical resources also inconsistently sometimes give his birthdate as 448; however, that would make him only six years older than his son Emperor Xianwen, and therefore appears to be rather unlikely.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Era names
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Xing'an (?? xing an) 452-454
1 CONT     * Xingguang (?? xing guang) 454-455
1 CONT     * Tai'an (?? tài an) 455-459
1 CONT     * Heping (?? hé píng) 460-465
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Personal information
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Father
1 CONT           o Tuoba Huang, Crown Prince Jingmu, posthumously honored as Emperor Jingmu, son of Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei
1 CONT     * Mother
1 CONT           o Consort Yujiulü, sister of Yujiulü Pi (????) the Prince of Hedong, posthumously honored as Empress Gong
1 CONT     * Wife
1 CONT           o Empress Feng (created 456), daughter of Feng Lang (??) the Duke of Liaoxi, son of Feng Hong the emperor of Northern Yan
1 CONT     * Major Concubines
1 CONT           o Consort Li, mother of Crown Prince Hong (forced to commit suicide 456)
1 CONT           o Consort Li, mother of Prince Changle
1 CONT           o Consort Cao, mother of Prince Lüe
1 CONT           o Consort Juqu, mother of Prince Jian
1 CONT           o Consort Yifu, mother of Prince Ruo
1 CONT           o Consort Yue, mother of Prince Meng
1 CONT           o Consort Xuan, mother of Prince Anping
1 CONT     * Children
1 CONT           o Tuoba Hong (???), the Crown Prince (created 456), later Emperor Xianwen of Northern Wei
1 CONT           o Tuboa Changle (????), initially the Prince of Jianchang (created 470), later Prince Li of Anle (created 475, forced to commit suicide 479)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Lüe (???), Prince Zhuang of Guangchuan (created 472, d. 480)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Jian (???), Prince Shun of Qi Commandery (created 481, d. 499)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Ruo (???), died early, posthumously created Prince Xiao of Hejian
1 CONT           o Tuoba Meng (???), Prince Kuang of Anfeng (created 481, d. 489)
1 CONT           o Tuoba Anping (????), Prince Ai of Han, died early
1 CONT           o Princess Dunqiu
1 CONT           o Princess Wuyi
1 CONT           o Princess Jianxing
1 CONT           o Princess Pingyang
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Tuoba Yu Emperor of Northern Wei
1 CONT 452-465 Succeeded by
1 CONT Emperor Xianwen of Northern Wei
0 @N6000000002187576196@ NOTE Arcadius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Arcadius (377/378–May 1, 408) was Roman Emperor in the Eastern half of the Roman Empire from 395 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arcadius was the elder son of Theodosius I and Aelia Flaccilla, and brother of Honorius, who would become a Western Roman Emperor. His father declared him an Augustus in January, 383. His younger brother was also declared an Augus
1 CONC tus in 393.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Emperors, Honorius was under the control of the Romanized Vandal magister militum Flavius Stilicho while Arcadius was dominated by one of his ministers, Rufinus. Stilicho is alleged by some to have wanted control of both empero
1 CONC rs, and is supposed to have had Rufinus assassinated by Gothic mercenaries in 395, but definite proof of these allegations is lacking. In any case, Arcadius' new advisor Eutropius simply took Rufinus' place as the power behind the
1 CONC  Eastern imperial throne. Arcadius was also dominated by his wife Aelia Eudoxia, who convinced her husband to dismiss Eutropius in 399. Eudoxia was strongly opposed by John Chrysostom, the Patriarch of Constantinople, who felt tha
1 CONC t she had used her family's wealth to gain control over the emperor. Eudoxia used her influence to have Chrysostom deposed in 404, but she died later that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arcadius was dominated for the rest of his rule by Anthemius, the Praetorian Prefect, who made peace with Stilicho in the West. Arcadius himself was more concerned with appearing to be a pious Christian than he was with political 
1 CONC or military matters, and he died, only nominally in control of his empire, in 408.
