0 @N6000000002187918788@ NOTE Shapur II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shapur II (sometimes called The Great) was ruler of the Sassanid Empire from 309 to 379. During his long reign, the Sassanid Empire saw its first golden era since the reign of Shapur I (241–272).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early childhood
1 CONT     * 2 Conquests
1 CONT     * 3 Contributions
1 CONT     * 4 See also
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Early childhood
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Hormizd II (302–309) died, the Persian magnates killed his eldest son, blinded the second, and imprisoned the third (Hormizd, who afterwards escaped to the Roman Empire). The throne was reserved for the unborn child of o
1 CONC ne of the wives of Hormizd II, who was Jewish. It is said that Shapur II may have been the only king in history to been crowned in utero: the crown was placed upon his mother's belly. This child, named Shapur, was therefore born k
1 CONC ing; the government was conducted by his mother and the magnates. But when Shapur II came of age, he turned out to be one of the greatest monarchs of the dynasty.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquests
1 CONT Bust of Shapur II.
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Bust of Shapur II.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 337, just before the death of Constantine I (324–337), Shapur II broke the peace concluded in 297 between Narseh (293–302) and Emperor Diocletian (284–305), which had been observed for forty years. A twenty-six year conflict (3
1 CONC 37–363) began in two series of wars, the first from 337 to 350. Shapur II attempted with varying success to conquer the great fortresses of Roman Mesopotamia: Singara, Nisibis (which he invaded three times in vain), and Amida (Dia
1 CONC rbekr).
1 CONT
1 CONT The Roman Emperor Constantius II (353–361) was always beaten in the field. Nevertheless Shapur II made scarcely any progress; the military power of his kingdom was not sufficient for a lasting occupation of the conquered territory
1 CONC . At the same time he was attacked in the east by nomad tribes, among whom the Chionites are named. After a prolonged struggle (353–358) they were forced to conclude a peace, and their king, Grumbates, accompanied Shapur II in the
1 CONC  war against the Romans.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 358 Shapur II was ready for his second series of wars against Rome, which met with much more success. In 359, Shapur II conquered Amida after a siege of seventy-three days, and he took Singara and some other fortresses in the n
1 CONC ext year (360). In 363 the Emperor Julian (361–363), at the head of a strong army, advanced to Shapure's capital at Ctesiphon and defeated a superior Sassanid army in the Battle of Ctesiphon, but was killed during his retreat. His
1 CONC  successor Jovian (363–364) made an ignominious peace, by which the districts on the Tigris and Nisibis (a total of five Roman provinces) were given to the Persians, and the Romans promised to interfere no more in Armenia. The gre
1 CONC at success is represented in the rock-sculptures near the town Bishapur in Persis (Stolze, Persepolis, p. 141); under the hoofs of the king's horse lies the body of an enemy, probably Julian, and a suppliant Roman, the Emperor Jov
1 CONC ian, asks for peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shapur II now invaded Armenia, where he took King Arsaces III, the faithful ally of the Romans, prisoner by treachery and forced him to commit suicide. He then attempted to introduce Zoroastrian orthodoxy into Armenia. However, th
1 CONC e Armenian nobles resisted him successfully, secretly supported by the Romans, who sent King Pap, the son of Arsaces III, into Armenia. The war with Rome threatened to break out again, but Valens sacrificed Pap, arranging for his 
1 CONC assassination in Tarsus, where he had taken refuge (374). Shapur II had conducted great hosts of captives from the Roman territory into his dominions, most of whom were settled in Susiana. Here he rebuilt Susa, after having killed
1 CONC  the city's rebellious inhabitants.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his death in 379 the Persian Empire was stronger than ever before, considerably larger than when he came to the throne, the eastern enemies were pacified and Persia had gained control over Armenia.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Contributions
1 CONT
1 CONT Under Shapur II's reign the collection of the Avesta was completed, heresy and apostasy punished, and the Christians persecuted. This was a reaction against the Christianization of the Roman Empire by Constantine I. He was success
1 CONC ful in the east, and the great town Nishapur in Khorasan (eastern Parthia) was founded by him. He founded some other towns.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Raba
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hormizd II Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 309–379 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ardashir II
0 @N6000000001353874460@ NOTE Shapur II, byname SHAPUR THE GREAT (b. AD 309--d. 379), 10th king of the Sasanian Empire of Persia, who withstood Roman strength by astute military strategy and diplomacy and brought the empire to the zenith of its power.
1 CONT Early life and accession.
1 CONT The name Shapur, meaning "son of a king," was common in the Sasanian period and was often given to sons other than princes. Numerical designations were not used to distinguish kings of the same name; instead, the family genealogy 
1 CONC was cited. Thus, in one inscription, Shapur styles himself,
1 CONT "the Mazdah-worshipping god Shapur, king of kings of Iran and non-Iran, who is a scion of the Gods, the son of Hormizd (Ormizd II), the grandson of Narses."
1 CONT According to tradition, his father died before Shapur was born, and the child was proclaimed king by the Persian nobility at his birth in 309, in preference to his brothers. (At the death of Hormizd II, the Persian nobles killed t
1 CONC he eldest son, blinded the second and imprisoned the third. The throne was reserved for the unborn child of one of the wives of Hormizd. This was Shapur, who was thus born king. [Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 20, p. 473
1 CONC , SHAPUR II]) After a regency, he apparently took the realm into his own hands in 325 at the age of 16.
1 CONT A contemporary account describes his appearance and courage in battle:
1 CONT "And he himself, mounted on his charger, and being taller than the rest, led his whole army, wearing instead of a crown a golden figure ofa ram's head inlaid with jewels; being also splendid from the retinueof men of high rank and
1 CONC  of different nations which followed him . . .He rode up to the gates [of Amida]; escorted by the cohort of his royal guard; and while pushing on more boldly, so that his very features might be plainly recognized, his ornaments ma
1 CONC de him such a mark for arrows and other missiles, that he would have been slain, if the dust had not hindered the sight of those who were shooting at him; so that after a part of his robe had been cut off by a blow of a javelin, h
1 CONC e escaped to cause vast slaughter at a future time."
1 CONT Persecution of Christians.
1 CONT In 337 Shapur sent his forces across the Tigris River, the uneasy frontier, to recover Armenia and Mesopotamia, which his predecessors had lost to the Romans. Until 350 conflict raged in northern Mesopotamia, with neither side a c
1 CONC lear-cut victor. Shortly after 337, Shapur took an important policy decision. Although the state religion of the Sasanian Empire was Mazdaism (Zoroastrianism), Christianity flourished within its boundaries. The Roman emperor Const
1 CONC antine the Great had grantedtoleration to Christians in 313. With the subsequent Christianizationof the empire, Shapur, mistrustful of a potential force of a fifth column at home while he was engaged abroad, ordered the persecutio
1 CONC n and forcible conversion of the Christians; this policy was in force throughout his reign.
1 CONT In 358 he was ready for a second encounter with Rome and sent an ambassador to the emperor Constantius II, bearing presents and a letter wrapped in white silk. This letter read, in part,
1 CONT "I, Sapor, king of kings, partner of the stars, brother of the sun and the moon, to Constantius Caesar my brother send much greeting . . .Because . . . the language of truth ought to be unrestrained and free, and because men in th
1 CONC e highest rank ought only to say what they mean, I will reduce my propositions into a few words . . . Even your own ancient records bear witness that my ancestors possessed all the country up to the Strymon and the frontier of Mac
1 CONC edonia. And these lands itis fitting that I who (not to speak arrogantly) am superior to those ancient kings in magnificence, and in all eminent virtues, should now reclaim. But I am at all times thoughtful to remember that, from 
1 CONC my earliest youth, I have never done anything to repent of."
1 CONT When Constantius politely refused to hand over these lands, Shapur marched into northern Mesopotamia, this time with marked success. In 363, however, the emperor Julian led a huge army into Persia, creating havoc and advancing to 
1 CONC the very gates of Ctesiphon on the Tigris, a major Sasanian city. Julian was mortally wounded in a skirmish, and his successor, Jovian, was compelled to accept an ignominious 30 years' truce and surrender of five Roman provinces.
1 CONT Conquest of Armenia.
1 CONT Shapur then turned his attention to Armenia. He defeated its pro-Roman ruler and attempted to force Zoroastrianism on the country. The Romans, however, regained their influence in Armenia, and a precarious balance between Romans a
1 CONC nd Persians existed from about 374. For five years, Armenia was weakened by internecine strife, and one of Shapur's last acts was to win it over by discreet gifts. Shapur's death in 379 left Persia with its territory considerably 
1 CONC augmented: he had pacified the eastern frontier and gained control over Armenia; the Persian Empire had never been stronger. But war with Rome had become habitual and eventually undermined the strength of the Sasanians.
1 CONT As did the other great rulers of his line, Shapur considered himself an heir of the great Achaemenid Empire and strove to renew its glory. Indeed, his letter to Constantius contains a distant echo of the wording of the inscription
1 CONC s of Darius I the Great, the Achaemenid ruler, when he writes of his devotion to truth and righteousness. Shapur was called a god, and he lived up to this exalted status, becoming irascible, even violent, when individuals summoned
1 CONC  to his presence appeared topay insufficient respect to his awesome majesty.
1 CONT The figure of Shapur survives. A large silver plate has a scene in relief that shows him hunting lions with bow and arrow, and countless silver coins portray his face in profile. At Bishapur in southwestern Iran, a tremendous rock
1 CONC -cut relief depicts him seated on a throne and witnessing a triumph of his army: in the top row he is flanked by noblesof the court, and the lower row contains soldiers who present captives and trophies of victory. [Encyclopaedia 
1 CONC Britannica CD '97, SHAPUR]
