0 @N6000000002188311737@ NOTE Ecgberht of Kent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgberht, or Egbert (d. July 4, 673) was a King of Kent who ruled from 664 to 673, succeeding his father Eorcenberht.
1 CONT
1 CONT He may have still been a child when he became king following his father's death on July 14, 664, because his mother Seaxburh was recorded as having been regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgberht's court seems to have had many diplomatic and ecclesiastic contacts. He hosted Wilfrid and Benedict Biscop, and provided escorts to Theodore and Hadrian for their travels in Gaul.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Mildrith legend reports that he had his cousins Aethelred and Aethelberht (sons of his uncle Eormenred) killed; this may reflect a dynastic struggle that ended in the success of Eorcenberht's line.
1 CONT
1 CONT A charter records his patronage of the monastery at Chertsey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ecgberht was succeeded by his brother Hlothhere, who was in turn succeeded by Ecgberht's son Eadric and still later by his other son Wihtred.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT List of monarchs of Kent
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bede, The Ecclesiastical History of the English People
1 CONT     * D. P. Kirby, The Earliest English Kings (London: Unwin Hyman, 1991), pp. 43-44
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Eorcenberht King of Kent
1 CONT 664-673
1 CONT with Seaxburh (664-?) Succeeded by
1 CONT Hlothhere
0 @N6000000003635701953@ NOTE [3448] AUREJAC.GED file, de 664 au 4 Juillet 673 Roi de Kent
1 CONT
1 CONT "Anglo Saxon Chronicle", Part 1, A.D. 672: This year died King Cenwal; and Sexburga his queen held the government one year after him.'
0 @N6000000002188311747@ NOTE Hlothhere of Kent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hlothhere (died February 6, 685) was a King of Kent who ruled from 673 to 685.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his brother Ecgberht I in 673. He must have come into conflict with Mercia, since in 676 the Mercian king Æthelred invaded Kent and caused great destruction; according to Bede, even churches and monasteries were not s
1 CONC pared, and Rochester was laid waste.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hlothhere's rule survived this onslaught, however. He appears for a time to have reigned jointly with his nephew Eadric, son of Ecgberht I, since a code of laws still extant was issued under both their names. In 685, Eadric went i
1 CONC nto exile and led the South Saxons against Hlothhere, who was defeated and died of his wounds.
1 CONT
1 CONT The above information is derived from Bede, but Hlothhere is the earliest Kentish king for whom genuine charters survive. One charter [1] is precisely dated to 1 April 675 in the first year of Hlothhere’s reign, which conflicts wi
1 CONC th accession date attributed to him by Bede. Two further charters attributed to Hlothere (S1648, S1648a), appear to have been altered copies of charters of Swæfheard (S10[2]) and Swæfberht (S11[3]) (Kelly 1995).
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ecgberht I King of Kent
1 CONT 673-685
1 CONT with Eadric Succeeded by
1 CONT Eadric
0 @N6000000002188311498@ NOTE Ermenilda of Ely
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Ermenilda (or Eormenhild, Ermenildis, Ermengild) (d.700 or 703) was the daughter of king Eorcenberht of Kent and saint Seaxburh of Ely. She married Wulfhere of Mercia, with whom she had a daughter, saint Werburgh, and a son,
1 CONC  Cenred.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ermenilda became a Benedictine nun after her husband died in 675, and eventually became abbess of Minster and Ely consecutively.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her feast day is 13 February.
