0 @N6000000002187918807@ NOTE Hormizd II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hormizd II, was a Persia king of the Sassanid dynasty who reigned for seven years and five months, from 302 to 309. He was the son of Narseh (293–302),
1 CONT
1 CONT Almost nothing is known of Hormizd II's reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death his oldest son Adarnases was killed by the grandees after a very short reign, as he showed a cruel disposition; another son, Hormizd, was kept a prisoner, with the throne being reserved for Shapur II, the child of 
1 CONC his concubine. Another version says that Shapur II was the son of the first wife that Hormizd II had, and she was made to wear a crown over her pudenda while still pregnant so that the baby would be born as a King whenever the lab
1 CONC or pains began.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son Hormizd escaped from prison by the help of his wife in 323, and found refuge at the court of Constantine I (324–337) (Zosimus ii. 27; John of Antioch, fr. 178; Zonaras 13–5). In 363, Hormizd served in the army of Emperor J
1 CONC ulian (361–363) against Persia; his son, with the same name, afterwards served in the Roman government as a proconsul (Ammianus Marcellinus 26. 8. 12).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Narseh Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 302–309 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shapur II
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
