0 @N6000000001353749733@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT King of the Visigoths 395-410
1 CONT Alaric I inherited the kingdom when the Visigoths lived on the delta of the Danube river. Due to Hunnic attacks, they were pushed west. In 394, the Visigoths allied with Rome, and Alaric led the Visigoths in the Roman army against
1 CONC  the Huns. When the Roman Emperor Theodosius I died in 395 and divided the empire, the Visigoths renounced their allegiance with Rome. After ravaging the Empires, Alaric was defeated by General Flavius Stilicho. He then received f
1 CONC rom the Eastern Emperor Arcadius the command of the province of Illyricum. In 402, Alaric again invaded Italy, and was again defeated by Stilicho. After allying himself with the Western Emperor Honorius, and the execution of Stili
1 CONC cho, Alaric in 410 sacked and captured the city of Rome itself. Alaric's death stopped him from carrying out his next campaign; to Sicily and north Africa.
0 @N6000000003299246888@ NOTE Alaric I (Alareiks in the original Gothic; Alarik or Alarich in modern Germanic languages; Alaricus in Latin; and Alarico in Italian, Portuguese, and Spanish), was likely born about 370 on an island named Peuce (the Fir) at the mo
1 CONC uth of the Danube. He was king of the Visigoths from 395-410 and the first Germanic leader to take the city of Rome. Having originally desired to settle his people in the Roman Empire, he finally sacked the city, marking the decli
1 CONC ne of imperial power in the west.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alaric, whose name means literally "king of all" was well-born, his father kindred to the Balti, a tribe competing with the Amali among Gothic fighters. He belonged to the western Gothic branch, the Visigoths. At the time of his b
1 CONC irth the Visigoths dwelt in Bulgaria, having fled beyond the wide estuary marshes of the Danube to its southern shore so as not to be followed by their foes from the steppe, the Huns. There is evidence, however, as suggested for e
1 CONC xample by Peter Heather, that the Huns were not near the Danube until closer to the 5th century; however, there is no doubt that the migration of the Visigoths westward was in response to what was the threat of the Huns.
0 @N6000000003637326582@ NOTE Alarico I nació circa 370 en Peuce en Rumanía, hijo de Rocesthes, famoso por el saqueo de Roma en el 410. Sacó a los visigodos de Rumania y aún no siendo un general particularmente bueno, porque fue vencido siempre por el general 
1 CONC romano Stilicho, era un político de primera que lograba sacar ventajas de cualquier circumstancia.
1 CONT El Emperador Romano Honorio que no era muy popular, mandó ejecutar a Stilicho y él no fue capaz de enfrentarse a los visigodos en el 409. Alarico dejó a Honorio en Ravena sin posibilidades de comunicación con Roma. Marchando hacia
1 CONC  el sur, Alarico entró en Roma pero murió al poco tiempo en Lucania.
0 @N6000000001353749722@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT Although her name is unknown, she was the daughter of King Alaric I who married Theodoric and had with him Euric.
