0 @N6000000002188311523@ NOTE Æthelred of Mercia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Æthelred
1 CONT King of Mercia
1 CONT Reign 675–704
1 CONT Predecessor Wulfhere
1 CONT Successor Cenred
1 CONT Consort Osthryth
1 CONT Issue Ceolred
1 CONT Ceolwald ?
1 CONT Father Penda
1 CONT Mother Cynewise
1 CONT
1 CONT Æthelred (IPA: /'æ??lr?d/) (died after 704) was King of Mercia from 675 until 704. He was the son of Penda of Mercia and came to the throne in 675, when his brother, Wulfhere of Mercia, died. Within a year of his accession he inva
1 CONC ded Kent, where his armies destroyed the city of Rochester. In 679 he defeated his brother-in-law, Ecgfrith of Northumbria, at the Battle of the Trent: the battle was a major setback for the Northumbrians, and effectively ended th
1 CONC eir military involvement in English affairs south of the Humber. It also permanently returned the kingdom of Lindsey to Mercia's possession. However, Æthelred was unable to re-establish his predecessors' domination of southern Bri
1 CONC tain.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was known as a pious and religious king, and made many grants of land to the church. It was during his reign that Theodore, the Archbishop of Canterbury, reorganized the church's diocesan structure, creating several new sees in
1 CONC  Mercia and Northumbria. Æthelred befriended Bishop Wilfrid of York when Wilfrid was expelled from his see in Northumbria; Æthelred made Wilfrid Bishop of the Middle Angles during his exile, and supported him at the synod of Auste
1 CONC rfield in about 702, when Wilfrid argued his case for the return of the ecclesiastical lands had had been deprived of in Northumbria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Æthelred's wife, Osthryth, was a daughter of King Oswiu, one of the dominant seventh-century Northumbrian kings. Osthryth was murdered in unknown circumstances in 697, and in 704 Æthelred abdicated, leaving the throne to Wulfhere'
1 CONC s son Cenred. Æthelred became a monk at Bardney, a monastery which he had founded with his wife, and was buried there. Ceolred, who was Æthelred's son (though apparently not by Osthryth), became king after Cenred; it is also possi
1 CONC ble that Æthelred had another son named Ceolwald who was briefly king before Ceolred.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
