0 @N6000000002187923901@ NOTE Euric
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Euric, also known as Evaric, Erwig, or Eurico in Spanish, (c. 415–484 CE), was the younger brother of Theodoric II and ruled as king of the Visigoths, with his capital at Toulouse, from 466 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT He inherited a large portion of the Visigothic possessions in the Aquitaine region of Gaul, an area that had been under Visigothic control since 415. Over the decades the Visigoths had gradually expanded their holdings at the expe
1 CONC nse of the weak Roman government, advancing well into Hispania in the process.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon becoming king, Euric defeated several other Visigothic kings and chieftains in a series of civil wars and soon became the first ruler of a truly unified Visigothic nation. Taking advantage of the Romans' problems, he extended
1 CONC  Visigothic power in Hispania, driving the Suevi into the northwest of Iberia. By the time the western empire ended in 476 he controlled nearly the entire Iberian peninsula.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 470 Euric defeated an attempted invasion of Gaul by the Celtic magnate Riothamus and expanded his kingdom even further north, possibly as far as the Somme River, the march of Frankish territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT Previous Visigothic kings had officially ruled only as legates of the Roman Emperor but Euric was the first to declare his complete independence from the puppet emperors. In 475 he forced the western emperor Julius Nepos to recogn
1 CONC ize his full independence in exchange for the return of the Provence region of Gaul. The Roman citizens of Hispania then pledged their allegiance to Euric, recognizing him as their king. In the same year Clermont(-Ferrand) surrend
1 CONC ered to him after a long siege, and its bishop, Sidonius Apollinaris, sued for peace. He divided the Western Roman Empire with Odoacer.
1 CONT
1 CONT Euric was one of the more learned of the great Visigothic kings and was the first German to formally codify his people's laws. The Code of Euric of 461 codified the traditional laws that had been entrusted to the memory of designa
1 CONC ted specialists who had learned each article by heart.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Euric's death in 484 the Kingdom of the Visigoths encompassed all of Iberia except for the region of Galicia (ruled by the Suebi) and more than two-thirds of modern France. Edward Gibbon, in Chapter 38 of the History of the Dec
1 CONC line and Fall of the Roman Empire, remarks:
1 CONT
1 CONT     The fortune of nations has often depended on accidents; and France may ascribe her greatness to the premature death of the Gothic king, at a time when his son Alaric was a helpless infant, and his adversary Clovis an ambitious
1 CONC  and valiant youth.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Edward Gibbon, History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire Chapter 37
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Edward Gibbon, History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire Chapter 38
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Theodoric II King of the Visigoths
1 CONT 466–484 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alaric II
0 @N6000000003949902942@ NOTE The most notable of the Spanish Visigothic kings was Euric, who reigned [lived] from about 420 to 484 (actually only reigned from 466 - 484). He was a son of Theodoric I. Under Euric, who declared his rule to be independent of any
1 CONC  federation with Rome, the kingdom of Toulouse included almost all of Spain and most of Gaul west of the Rhône River and south of the Loire River. Euric introduced many aspects of Roman civilization and drew up a code of law combi
1 CONC ning Roman and German elements. The kingdom was, however, continually beset by both internal and external difficulties. The kingship was nominally elective, and the powerful Visigothic nobles stood against attempts to found a here
1 CONC ditary royal house. Externally, the Byzantine Empire and the Franks menaced the Visigothic lands. In order to instill greater loyalty in his rebellious Roman and Christian subjects, Alaric II in 506 introduced the collection of la
1 CONC ws known as the Breviary of Alaric. A year later, Clovis I, king of the Franks, defeated the Visigoths at the Battle of Vouillé, in which Alaric II was killed.. He was succeeded by his son Alaric II  See "History of the Goths" by 
1 CONC Herwig Wolfram, 1987
0 @N6000000002188327664@ NOTE Julius Constantius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Julius Constantius (d. September, 337) was a son of Western Roman Emperor Constantius Chlorus and his second wife Flavia Maximiana Theodora. He was a younger half-brother of Roman Emperor Constantine I.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father died on July 25, 306 when Julius Constantius was either a child or an adolescent. He is considered to have spent much of the 300s, 310s and 320s under virtual house arrest in Tolosa , Gallia Narbonensis, Gaul, Western R
1 CONC oman Empire under orders of his brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT He first married Galla, sister of both Vulcacius Rufus and Neratius Cerealis. They had three known children.:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Constantius Gallus (325/326 - 354).
1 CONT     * A son. Murdered in 337.
1 CONT     * A daughter. First Empress consort of his nephew Constantius II.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married for a second time to Basilina, daughter of Caeionius Iulianus Camenius. They only had one known son:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Flavius Claudius Iulianus (331/332 - 363).
1 CONT
1 CONT His half-brother favored Julius Constantius by naming him a patrician and by appointing him consul in 335. When Constantine died on May 22, 337, Julius Constantius was in position to claim the throne for himself. He was murdered w
1 CONC ithin months of Constantine's death along with most males of their family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Only five males survived the series of assassinations. His nephews and new Roman Emperors Constantine II, Constantius II and Constans along with his two own young sons. Constantius II is suspected to have ordered the assassination
1 CONC  of his uncle.
0 @N6000000002188327699@ NOTE Constantius Gallus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius Gallus
1 CONT Caesar of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Gallus coin celebrating Rome and Constantinople.
1 CONT Reign 15 March 351 - 354 (Caesar of the east under Constantius II)
1 CONT Full name Flavius Claudius Constantius Gallus
1 CONT Born ca. 325/6
1 CONT Massa Veternensis
1 CONT Died 354
1 CONT Pola
1 CONT Wife/wives Constantina
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Julius Constantius
1 CONT Mother Galla
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Claudius Constantius Gallus (ca. 325/326 - 354), better known as Constantius Gallus, was a member of the Constantinian dynasty and Caesar of the Roman Empire (351-354). Gallus was consul three years, from 352 to 354.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family
1 CONT     * 2 Appearance
1 CONT     * 3 Youth
1 CONT     * 4 Caesar
1 CONT     * 5 Fall and death
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT     * 7 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Julius Constantius by his first wife Galla, Gallus' paternal grandparents were Western Roman Emperor Constantius Chlorus and his second wife Flavia Maximiana Theodora.
1 CONT
1 CONT Julius Constantius was also a half-brother of Roman Emperor Constantine I, and thus Gallus was a first cousin of Emperors Constantine II, Constantius II and Constans.
1 CONT
1 CONT The older sister of Gallus, of unknown name, was the first wife of Constantius II. Julius Constantius had married a second wife, Basilina, who had borne him a son, Flavius Claudius Iulianus, later emperor, and known as Julian.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Appearance
1 CONT
1 CONT Gallus was very good looking, with soft blond hair.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Youth
1 CONT
1 CONT Gallus was born in Massa Veternensis, Italia, after his father had returned from exile. In 337, during the purges that hit the imperial family after the death of Constantine I, Gallus saw his father and his elder brother killed, p
1 CONC robably by order of his cousin Constantius. The only imperial males surviving were the three Emperors, Gallus, and Julian, who were probably too young or ill (Banchich) to be a menace to Constantius.
1 CONT
1 CONT With regards to Gallus' youth, the most accepted view is that he lived with Julian under Eusebius, bishop of Nicomedia, then in Constantinople since 340 (when Eusebius became bishop of the capital). After 341 (death of Eusebius), 
1 CONC Constantius sent Gallus and Julian to the imperial manor in Macellum, Cappadocia. An alternative view accepts hints from sources that want Gallus in Ephesus to study, and then going to Macellum from an exile in Tralles.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Caesar
1 CONT Location of Antioch in present-day Turkey
1 CONT Location of Antioch in present-day Turkey
1 CONT
1 CONT In 350, Magnentius had rebelled and killed the emperor Constans, claiming the purple. Constantius II prepared to move against the usurper, but needed a representative in the East, so he called Gallus at Sirmium, raised him to the 
1 CONC rank of caesar (15 March 351), gave him the name Constantius, and strengthened the bonds with his cousin by allowing Gallus to marry his sister Constantina. Gallus and Constantina, who probably shared her brother's aim of controll
1 CONC ing the young caesar, set up residence in Antioch.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule, Gallus had to deal with a Jewish rebellion in Judea/Palestine. The rebellion, possibly started before Gallus' elevation to caesar, was crushed by Gallus' general, Ursicinus, who ordered all the rebels slain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gallus was saved from an assassination plot by a woman, who revealed that some members of her household were planning the murder. Some sources, among whom are Joannes Zonaras, claim that this plot had been organized by Magnentius 
1 CONC in order to distract Constantius from Magnentius himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some sources (Philostorgius) claim Gallus' generals won a campaign against the Sassanids. Others, basing their views on an almost-peaceful situation between Sassanids and Romans, dismiss this claim. In 354, Gallus sent the Comes O
1 CONC rientis, Nebridius, against the Isaurians, who had been raiding the city of Seleucia on the Tigris.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a consequence of the need to gather food for the troops of a Persian campaign or because of drought, the grain supply in Antioch decreased. In order to counter the higher price of grain, Gallus forced the passage of some laws r
1 CONC egardless of the opinion of the Senate, thus alienating the support of the senatorial class of Antioch. Ammianus Marcellinus, a philo-senatorial writer, tells how the anger of the people of Antioch for the famine was diverted by G
1 CONC allus towards the consularis Syriae Theophilus, who was killed by the mob.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ammianus reports also that Gallus and Constantina started several trials for magic against wealthy people, ending in the execution of innocents and in the confiscation of their wealth. The same source claims that Gallus walked ano
1 CONC nymously in Antioch by night, asking passersby for their opinion on their caesar, while Julian records the great amount of time spent by Gallus at the Hippodrome, probably to obtain popular support.
1 CONT
1 CONT Doubting his cousin's loyalty, Constantius reduced the troops under Gallus, and sent the Praetorian Prefect Domitianus to Antioch to urge Gallus to go to Italy. Different sources tell different stories, but all agree that Gallus a
1 CONC rrested Domitianus and the quaestor Montius who had come to his aid, and that the two officers were killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT The arrest of Montius led to the discovery of what seems to be a plot to elevate an usurper against Gallus. The conspirators had the support of two tribuni fabricarum (officers of the weapons factories) who had promised the weapon
1 CONC s for an uprising (Ammianus Marcellinus, 14.7.18), and probably of the troops in Mesopotamia, as well as of the rector of the province of Phoenice. All of those involved in the plot were sentenced to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fall and death
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius was informed of the trials in Antioch during a campaign against the Alamanni. Having signed a peace with the Germanic tribe, Constantius decided to settle the matter with his cousin. First he summoned Ursicinus to the 
1 CONC West, whom he suspected to have been inciting Gallus in order to create the occasion for a revolt and the usurpation of his own son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Next, Constantius summoned Gallus and Constantina to Milan. Constantina left first, in order to gain some of her brother's trust, but died at Caeni Gallicani in Bithynia. Gallus, whose bonds to Constantius had been weakened, staye
1 CONC d in Antioch. Constantius tried to lure Gallus, sending the tribunus scutariorum Scudilo to tell Gallus that Constantius wanted to raise him to Augustus. Gallus desiring to finally obtain the rank of Augustus, took Constantius's b
1 CONC ait and left Antioch to meet him. Gallus in an exhibition of his presumed soon to be Augustus powers, staged a chariot race in Constantinople's Hippodrome and crowned the victor, an honor reserved only for those that are Augustus.
1 CONC  This insolence of Gallus enraged Constantius, further adding to his dislike for the upstart Caesar. In an attempt to further isolate Gallus from any form of military protection, Constantius had the garrisons removed from the town
1 CONC s in Gallus's path.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Gallus arrived to Poetovio in Noricum, Barbatio, an officer who had been supporting Gallus' dismissal within Constantius' court, surrounded the palace of the caesar and arrested him, stripping Gallus of the imperial robes, bu
1 CONC t assuring him that no harm would come to him. Gallus was led to Pola, Istria (now Pula, Croatia). Here he was interrogated by some of the highest officials of Constantius' court, including the eunuch praepositus cubiculi Eusebius
1 CONC . Gallus tried to put the blame of all of his actions on Constantina, but Constantius sentenced him to death; The emperor later changed his mind, and ordered the caesar to be spared, but Eusebius ordered that the news was not to r
1 CONC each the executioners.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Banchich, Thomas M., "Gallus Caesar (15 March 351 - 354 A.D.)", DIR (1997)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Wikimedia Commons logo Media related to Gallus Caesar from the Wikimedia Commons.
