0 @N6000000002188327749@ NOTE Flavia Julia Constantia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavia Julia Constantia, (after 293 – c. 330), was the daughter of the Roman Emperor Constantius Chlorus and his second wife, Flavia Maximiana Theodora.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 313, Emperor Constantine I, who was half-brother of Constantia, gave her in marriage to his co-emperor Licinius, on occasion of their meeting in Mediolanum. She bore a son, Valerius Licinianus Licinius, in 315, and when the str
1 CONC uggle between Constantine and Licinius began in 316, she stayed on her husband's side. A second war started between the two emperors in 324; after Licinius' defeat, Constantia interceded with Constantine for her husband's life. Co
1 CONC nstantine spared Licinius life, and obliged him to live in Thessalonica as a private citizen, but the following year (325), he ordered that Licinius be killed. A second blow for Constantia was the death, also by order of Constanti
1 CONC ne, of her son Licinianus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following years, Constantia lived at her brother's court, receiving honors (her title was nobilissima femina). She converted to Christianity,[1] supporting the Arian party at the First Council of Nicaea (325).[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Jerome, Epist., 133.4; Tyrannius Rufinus, Hist. Eccl. 1.11.
1 CONT    2. ^ Philostorgius, Hist. Eccl. 1.9.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Constantia, at De Imperatoribus Romanis
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Fausta Empress of Rome
1 CONT 313–324
1 CONT (with Fausta)
1 CONT Succeeded by
1 CONT Fausta
0 @N6000000002188327758@ NOTE Licinius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Licinius
1 CONT Augustus in the East
1 CONT Coin featuring Licinius
1 CONT Reign 11 November 308 - 311 (as Augustus in the west, with Galerius in the east);
1 CONT 311 - 313 (joint Augustus with Maximinus)
1 CONT 313 - 324 (Augustus in the east, with Constantine in the west - in 314 and 324 in competition with him);
1 CONT Full name Valerius
1 CONT Licinianus Licinius
1 CONT Born c. 250
1 CONT Moesia Superior, near Zajecar in Serbia.
1 CONT Died 325
1 CONT Thessalonica
1 CONT Predecessor Severus
1 CONT Successor Constantine I
1 CONT Wife/wives Flavia Julia Constantia
1 CONT Issue Licinius
1 CONT Aureus of Licinius, celebrating his tenth year of reign and the fifth year of his son Licinius (on the obverse).
1 CONT Aureus of Licinius, celebrating his tenth year of reign and the fifth year of his son Licinius (on the obverse).
1 CONT
1 CONT     For other Romans of this name, see Licinius (gens).
1 CONT
1 CONT Valerius Licinianus Licinius (c. 250 - 325) was Roman emperor from 308 to 324.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Dacian peasant origin, born in Moesia Superior near modern Zajecar in Serbia, Licinius accompanied his close childhood friend, the Emperor Galerius, on the Persian expedition in 297. After the death of Flavius Valerius Severus,
1 CONC  Galerius elevated Licinius to the rank of Augustus in the West on November 11, 308. He received as his immediate command the provinces of Illyricum, Thrace and Pannonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of Galerius, in May 311, Licinius shared the entire empire with Maximinus Daia, the Hellespont and the Bosporus being the dividing line.
1 CONT
1 CONT In March 313 he married Flavia Julia Constantia, half-sister of Constantine, at Mediolanum (now Milan), the occasion for the jointly-issued "Edict of Milan" that restored confiscated properties to Christian congregations and allow
1 CONC ed Christianity to be professed in the empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following month, on April 30, Licinius inflicted a decisive defeat on Maximinus at the Battle of Tzirallum, after Maximinus had tried attacking him. Then, Licinius established himself master of the East, while his brother-i
1 CONC n-law, Constantine, was supreme in the West.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 314, a civil war erupted between Licinius and Constantine, in which Constantine prevailed at the Battle of Cibalae in Pannonia (October 8, 314) and again two years later (after naming Valerius Valens co-emperor) in the plain of
1 CONC  Mardia (also known as Campus Ardiensis) in Thrace. The emperors were reconciled after these two battles and Licinius had his co-emperor Valens killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Licinius' fleet of 350 ships was defeated by Constantine I's fleet in 323. In 324, Constantine, tempted by the "advanced age and unpopular vices" of his colleague, again declared war against him, and, having defeated his army of 1
1 CONC 70,000 men at the Battle of Adrianople (July 3, 324), succeeded in shutting him up within the walls of Byzantium. The defeat of the superior fleet of Licinius in the Battle of the Hellespont by Crispus, Constantine’s eldest son an
1 CONC d Caesar, compelled his withdrawal to Bithynia, where a last stand was made; the Battle of Chrysopolis, near Chalcedon (September 18), resulted in Licinius' final submission. While Licinius' co-emperor Sextus Martinianus was kille
1 CONC d, Licinius himself was spared due to the pleas of his wife, Constantine's sister, and interned at Thessalonica. The next year, Constantine had him killed, accusing him of conspiring to raise troops among the barbarians.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Commons has media related to Licinius
1 CONT     * De Imperatoribus Romanis website: Licinius
1 CONT     * Socrates Scholasticus account of Licinius' end
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Flavius Valerius Severus Roman Emperor
1 CONT 308-324
1 CONT with Galerius, Constantine I, Maximinus Daia, Valerius Valens and Martinianus Succeeded by
1 CONT Constantine I
0 @N6000000002188327735@ NOTE Julian the Apostate
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Claudius Julianus
1 CONT Emperor of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Flavius Claudius Iulianus, also known as Julian the Apostate, was the last polytheist Roman Emperor.
1 CONT Reign Caesar: 6 November 355 - February 360. Augustus: February 360 - 3 November 361. Sole Augustus: 3 November 361 -
1 CONT 26 June 363
1 CONT Born May or June 332
1 CONT Constantinople
1 CONT Died 26 June 363
1 CONT Maranga, Mesopotamia
1 CONT Predecessor Constantius II, cousin
1 CONT Successor Jovian, general present at the time of his death
1 CONT Consort to Helena (355)
1 CONT Issue None known
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian dynasty
1 CONT Father Julius Constantius
1 CONT Mother Basilina
1 CONT
1 CONT Julian the Apostate (Flavius Claudius Julianus, May or June 332 AD to 26 June 363 AD), was Roman Emperor (Caesar, November 355 to February 360; Augustus, February 360 to June 363) of the Constantinian dynasty. He was the last poly
1 CONC theist Roman Emperor and advocated the re-institution of Greco-Roman religious tradition over the Christianization of the Roman Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is known as Julian the Apostate, because of his rejection of Christianity, conversion to Theurgy (a late form of Neoplatonism), and attempt to rid the empire of Christianity.[1] Julian sympathised with the Jews who called him J
1 CONC ulian the Hellene[2].
1 CONT
1 CONT In 363, Julian began a campaign against the Sassanid Empire. He died later that year from a wound received during a retreat during the campaign.
