1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Iulius Constantius, known in English as Constantius II, born in Sirmium (now Sremska Mitrovica, Serbia), (7 August 317 - October 5, 361) was a Roman Emperor (337 - 361) of the Constantinian dynasty.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Biography
1 CONT           o 1.1 Rise to power
1 CONT           o 1.2 Struggles against usurpers and foreign threats
1 CONT           o 1.3 Death and succession
1 CONT           o 1.4 Religious issues
1 CONT     * 2 Notes
1 CONT     * 3 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Rise to power
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius was the second of the three sons of Constantine I and his second wife Fausta. Constantius was born in Sirmium now called Sremska Mitrovica (in Panonia) and named Caesar by his father. He married three times, first to a
1 CONC  daughter of Julius Constantius, then to Eusebia, and last to Faustina, who gave birth to a posthumous daughter called Constantia, who later married Emperor Gratian.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Constantine died in 337, Constantius II led the massacre of his relatives descended from the second marriage of his grandfather Constantius Chlorus and Theodora,[1] leaving himself, his older brother Constantine II, his young
1 CONC er brother Constans and two cousins (Gallus and his half-brother Julian) as the only surviving males related to Constantine. The three brothers divided the Roman Empire among them, according to their father's will. Constantine II 
1 CONC received Britannia, Gaul and Hispania; Constans ruled Italia, Africa, and Illyricum; and Constantius ruled the East.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Struggles against usurpers and foreign threats
1 CONT Constantius II coin, celebrating his 15th year of reign.
1 CONT Constantius II coin, celebrating his 15th year of reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT This division changed when Constantine II died in 340, trying to overthrow Constans in Italy, and Constans became sole ruler in the Western half of the empire. The division changed once more in 350 when Constans was killed in batt
1 CONC le by forces loyal to the usurper Magnentius. Until this time, Constantius was preoccupied with fighting the Sassanid Empire, and he was forced to elevate his cousin Gallus to Caesar of the East to assist him, while he turned his 
1 CONC attention to this usurper.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius eventually met and crushed Magnentius in the Battle of Mursa Major, one of the bloodiest battles in Roman history, in 351. Magnentius committed suicide in 353, and Constantius soon after put his cousin Gallus to death.
1 CONC  However, he still could not handle the military affairs of both the Eastern and German frontiers by himself, so in 355 he elevated his last remaining relative, Julian, to the rank of Caesar.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11 August 355, the magister militum Claudius Silvanus revolted in Gaul. Silvanus had surrendered to Constantius after the battle of Mursa Major. Constantius had made him magister militum in 353/353, with the purpose of blocking
1 CONC  the German threats, a feat that Silvanus achieved by bribing the German tribes with the money he had collected. A plot organized by members of Constantius' court led the emperor to recall Silvanus. After Silvanus revolted, he rec
1 CONC eived a letter by Constantius that recalled him to Milan, but which made no reference to the revolt. Ursicinus, who should have replaced Silvanus, bribed some troops, and Silvanus was killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arbitio was the main general (magister militum) of Constantius. He was given a consulship in 355.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and succession
1 CONT
1 CONT As Julian was hailed Augustus by the army in Gaul, in 361, Constantius saw no alternative but to face the usurper with violent force. As the two armies sought engagement, Constantius died from a fever near Tarsus on October 5th, 3
1 CONC 61, and Julian was proclaimed Augustus throughout the Roman Empire. Many of his ministers were put to trial at the Chalcedon tribunal
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Religious issues
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantius took an active part in the affairs of the Christian church — convening one council at Rimini and its twin at Seleuca, which met in 359 and 360. "Unfortunately for his memory the theologians whose advice he took were ul
1 CONC timately discredited and the malcontents whom he pressed to conform emerged victorious," writes the historian A.H.M. Jones. " The great councils of 359-60 are therefore not reckoned oecumenical in the tradition of the church, and 
1 CONC Constantius II is not remembered as a restorer of unity, but as a heretic who arbitrarily imposed his will on the church."[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Jones also notes that Constantius "appears in the pages of Ammianus as a conscientious emperor but a vain and stupid man, an easy prey to flatterers. He was timid and suspicious, and interested persons could easily play on his fea
1 CONC rs for their own advantage."[3] Ammianus also notes that Constantius II repeatedly murdered Roman aristocrats on the merest hint that they might be seeking the Empire themselves, including killing one because he had a dream about 
1 CONC him, and another because he had a purple tablecloth which might have been fashioned into the robe of an Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ X. Lucien-Brun, "Constance II et le massacre des princes," Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé ser. 4 (1973): 585-602; Joe W. Leedom, "Constantius II: Three Revisions," Byzantion 48 (1978): 132-145, and Michael DiMaio
1 CONC  and Duane Arnold, "Per Vim, Per Caedem, Per Bellum: A Study of Murder and Ecclesiastical Politics in the Year 337 A.D," Byzantion, 62(1992), 158ff. Cited in DiMaio and Frakes.
1 CONT    2. ^ A.H.M. Jones, The Later Roman Empire, 284-602: a Social, Economic and Administrative Survey (Baltimore: Johns Hopkins University, 1986), p. 118.
1 CONT    3. ^ Jones, Later Roman Empire, p. 116.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to:
1 CONT Constantius II
1 CONT
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, and Robert Frakes, "Constantius II," De Imperatoribus Romanis site.
1 CONT     * Ch. Vogler, Constance II et l’administration impériale (Strasbourg, 1979).
1 CONT     * David Rohrbacher, "Why didn't Constantius II Eat Fruit?," Classical Quarterly 55,1 (2005), 323-326.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Constantine I Roman Emperor
1 CONT 337–361
1 CONT with Constans and Constantine II Succeeded by
1 CONT Julian
0 @N6000000002188327622@ NOTE Constantina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT For other uses, see Constantina (disambiguation).
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantina (also named Constantia and Constantiana; b. after 307/before 317 - d. 354) was the eldest daughter of Roman Emperor Constantine I and his second wife Fausta, daughter of Emperor Maximian. Constantina received the title
1 CONC  of Augusta by her father, and is venerated as saint.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT In 335, Constantina married her cousin Hannibalianus, son of Flavius Dalmatius, whom Constantine had created "King of Kings and Ruler of the Pontic Tribes". Hannibalianus was murdered in 337, after the death of Constantine.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT For the second time, during the reign of her middle brother Constantius II, Constantina was married to Hannibalianus' and her own first cousin Gallus, who had been created a Caesar of the East and renamed Constantius around 349/35
1 CONC 0, which also presumably was the time of their marriage. Gallus was twenty-five or twenty-six at the time, whereas Constantina was substantially his senior.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Passio Artemii (12) alleges that the marriage was meant to ensure Gallus' loyalty but it may have had at least as much to do with Constantina who, besides having known power as Constantine's daughter and Hannibalianus' wife, h
1 CONC ad prompted the opposition of Vetranio (PLRE I, p. 954) to Magnentius, and whose hand had been sought from Constantius by ambassadors of Magnentius himself (Peter the Patrician fr. 16, Müller FHG IV, p. 190).
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage, besides benefiting Constantius, extricated her from a dangerous situation in the Roman Empire and placed her in a position from which she might control the younger and inexperienced Caesar. On the other hand, it is p
1 CONC ossible that Constantius saw the marriage as a way to remove his intrusive — perhaps treasonous — sister from the volatile west. If the mention in the Passio Artemii (11) of letters from Constantina to her brother preserves a genu
1 CONC ine tradition, it is possible Constantina even initiated the proposal that she marry Gallus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her second marriage produced a daughter, whose name and fate are unknown.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Gallus ruled over the East from Antioch, and his purpose was to keep under control the Sassanid menace. Gallus, however, alienated the support of his subjects with his arbitrary and merciless rule. Constantina supported her husban
1 CONC d. When, after receiving the complaints of the Anthiocheans, Constantius called for Gallus, the caesar sent Constantina to her brother, with the purpose to mitigate his position in Constantius' consideration.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantina, however, never reached Milan. She died in Caeni Gallicani, Bithynia. Her body was buried near Via Nomentana in Rome, in a mausoleum that later became the church of Santa Costanza, when Constantia was venerated as sain
1 CONC t. Her magnificent porphyry sarcophagus is on exhibit in the Vatican Museums.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Character assessment
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Gibbon likened Constantina to one of the internal furies tormented with an insatiate thirst of human blood. The historian said that she encouraged the violent nature of Gallus rather than persuading him to show reason and c
1 CONC ompassion. Gibbon stated that her vanity was accentuated while the gentle qualities of a woman were absent in her makeup. She would have accepted a pearl necklace in return for consenting to the execution of a worthy nobleman.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Ammianus Marcellinus, xiv 1.2.
1 CONT    2. ^ Flavius Claudius Iulianus, Epistola ad Athenienses, 272 D.
1 CONT    3. ^ Edward Gibbon, The History Of The Decline And Fall Of The Roman Empire, Dell Publishing, April 1963 | 344.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ammianus Marcellinus, Res Gestae Libri XXXI.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Morris, John; Arnold Hugh Martin Jones, and John Robert Martindale (1992). The prosopography of the later Roman Empire. Cambridge University Press, pp. 696-697. ISBN 0-521-07233-6. 
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Constantina (daughter of Constantine I)", De Imperatoribus Romanis
