0 @N6000000002188198211@ NOTE Vasudeva I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT His name, Vasudeva, is that of the father of Krishna, the popular Hindu god, and he was the first Kushan king to have an Indian name.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vasudeva I (Kushan: ??????? "Bazodeo", Chinese: ?? "Bodiao") was the last of the "Great Kushans." Named inscriptions dating from year 64 to 98 of Kanishka's era suggest his reign extended from at least 191 to 225 CE.
1 CONT
1 CONT The last named inscription of his predecessor, Huvishka, was in the year 60 = 187 CE, and the Chinese evidence suggests he was still ruling as late as 330 CE. He was the last great Kushan emperor, and the end of his rule coincides
1 CONC  with the invasion of the Sassanians as far as northwestern India, and the establishment of the Indo-Sassanians or Kushanshahs from around 240 CE.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Contacts with China
1 CONT     * 2 Christian connection
1 CONT     * 3 References
1 CONT     * 4 Notes
1 CONT     * 5 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Contacts with China
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Chinese historical chronicle Sanguozhi (???), he is recorded to have sent tribute to the Chinese emperor Cao Rui of the Wei in 229 CE (3rd year of Taihe ??), :
1 CONT
1 CONT     "The king of the Da Yuezhi, Bodiao (??) (Vasudeva), sent his envoy to present tribute and His Majesty granted him a title of "King of the Da Yuezhi Intimate with Wei (?)"." (Sanguozhi)
1 CONT
1 CONT He is the last Kushan king to be mentioned in Chinese sources. His rule corresponds to the retreat of Chinese power from Central Asia, and it is thought that Vasudeva may have filled the power vacuum in that area. The great expans
1 CONC ion of the Dharmaguptaka Buddhist group in Central Asia during this period has also been related to this event.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Christian connection
1 CONT
1 CONT Vasudeva may have been the Indian king who returned the relics of the Apostle St. Thomas from India in 232 CE, on which occasion his Syriac Acts (the 3rd century Acts of Thomas) were written. The relics were transferred triumphall
1 CONC y to the town of Edessa, Mesopotamia. The Indian king is named as "Mazdai" in Syriac sources, "Misdeos" and "Misdeus" in Greek and Latin sources, which has been connected to the "Bazdeo" on the Kushan coinage of Vasudeva, the tran
1 CONC sition between "M" and "B" being a current one in Classical sources for Indian names. [1] The martyrologist Rabban Sliba dedicated a special day to both the Indian king, his family, and St Thomas:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Coronatio Thomae apostoli et Misdeus rex Indiae, Johannes eus filius huiusque mater Tertia" ("Coronation of Thomas the Apostole, and Misdeus king of India, together with his son Johannes (thought to be a Latinization of Vizan
1 CONC ) and his mother Tertia") Rabban Sliba [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Falk, Harry (2001). "The yuga of Sphujiddhvaja and the era of the Ku?â?as." Silk Road Art and Archaeology VII, pp. 121-136.
1 CONT     * Falk, Harry (2004). "The Kani?ka era in Gupta records." Harry Falk. Silk Road Art and Archaeology X , pp. 167-176.
1 CONT     * Sims-Williams, Nicholas (1998). "Further notes on the Bactrian inscription of Rabatak, with an Appendix on the names of Kujula Kadphises and Vima Taktu in Chinese." Proceedings of the Third European Conference of Iranian Stu
1 CONC dies Part 1: Old and Middle Iranian Studies. Edited by Nicholas Sims-Williams. Wiesbaden. Pp, 79-93.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Mario Bussagli, "L'Art du Gandhara", p255
1 CONT    2. ^ Mario Bussagli, "L'Art du Gandhara", p255
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Coins of Vasudeva
1 CONT     * Coins of late Kushan emperors
1 CONT     * Vasudeva coin
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Huvishka Kushan Ruler Succeeded by
1 CONT Kanishka II
0 @N6000000001353874500@ NOTE Ruled BET. 226 - 241 Sassanid King of Persia 1
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Ardashir I (fl. mid-3rd century), the founder of the Sasanian empire in ancient Persia (reigned AD 224-241).
1 CONT Ardashir was the son of Babak, who was the son or descendant of Sasanand was a vassal of the chief petty king in Persis, Gochihr. After Babak got Ardashir the military post of argabad in the town of Darabgerd(near modern Darab, Ir
1 CONC an), Ardashir extended his control over severalneighbouring cities. Meanwhile, Babak had slain Gochihr and taken thetitle of king. Although Babak's request that the Parthian king Artabanus V allow him to transmit the crown to his 
1 CONC eldest son, Shapur, was refused, Shapur nevertheless succeeded him. In the ensuing struggle between him and Ardashir, Shapur was killed, and Ardashir was crowned king of Persis in 208. Having suppressed a revolt in Darabgerd, he g
1 CONC radually conquered the neighbouring province of Kerman and the coastal Persian Gulf lands. He made his capital at Gur (modern Firuzabad), which he renamed Ardashir-Kwarrah.
1 CONT Ardashir then moved against western Iran, taking Esfahan, Kerman, Elymais, and Mesene. Withdrawing again to Persis, he met the Parthian army at Hormizdagan (site unknown) in AD 224 and won a decisive victory,slaying Artabanus. Soo
1 CONC n after, Ardashir entered the Parthian capital of Ctesiphon, in Mesopotamia, in triumph and was crowned "king of kings of Iran."
1 CONT With his son and successor, Shapur I, Ardashir established the Sasanian empire. Nothing is known of Ardashir's personal life; his deeds, however, indicate that he was ruthless, a great soldier, and a capable king. He founded or re
1 CONC built many cities and is credited with digging canals and building bridges. Several great rock carvings commemorate hisreign.
1 CONT Ardashir made Zoroastrianism the state religion, and he and his priest Tosar are credited with collecting the holy texts and establishinga unified doctrine. Two treatises, The Testament of Ardashir and The Letter of Tosar, are att
1 CONC ributed to them. As patron of the church, Ardashir appears in Zoroastrian tradition as a sage. As founder of the dynasty, he is celebrated in a 5th-century book in Pahlavi, the Karnamag-i Ardashir.
