0 @N6000000002188195798@ NOTE Empress Murong (Daowu)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Murong (????, personal name unknown), formally Empress Daowu (????), was an empress of the Chinese/Xianbei dynasty Northern Wei Dynasty. Her husband was the founding emperor Emperor Daowu (Tuoba Gui).
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the youngest daughter of the Later Yan emperor Murong Bao (Emperor Huimin). When Northern Wei launched a major attack against Later Yan in 396, causing Murong Bao to abandon the capital Zhongshan (??, in modern Baoding, He
1 CONC bei) in 397, she was unable to follow her father in flight, and when Zhongshan fell to Northern Wei later that year, she was captured by Tuoba Gui and made one of his consorts. In 400, after he had declared imperial title, he cons
1 CONC idered whom to create empress. He had favored Consort Liu, the daughter of the Xiongnu chief Liu Toujuan (???) the most, and she also bore his oldest son Tuoba Si. However, the Tuoba Tribe's traditions dictated that when the ruler
1 CONC  were to consider whom to make his wife, he was to make the candidates try to forge golden statues by hand, as a way of discerning divine favor. Consort Liu was unable to forge a golden statue, while Consort Murong was able to, an
1 CONC d so was created empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nothing more is known about Empress Murong. She was implied to be still alive in 402, when Later Qin's emperor Yao Xing rejected Emperor Daowu's overture to marry one of his daughters, because Yao Xing's refusal was based on his i
1 CONC nformation that Emperor Daowu already had Empress Murong as empress.
