1 CONT Constantine was of British birth and education and is known as the first Christian Emporer. With a British army he set out to put down the persecution of Christians forever. The greatest of all Roman Emporers, he annexed Britain t
1 CONC o the Roman Empire and founded Constantinople. In 321 he decreed that the Christian Sunday be truly observed as a day of rest. In 325 he assembled the Council of Nicea in Bithynia, Asia Minor, which he attended in person.
0 @N6000000008640590548@ NOTE Age 33 in 305.  From 292 The Western Caesar was Constantius who had Gaul, Britian and Spain while Maximian had Italy and Africa. Diocletian had Asia and Egypt while his Caesar,(son in law Galerius) had the Balkans.
1 CONT Maximian made Milan focus of empire rather than Rome.. Diocletian made focus of Empire Eastern Empire not Rome/west.
1 CONT In 305 Constantius became Augustus of the West, at Treves (Augusta Trevirorium), Galerius took the Danube and Asia Minor, the two new Ceasars were Daza- better known as Maximan nephew of Galerius,  and  Severus. This was confirmed
1 CONC  by Diocletian as his last public act, ignoring the son of Constantius - Constantine (later "The Great")  and Maxentius son of Maxentius. Daza/Maximan was sent to the East, Severus to the Milan.
1 CONT The empire went to pot. The rich evaded/ avoided not only the old taxes but all new taxes ruining the middle class- the rich got Very Rich and the middle class disappeared into the very poor.
1 CONT In 306 Constantius died at York. (15 mo after the abdication)
1 CONT Constantine informed Galerius that the Legions had compelled him to assume the title, and Galerius only dared to confirm ratify the appointment/title.
1 CONT Thew senate of Rome wanted to reasert itself and appointed Maxentius as Augustus and a power thirsty Maximan emerged from "retirement" to support this title. Severus marched "hot foot" from Milan, found Rome already lost and event
1 CONC ually was tricked into surrender by Maximan who allowed him to commit "honorable suicide". Maximianus gave his daughter Fausta in marriage to Constantine to cement the status quo and Constantine accepted both offers.
0 @N6000000002188327778@ NOTE Nepotianus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Nepotianus
1 CONT Usurper of the Roman Empire
1 CONT Nepotianus on a coin bearing
1 CONT his claimed title of Augustus
1 CONT Reign 3 - 30 June 350
1 CONT (in competition with Magnentius)
1 CONT Full name Flavius Iulius Popilius
1 CONT Nepotianus Constantinus
1 CONT Died 30 June 350
1 CONT Rome
1 CONT Dynasty Constantinian
1 CONT Father Virius Nepotianus
1 CONT Mother Eutropia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavius Iulius Popilius Nepotianus Constantinus (died 30 June 350) was a member of the Constantinian dynasty, and a short-lived usurper of the Roman Empire. He ruled the city of Rome for twenty-eight days, before being killed by h
1 CONC is rival usurper Magnentius' general Marcellinus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Nepotianus was the son of Eutropia, half-sister of Emperor Constantine I, and of Virius Nepotianus. On his mother's side, he was the grandson of Emperor Constantius Chlorus and Flavia Maximiana Theodora.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Events
1 CONT
1 CONT After the revolt of Magnentius, Nepotianus proclaimed himself "emperor" and entered Rome with a band of gladiators on 3 June 350. After attempting to resist Nepotianus with an undisciplined force of Roman citizens, the defeated Pr
1 CONC aefectus urbi Titianus (or Anicius, or Anicetus), a supporter of Magnentius, fled the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnentius quickly dealt with this revolt by sending his trusted magister officiorum Marcellinus to Rome. According to Eutropius, Nepotianus was killed in the resulting struggle (on 30 June), his head put on a lance and brought ar
1 CONC ound the city. In the following days, his mother Eutropia was also killed, during the persecution of the supporters of Nepotianus, most of whom were senators.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * DiMaio, Michael, "Nepotian (350 A.D.)", DIR
1 CONT     * Aurelius Victor De Caesaribus 42.6, Epitome 42.3
1 CONT     * Eutropius x.11
1 CONT     * Zosimus, ii.59
0 @N6000000002188327580@ NOTE Fausta
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Fausta Flavia Maxima, Roman Empress, (289-326A.D.) She was the daughter of the Roman Emperor Maximianus. To seal the alliance between them for control of the Tetrarchy, Maximianus married her to Constantine I in 307. Constantine a
1 CONC nd Fausta had been betrothed since 293.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fausta had a part in her father's downfall. In 310 Maximian died as a consequence of an assassination plot against Constantine. Maximian decided to involve his daughter Fausta, but she revealed the plot to her husband, and the ass
1 CONC assination was disrupted. Maximian died, by suicide or by assassination, in July of that same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Empress Fausta was held in high esteem by Constantine and proof of his favour was that in 323 she was proclaimed Augusta, previously she held the title of Nobilissima Femina. However 3 years later Fausta was put to death by Consta
1 CONC ntine. Although the real reasons are not clear, Constantine put her to death following the execution of Crispus, his eldest son by a previous marriage to Minervina, in 326. According to the ancient sources, she accused Crispus of 
1 CONC rape, and Constantine had Crispus executed. Fausta was later executed by suffocation in an over-heated bath,[1] when her charge was discovered to be false. Modern commentators have tended to ignore the allegation of rape and to se
1 CONC ek some other explanation for what happened. It has often been argued that Fausta wanted to get rid of Crispus who was a dangerous rival for her sons in the competition to succeed Constantine. The Emperor ordered the damnatio memo
1 CONC riae of his wife. Significantly her sons once in power never revoked this order.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her sons became Roman Emperors: Constantine II reigned 337 - 340, Constantius II reigned 337 - 361, and Constans reigned 337 - 350. She also bore three daughters Constantina, Helena and Fausta. Of these, Constantina married her co
1 CONC usins, firstly Hannibalianus and secondly Constantius Gallus, and Helena married Emperor Julian. Apparently a genealogical claim that her daughter Fausta became mother of Emperor Valentinian I is without foundation (Valentinian I 
1 CONC and children of Constantine I's second marriage were born in years close to each other, i.e they were of the same generation).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Roman Emperors - DIR Fausta
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Bibliography
1 CONT
1 CONT J.W. Drijvers, 'Flavia Maxima Fausta: Some Remarks', Historia 41 (1992) 500- 506.
1 CONT
1 CONT D. Woods, ‘On the Death of the Empress Fausta’, Greece & Rome 45 (1998), 70-86.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Wikimedia Commons logo Media related to Fausta from the Wikimedia Commons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Galeria Valeria Empress of Rome
1 CONT 307–326
1 CONT (with Galeria Valeria, Valeria Maximilla and Flavia Julia Constantia)
0 @N6000000002188327650@ NOTE Flavia Maximiana Theodora
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Flavia Maximiana Theodora (known as Theodora) was the step-daughter of Maximian. Her parents were Afanius Hannibalianus and Eutropia, later wife of Maximian. Theodora's father was consul in 292, and praetorian prefect under Diocle
1 CONC tian. In 293, Theodora married Flavius Valerius Julius Constantius (later known as Constantius Chlorus), after he had divorced from his first wife, Helena, to strengthen his political position.
1 CONT
1 CONT The couple had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Flavius Dalmatius;
1 CONT     * Julius Constantius, father of Roman Emperor Julian;
1 CONT     * Hannibalianus (must have died before the imperial purges that occurred in 337 because he is not listed among its victims);
1 CONT     * Anastasia;
1 CONT     * Flavia Julia Constantia, wife of Roman Emperor Licinius;
1 CONT     * Eutropia, mother of Nepotianus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Commons has media related to Flavia Maximiana Theodora
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Prisca
1 CONT -
1 CONT Eutropia Empress of Rome
1 CONT 305–306
1 CONT (with Galeria Valeria)
1 CONT Succeeded by
1 CONT Galeria Valeria
