0 @N6000000002188198253@ NOTE Kanishka
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka (Kushan language: ???????, Ancient Chinese: ????) was a king of the Kushan Empire in South Asia, in the 2nd century of the common era, famous for his military, political, and spiritual achievements. His capital was at the
1 CONC  location of the modern city of Peshawar in Pakistan.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 A great Kushan king
1 CONT     * 2 Conquests in India and Central Asia
1 CONT     * 3 Kanishka's coinage
1 CONT           o 3.1 Hellenistic phase
1 CONT           o 3.2 Iranian/Indian phase
1 CONT     * 4 Kanishka and Buddhism
1 CONT           o 4.1 Buddhist coinage
1 CONT                 + 4.1.1 The Buddha
1 CONT                 + 4.1.2 The "Shakyamuni Buddha"
1 CONT                 + 4.1.3 The "Maitreya Buddha"
1 CONT           o 4.2 Kanishka casket
1 CONT           o 4.3 Kanishka in Buddhist tradition
1 CONT           o 4.4 Transmission of Buddhism to China
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT     * 6 See also
1 CONT     * 7 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] A great Kushan king
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka was a Kushan of Yuezhi ethnicity. He probably spoke an Indo-European language related to Tocharian, and he used the Greek script in his inscriptions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka was the successor of Vima Kadphises, as demonstrated by an impressive geneaology of the Kushan kings, known as the Rabatak inscription.[1] [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT A number of legends about Kanishka were preserved in Buddhist religious traditions. Along with the Indian king Ashoka, the Indo-Greek king Menander I (Milinda), and Harshavardhana, he is considered by Buddhists to have been one of
1 CONC  the greatest Buddhist kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka's era is now generally accepted to have begun in 127 CE on the basis of Harry Falk's ground-breaking research.[3] [4] Kanishka's era was used as a calendar reference by the Kushans for about a century, until the decline o
1 CONC f the Kushan realm.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Conquests in India and Central Asia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka's empire was certainly vast. It extended from the Oxus in the west to Varanasi and Mathura in the east (the Rabatak inscription even claims he held Pataliputra), and from Kashmir in the North to the coast of Gujarat in th
1 CONC e south, including Malwa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Knowledge of his hold over Central Asia is less well established. Chinese records indicate that general Ban Chao fought battles with a Kushan army of 70,000 men led by an otherwise unknown Kushan Viceroy named Xie (Chinese: ?) nea
1 CONC r Khotan in 90 CE. Though Ban Chao claimed to be victorious, forcing the Kushans to retreat by use of a scorched-earth policy the region fell to Kushan forces in the early 2nd century.[5] As a result, for a period (until the Chine
1 CONC se regained control c. 127 CE) [6] the territory of the Kushans extended to Kashgar, Khotan and Yarkand, which were Chinese dependencies in the Tarim Basin, modern Xinjiang. Several coins of Kanishka have been found in the Tarim B
1 CONC asin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Controlling both the land and sea trade routes between South Asia and Rome seems to have been one of Kanishka's chief imperial goals.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Kanishka's coinage
1 CONT Gold coin of Kanishka I with the Hellenistic divinity Helios. (c.120 AD).Obv: Kanishka standing, clad in heavy Kushan coat and long boots, flames emanating from shoulders, holding standard in his left hand, and making a sacrifice 
1 CONC over an altar. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka's coins show Hindu, Buddhist, Greek, Persian and even Sumerian-Elamite images of gods. They are demonstrative of religious syncretism in his beliefs. Kanishka's coins at the beginning of his reign were written in the Gree
1 CONC k language, with the Greek script, and depicted Greek divinities. Later in his reign, Kanishka ordered that the Bactrian language be used (the Iranian language the Kushans apparently spoke), and Greek divinities were replaced by c
1 CONC orresponding Iranian ones. All of Kanishka's coins (even the Bactrian language) were written in corrupted Greek script, with the addition of the Kushan letter ?, pronounced "Sh", as in the word "Kushan".
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka is shown as a profusely bearded man in a long coat and trousers, with flames emanating from his shoulders. He wears large rounded boots, and is armed with a long sword similar to a scimitar as well as a lance. He is often
1 CONC  shown making a sacrifice on a small altar.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Hellenistic phase
1 CONT
1 CONT A few coins at the beginning of his reign have a legend in the Greek language and Greek script: ???????? ??????O? ???????? ("Basileus Basileon Kanishkou"): "King of Kings Kanishka". This is a heretoforth unknown form in Greek, whi
1 CONC ch is nevertheless proper grammatically (nominative form), indicating that Greek was still a "living" language among the Kushans at that time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Greek deities, with Greek names are represented on these early coins:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * ????? (Sun god Helios), ?F?????? (Fire god Hephaistos), ?????? (Moon god Selene), ?????? (Wind god Anemos)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Iranian/Indian phase
1 CONT Kushan Carnelian seal representing the Iranian divinity Adsho ("?T??" legend in Greek letters), with triratana symbol left, and Kanishka's dynastic mark right. The divinity uses stirrups.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the transition to the Bactrian language on coins, numerous Iranian divinities were adopted in replacement (and extension) of Greek ones:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * ??? (Moon god Mao), ???? (Wind god Oado)
1 CONT     * ??T?? ("Mitro", Mithra), ?????, ?????, ?????
1 CONT     * ????, ??????, ???????G?, ???????
1 CONT     * ????????, F????, ?????G??, ?T??, ???????, ?????????, F????
1 CONT
1 CONT Only a few Indian divinities were used as well:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * ????? ("Boddo", the name of the Buddha in its Greek form), ???????? ????? ("Shakamano Boddho", the historical Shakyamuni Buddha), ?????G? ????? ("Metrago Boddo", the bodhisattava Maitreya).
1 CONT     * ???? ("Oesho", long considered as the Hindu god Shiva, although recent studies tend to indicate that Oisho is an Iranian god, derived from Herakles, with admittedly many of the attributes of Shiva [7])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Kanishka and Buddhism
1 CONT Gold coin of Kanishka I with a representation of the Buddha (c.120 AD).Obv: Kanishka standing, clad in heavy Kushan coat and long boots, flames emanating from shoulders, holding standard in his left hand, and making a sacrifice ov
1 CONC er an altar. Kushan-language legend in Greek script (with the addition of the Kushan ? "sh" letter): ?????????? ??????? ?????? ("Shaonanoshao Kanishki Koshano"): "King of Kings, Kanishka the Kushan".Rev: Standing Buddha in Helleni
1 CONC stic style, forming the gesture of "no fear" (abhaya mudra) with his right hand, and holding a pleat of his robe in his left hand. Legend in Greek script: ????? "Boddo", for the Buddha. Kanishka monogram (tamgha) to the right.
1 CONT Gold coin of Kanishka I with a representation of the Buddha (c.120 AD).
1 CONT Obv: Kanishka standing, clad in heavy Kushan coat and long boots, flames emanating from shoulders, holding standard in his left hand, and making a sacrifice over an altar. Kushan-language legend in Greek script (with the addition 
1 CONC of the Kushan ? "sh" letter): ?????????? ??????? ?????? ("Shaonanoshao Kanishki Koshano"): "King of Kings, Kanishka the Kushan".
1 CONT Rev: Standing Buddha in Hellenistic style, forming the gesture of "no fear" (abhaya mudra) with his right hand, and holding a pleat of his robe in his left hand. Legend in Greek script: ????? "Boddo", for the Buddha. Kanishka mono
1 CONC gram (tamgha) to the right.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka's reputation in Buddhist tradition is based mainly on the Buddhist tradition that he convened the 4th Buddhist Council in Kashmir.
1 CONT
1 CONT He provided encouragement to both the Gandhara school of Greco-Buddhist Art and the Mathura school of Hindu art (An inescapable religious syncretism pervades Kushana rule). Kanishka personally seems to have embraced both Buddhism 
1 CONC and the Persian cult of Mithra.
1 CONT
1 CONT His greatest contribution to Buddhist architecture was the Kanishka stupa at Peshawar. Archaeologists rediscovered the base of it in 1908-1909 ascertained that this stupa had a diameter of 286 feet. Reports of Chinese pilgrims suc
1 CONC h as Xuan Zang indicate that its height was 600 to 700 (Chinese) "feet" (= roughly 180-210 metres or 591-689 ft.) and was covered with jewels.[8] Certainly this immense multi-storied building ranks among the wonders of the ancient
1 CONC  world.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka is said to have been particularly close to the Buddhist scholar Ashvaghosha, who became his religious advisor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Buddhist coinage
1 CONT
1 CONT The Buddhist coins of Kanishka are comparatively very few (well under one percent of all known coins of Kanishka). Several of them display Kanishka himself on the obverse, and the Buddha standing on the reverse, in Hellenistic sty
1 CONC le. A few also show the Shakyamuni Buddha and Maitreya. Like all coins of Kanishka, their design is rather rough and proportions tend to be imprecise, and the image of the Buddha is slightly corrupted, as seen in the huge oversize
1 CONC  ears, and the feet spread apart in the same fashion as the Kushan king, indicating a rather rough imitation of pre-existing Hellenistic images.
1 CONT
1 CONT Three types of Kanishka's Buddhist coins are known:
1 CONT Known depictions of the "Buddha" (with coin legend ????? "Boddo") in Kanishka's coinage.
1 CONT Known depictions of the "Buddha" (with coin legend ????? "Boddo") in Kanishka's coinage.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Buddha
1 CONT
1 CONT The standing Buddha in Hellenistic style, bearing the mention "Boddo" in Greek script, holding the left corner of his cloack in his hand, and forming the abhaya mudra. Only six Kushan coins of the Buddha are known (the sixth one i
1 CONC s the centerpiece of an ancient piece of jewelry, consisting in a Kanishka Buddha coin decorated with a ring of heart-shaped ruby stones). All these coins were minted in gold under Kanishka I, but are quite small (about the size o
1 CONC f an obol) compared to the other gold coins of Kanishka.
1 CONT Bronze standing Buddha with features similar to those of Kanishka's coins. Gandhara, usually dated 3rd-4th century.
1 CONT Bronze standing Buddha with features similar to those of Kanishka's coins. Gandhara, usually dated 3rd-4th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Buddha is represented wearing the monastic robe, the antaravasaka, the uttarasanga, and the overcoat sanghati.
1 CONT
1 CONT The ears are extremely large and long, a symbolic exageration possibly rendered necessary by the small size of the coins, but otherwise visible in some later Gandharan statues of the Buddha typically dated to the 3rd-4th century C
1 CONC E. He has an abundant topknot covering the usnisha, often highly stylicized in a curly or often globular manner, also visible on later Buddha statues of Gandhara.
1 CONT
1 CONT In general, the representation of the Buddha on these coins is already highly symbolic, and quite distant from the more naturalistic and Hellenistic images seen in early Gandhara sculptures. On several design, a mustache is appare
1 CONC nt. The palm of his right hand bears the Chakra mark, and his brow bear the urna. An aureola, formed by one, two or three lines, surrounds him.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The "Shakyamuni Buddha"
1 CONT Depictions of the "Shakyamuni Buddha" (with legend ???????? ????? "Shakamano Boddo") in Kanishka's coinage.
1 CONT Depictions of the "Shakyamuni Buddha" (with legend ???????? ????? "Shakamano Boddo") in Kanishka's coinage.
1 CONT Depictions of "Maitreya" (with legend ?????G? ????? "Metrago Boddo") in Kanishka's coinage.
1 CONT Depictions of "Maitreya" (with legend ?????G? ????? "Metrago Boddo") in Kanishka's coinage.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Shakyamuni Buddha (with the legend "Sakamano Boudo", ie Shakamuni Buddha, another name for the historic Buddha Siddharta Gautama), standing to front, with left hand on hip and forming the abhaya mudra with the right hand. All 
1 CONC these coins are in copper only, and usually rather worn.
1 CONT
1 CONT The gown of the Shakyamuni Buddha is quite light compared to that on the coins in the name of Buddha, clearly showing the outline of the body, in a nearly transparent way. These are probably the first two layers of monastic clothi
1 CONC ng the antaravasaka and the uttarasanga. Also, his gown is folded over the left arm (rather than being held in the left hand as above), a feature only otherwise known in the Bimaran casket and suggestive of a scarf-like uttariya. 
1 CONC He has an abundant topknot covering the ushnisha, and a simple or double halo, sometimes radiating, surrounds his head.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The "Maitreya Buddha"
1 CONT
1 CONT The Bodhisattva Maitreya (with the legend "Metrago Boudo") cross-legged on a throne, holding a water pot, and also forming the Abhaya mudra. These coins are only known in copper and are badly worn. On the clearest coins, Maitreya 
1 CONC seems to be wearing the armbands of an Indian prince, a feature often seen on the staruary of Maitreya. The throne is decorated with small columns, suggesting that the coin representation of Maitreya was directly copied from pre-e
1 CONC xisting statuary with such well-known features. The qualification of "Buddha" for Maitreya is innacurate, as he is instead a Bodhisattva (he is the Buddha of the future). This may indicate a limited knowledge of Buddhist cosmology
1 CONC  on the part of the Kushans.
1 CONT
1 CONT The iconography of these three types is very different from that of the other deities depicted in Kanishka's coinage. Whether Kanishka's deities are all shown from the side, the Buddhas only are shown frontally, indicating that th
1 CONC ey were copied from contemporary frontal representations of the standing and seated Buddhas in statuary.[9] Both representations of the Buddha and Shakyamuni have both shoulders covered by their monastic gown, indicating that the 
1 CONC statues used as models were from the Gandhara school of art, rather than Mathura.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Kanishka casket
1 CONT The "Kanishka casket", dated to 127 CE, with the Buddha surrounded by Brahma and Indra, and Kanishka standing at the center of the lower part, British Museum.
1 CONT The "Kanishka casket", dated to 127 CE, with the Buddha surrounded by Brahma and Indra, and Kanishka standing at the center of the lower part, British Museum.
1 CONT Remnants of the Kanishka Stupa in Shah-Ji-Ki-Dheri.
1 CONT Remnants of the Kanishka Stupa in Shah-Ji-Ki-Dheri.
1 CONT
1 CONT The "Kanishka casket" or "Kanishka reliquary", dated to the first year of Kanishka's reign in 127 CE, was discovered in a deposit chamber under Kanishka's stupa, during the archeological excavations in 1908-1909 in Shah-ji-Dheri o
1 CONC n the outskirts of Peshawar.[10] [11] It is today at the Peshawar Museum, and a copy is in the British Museum. It is said to have contained three bone fragments of the Buddha, which are now housed in Mandalay, Burma.
1 CONT
1 CONT The casket is dedicated in Kharoshthi. The inscription reads:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "(*mahara)jasa kanishkasa kanishka-pure nagare aya gadha-karae deya-dharme sarva-satvana hita-suhartha bhavatu mahasenasa sagharaki dasa agisala nava-karmi ana*kanishkasa vihare mahasenasa sangharame"
1 CONT
1 CONT Detail of Kanishka, surrounded by the Iranian Sun-God and Moon-God, on the Kanishka casket. British Museum.
1 CONT Detail of Kanishka, surrounded by the Iranian Sun-God and Moon-God, on the Kanishka casket. British Museum.
1 CONT Buddha relics from Kanishka's stupa in Peshawar, Pakistan, sent by the British to Mandalay, Burma in 1910. Teresa Merrigan, 2005
1 CONT Buddha relics from Kanishka's stupa in Peshawar, Pakistan, sent by the British to Mandalay, Burma in 1910. Teresa Merrigan, 2005
1 CONT
1 CONT The text is signed by the maker, a Greek artist named Agesilas, who oversaw work at Kanishka's stupas (caitya), confirming the direct involvement of Greeks with Buddhist realizations at such a late date: "The servant Agisalaos, th
1 CONC e superintendent of works at the vihara of Kanishka in the monastery of Mahasena" ("dasa agisala nava-karmi ana*kaniskasa vihara mahasenasa sangharame").
1 CONT
1 CONT The lid of the casket shows the Buddha on a lotus pedestal, and worshipped by Brahma and Indra. The edge of the lid is decorated by a frieze of flying geese. The body of the casket represents a Kushan monarch, probably Kanishka in
1 CONC  person, with the Iranian sun and moon gods on his side. On the sides are two images of a seated Buddha, worshiped by royal figures. A garland, supported by cherubs goes around the scene in typical Hellenistic style.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Kanishka in Buddhist tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT In Buddhist tradition, Kanishka is often described as a violent, faithless ruler before his conversion to Buddhism, as in the Sri-dharma-pitaka-nidana sutra:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "At this time the King of Ngan-si (Pahlava) was very stupid and of a violent nature….There was a bhikshu (monk) arhat who seeing the evil deeds done by the king wished to make him repent. So by his supernatural force he caused
1 CONC  the king to see the torments of hell. The king was terrified and repented." Sri-dharma-pi?aka-nidana sutra [12]
1 CONT
1 CONT Additionally, the arrival of Kanishka was reportedly foretold by the Buddha, as well as the construction of his stupa:
1 CONT
1 CONT     ". . . the Buddha, pointing to a small boy making a mud tope….[said] that on that spot Ka?i?ka would erect a tope by his name." Vinaya sutra [13]
1 CONT
1 CONT Coin of Kanishka with the Bodhisattva Maitreya "Metrago Boudo".
1 CONT Coin of Kanishka with the Bodhisattva Maitreya "Metrago Boudo".
1 CONT
1 CONT The same story is repeated in a Khotanese scroll found at Dunhuang, which first described how Kanishka would arrive 400 years after the death of the Buddha. The account also describes how Kanishka came to raise his stupa:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "A desire thus arose in [Kanishka to build a vast stupa]….at that time the four world-regents learnt the mind of the king. So for his sake they took the form of young boys….[and] began a stupa of mud....the boys said to [Kanis
1 CONC hka] ‘We are making the Ka?i?ka-stupa.’….At that time the boys changed their form....[and] said to him, ‘Great king, by you according to the Buddha’s prophecy is a Sa?gharama to be built wholly (?) with a large stupa and hither re
1 CONC lics must be invited which the meritorious good beings...will bring." [14]
1 CONT
1 CONT Chinese pilgrims to India, such as Xuanzang, who travelled there around 630 CE also relays the story:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Ka?i?ka became sovereign of all Jambudvipa (Indian subcontinent) but he did not believe in Karma, and he treated Buddhism with contumely. When he was hunting in the wild country a white hare appeared; the king gave a chase an
1 CONC d the hare suddenly disappeared at [the site of the future stupa]….[when the construction of the stupa was not going as planned] the king now lost patience and threw the [project] up….[but] the king became alarmed, as he [realized
1 CONC ] he was evidently contending with supernatural powers, so he confessed his errors and made submission. These two topes are still in existence and were resorted to for cures by people afflicted with diseases." [15]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Transmission of Buddhism to China
1 CONT
1 CONT Main article: Silk Road transmission of Buddhism
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka's expansion into the Tarim Basin probably initiated the transmission of Buddhism to China.
1 CONT
1 CONT Buddhist monks from the region of Gandhara played a key role in the development and the transmission of Buddhist ideas in the direction of northern Asia from the middle of the second century CE. The Kushan monk, Lokaksema (c. 178 
1 CONC CE), became the first translators of Mahayana Buddhist scriptures into Chinese and established a translation bureau at the Chinese capital Loyang. Central Asian and East Asian Buddhist monks appear to have maintained strong exchan
1 CONC ges for the following centuries.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka was probably succeeded by Huvishka. How and when this came about is still uncertain. The fact that there were other Kushana kings called Kanishka is just another complicating factor.
1 CONT
1 CONT The airplane that was destroyed in the 1985 bombing of Air India Flight 182 was named after him.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Kanishka with the divinity Mozdoano.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Coin of Kanishka.
1 CONT
