0 @N6000000002188198271@ NOTE Vima Kadphises
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vima Kadphises (Kushan language:????? ???F????, Ancient Chinese:???) was a Kushan emperor from around 90-100 CE. As detailed by the Rabatak inscription, he was the son of Vima Takto and the father of Kanishka.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vima Kadphises added to the Kushan territory by his conquests in Afghanistan and north-west India.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the first to introduce gold coinage in India, in addition to the existing copper and silver coinage. Most of the gold seems to have been obtained through trade with the Roman Empire. The gold weight standard of approximatel
1 CONC y eight grams corresponds to that of the Roman coins of the 1st century. Gold bullion from Rome would be melted and used for the Kushan mints, into three denominations: the double stater, the stater, and the quarter starter (or di
1 CONC nara).
1 CONT
1 CONT The usage of gold testifies to the prosperity of the Kushan Empire from the time of Vima, being the center of trade between China (where Vima was known as ???), Central Asia and Alexandria and Antioch in the West. The Kushan were 
1 CONC able to maintain and protect the Silk road, allowing silk, spices, textiles or medicine to move between China, India and the West. In particular, many goods were sent by ship to the Roman empire, creating a return flow of gold coi
1 CONC ns, Greek wine and slaves. Works of arts were also imported from all directions, as indicated by the variety and quality of the artefacts found in the Kushan summer capital of Bagram in Afghanistan. A strong artistic syncretism wa
1 CONC s stimulated, as indicated by the Greco-Buddhist art of Gandhara.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman history relates the visit of ambassadors from the Indian kings to the court of Trajan (98-117 CE), bearing presents and letters in Greek, which were sent either by Vima Kadphises or his son Kanishka.
1 CONT
1 CONT Most of Vima's coins feature the Buddhist symbol of the Triratana on the reverse (or possibly Shiva's symbol for Nandi, the Nandipada), together with representations of Shiva, with or without his bull.
